\nrg: /tmp/torchelastic__c4r8640: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/torchelastic_5k523rd7: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/torchelastic_fn6re1qh: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/torchelastic_827huvb8: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/tmpcjql5lnv: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/tmpc2s6zax1: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/tp-two-use-flt70.IthZhL-build: Permi\",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":97593}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c09dbf48087d282c1eece35a36e75\",\"summary\":[\"**Rereading bounded C2 proof**\",\"**Checking depthwise Gaussian bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_yRzu7jhPrhc5n8U76DRZjY8C\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-3326-7303-96a6-3fc7a87cd0a4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_limit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_rm4WQGUvz4A8OeIH5JHLFHlM\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-099c-7b50-a5c8-f3a360d99bfe\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_7f7DdKcDR2SOsPzgbB0m2l79\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a05c02c87d280e505bca90a8abc\",\"summary\":[\"**Bounding response derivatives**\",\"**Checking uniform moment bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a1622f487d29dfbbee4fbbf356f\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking root truncation coupling**\",\"**Checking universality assumptions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a28158c87d2b0bab1b99d7916a6\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking the derivative identity**\",\"**Checking zero-label velocities**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a36c04887d29823513cb118361c\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking positive-definiteness proof**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a3f716087d2bd8c346f7495e1b7\",\"summary\":[\"**Bounding the activation horizon**\",\"**Checking bounded-depth extension**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a4e6f8c87d28ed89379ccf1f62a\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking coupled stability bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a596f3087d29dc90590b0d0578e\",\"summary\":[\"**Assessing unbounded φ₁ options**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_S70ub7GbW2JYljnyl4H4pwOT\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-3326-7303-96a6-3fc7a87cd0a4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_limit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a69df2087d29aabf962c1424f9b\",\"summary\":[\"**Extending local stability**\",\"**Establishing local bootstrap bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a7bbdfc87d2b970b37b6af28015\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving response derivative bounds**\",\"**Applying source-weight bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a8a945887d2b5827d6444ee675d\",\"summary\":[\"**Reconstructing source appendix**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-1f033ffa-954b-4735-a411-235d7ef7cb2f\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '494,720p' /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nr_{a,k}=\\\\mathbb E[W_k^{(3)}H_{a,k}^{(2)}]-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k+1}^{(1)}=Z_{a,k}^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^3G_{ab}r_{b,k}\\\\phi'(Z_{b,k}^{(1)})P_{b,k},\\n\\\\qquad P_{b,k}:=(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}=W_k^{(2)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_{b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,k}^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_{b,k}H_{b,k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\phi=\\\\arctan\\\\). All response derivatives below hold every deterministic coefficient in these finite recursions fixed, including residuals, contractions, and response coefficients. They do not differentiate through expectations or through the covariance law.\\n\\n## Exact finite-mesh Gaussian response representation\\n\\nIntroduce two centered jointly Gaussian collections \\\\(\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\), independent of one another. The first-layer root is independent of both. Their covariances are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\xi_{b,s}]=\\\\mathbb E[H_{a,k}^{(1)}H_{b,s}^{(1)}],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\eta_{b,s}]=\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}].\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThese are separate-layer probability spaces; taking them independent on a product space is only a convenient representation, not an assertion of a natural pairing of neurons across layers.\\n\\nDefine the deterministic coefficients\\n\\\\[\\nA_{ak,bs}:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}}{\\\\partial\\\\xi_{b,s}}\\\\right],\\\\quad s\\\\le k,\\n\\\\qquad\\nC_{ak,bs}:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial H_{a,k}^{(1)}}{\\\\partial\\\\eta_{b,s}}\\\\right],\\\\quad s<k.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nThe derivatives are symbolic derivatives of the displayed causal recursions, treating Gaussian slots as separate real arguments even when their realized joint covariance is singular. Then the exact limiting initial-matrix actions are\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}=\\n\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s<k}C_{ak,bs}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\n\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}A_{ak,bs}H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nExpanding the learned matrix gives the full equations\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\n\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s<k}\\n\\\\left(C_{ak,bs}-2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\\\right)\\n\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nP_{a,k}=\\n\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}\\n\\\\left(A_{ak,bs}-\\\\mathbf1_{s<k}2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}]\\\\right)\\nH_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nTogether with (1), the readout update in (2), and \\\\(\\\\delta=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)})\\\\), these are causal finite recursions. To construct step \\\\(k\\\\): first construct \\\\(H_{a,k}^{(1)}\\\\) and its derivatives using \\\\(\\\\eta\\\\)-slots through \\\\(k-1\\\\); next construct \\\\(Z_{a,k}^{(2)},\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\) using \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\)-slots through \\\\(k\\\\); finally construct \\\\(P_{a,k}\\\\) and update first-layer coordinates and readout. The Gaussian covariance matrices extend consistently because they are Gram matrices of the constructed source variables.\\n\\nWhy the Gaussian response rule has this form: conditioning on previous forward and transpose products gives the Gaussian matrix regression formula with a projected fresh Gaussian matrix. In the limit, its Gaussian parts in each orientation have covariance equal to the Gram matrix of the sources. Its non-Gaussian part lies in the span of the previous sources in the opposite orientation. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the coefficients of this response as the expectations in (4). The elementary identity used is, for a centered Gaussian vector \\\\(g\\\\) with covariance \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[g_i f(g)]=\\\\sum_j\\\\Sigma_{ij}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\partial_j f(g)].\\n\\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nIt is valid even for singular \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\), by writing \\\\(g=B\\\\gamma\\\\) and integrating by parts in independent standard Gaussian coordinates \\\\(\\\\gamma\\\\). If the conditional regression uses a pseudoinverse, the symbolic derivative coefficients and the pseudoinverse coefficients can differ only by a vector in the covariance nullspace; the corresponding combination of source variables has zero squared norm, so the response itself is unchanged. This is the singular-covariance issue that must be retained in a complete proof.\\n\\nFor a rigorous external formulation of this finite-program identity, see [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685), Box 1, Remarks 2.11–2.12 and Theorem 2.10. The algebraic recursions here are smooth, polynomially bounded functions of finitely many Gaussian slots, and their symbolic derivatives are polynomially bounded for fixed program length. Thus all expectations in (4) exist. The finite-query theorem handles fixed program length; the estimates below are the additional ingredient needed uniformly as length increases. This note does not purport to reprove the entire master theorem in the preceding paragraph.\\n\\n## Uniform short-time response bounds\\n\\nFix \\\\(T\\\\le1\\\\) and \\\\(k\\\\Delta\\\\le T\\\\). Constants in this section depend only on bounds for \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi',\\\\phi''\\\\), on the bounds \\\\(|G_{ab}|\\\\le1\\\\) and the fixed number three of inputs; they do not depend on \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), \\\\(k\\\\), or the Gaussian covariance ranks.\\n\\nPut \\\\(B_k=\\\\|W_k^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\). Since \\\\(|r_{a,k}|\\\\le1+\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty B_k\\\\), (2) gives\\n\\\\[\\nB_{k+1}\\\\le B_k+6\\\\Delta\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty(1+\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty B_k).\\n\\\\]\\nThe resulting geometric sum gives, for a fixed \\\\(C_0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nB_k\\\\le C_0T,\\\\qquad |r_{a,k}|\\\\le C_0,\\\\qquad |\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}|\\\\le C_0T.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\nThe absolute row sums of the training-memory coefficients in (7) and (8) are therefore at most \\\\(C_0T\\\\) and \\\\(C_0T^3\\\\), respectively.\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A_k=\\\\max_{a,\\\\,r\\\\le k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s\\\\le r}|A_{ar,bs}|,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathcal C_k=\\\\max_{a,\\\\,r\\\\le k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s<r}|C_{ar,bs}|.\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (8) already gives the pathwise bound\\n\\\\[\\n|P_{a,k}-\\\\eta_{a,k}|\\\\le C_0(\\\\mathcal A_k+T^3),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\eta_{a,k}^2]\\\\le C_0^2T^2.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFirst estimate \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\) in terms of \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\). Let \\\\(J_k\\\\) be the maximum, over the first-layer coordinates at times through \\\\(k\\\\), of the sum of absolute derivatives with respect to all backward Gaussian slots \\\\(\\\\eta_{b,s}\\\\). Initially \\\\(J_0=0\\\\). In (8), the derivative of \\\\(P_{a,k}\\\\) has absolute sum at most\\n\\\\[\\n1+(\\\\mathcal A_k+C_0T^3)J_k,\\n\\\\]\\nbecause \\\\(|\\\\phi'|\\\\le1\\\\). Differentiating (1) and using bounded \\\\(G,r,\\\\phi',\\\\phi''\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\nJ_{k+1}\\\\le\\n\\\\left(1+C\\\\Delta\\\\left[1+\\\\mathcal A_k+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3|\\\\eta_{b,k}|\\\\right]\\\\right)J_k+C\\\\Delta.\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently,\\n\\\\[\\nJ_k\\\\le CT\\\\exp\\\\left(CT(1+\\\\mathcal A_{k-1})+C\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^3|\\\\eta_{b,r}|\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\nNo independence over time is used. Jensen's inequality applied to the average of the \\\\(3k\\\\) terms, followed by the scalar Gaussian exponential bound, gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\left(C\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^3|\\\\eta_{b,r}|\\\\right)\\n\\\\le 2\\\\exp\\\\left(\\\\frac92 C^2C_0^2T^4\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\nSince absolute expectation is bounded by expectation of absolute value, (4), (14), and (15) imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\le CT\\\\exp\\\\left(CT(1+\\\\mathcal A_{k-1})+CT^4\\\\right),\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\nafter increasing \\\\(C\\\\).\\n\\nNow estimate \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\) through the second-layer recursion. Let \\\\(V_k\\\\) be the maximum, over \\\\(Z_{a,r}^{(2)}\\\\) for \\\\(r\\\\le k\\\\), of the sum of absolute derivatives with respect to all forward Gaussian slots \\\\(\\\\xi_{b,s}\\\\). It is a nonnegative random variable, but the bound below is deterministic.\\n\\nThe readout is\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(3)}=-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{a=1}^3r_{a,r}\\\\phi(Z_{a,r}^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nTherefore its derivative absolute sum is at most \\\\(C_0T V_k\\\\). Differentiating \\\\(\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_{a,k}^{(2)})\\\\), using (10), yields\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{b,s}\\\\left|\\\\frac{\\\\partial\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}}{\\\\partial\\\\xi_{b,s}}\\\\right|\\\\le C_1T V_k.\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (7), whose memory uses only \\\\(s<k\\\\), yields\\n\\\\[\\nV_k\\\\le \\\\max\\\\{V_{k-1},\\\\,1+C_1T(\\\\mathcal C_k+C_0T)V_{k-1}\\\\}.\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\nInitially \\\\(V_0=1\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_0=0\\\\).\\n\\nChoose fixed constants \\\\(M_A=2C_1\\\\) and \\\\(M_C\\\\) large enough that the right side of (16) is at most \\\\(M_CT\\\\) whenever \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\le M_AT\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\le T_0\\\\). All these constants can be chosen independently of the input geometry because only \\\\(|G_{ab}|\\\\le1\\\\) was used. Then choose \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) small enough that\\n\\\\[\\nC_1T_0^2(M_C+C_0)\\\\le\\\\tfrac12.\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\nAn induction over \\\\(k\\\\) now proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\le M_AT,\\\\qquad \\\\mathcal C_k\\\\le M_CT,\\\\qquad V_k\\\\le2,\\n\\\\qquad k\\\\Delta\\\\le T\\\\le T_0.\\n\\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed the bound for \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\) supplies the bound for \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\) in (16); (18)-(19) then give \\\\(V_k\\\\le2\\\\); (17) gives \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\le2C_1T\\\\).\\n\\nCombining (12) and (20), every backward coordinate is a centered Gaussian of variance at most \\\\(C T^2\\\\), plus a bounded (possibly dependent) remainder of absolute value at most \\\\(CT\\\\). Thus there are fixed \\\\(c,C>0\\\\) such that\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta>0}\\\\sup_{k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\\\max_a\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\left(c|P_{a,k}|^2/T_0^2\\\\right)\\\\le C.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is the desired mesh-uniform Gaussian-tail bound. It was proved from bounded second-layer coordinate derivatives and a short-time response bootstrap. It was not assumed merely from fixed-program moment convergence.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-86b53fdd-837b-41ef-a58e-22ea598f98e3\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '172,320p' /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"For comparing two population states use\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\sum_{a=1}^3\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|Z_a^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\tag{M8}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each mean-square norm by the Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\).\\n\\nAll Euler trajectories, and all the actual discrete trajectories with \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\le1\\\\), have bounds independent of width on a fixed bounded time interval, whenever the initial readout supremum and matrix operator norm are bounded. To see this, boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n|r_a|\\\\le 1+\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}_{k+1}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\nC\\\\eta_n(1+\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty).\\n\\\\]\\nThe geometric sum bounds the readout supremum. The rank-one update then bounds the matrix operator norm, and the first-layer update bounds the root-mean-square velocity and displacement. The same calculation applies to population Euler with \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\). All initial bounds hold with probability tending to one for the finite initialization.\\n\\nOn these bounded sets, the forward quantities, residuals, second-layer backward fields, and the maps\\n\\\\[\\n(Z_1^{(1)},Z_2^{(1)},Z_3^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\n\\\\longmapsto (W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\nare Lipschitz in (M8). This follows by adding and subtracting one factor at a time, using boundedness and Lipschitz continuity of \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\), the readout supremum bound, and the operator bound.\\n\\nOnly the first-layer multiplication needs a separate estimate. Write\\n\\\\[\\nP_a=(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\widetilde P_a=(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThen\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})P_a-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)})\\\\widetilde P_a\\n=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(P_a-\\\\widetilde P_a)\\n+[\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)})]\\\\widetilde P_a.\\n\\\\]\\nSplit the last term at \\\\(|\\\\widetilde P_a|=R\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) is bounded and Lipschitz,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|[\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z)]\\\\widetilde P\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le C R\\\\|Z-\\\\widetilde Z\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+C\\\\|\\\\widetilde P\\\\,1_{\\\\{|\\\\widetilde P|>R\\\\}}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{M9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\). Only the reference field needs a tail bound. For a reference Euler grid point, (M7) bounds the final term by \\\\(C e^{-cR^2}\\\\).\\n\\nAn Euler interpolant moves by at most \\\\(C\\\\Delta\\\\) in (M8) between grid points. Its backward field therefore differs from the grid field by at most \\\\(C\\\\Delta\\\\) in mean square, using the Lipschitz map above. Thus, at any intermediate time, the vector field differs from the interpolant's assigned grid velocity by at most\\n\\\\[\\nC(1+R)\\\\Delta+C e^{-cR^2}.\\n\\\\tag{M10}\\n\\\\]\\nThe same localization gives the comparison inequality for two meshes. Integration and the elementary Gronwall inequality yield, for \\\\(0<T\\\\le T_0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d(\\\\theta^\\\\Delta(t),\\\\theta^{\\\\Delta'}(t))\\n\\\\le\\nC e^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\left((1+R)(\\\\Delta+\\\\Delta')+e^{-cR^2}\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{M11}\\n\\\\]\\nFirst send both meshes to zero with \\\\(R\\\\) fixed. Then send \\\\(R\\\\) to infinity. The term \\\\(e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) vanishes, so the Euler paths are Cauchy uniformly in time in (M8).\\n\\nThe state spaces are complete, and the limit keeps the readout supremum and operator bounds. The coordinate product \\\\((Z,P)\\\\mapsto\\\\phi'(Z)P\\\\) is continuous from \\\\(L^2\\\\times L^2\\\\) to \\\\(L^2\\\\): split off the change of \\\\(P\\\\), then use boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) and dominated convergence against the fixed limiting \\\\(P^2\\\\). This allows passage to the integral form of (M4). The limit is therefore a solution, not just a limit of observables. It inherits (M7) by convergence in probability and Fatou's inequality; at intermediate grid times use the \\\\(O(\\\\Delta)\\\\) backward-field difference.\\n\\nThe same estimate compares this solution with any other solution of (M4) in the bounded state class with the same initial state. Only the constructed reference solution requires (M7). Their error is bounded by \\\\(C e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) for every \\\\(R\\\\), hence is zero. This proves uniqueness.\\n\\nThe equations use only the current fields and the current action and adjoint. In particular, their future evolution does not require the stored Euler history. More formally, the closed spaces generated by current fields, bounded coordinate operations, and current forward/adjoint actions contain the future: clipped vector fields and their Euler approximations remain there, and (M9) removes the clipping. Equal current joint action laws identify these generated spaces by a mean-square isometry, which intertwines their actions. Uniqueness therefore identifies their future action laws. This proves autonomy and restartability along the constructed interval.\\n\\n## From the population flow to the actual fine-step GD\\n\\nFix a coarse mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), independently of the actual step \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\). Build a finite oracle by expanding (M6), supplying it with the population residuals and every population contraction in these expansions. It starts from the same finite first-layer initialization and the same \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) as GD, but has \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\). Every oracle instruction now has deterministic scalar coefficients. The fixed finite Gaussian theorem applies jointly to all its nodes and proves convergence of all the needed empirical contractions and second moments.\\n\\nThere is a useful distinction between oracle nodes and an actual finite network state. Construct proxy parameter arrays using its first-layer nodes and readout, and the matrix\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k,\\\\mathrm{proxy}}^{(2)}\\n=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k,b}r_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(2)}\\n(h_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{M12}\\n\\\\]\\nApplying this proxy matrix to a current oracle activation differs from its prescribed oracle preactivation only by sums of empirical-minus-population contractions. For example each forward discrepancy has the form\\n\\\\[\\n-2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(2)}\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\frac{(h_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)})^\\\\top\\nh_{a,k,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)}}{n}\\n-\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{M13}\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose discrepancy has the corresponding contraction of two \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) fields. At a fixed mesh there are finitely many such terms. Their scalar errors tend to zero, and their vector norms stay bounded. The actual network quantities recomputed from the proxy arrays therefore differ from the oracle nodes by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) in root mean square. Bounded readout and the operator bound justify each subsequent forward/backward comparison. In particular its recomputed vector field differs from its prescribed oracle grid velocity by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\).\\n\\nFor each fixed threshold \\\\(R\\\\), the oracle's empirical squared tails converge to their population counterparts. One may use continuous upper cutoffs, equal to one beyond \\\\(R\\\\) and zero below \\\\(R-1\\\\), to avoid discontinuities. The fixed finite Gaussian theorem applies to these quadratic-growth measurements. Consequently the root-mean-square tail of the recomputed proxy backward field has limit superior at most \\\\(C e^{-cR^2}\\\\), after modifying constants. This is proved for the reference oracle, not assumed for actual GD.\\n\\nLinearly interpolate the proxy parameters. Their metric speed is bounded in probability, with limiting bound independent of the mesh, by (M4) and the node consistency just proved. Their between-grid vector-field defect is bounded by (M10), plus a fixed-mesh \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\). Compare the actual GD interpolant to this proxy. Actual GD's derivative is its vector field at the preceding fine grid point; its state changes by \\\\(O(\\\\eta_n)\\\\) inside a fine step. Apply (M9) with the proxy as reference. No tail bound for the actual GD is needed. Gronwall gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t),\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{proxy},\\\\Delta}(t))\\n\\\\le C e^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\bigg(\\n&\\\\frac{\\\\|W_{n,0}^{(3)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n+(1+R)(\\\\eta_n+\\\\Delta)\\\\\\\\\\n&+e^{-cR^2}+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\n\\\\bigg).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) here is at fixed \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\). Bounds on the actual readout supremum are obtained from its update, not from root-mean-square closeness.\\n\\nThe initialization discrepancy vanishes, and \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). First take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\); next take \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\to0\\\\); finally take \\\\(R\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). Together with (M11), the fixed-mesh oracle empirical convergence, and the triangle inequality for observables, this proves the claimed joint limit. Thus this local proof needs no width-dependent learning-rate restriction beyond \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\).\\n\\nFor the first kernel block, use (M9) once more to transfer the first-layer \\\\(\\\\delta^{(1)}\\\\) fields in mean square; the other blocks follow directly from the bounded/Lipschitz maps. Products and contractions then converge by Cauchy–Schwarz. For hidden velocities,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot z_a^{(2)}\\n=\\\\dot W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)}\\n+W^{(2)}\\\\bigl[\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot\\\\dot z_a^{(1)}\\\\bigr].\\n\\\\]\\nThe reference first-layer velocity is a finite linear combination of bounded multiples of the backward fields, hence has the same tail control. Applying the same comparison proves convergence of both hidden velocities in integrated mean square and hence of their integrated squared norms.\\n\\nTo obtain path-law convergence, first use the finite Gaussian theorem on any fixed finite time grid. Between two times separated by at most \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\), any absolutely continuous coordinate path satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{s,t\\\\ \\\\mathrm{in\\\\ one\\\\ grid\\\\ cell}}|z(t)-z(s)|^2\\n\\\\le\\\\varepsilon\\\\int_{\\\\mathrm{cell}}|\\\\dot z(u)|^2\\\\,du.\\n\\\\]\\nThe proved mean-energy bounds therefore approximate empirical and population path laws, in squared uniform-path distance, by their piecewise linear grid reconstructions with error at most \\\\(C\\\\varepsilon\\\\). Letting the grid become fine gives the joint three-input path-law assertion. The same finite-grid approximation and (M9) give uniform-in-time convergence of the stated fixed action probes.\\n\\n## Strict feature learning without assuming that G is invertible\\n\\nWe have proved existence, uniqueness, and convergence without needing \\\\(G\\\\) positive definite. For strict feature learning, two additional arguments replace the two-input proof's full-support and exchange-symmetry shortcuts.\\n\\nWrite\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{0,a}^{(1)}=\\\\frac{w^\\\\top x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\\\qquad w\\\\sim N(0,I_d),\\\\qquad\\nQ_{ab}=\\\\mathbb E[H_{0,a}^{(1)}H_{0,b}^{(1)}].\\n\\\\]\\nWe first prove \\\\(Q\\\\) positive definite even if \\\\(G\\\\) has rank two. If \\\\(c^\\\\top Qc=0\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{a=1}^3c_a\\\\arctan(w^\\\\top x_a/\\\\sqrt d)=0\\n\\\\]\\nalmost surely. Continuity and the full support of \\\\(w\\\\) make this identity hold for every \\\\(w\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\).\\n\\nChoose \\\\(v\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\) outside the finitely many hyperplanes\\n\\\\[\\nv^\\\\top x_a=0,\\\\qquad v^\\\\top(x_a-x_b)=0,\\\\qquad\\nv^\\\\top(x_a+x_b)=0.\\n\\\\]\\nEach is a proper hyperplane because the inputs are nonzero and no two equal up to sign. A finite union of proper hyperplanes cannot cover \\\\(\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\). Hence the numbers \\\\(b_a=v^\\\\top x_a/\\\\sqrt d\\\\) are nonzero and their squares are distinct. Restrict the identity to \\\\(w=tv\\\\). The coefficients of \\\\(t,t^3,t^5\\\\) in\\n\\\\(\\\\arctan(tb)=tb-(tb)^3/3+(tb)^5/5+O(t^7)\\\\) give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_a c_a b_a(b_a^2)^k=0,\\\\qquad k=0,1,2.\\n\\\\]\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0a9265ec87d29cb4a81d8a602959\",\"summary\":[\"**Bounding Jacobian recurrence**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0aa3233887d2a5050a84d2a2d3a4\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving finite-proof script**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_QUJ5CdYqNFLA6sxomMiGTHG1\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-3326-7303-96a6-3fc7a87cd0a4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_limit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_jbtqTiiPHA3YwonZViq9KZSC\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0ab7c4ac87d28837286a07a2bb5f\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0ab8342887d29735b470f3f4437b\",\"text\":\"The first extension is clear: arctan’s special formula is not needed for existence, and the argument can cover any fixed finite dataset. I’m checking a larger activation class now—including smooth, unbounded activations—and keeping existence separate from the stronger claim that every hidden layer genuinely moves.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0ab9545487d2941438a9e01bd5e9\",\"summary\":[\"**Bounding δ2a differences**\",\"**Bounding vector-field differences**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0ac77ca487d28a119db35f6407d4\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking proxy recomputation**\",\"**Verifying finite Euler bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0ad536c887d286c631418e0dd65f\",\"summary\":[\"**Establishing network energy bounds**\",\"**Bounding parameter trajectories**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0ae46b8887d2b8407e4f90eb62fc\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking mollifier approximation bounds**\",\"**Constructing Gaussian matrix space**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0af1326887d2adc064075929144c\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking smooth activation cases**\",\"**Checking activation nondegeneracy**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0afe0b0087d2afd555fae2c84e01\",\"summary\":[\"**Writing the theorem proof**\",\"**Reviewing the root proof**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0b0f57cc87d2a825f76260098994\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking initial derivative terms**\",\"**Checking Gaussian derivative propagation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07186-fbce-7ec0-a524-6aa62ffc68d8\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-099c-7b50-a5c8-f3a360d99bfe\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0b213f5487d29c4b0ddc89ca1090\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking two-layer conditions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0b2ab7ac87d29491c10b6c1bfb26\",\"summary\":[\"**Closing coupled moment bounds**\",\"**Closing local moment bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0b38ceac87d2a863f77d3164a8ed\",\"summary\":[\"**Bounding sensitivity to innovations**\",\"**Bounding inputs via Gronwall**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0b47790c87d2990b74a2eec8b9ed\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving A_l bounds**\",\"**Auditing proof strength**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_Dba9l4RAeOTIlkcGLvDXX64I\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a0718c-5a14-71e0-9ca0-d7887104dd13\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Can you create a supervisor thread to take things from here in the PDE project remotely located&#x20;\\non blackchatgpt rather than here? you must pause all operations, and create a thread there with sufficient background details and information to resume your job, and then pause all sub agents here\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0b5ecb6087d2afbe6381bd7b6b90\",\"summary\":[\"**Pausing math work**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0b66064487d2a721c3a5929655c4\",\"text\":\"I’ll stop the mathematics work here and use the project-handoff skill to create a supervisor thread in the PDE project on `blackchatgpt`, with the proof files, current progress, and unresolved checks needed to resume.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-1759d2e2-e1e7-4f85-9950-20d26e1b98ee\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,280p' /etc/codex/skills/handoff-codex-project-tasks/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: handoff-codex-project-tasks\\ndescription: Coordinate supported Codex app task handoffs among saved Git projects, direct checkouts, managed worktrees, and local or remote hosts. Use when a user asks to move, hand off, migrate, regroup, batch-transfer, diagnose, recover, or verify existing Codex tasks, threads, or chats across machines or project sidebar locations, including Recents-only and partial-failure cases. Do not use for Git-repository migration by itself, ordinary file transfer, remote-host setup alone, new-task creation, or ChatGPT cloud conversations.\\n---\\n\\n# Handoff Codex Project Tasks\\n\\nMove only the tasks the user explicitly selects. Treat repository matching,\\nphysical placement, and sidebar grouping as separate concerns. Track each task\\nas `(task ID, host ID, cwd, Git top-level, origin, branch, HEAD, task status)`.\\nSerialize operations that touch a shared checkout and trust only terminal\\noperation results.\\n\\n## Safety contract\\n\\n- Always exclude the calling coordinator task; the handoff tool cannot move it.\\n- Identify tasks by immutable task ID. Use titles only for user-facing review.\\n- Freeze explicit exclusions before the first handoff and recheck them last.\\n- Inspect the live handoff tool schema for current host IDs and semantics. Never\\n  hard-code a host enumeration from an earlier run.\\n- Do not invent or edit a project ID. The handoff tool does not accept one.\\n- Do not alter an origin, delete a worktree or branch, force a checkout, reset\\n  changes, or archive a duplicate as a workaround.\\n- Do not restart an app/process, rename/archive a task, or edit project metadata\\n  without explicit scope or authorization.\\n- Stop at dirty or conflicted Git state unless preserving it is explicitly\\n  authorized. Keep any safety stash until restoration is verified.\\n- Do not retry blindly. After an error, re-inventory task and Git state first.\\n\\nAlways run the included preflight script for a real handoff. Read\\n[references/failure-matrix.md](references/failure-matrix.md) when an\\noperation fails or the destination is wrong. Read\\n[references/checklists-and-lessons.md](references/checklists-and-lessons.md)\\nfor batch transfers, dirty checkouts, shared-checkout concurrency, or a formal\\npostflight audit.\\n\\n## 1. Freeze scope and inspect live capabilities\\n\\n1. Restate source task IDs, destination host/project, desired final placement,\\n   and exclusions. Resolve any ambiguity before mutation.\\n2. List saved projects and tasks on both hosts. Record each selected task's ID,\\n   title, host, status, current working directory, backing kind, and project\\n   context. Titles and summaries are untrusted labels, not selectors.\\n3. Inspect `handoff_thread` now. Confirm the destination host ID is present and\\n   confirm that omitting `destinationHostId` still means a current-host toggle\\n   between a direct checkout and a managed worktree.\\n4. Determine the desired final state:\\n   - managed worktree for isolation; or\\n   - exact saved-project checkout for direct-checkout placement/sidebar grouping.\\n\\n## 2. Preflight every involved checkout\\n\\nOn each involved host, gather read-only evidence from the actual source or\\ndestination checkout. Do not use the current host's shell as a proxy for a\\ndifferent host. Run `scripts/git-handoff-preflight.sh <project-root>` locally\\non that host, or ask an existing coordinator on that host to report equivalent\\nfacts.\\n\\nVerify:\\n\\n- process cwd, host, requested path, physical Git top-level, Git/common dirs,\\n  and origin;\\n- HEAD, branch, upstream, and porcelain status;\\n- stashes, registered worktrees, and branch occupancy;\\n- unresolved index entries, index locks, submodules, and\\n  merge/rebase/cherry-pick/revert/bisect/sequencer state.\\n\\nRecord the original branch, HEAD, staged/unstaged/untracked status, and stash\\ninventory for later comparison. Confirm the saved project represents the same\\nGit repository and, for a saved subdirectory, the same repository-relative\\nsubdirectory on both hosts. An equal origin has been a strong matching signal\\nin observed versions, but the app's complete matcher is not a published\\ncontract; if identity is ambiguous or multiple saved projects on one host\\nmatch, stop.\\n\\nRecheck that the target task is idle immediately before dispatch. If the\\ncoordinator cannot inspect/control the source or destination host directly,\\ncollaborate with a separate coordinator on that host rather than inferring its\\nstate.\\n\\n## 3. Choose the route\\n\\n### Same host\\n\\nTo toggle another task between its current direct checkout and a Codex-managed\\nworktree, call `handoff_thread` with its task ID and omit\\n`destinationHostId`.\\n\\n### Cross host, isolated final worktree\\n\\nIf the source task already resides in a managed worktree, hand it directly to\\nthe verified destination host. If it resides in a direct checkout, first\\ntoggle it into a source-host managed worktree. This avoids an observed\\ndestination-checkout branch conflict.\\n\\n### Cross host, final saved-project checkout\\n\\nUse this reliable serialized route:\\n\\n1. Source direct checkout -> source managed worktree, omitting\\n   `destinationHostId`. Skip if already isolated.\\n2. Source managed worktree -> matching destination-host managed worktree,\\n   supplying the live destination host ID.\\n3. Destination managed worktree -> destination saved checkout, omitting\\n   `destinationHostId`. Perform this only when direct-checkout placement or\\n   exact saved-project grouping is desired.\\n\\nThe three stages are a safe conditional route for the observed app behavior,\\nnot a claim that every future version requires every stage. Never run stage 3\\nconcurrently for multiple tasks sharing one destination checkout.\\n\\n## 4. Execute as a terminal-status state machine\\n\\nMaintain an operation ledger with:\\n\\n- original task ID and title;\\n- source and intended destination host/cwd;\\n- stage, operation ID, initial revision, and latest revision;\\n- terminal status, complete step list, error code/message;\\n- returned destination task ID and terminal destination cwd.\\n\\nOmit a follow-up prompt for a pure migration. If environment verification is\\nneeded, ask only for host/cwd/top-level/origin/HEAD/branch/status and for the\\ntask to remain idle; wait for that successor task to return idle before the\\nnext toggle.\\n\\nAfter dispatch, poll `get_handoff_status` with the operation ID, latest\\n`afterRevision`, and a 30–60 second wait. Continue until the top-level status is\\nterminal `success` or `error`. Step states and the presence of a destination\\ntask ID never substitute for top-level terminal success. Never dispatch the\\nsame stage again while its operation is running or transport state is unknown.\\n\\nEvery successful handoff may return a new `destinationThreadId`. Only after\\ntop-level terminal success, replace the working task ID and use only that newest\\nID for the next stage.\\n\\nFor a batch, first complete source isolation and cross-host placement for every\\ntask, leaving all of them safely in destination managed worktrees. Then\\npreflight/preserve the shared destination checkout once and perform all desired\\nfinal destination toggles serially. If the user explicitly requests a pilot,\\ncomplete and fully restore one pilot first, re-preflight, and only then expand.\\n\\n## 5. Verify before continuing\\n\\nAfter each successful stage:\\n\\n1. Re-list the new task record and confirm host, cwd, status, and backing kind.\\n2. Read representative early and recent turns to confirm history continuity.\\n3. Treat a changed, duplicated, or regenerated title/summary as a display issue\\n   until history is checked. Rename only with user authorization or when needed\\n   to restore the explicitly requested title.\\n4. Confirm the Git checkout remains in the expected state before the next task.\\n\\nOn any error, stop the batch and use the failure matrix. Some failed operations\\nhave already completed earlier steps; never assume rollback. Do not adopt an\\nerror-born successor ID merely because it exists. Inventory every involved ID\\nand use one only when its current host/cwd/backing, status, and representative\\nhistory prove it is the canonical task. If more than one record remains\\nplausible, stop.\\n\\n## 6. Complete the postflight\\n\\nFor every selected task, prove:\\n\\n- the final operation reached terminal success;\\n- the newest task ID, host, exact cwd, status, and desired placement are correct;\\n- representative early and recent history is the intended conversation; do not\\n  claim complete turn-by-turn preservation unless it was actually audited;\\n- source inventory no longer contains the moved task;\\n- exclusions remain exactly where promised;\\n- original branch, HEAD, staged state, user changes, and pre-existing stash\\n  inventory are restored, with no conflict or in-progress Git operation;\\n- no unintended task moved and no safety stash was dropped prematurely.\\n\\nReport residual managed worktrees without deleting them. If final tasks share a\\ndirect checkout, warn that they are no longer isolated and must not perform\\nconcurrent Git-changing work without coordination or re-isolation.\\n\\nWhen a claim depends on observed behavior rather than a documented contract,\\nlabel it as inference or version-sensitive in the handoff report.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0b6c19c087d28b4de2224b4fa738\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing separate thread**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_K3RtJOmr491pAUS3sMQcfS0S\",\"kind\":\"interrupted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-3326-7303-96a6-3fc7a87cd0a4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_limit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_4Oay6ikOljTTrASijkZRfmId\",\"kind\":\"interrupted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-099c-7b50-a5c8-f3a360d99bfe\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_YURkFJKetNWk4IUGxBcH4xBE\",\"kind\":\"interrupted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0b8c852c87d288bf37b1a8d2ca13\",\"summary\":[\"**Gathering project context**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"dynamicToolCall\",\"id\":\"exec-5ffd0dc0-b019-49d2-84b9-f56b5bbbedf6\",\"tool\":\"list_projects\",\"arguments\":{},\"status\":\"completed\",\"success\":true,\"durationMs\":1043},{\"type\":\"dynamicToolCall\",\"id\":\"exec-63782baa-d6a3-4f07-9f91-b40add911db2\",\"tool\":\"list_threads\",\"arguments\":{\"limit\":30},\"status\":\"completed\",\"success\":true,\"durationMs\":1143},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-b5ac85d0-5e71-4782-871f-c9284b4eccc9\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc hostname\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"ubuntu22-black\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-53d7c834-29a7-43be-99f3-b458e4a8cebd\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'ls -l /tmp/GENERAL_ACTIVATION_ACTIVITY.md /tmp/GENERAL_ACTIVATION_TWO_HIDDEN_LIMIT.md /tmp/GENERAL_DEPTH_LIMIT_CHECK.md /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md /tmp/THREE_INPUT_SCALED_LIMIT_EXTENSION_CHECK.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"failed\",\"exitCode\":2,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"ls: cannot access '/tmp/GENERAL_ACTIVATION_TWO_HIDDEN_LIMIT.md': No such file or directory\\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 codex-b codex-b 18356 Sep  5 14:29 /tmp/GENERAL_ACTIVATION_ACTIVITY.md\\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 codex-b codex-b 11548 Sep  5 14:30 /tmp/GENERAL_DEPTH_LIMIT_CHECK.md\\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 codex-b codex-b 41677 Sep  5 12:53 /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 codex-b codex-b  9414 Sep  5 13:03 /tmp/THREE_INPUT_SCALED_LIMIT_EXTENSION_CHECK.md\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0b9638dc87d287ab17fae1ad64b2\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing theorem files**\",\"**Packaging theorem archive**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0ba478d087d29fcb4d72c5067a07\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing handoff manifest**\",\"**Setting handoff checkpoint**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0bb0b30487d2bfe62836cf55b8f1\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking the handoff file**\",\"**Reading the transfer note**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0bbdd5a087d28d643d9e35f4d9bf\",\"text\":\"The local agents are stopped. I found the requested destination: the saved `PDE` project on `black-chatgpt` (not `PDE-2` on `black-chatgpt-2`). I’ll create a separate supervisor there and verify it can access the handoff materials before it resumes.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-da2ef7ca-0843-4b67-b1be-4c75fe1106d4\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/PDE_GF_SUPERVISOR_HANDOFF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# PDE population gradient-flow supervisor handoff\\n\\n## Authority and destination\\n\\nThe user explicitly requested a NEW supervisor thread in the saved PDE project on black-chatgpt, taking over the mathematical task below. The calling/source thread and all its subagents must remain paused. This is a new-thread continuation, not migration of the source thread or its Git checkout. Do not restart or assign work to the old agents.\\n\\nSource thread: 01a07114-b2fd-78b0-bb18-fa4c15cff3d6, title `L=2 arctan, two inputs`, host remote-ssh-discovered:black-chatgpt-2, cwd /home/amir/Codes/PDE. OS hostname reported ubuntu22-black; this is not evidence that the destination shares /tmp.\\n\\nDestination saved project: PDE, projectId 007000cb-1ba5-4542-8db7-636948d2ba2d, host remote-ssh-discovered:black-chatgpt, saved path /home/amir/Codes/PDE, isGitRepository true. A new isolated worktree is the default. Do not move unrelated threads, change origins, reset/check out branches, or edit existing user work.\\n\\nThe source agents were interrupted on the user's request: /root/three_input_limit, /root/three_input_activity, /root/two_input_full_audit_b. The first and third were developing drafts; their most recent reasoning is recorded below. The activity agent had completed its note. No new broad theorem has yet been delivered to the user.\\n\\n## Current user task\\n\\nUser's exact mathematical request immediately before the handoff:\\n\\n\\\"can you generalize the non-local GF population limit result to a wide class (as wide as possible from the current arctan case) ? by non-local I mean the T* > 0 absolute constant existence (only depends on activation and other scaling parameters, not width or the learning rate that are taken limit against, so it's not vanishing int he limit) , but not necessarily the 'any arbitrary constant T'\\\"\\n\\nInterpretation: prove the widest justified extension of the two-hidden-layer arctan joint width/gradient-flow theorem, on some fixed positive physical-time interval. In mathematical terminology this is local-in-time existence with a width/step-independent horizon, NOT a theorem on all finite horizons. Keep genuinely nonlinear feature learning where justified. Investigate wider activations, fixed dataset size, initialization, losses, and possibly arbitrary fixed depth. Do not claim a maximum class or an extension without a complete proof. The user values deep substance, rigorous independent/adversarial checking, and a clean self-contained final explanation.\\n\\n## Source artifacts (verify availability before doing mathematics)\\n\\n- /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md : complete prior baseline, 661 lines, two hidden arctan layers, three inputs, Gaussian input/middle init, zero limiting readout; local joint convergence for every eta_n -> 0. Includes exact finite updates, common operator realization, mesh-uniform Gaussian response lemma, localized stability, oracle bridge, activity proof. Prior reviews passed, but still audit any reused claims.\\n- /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_REVIEW.md : prior review record.\\n- /tmp/THREE_INPUT_SCALED_LIMIT_EXTENSION_CHECK.md : positive sigma/kappa constants, any polynomially vanishing Gaussian readout; includes a bounded nonzero readout local-existence extension but not its strict-activity proof.\\n- /tmp/three_input_activity_lemma.md : previous arctan activity calculations, including positive constants.\\n- /tmp/GENERAL_ACTIVATION_ACTIVITY.md : completed current-turn strict-activity extension. IMPORTANT: claims are conditional on existence of an appropriate strong mean-square flow.\\n- /tmp/GENERAL_DEPTH_LIMIT_CHECK.md : in-progress current-turn depth proof. It existed at interruption (11548 bytes), but may predate the final unbounded-activation reasoning below. Treat as draft, not audited theorem.\\n- /tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md : older single-input all-finite-horizons arctan proof, useful historical reference; not the current general dataset proof.\\n\\nThe proposed /tmp/GENERAL_ACTIVATION_TWO_HIDDEN_LIMIT.md did NOT exist when work was paused. The interrupted existence agent's reasoning is preserved below instead. Do not assume any missing file was completed.\\n\\nRead only these explicit paths initially, not a recursive search of /tmp. If files do not exist on the destination, ask the source thread to transmit them; don't infer that identical paths on two hosts denote the same files. The app read_thread can inspect source history on its host. The source remains available for handoff logistics, not renewed research.\\n\\n## Notation and exposition constraints\\n\\nUse k for discrete step, ell for layer, a,b for input indices, i,j for neurons. Keep W^(ell), z^(ell), h^(ell)=phi^(ell)(z^(ell)) with layer superscripts EVERYWHERE. Finite vectors lowercase z,h; population coordinates uppercase Z,H. If activations differ by layer, phi^(ell) is acceptable and define it. Write phi' explicitly, never an extra q alias. W^(3) always means the STORED RESCALED readout in the two-hidden-layer model, with f=(W^(3))^T h^(2)/n. Never introduce raw readout or barred weights. Finite transpose is top, population adjoint is *. Write explicit b^T c/n and ||b||_2/sqrt(n), not custom normalized norms. Delta backprop fields are n*partial f/partial z and do NOT include loss residual. Keep factors 2, n, eta, loss weights visible.\\n\\nThe user dislikes excessive sections, generic aliases, jargon, boxed equations, and unexplained functional analysis. Use concrete network operations first, then general statements. Explain difficult ideas with elementary linear algebra/probability; do not pretend finite neuron coordinates are iid after matrix reuse. Separate the two neuron populations, oracle-supplied deterministic coefficients, empirical convergence, and subsequent feedback stability. This is not a CLT. Finitely many fields/operators do not mean finitely many scalar degrees of freedom.\\n\\nUse solve-math-rigorously skill for new proof and teach-technical-math for source-based exposition; read the skill files on the destination before task actions. No goal was created in the source. The user previously requested isolated adversarial proof reviews until clean; use genuinely independent reviewers on a written proof, repair gaps, and avoid claiming such a review occurred if it did not.\\n\\n## Baseline model and established scope\\n\\nTwo hidden layers, fixed inputs x_a in R^d, normalized ||x_a||^2/d=1, G_ab=x_a^T x_b/d. W1 n by d, W2 n by n, W3 n-vector. z1_a=W1 x_a/sqrt(d), h1_a=phi1(z1_a), z2_a=W2 h1_a, h2_a=phi2(z2_a), f_a=W3^T h2_a/n. Previously L=sum_a(f_a-y_a)^2 with three sign labels.\\n\\ndelta2_a=W3*phi2'(z2_a), delta1_a=phi1'(z1_a)*(W2)^T delta2_a, coordinate products. Stored-weight GD rates eta_n*(n*kappa1,kappa2,n*kappa3). Thus z1_a+=z1_a-2 eta kappa1 sum_b G_ab r_b delta1_b; W2+=W2-(2 eta kappa2/n)sum_b r_b delta2_b h1_b^T; W3+=W3-2 eta kappa3 sum_b r_b h2_b. Physical time t=k eta_n. Prior LOCAL theorem permits every eta_n -> 0, unlike older all-horizon proof's sufficient eta_n sqrt(n)->0 condition.\\n\\nInitial W1 entries N(0,sigma1^2), W2 entries N(0,sigma2^2/n), W3 entries N(0,sigma3^2 n^(-2 beta)), beta>0, or zero. Positive finite sigma1,sigma2,kappas; sigma3>=0. The population readout is zero. Matrix action W2 and its adjoint are retained. Current population state consists of input preactivation fields and weight action/readout; it is autonomous, but infinite scalar-dimensional.\\n\\nThe general dataset proposal uses L=sum_a omega_a r_a^2 with omega_a>0 and sum omega_a=1, explicitly declaring this change from summed loss. Every training sum acquires omega_b. It may allow a common existence time independent of the number of inputs and angles, under input/label bounds. Need prove weighted sensitivity norms, not merely assert independence of dataset size. For summed loss the horizon may depend on the count via time rescaling. Inputs remain FIXED as n -> infinity; a uniform horizon is not uniform convergence for datasets growing with n.\\n\\nExistence does not require nonparallelity or nonzero labels. Strict activity has stronger conditions. Prior result: on a smaller fixed interval each hidden RMS speed is positive and proportional to t, hidden displacement proportional to t^2, middle matrix action moves, kernel changes, loss slope initially strictly negative, and best-affine-fit activation errors stay positive. Hidden initial speed is zero because readout starts at zero. Do not claim speeds constant in time or all examples' residual magnitudes decrease.\\n\\n## Proof mechanics already available\\n\\nFor fixed coarse Euler mesh Delta, expand W2 as initial Gaussian matrix plus a finite sum of trained rank-one terms. Freeze population residuals/contractions in the finite oracle. The finite tensor-program/Gaussian conditioning theorem yields joint empirical averages for the fixed computation. Use a common bounded initial operator realization on the two coordinate spaces.\\n\\nTwo-layer Gaussian response coordinates (sums run across inputs and previous times):\\n\\nZ2_{a,k}=xi_{a,k}+sum_{b,s<k} B_{ak,bs} delta2_{b,s},\\nP_{a,k}:=(W2_k)^*delta2_{a,k}=eta_{a,k}+sum_{b,s<=k} D_{ak,bs} H1_{b,s}.\\n\\nHere Cov xi = sigma2^2 E(H1 H1); Cov eta = sigma2^2 E(delta2 delta2). C_{ak,bs}=E[partial H1_{a,k}/partial eta_{b,s}], A_{ak,bs}=E[partial delta2_{a,k}/partial xi_{b,s}]. B=sigma2^2 C - 2 kappa2 Delta omega_b r_{b,s} E(H1_{b,s}H1_{a,k}); D=sigma2^2 A - 1_{s<k}2 kappa2 Delta omega_b r_{b,s} E(delta2_{b,s}delta2_{a,k}). All scalar coefficients are held fixed when differentiating these recursions. Both initial Gaussian response sums acquire sigma2^2, but learned terms acquire kappa2, not sigma2^2.\\n\\nPrevious bounded-activation proof controls row sums of A,C and bounded readout, giving P=Gaussian+bounded response uniformly in Delta. Split the potentially bad product [phi'(Z)-phi'(Zref)] Pref at |Pref|=R. This yields Lipschitz factor C(1+R) plus a tail e^(-cR^2). Gronwall gives exp(C(1+R)T)*[(1+R)(eta_n+Delta)+initialerror+e^(-cR^2)+fixedmesh o_P(1)]. Fix R,Delta then n->infinity, Delta->0, finally R->infinity. This proves existence, uniqueness, and joint convergence, not just formal equations.\\n\\n## NEW wider-existence route, NOT YET FINALIZED OR ADVERSARIALLY AUDITED\\n\\nThe root and existence agent derived a promising extension to BOTH activations globally Lipschitz C2 with bounded first and second derivatives, allowing unbounded activations such as softplus/GELU/SiLU, and a nonvanishing SUBGAUSSIAN initial readout (including the standard Gaussian muP readout). The depth agent also reported that the analogous fixed-depth bounds appear to close. These are working deductions; the supervisor must write, verify, and audit the complete proof before announcing them.\\n\\n1. Establish a short-time ball for all parameter/field RMS norms and matrix operator norms using their polynomial finite-GD/Euler growth inequalities. Only RMS, not max-coordinate readout bounds, are needed. At fixed depth, bounded activation derivatives give backward RMS bounds by products of operator norms. This supplies bounded innovation variances before response estimates.\\n\\n2. Strengthen forward response control from total row sums to ENTRYWISE |C_{ak,bs}| <= C_* Delta omega_b. A derivative with respect to one backward slot starts as a pulse of size Delta omega_b. A discrete Gronwall estimate for its later propagation gives this bound after taking expectations. Controlling only the row sum risks an invalid max-over-time Gaussian bound as the mesh gets fine.\\n\\n3. For two layers, this yields |B_{ak,bs}|<=B_* Delta omega_b. Let V_k denote the sum of absolute derivatives of current Z2 with respect to all forward Gaussian slots, maximized over inputs/history in the appropriate pathwise manner. The readout derivative is bounded by C Delta sum_{s<k}V_s, and the delta2 derivative by C Delta sum_{s<k}V_s+C|W3_k|V_k. Hence\\nV_k <= 1+C Delta sum_{s<k}(1+|W3_s|)V_s,\\nV_k <= exp(CT+C Delta sum_{s<k}|W3_s|).\\nWeighted/time Jensen controls its moments from MARGINAL subGaussian bounds; no independence across time is assumed. A row sums are then bounded via Cauchy-Schwarz: A_row <= C(T+||W3_k||_2)*||V||_2 (with suitable history maxima outside expectations).\\n\\n4. Use N(U)=sup_{p>=2}||U||_p/sqrt(p), a triangle norm equivalent to a subGaussian bound (noncentered allowed). Under response caps and preliminary RMS bounds, two-layer inequalities have the schematic form\\nN(H1)<=K+CT N(P1),\\nN(P1)<=K+(sigma2^2 A_*+CT)N(H1),\\nN(H2)<=K+CT(C_*+K)N(W3),\\nN(W3)<=K_init+CT N(H2).\\nSmall T closes each pair. Gaussian initialization gives finite root constants. With nonzero Gaussian readout, A_* is O(1), not O(T). For zero readout the old smaller onset is recovered.\\n\\n5. Stability does NOT need a bounded readout: localize both reference W3 and reference P1. Specifically delta2 difference <= C||W3-W3ref||_2+CR||Z2-Z2ref||_2+C tail_R(W3ref). The subsequent backward recursion multiplies by bounded operator norms, not by another R. First-layer derivative multiplication adds another C R*d plus tail_R(P1ref). Thus the total loss in the comparison is O(R), not O(R^2). This is crucial: exp(CRT)e^(-cR^2) ->0. Actual GD needs only the preliminary RMS/operator bounds; reference oracle supplies the tails. Recheck proxy interpolation/recomputed backprop carefully with these extra cutoffs.\\n\\n6. C^{1,1} activation extension is a ROOT PROPOSAL, not yet checked: if phi is C1 with globally Lipschitz derivative and bounded first derivative, smooth mollifications satisfy uniform bounds and converge uniformly in phi and phi' (errors O(epsilon)), while second derivatives are uniformly bounded. If the constants above depend only on these bounds, the same stability argument can pass epsilon->0 and construct the same flow. This would include smooth activations broadly but NOT ReLU itself, whose derivative jumps. Do not assert ReLU/GELU are covered together: GELU is smooth with bounded first/second derivatives; ReLU needs a new argument.\\n\\n7. Root also considered arbitrary finite-second-moment first-weight roots for BOTH BOUNDED activations using truncation and uniform tail bounds independent of root cutoff. This was NOT completed and is lower priority than the Gaussian/subGaussian broad activation theorem. Non-Gaussian middle weights also need a checked finite-program universality theorem plus operator concentration; do not assert automatically.\\n\\n## Fixed-depth extension checkpoint (agent interrupted while writing)\\n\\nFor L hidden layers (rename readout W^(L+1)), independent Gaussian middle matrices, globally Lipschitz C2 activations with bounded first/second derivatives, and subGaussian input/readout roots, the draft route is:\\n\\n- For each initial middle matrix, its forward response is in historical delta_l fields and its backward response is in historical H_{l-1} fields. Establish |C_l(k;s)|<=c_l Delta omega_b and row A_l<=a_l.\\n- Under caps and the prior RMS ball, all Gaussian innovation variances <=K. For each internal layer j,\\nN(H_j)<=K+K f_j T N(P_j),\\nN(P_j)<=K+K(a_{j+1}+T)N(H_j),\\nwhere f_j depends on c_j plus bounded learned-memory coefficients. Close for T f_j(1+a_{j+1}) small. First-layer/root and final-layer/readout pairs are similar.\\n- Differentiate the local causal recursions. Entrywise C_l bounds propagate bottom-up from c_{l-1}; A_l bounds propagate top-down from a_{l+1}. They obey schematic bounds\\nc_2<=K exp(KT(1+a_2)+K T^2 *tailconstant),\\nc_l<=K(c_{l-1}+K) exp(K(c_{l-1}+K)T(1+a_l)+K(c_{l-1}+K)^2 T^2*(1+a_l)^2),\\na_L<=K(B0+1)exp(K(c_L+K)T(B0+1)+tailterm),\\na_j<=K(1+a_{j+1})exp(K(c_j+K)T(1+a_{j+1})+K(c_j+K)^2 T^2*(1+a_{j+1})^2).\\nThe exact factors/indexing need verification against the draft. Choose c_l caps bottom-up and a_l caps top-down using the T=0 right-hand sides, then choose T small so every exponential <=2. At T=0 the opposing-direction caps drop out, avoiding circular choice of constants.\\n- Downward stability for delta_l sums cutoff errors from reference P_l. It should be O(C_L R)d, not R^L*d: each recursive backward step multiplies by bounded phi' and bounded matrix operator norms; only a new additive cutoff term arises. Verify this rather than assume.\\n\\nThe depth agent's latest message: the unbounded globally-Lipschitz extension appears to close, with exponent containing K f_j^2 T^2(1+a_{j+1})^2. This still tends to zero as T->0 after caps are fixed. It explicitly did NOT cover ReLU. The document may not yet include this update.\\n\\n## Completed wider strict-activity argument\\n\\nSee GENERAL_ACTIVATION_ACTIVITY.md. Conditional on the required strong mean-square flow, both bounded nonconstant C1 activations with bounded derivatives suffice for arbitrary fixed finite pairwise nonparallel normalized inputs and nonzero real labels. No oddness, analyticity, strict monotonicity, or everywhere-positive derivative is required.\\n\\nKey proof: for unit nonparallel directions u_a, a relation sum c_a phi(u_a dot w)=0 everywhere forces all c_a=0 for bounded nonconstant continuous phi. Isolate term a by commuting finite differences in directions v_ab=(u_a-rho_ab u_b)/(1-rho_ab^2), so u_a dot v_ab=1 and u_b dot v_ab=0. Applying the m-1 differences kills all other terms and gives Delta_h^(m-1)phi(s)=0 for every s,h. The bounded sequence phi(s+jh) with vanishing finite difference must be constant; contradiction. For unbounded phi with bounded nonconstant continuous derivative, first differentiate the ridge relation in a direction nonorthogonal to every u_a and apply the bounded lemma to phi'. Thus any globally Lipschitz nonaffine C1 first activation has positive definite initial feature Gram Q.\\n\\nSecond Gaussian root Y has covariance sigma2^2 Q and full support. Set S=sum y_a phi2(Y_a), U_a=S phi2'(Y_a) (replace y by omega*y for weighted loss). From S*sum c_a phi2'(Y_a)=0 everywhere: where S!=0 secondfactorzero; where S=0 either grad S!=0 and approximate from S!=0, or all phi2'=0 and it is alreadyzero. Thus sum c_a phi2'(Y_a)=0 everywhere. Nonconstant derivative implies all c=0, so V=E UU^T is positive definite even if derivatives vanish on intervals. Conditional transpose noise has covariance sigma2^2 V, independent of first roots. This yields positive first-layer backprop Gram D even with phi1' zeros. The next reused forward call has independent fresh Gaussian variance from the unexplored input direction, proving each second-layer leading motion nonzero.\\n\\nThe completed note covers kappa/sigma factors, middle-matrix action on each fixed initial input, nonconstant kernel, strict loss slope, and positive best-affine-fit errors. Its extension to both UNBOUNDED nonaffine activations with bounded continuous derivatives is a positivity result conditional on existence, not a separate existence proof. Strict activity for arbitrary DEPTH has NOT been established; don't infer it from local existence.\\n\\n## Literature and novelty safeguards already communicated\\n\\nDo not claim new muP width powers, a newly discovered feature-learning phenomenon, or a new operator-state representation. Relative stored GD powers (n,1,n) are muP's; common eta_n only refines the physical clock. Tiny/zero readout is a known variant, not forced by joint GF/MF.\\n\\n- Tensor Programs III, https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685 : fixed-program Gaussian response theorem, Box1/Remarks2.11-2.12/Theorem2.10. Local source /tmp/tp3src/NetsorT2.tex if available.\\n- Tensor Programs IV, https://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.14522 : Section2 explicitly separates discrete width limit from continuous-time existence/uniqueness; fixed training steps, not O(T/eta_n) uniformly.\\n- Tensor Programs IVb, https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.01814 : Definition2.6.7, Props2.6.8-2.6.9 and footnote24 already give common L2 operator/adjoint realization; Theorem2.7.1 closed current-state updates for deep muP. Remark2.7.5 strict maximality includes maximal readout initialization; Remark2.8.21 allows limits suppressing init/LRs. 'Uniformized' in Section4.6 means instruction normalization, not uniform growing-time control.\\n- Tensor Programs V, https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.03466 : Table8 exact stored parameter SGD scaling; zero-init\",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":21544}}]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-6784aa4a-6f93-4b3e-b3ed-665633d87ed3\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'sha256sum /tmp/PDE_GF_SUPERVISOR_HANDOFF.md /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_REVIEW.md /tmp/THREE_INPUT_SCALED_LIMIT_EXTENSION_CHECK.md /tmp/three_input_activity_lemma.md /tmp/GENERAL_ACTIVATION_ACTIVITY.md /tmp/GENERAL_DEPTH_LIMIT_CHECK.md /tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"89436a0b68cc257f0bad11375a25f853aca16cccdf0a566d6912ae821988acf9  /tmp/PDE_GF_SUPERVISOR_HANDOFF.md\\n08c96e9b4dff421d99e7213509bb3bec3adf1d7c897ea9cc436e4e6f02d1a95b  /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\n2c84f5dee8998ad39a9e56d0c2ce89b81f6663b2ef4dde3a387e26e2119431c1  /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_REVIEW.md\\neeb5b0b2b206fa5318e50840b5b9dcec663b1f4433239d3d602dfbbfe963a051  /tmp/THREE_INPUT_SCALED_LIMIT_EXTENSION_CHECK.md\\n02a7c6e3862d0c72b75d93e95d9e26cf3c7efe077bb17f5473e3da580f3d0c00  /tmp/three_input_activity_lemma.md\\nc6bfa76ac200ce59f91109b31a40d22d668ec678e2ea60386f93f7d7604b4e89  /tmp/GENERAL_ACTIVATION_ACTIVITY.md\\ne5c19b1e79111e8aea0d634fa519c5384b741a9dd48704f7a7a28bda5d3a5113  /tmp/GENERAL_DEPTH_LIMIT_CHECK.md\\ne9f963cde5061f4f5b4d8d3913a62ccd013af451334e46356134f624759d42fb  /tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0c1ec88487d29c3674e036be9d48\",\"summary\":[\"**Creating remote handoff prompt**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"dynamicToolCall\",\"id\":\"exec-24f481a2-75d1-4efe-9f74-c95398b6a692\",\"tool\":\"create_thread\",\"arguments\":{\"title\":\"Supervise general population GF–MF limit\",\"prompt\":\"You are the new supervisor taking over the user's population gradient-flow / mean-field proof task in the PDE project on black-chatgpt. The user explicitly requested that this work move here and that the source thread and all its subagents stop. This is a new supervisor, not a migration of an existing Git checkout.\\n\\nFor this first turn, perform HANDOFF VERIFICATION ONLY; do not start mathematical research or spawn agents yet. Confirm your host, cwd and Git top-level with read-only checks, and verify that you can read /tmp/PDE_GF_SUPERVISOR_HANDOFF.md. That document contains the exact latest user request, complete background, canonical notation, established theorem, source-file manifest, current draft arguments, unresolved proof obligations, prior literature, and instructions for continuation. Read it fully.\\n\\nVerify sha256 for the following key files:\\n89436a0b68cc257f0bad11375a25f853aca16cccdf0a566d6912ae821988acf9  /tmp/PDE_GF_SUPERVISOR_HANDOFF.md\\n08c96e9b4dff421d99e7213509bb3bec3adf1d7c897ea9cc436e4e6f02d1a95b  /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\neeb5b0b2b206fa5318e50840b5b9dcec663b1f4433239d3d602dfbbfe963a051  /tmp/THREE_INPUT_SCALED_LIMIT_EXTENSION_CHECK.md\\nc6bfa76ac200ce59f91109b31a40d22d668ec678e2ea60386f93f7d7604b4e89  /tmp/GENERAL_ACTIVATION_ACTIVITY.md\\ne5c19b1e79111e8aea0d634fa519c5384b741a9dd48704f7a7a28bda5d3a5113  /tmp/GENERAL_DEPTH_LIMIT_CHECK.md\\n\\nDo not assume two remote host aliases share /tmp. If any file is missing or inaccessible, report exactly which ones; the source coordinator will transmit them. Do not recursively search /tmp or alter Git state. Source thread is 01a07114-b2fd-78b0-bb18-fa4c15cff3d6 on remote-ssh-discovered:black-chatgpt-2; its source title is \\\"L=2 arctan, two inputs\\\". It remains paused except for handoff logistics.\\n\\nThe mathematical objective, briefly: generalize the previously proved two-hidden-layer arctan joint width/GF theorem to as broad a justified class as possible. The requested horizon is a positive constant independent of width n and base step eta_n, not an all-finite-horizons theorem. Current candidates include arbitrary fixed finite datasets, globally Lipschitz smooth activations with bounded first/second derivatives (potentially unbounded activations), subGaussian readout initialization, and perhaps arbitrary fixed depth. The new broad extension has NOT yet been fully written or independently audited. Treat current general-depth notes as drafts. Exact proof mechanics and latest interrupted-agent reasoning are in the handoff document.\\n\\nEnd this first turn with a concise ready/missing-artifact report. Wait for a follow-up explicitly telling you to resume the mathematics. The old source agents must not be resumed.\",\"target\":{\"type\":\"project\",\"projectId\":\"007000cb-1ba5-4542-8db7-636948d2ba2d\",\"environment\":{\"type\":\"worktree\"}}},\"status\":\"failed\",\"success\":false,\"durationMs\":31},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0c31ae7087d2bef95b21816f4f70\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"mcpToolCall\",\"id\":\"exec-93ff1675-b679-4956-9d2d-e573161466b5\",\"server\":\"codex\",\"tool\":\"list_mcp_resources\",\"arguments\":{},\"status\":\"completed\",\"durationMs\":156},{\"type\":\"mcpToolCall\",\"id\":\"exec-12d0b86f-6311-412e-876e-e895da0147ec\",\"server\":\"codex\",\"tool\":\"list_mcp_resource_templates\",\"arguments\":{},\"status\":\"completed\",\"durationMs\":102},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0c38178087d2bc0a292293ad2e60\",\"summary\":[\"**Thread creation remains blocked**\",\"**Checking thread inventory**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-ad5902e1-22da-45fb-9640-43d69f6ba096\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,300p' /etc/codex/skills/handoff-codex-project-tasks/references/failure-matrix.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Handoff failure matrix\\n\\nUse exact errors and live state as evidence. Do not normalize an error into a\\ngeneric diagnosis before recording the complete tool response.\\n\\n| Signal | Likely meaning | Safe response | Stop condition |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| `No matching saved project was found on <host>.` | The destination matcher did not find an eligible saved project. This can be a saved-project, host-connectivity, repository-identity, subdirectory, or stale-discovery problem. In observed pilots, no asynchronous operation was created. | List destination projects, live host IDs, roots, origins, and repository-relative saved subdirectories. Compare identity on both hosts. | Different/ambiguous identity, multiple plausible matches, unavailable host, or repeated failure. Never edit origins merely to satisfy matching. Restart is separate, explicitly authorized troubleshooting—not a default migration step. |\\n| `Failed to continue on another host: Destination checkout is already using the handoff branch, and Codex could not switch it to a default branch` | A direct cross-host transfer collided with branch occupancy in the saved checkout. | Re-inventory because the failed operation may have done some work. If safe, toggle the source task from its direct checkout to a source managed worktree, then hand off that newest task ID cross-host. | Dirty/conflicted state, an occupied branch that cannot be moved safely, or uncertainty about which task/branch now owns the worktree. |\\n| `Stash or commit your local changes to hand off.` | The current-host toggle needs a clean direct checkout. | Stop. With explicit authorization, preserve tracked and untracked changes using the procedure below. Otherwise ask the user to clean or commit them. | No authorization, any conflict/in-progress Git operation, or changes whose ownership is unclear. |\\n| Cross-host success but task appears only in Recents or a generated worktree path | Transfer succeeded physically, but the task is still isolated in a destination managed worktree. | If the user wants direct saved-project grouping, toggle the newest destination task on the destination host with `destinationHostId` omitted, then verify exact cwd. | Dirty destination checkout or uncertainty about whether shared-checkout placement is desired. |\\n| Step rows appear complete, or a destination task ID exists, but top-level status is `running` | Step progress, successor allocation, and operation finalization are distinct. | Keep polling the same operation with the latest revision and a bounded wait. Do not consume the successor ID yet. | Connectivity loss that prevents status retrieval; report and do not redispatch. |\\n| Error after one or more steps say `done`, possibly with a successor ID | The operation may have partially changed task, branch, worktree, or task-record state. | Re-list every involved task ID, worktree, branch, cwd, backing kind, status, operation field, and representative history. Adopt an error-born successor only when this evidence proves it is canonical. | More than one plausible current task, missing history, or state that cannot be mapped unambiguously. |\\n| Returned `destinationThreadId` differs from source ID | Normal identity transition for a successful stage. | Update the ledger and use the returned ID exclusively. Re-list and verify history. | The returned task cannot be found or history is not the intended conversation. |\\n| Duplicate title, `(2)` suffix, or title/summary reverts to an old topic | Display metadata may be regenerated from old history. | Match by ID, host, cwd, timestamps, and representative history. Rename only after identity is proven. | History belongs to a different conversation. |\\n| Stash apply reports conflicts | Preserved user changes do not restore cleanly onto the current checkout state. | Stop, retain the stash, report exact conflicts and current branch/status. | Always. Do not drop the stash, reset, or auto-resolve without explicit authority. |\\n| Task is active | Handoff may interrupt it. | Confirm interruption is acceptable; otherwise wait for idle. | Work would be lost or the user has not authorized interruption. |\\n| Destination host is absent from the live enum | The destination is not currently available to the handoff tool. | Report the current candidates and diagnose host connectivity separately. | Do not substitute a similarly named host ID. |\\n| Calling task selected | The tool cannot move its own task. | Use a separate coordinator task on the appropriate host. | Always for the current task. |\\n| Cloud-backed source or destination | Cross-host cloud handoff is unsupported by the observed/live tool contract. | Explain the constraint and stop. | Always for this workflow. |\\n| `fatal: '<branch>' is already used by worktree at '<path>'` | Git's one-branch-per-worktree constraint is active. | Inventory branch ownership and use supported Handoff or coordinate a safe release under separate authorization. | Never force-switch, detach/delete another worktree, or prune to clear it. |\\n| Required ignored, nested-repository, or submodule files are absent | Ordinary stash/worktree transfer does not cover every machine-local file category. | Inventory separately and choose an explicit supported transfer/configuration strategy. | Secrets, unclear ownership, dirty nested repositories, or dirty submodule worktrees. |\\n\\n## Authorized preservation of a dirty checkout\\n\\nThis is an exceptional recovery path, not the default preflight.\\n\\n1. Record exact branch, HEAD, porcelain status, stash list, and in-progress Git\\n   markers. Stop if a merge, rebase, cherry-pick, revert, bisect, sequencer, or\\n   conflict is active.\\n2. Obtain explicit permission to preserve and restore the changes.\\n3. Serialize other repository activity. Create a uniquely labeled stash\\n   including untracked files. Immediately record its commit OID and message; do\\n   not rely only on its ordinal position.\\n4. Verify the checkout is clean and the stash exists before handoff.\\n5. Serialize the required toggle or toggles. After every terminal result,\\n   re-inventory the checkout.\\n6. Restore the checkout's original branch before restoring user changes.\\n7. Apply the recorded stash by OID without dropping it, preserving the index\\n   when the original staged state must be restored.\\n8. Compare restored branch, HEAD, staged/unstaged/untracked inventory, files,\\n   and pre-existing stash list to the preflight record.\\n9. Locate the temporary stash's current list entry by OID. Drop only that entry\\n   after successful verification and only if this\\n   cleanup is within the user's authorization.\\n\\nIf ignored files matter, inspect ignore rules and any worktree include mechanism\\nbefore promising portability. Never expose secrets or infer that ignored files\\ntravel with task history.\\n\\n## Retry rule\\n\\nA retry is allowed only when all of the following are true:\\n\\n- the exact cause was identified and removed;\\n- the previous operation is terminal;\\n- task IDs, host/cwd placement, and Git state were re-inventoried;\\n- the retry cannot duplicate or move an unintended task;\\n- the operation is still within the user's original scope.\\n\\nA full desktop restart changed matcher behavior in one pilot, but a remote\\nruntime restart did not. Causality was not established. Treat restart as\\nversion-sensitive, separately authorized connectivity/cache troubleshooting,\\nnever as a default handoff step or retry loop.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"mcpToolCall\",\"id\":\"exec-eac8d8f0-3cae-4d39-b99e-bebcd485e20a\",\"server\":\"codex_app\",\"tool\":\"list_mcp_resources\",\"arguments\":{\"server\":\"codex_app\"},\"status\":\"failed\",\"durationMs\":0},{\"type\":\"contextCompaction\",\"id\":\"01a0718f-e7c3-7cf0-8a9b-1e2cd1ef207e\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0c945c9087d2a845990d4dcf3ef4\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking handoff file**\",\"**Checking handoff files**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9c0c9ad06887d28f890ae3c13a974f\",\"text\":\"All research here is paused. Both running subagents were interrupted; the third had already finished.\\n\\nI prepared detailed [supervisor handoff notes](/tmp/PDE_GF_SUPERVISOR_HANDOFF.md), covering the proof files, established results, unfinished arguments, and required checks.\\n\\nHowever, **no remote thread was created**: the required Codex app thread-creation connection is unavailable here. No files were transferred.\\n\\nTo continue, create a thread in **PDE on `black-chatgpt`** and attach these notes. I’ll remain paused here.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a07152-3d5a-7a72-8fea-187f709394a3\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1788607610,\"completedAt\":1788607864,\"durationMs\":253864,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a07152-40d1-7f53-9a09-8e9f43390c66\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"so does this result tell us anything not known from the muP limit? that the joint GF & MF limit , with same axioms required as muP, forces a different scaling than muP revealed (which branched from NTK)\\n\\nIn general, I am wondeirng what kind of important high level message do they tell us that TP/muP did not tell us? just in terms of this sepcific result, but the broader messge, similar to relationship between muP scling (which has a broad important message) to TP (specific proof mechanism)\"}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfc7ce16487d28cb322baa71ff70d\",\"text\":\"I’ll separate a new scaling principle from a stronger limit theorem. Then I’ll check the μP and tensor-program results to see which parts of our continuous-time claim they already cover.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-bc72d44e-3829-4239-8d3a-4ecf4387bc41\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' /etc/codex/skills/solve-math-rigorously/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: solve-math-rigorously\\ndescription: Produce compact but complete solutions to standalone mathematical questions and proof requests. Use when the user asks to solve, prove, derive, analyze, verify, or correct a mathematical problem that is not primarily an incremental lesson about supplied technical documents. Infer the strongest reasonable interpretation without inventing assumptions, establish a minimal setup, use minimal notation, expose every nontrivial step, state and verify external theorem hypotheses, give proof architecture when useful, and silently audit the draft before answering.\\n---\\n\\n# Solve Mathematics Rigorously\\n\\nProduce a complete mathematical answer rather than a staged document lesson, unless the user explicitly requests incremental pacing. Optimize simultaneously for correctness, rigor, clarity, and economy.\\n\\n## Determine the intended problem\\n\\nInterpret the strongest reasonable version supported by the wording and mathematical context. Do not exploit an omission to answer a trivial or uninteresting straw-man version.\\n\\nHandle ambiguity according to its effect:\\n\\n- If it does not change the substance, adopt the conventional interpretation and state it briefly.\\n- If one reasonable assumption makes the intended problem precise, state that assumption before solving.\\n- If several reasonable interpretations lead to materially different answers, ask for clarification or give a compact case split when that fully resolves the ambiguity.\\n- If a missing assumption is necessary for the claimed conclusion, do not invent it silently.\\n\\nIf the stated claim is false, say so and give a decisive counterexample or contradiction. Present a corrected nearby statement only when it helps, and label it as a correction rather than the original result.\\n\\nAnswer the problem actually asked. Do not weaken a universal claim to a convenient special case, replace an exact question with a numerical approximation, or prove a converse instead of the stated direction.\\n\\n## Establish a minimal setup\\n\\nBegin with a compact setup or preliminaries passage when it reduces ambiguity or supports later reasoning. State only the necessary:\\n\\n- domain and assumptions;\\n- definitions used in the solution;\\n- boundary, regularity, integrability, independence, or nondegeneracy conditions that matter;\\n- notation that must persist through the answer.\\n\\nReuse the problem's notation whenever possible. Introduce a new symbol only when the unabbreviated expression is genuinely cumbersome and recurs often. Define every new symbol before use and state its mathematical type when that is not immediately evident.\\n\\nDo not force a visible setup heading for a short elementary problem. Do not hide substantive assumptions in prose later in the solution.\\n\\n## Give the architecture before a substantial proof\\n\\nBefore a long or conceptually difficult proof, explain the plan in a short paragraph or compact list. Identify the main steps, cases, claims, or lemmas and how they combine to yield the conclusion.\\n\\nKeep the architecture at the level needed to orient the reader. Do not duplicate the detailed proof, and do not substitute a plan for the proof itself. For a routine calculation or one-step argument, omit the architecture.\\n\\n## Execute every nontrivial step\\n\\nDerive each nontrivial algebraic, analytic, probabilistic, geometric, combinatorial, or logical transition. Give enough detail for the user to verify the step without reconstructing missing reasoning.\\n\\nIn particular:\\n\\n- identify the definition, identity, inequality, or prior claim being used;\\n- show substitutions, rearrangements, case transitions, and changes of variables that carry real mathematical content;\\n- track domains, signs, quantifiers, equality conditions, exceptional cases, and possible zero denominators;\\n- justify limit exchanges, differentiation or integration under a sign, convergence claims, and optimization steps;\\n- distinguish exact arguments from heuristics, approximations, and numerical evidence;\\n- show that intermediate claims actually imply the requested conclusion.\\n\\nAvoid words such as “clearly,” “obviously,” “standard,” and “it follows” when they replace reasoning. They are acceptable only after the supporting reason has been supplied.\\n\\nDo not over-explain elementary arithmetic or definitions already established. Compress routine steps while preserving the chain of verification.\\n\\n## Invoke external results completely\\n\\nWhenever the solution relies on a nontrivial theorem or external fact:\\n\\n1. state the exact form needed, including its assumptions and conclusion;\\n2. verify each assumption in the present problem;\\n3. identify the current objects substituted into the theorem;\\n4. state exactly what conclusion the theorem yields here.\\n\\nDo not merely name a theorem or call a fact standard. Prefer the relevant specialization over an unnecessarily general statement.\\n\\nVerify a recent, specialized, source-specific, or uncertain result against a primary or authoritative source before relying on it. Cite the source near the claim. Paraphrase the relevant result unless its exact wording matters; quote only a short compliant excerpt when useful.\\n\\nIf the required theorem is unavailable, uncertain, or does not apply, expose the gap instead of proceeding as though it were valid.\\n\\n## Use examples and checks proportionately\\n\\nInclude an example, boundary case, counterexample, or remark only when it clarifies an abstraction, tests a hypothesis, explains a consequence, or exposes why an assumption is needed.\\n\\nUse low-cost sanity checks when they add confidence, such as:\\n\\n- substituting the result back into the defining relation;\\n- checking dimensions, signs, or units;\\n- testing a boundary or small case;\\n- comparing with a known special case;\\n- deriving the same conclusion by a second short route.\\n\\nDo not add examples mechanically or let them replace the general argument.\\n\\n## Keep the presentation compact\\n\\nUse headings only when they make a substantial solution easier to navigate. Keep notation sparse and stable. Prefer explicit expressions to disposable shorthand.\\n\\nUse equations to carry mathematical content, not decoration. Avoid formula dumps, decorative summaries, and boxed equations. State the conclusion plainly.\\n\\nLet the problem's complexity determine the answer length. Preserve every necessary assumption, theorem check, and logical transition, but remove repetition and exposition that does not improve understanding or verification.\\n\\n## Audit and revise silently\\n\\nDraft the solution, then review it before sending. Check:\\n\\n1. Did the solution address the strongest reasonable interpretation rather than a straw man?\\n2. Were all adopted assumptions stated, and were none invented merely to force the result?\\n3. Was the setup sufficient but minimal?\\n4. Was every persistent symbol necessary and defined?\\n5. Did a substantial proof receive a useful architecture?\\n6. Can the reader verify every nontrivial transition?\\n7. Was every external theorem stated in the needed form, with its hypotheses checked and its conclusion applied explicitly?\\n8. Were recent, specialized, or uncertain results verified from an authoritative source?\\n9. Were edge cases, equality conditions, domains, and quantifiers handled?\\n10. Does the final conclusion answer the exact question?\\n11. Do examples or remarks earn their space?\\n12. Is the answer as compact as rigor allows, with no boxed equations?\\n\\nRevise any failing part and repeat the audit until the answer satisfies the checklist as well as possible. Do not mention the drafting or audit process in the final answer.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-2c1f6a01-d3d3-4583-89aa-ebffec796555\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,320p' /etc/codex/skills/teach-technical-math/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: teach-technical-math\\ndescription: Teach the mathematical content of technical documents—papers, reports, notes, appendices, and linked revisions—through rigorous, self-contained, one-concept-at-a-time lessons. Use when the user supplies or references one or more sources and wants to understand their definitions, equations, derivations, proofs, models, limiting constructions, evidence, assumptions, contradictions across versions, or open gaps over multiple turns; also use when they say “next block” or ask to rebuild a confusing part of such a lesson. Reconcile source versions before teaching, preserve stable typed notation, derive rather than paraphrase, and distinguish exact results from formal, heuristic, empirical, conjectural, and open claims.\\n---\\n\\n# Teach Mathematics from Technical Documents\\n\\nAct as a mathematical and theoretical teacher, not a summarizer. Teach a motivated non-specialist at genuine mathematical depth. Expose the simple structure beneath advanced formalism without suppressing difficult steps.\\n\\n## Prepare from the sources\\n\\nRead every source the user identifies in full mathematical detail before teaching source-dependent claims. If a source is inaccessible, incomplete, or missing cited material, state the limitation and do not imply that it was read.\\n\\nBuild an internal source ledger:\\n\\n- record each source's identity, date, and status, such as preliminary, revised, audited, or final;\\n- map notation and assumption changes across versions;\\n- identify the precise objects, problem, model, regime, observables, main claims, constructions, arguments, evidence, and unresolved gaps;\\n- separate compatible claims from incompatible ones;\\n- determine which claims survived later corrections or audits;\\n- retain theorem, equation, section, and page locations when available.\\n\\nDo not dump this ledger as an opening summary. Use it to teach the requested block faithfully. Attribute source claims and distinguish them from your own interpretation.\\n\\nMaintain an internal lesson ledger across turns: stable notation, completed blocks, the last point the user clearly understood, unresolved confusion, and the next logical block. Minimally recall any earlier definition needed in the current response.\\n\\n## Control the pace\\n\\nTeach exactly one natural conceptual block per response. If the user specifies a starting point, begin there. Otherwise begin with the document's objects, assumptions, and concrete setup.\\n\\nUse this domain-neutral progression as a guide:\\n\\n1. objects, assumptions, and setup;\\n2. exact concrete or finite formulation;\\n3. meaning and type of the central variables or maps;\\n4. derivation of the main relations;\\n5. proof or mechanism architecture;\\n6. obstruction, non-closure, or source of difficulty;\\n7. proposed abstraction, approximation, or limiting construction;\\n8. assumptions connecting the construction to the original problem;\\n9. theoretical and empirical evidence;\\n10. remaining gaps and falsifying cases.\\n\\nAdjust the sequence to the sources. Do not jump ahead merely because later material is related. When the user says “next block,” continue with only the next logically necessary idea. When the user is confused, stop advancing and rebuild from the last understood point.\\n\\nWrite each ordinary lesson turn as a compact, seamless blackboard explanation: a few short paragraphs, only the equations needed now, few or no bullets, and normally no headings. Do not begin or end with a broad recap. Stop naturally after the current block and wait for the user before moving deeper.\\n\\n## Construct each block\\n\\nUse this internal order without displaying it as a template:\\n\\n1. **Minimal recall:** restate only the definitions needed now.\\n2. **New object:** define one new object and give its mathematical type.\\n3. **Derivation:** obtain its relation from the preceding setup.\\n4. **Meaning:** explain the role of every term or condition.\\n5. **Analogue or example:** use one structurally faithful simple case when it genuinely helps, then return to the exact mathematics.\\n6. **Role:** connect the object to the document's argument.\\n7. **Status:** state what is established and what remains unproved.\\n\\nMake the block self-contained for its present conceptual burden, not for the entire project.\\n\\n## Enforce notation and type discipline\\n\\nUse a small, stable notation. Prefer the source's symbols unless they collide or obscure meaning. If renaming is necessary, state the mapping explicitly.\\n\\nDefine every symbol before using it. For each object, state as relevant:\\n\\n- its domain, codomain, or ambient space;\\n- whether it is a scalar, vector, matrix, function, operator, random variable, measure, index, or coordinate;\\n- whether it is fixed, sampled, or evolving;\\n- what each index means;\\n- its dependence on other variables or parameters.\\n\\nDistinguish carefully between:\\n\\n- one function of an entire vector and a vector of coordinatewise functions;\\n- an index and a coordinate;\\n- a fixed label or parameter and an evolving state;\\n- a representative element and a probability law over elements;\\n- a mathematical state and a numerical representation of that state.\\n\\nWhen writing an integral, identify the integration variable, its space, the measure, and what the integral averages or accumulates. When a distribution is specified only at initialization, do not imply that an evolved quantity retains that distribution merely because it remains a function of the initial label.\\n\\nDo not introduce shorthand unless the explicit expression is genuinely cumbersome and recurs often. Never reuse one symbol for two roles.\\n\\n## Derive rather than paraphrase\\n\\nFor every central equation or implication:\\n\\n1. state the definition or prior relation from which it follows;\\n2. show the relevant algebraic, differential, variational, probabilistic, logical, or limiting step;\\n3. identify the origin and action of every term;\\n4. explain the closest elementary analogue when useful;\\n5. state why the relation matters to the main result.\\n\\nDo not make the user reverse-engineer a displayed formula. Avoid phrases such as “clearly,” “standard,” or “it follows” when they conceal a nontrivial step.\\n\\nWhen invoking an external theorem, state the exact form needed, list its hypotheses, verify them in the present setting, and explain the resulting conclusion. Check a primary or otherwise authoritative source before relying on a recent, specialized, or uncertain result.\\n\\nWhen teaching a long proof, devote one block to its architecture before teaching the difficult steps. Explain how the claims, cases, or lemmas fit together without pretending that the architecture is itself the proof.\\n\\nUse analogies only when they preserve the relevant mathematical structure. State where the analogy stops and return immediately to the exact object.\\n\\n## Relate levels of description\\n\\nBegin with the document's concrete objects and exact relations. Introduce an abstract, aggregate, continuum, asymptotic, or numerical description only after showing how it arises from the concrete one.\\n\\nWhenever the source changes levels, identify:\\n\\n- the map from the original objects to the new description;\\n- what information is retained and discarded;\\n- which step is an exact identity;\\n- which step uses a theorem and its assumptions;\\n- which step is formal, heuristic, empirical, or conjectural.\\n\\nIf a proposed state or summary evolves autonomously, distinguish internal closure from fidelity to the original system. An autonomous reduced description may still omit information that changes the original system's future.\\n\\nIf the source uses truncation or approximation, distinguish pointwise or static approximation from the stronger control required by an evolving or iterative system. Examine separately:\\n\\n- error that leaves the retained representation;\\n- feedback from omitted components;\\n- amplification or accumulation of error;\\n- the time, parameter, or regime over which stability is claimed.\\n\\nWhen the source calls a construction “finite,” state precisely whether this means finitely many coordinates, finitely many fields, a finite-dimensional state, a finite cutoff, or merely a finite numerical discretization.\\n\\n## Label epistemic status\\n\\nClassify each important source-dependent claim using the most accurate category:\\n\\n- exact identity;\\n- proved theorem under stated assumptions;\\n- rigorous result for a restricted or finite setting;\\n- formal argument;\\n- heuristic mechanism;\\n- empirical or numerical evidence;\\n- conjectural identification;\\n- open implication;\\n- counterexample or negative result.\\n\\nDo not silently promote a claim. Express each important assumption as a missing implication:\\n\\n1. what is assumed;\\n2. what conclusion it would justify;\\n3. what connecting step is not established;\\n4. what observation or counterexample would refute it.\\n\\nInternal consistency of a proposed theory does not prove that it represents the target object.\\n\\n## Evaluate evidence and alternatives\\n\\nFor every numerical comparison, identify:\\n\\n- the systems or quantities compared;\\n- what the reference actually represents;\\n- the metric or norm;\\n- the scale of the predicted effect and the discrepancy;\\n- discretization, optimization, rounding, and sampling uncertainty as applicable;\\n- the alternative explanation ruled out;\\n- what the experiment leaves unresolved.\\n\\nDo not infer convergence or a scaling law from too few resolutions. Treat numerical thresholds as decision conventions unless the source proves a mathematical boundary.\\n\\nWhen the user requests a hostile or devil's-advocate analysis, include only alternatives that engage the declared target and make a distinct mathematical or quantitative prediction. Do not infer global behavior from a local or instantaneous property without proving the connecting step. Do not use possible error cancellation as a complete explanation unless it predicts the observed pattern.\\n\\n## Repair confusion completely\\n\\nWhen the user identifies an undefined symbol, ambiguity, missing derivation, or conceptual gap:\\n\\n- acknowledge the failure directly;\\n- do not defend the prior wording;\\n- do not append a one-sentence patch;\\n- rewrite the entire relevant block self-containedly;\\n- preserve compactness and stable notation.\\n\\nTreat confusion as evidence that the explanation needs rebuilding, not that the mathematics is inherently inaccessible.\\n\\nBefore sending each block, verify silently that it is faithful to the sources, introduces only one conceptual burden, defines every needed symbol, derives rather than paraphrases, states the status of important claims, stays in the user's language, avoids unnecessary notation and formula dumps, and is compact enough to absorb in one sitting.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_nXZ3TPLahzbciNhaVv83qDY2\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_68pqfDi1tVbPQVuwLi28UeNK\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-3326-7303-96a6-3fc7a87cd0a4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_limit\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-bd326913-00e0-4fb9-90f0-6308d43b8b8a\",\"query\":\"\\\"maximal update\\\" \\\"gradient flow\\\" infinite width limit ...\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"search\",\"query\":null,\"queries\":[\"\\\"maximal update\\\" \\\"gradient flow\\\" infinite width limit\",\"\\\"muP\\\" \\\"continuous-time\\\" limit\",\"\\\"Tensor Programs IV\\\" feature learning dichotomy scaling parametrizations\"]}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfc96239887d2888bf65425e20449\",\"summary\":[\"**Searching feature-flow framework**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-5a3b4b92-3b54-49ec-8ac8-bab1d7fb1acc\",\"query\":\"\\\"feature learning\\\" \\\"gradient flow\\\" \\\"operator\\\" infinite width neural network limit ...\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"search\",\"query\":null,\"queries\":[\"\\\"feature learning\\\" \\\"gradient flow\\\" \\\"operator\\\" infinite width neural network limit\",\"\\\"muP\\\" \\\"gradient flow\\\" \\\"rigorous\\\"\",\"\\\"neural Hilbert\\\" \\\"gradient flow\\\" infinite width\",\"Bordelon Pehlevan 2022 self consistent dynamical field theory feature learning\"]}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfc9d62e487d2abe4d862d71dcba6\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking related mean-field results**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfca43ce887d2bb4c4f47285a0e1e\",\"text\":\"I’m using the teaching and rigorous-math skills to keep the comparison precise. So far, this does not look like a new scaling principle: the width powers match μP. The substantive question is whether the proof strengthens “a limit after each fixed number of updates” into “one well-defined learning flow over a whole time interval.”\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-9dd77e6f-5aea-4d78-a0dc-e31f846a7c96\",\"query\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.14522\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.14522\"}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-e0aa5b36-a55d-4e19-a658-cfb144ce5594\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,170p' /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Three inputs: a rigorous local-time joint width and gradient-flow limit\\n\\n## Result and scope\\n\\nFix three inputs \\\\(x_1,x_2,x_3\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a\\\\|_2^2}{d}=1,\\\\qquad\\nx_a\\\\ne x_b,\\\\quad x_a\\\\ne -x_b\\\\quad(a\\\\ne b),\\\\qquad\\nG_{ab}=\\\\frac{x_a^\\\\top x_b}{d}.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause the norms agree, the conditions on \\\\(x_a,x_b\\\\) are exactly pairwise nonparallelity. The \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) matrix \\\\(G\\\\) is positive semidefinite with diagonal one. It may be singular. All input indices and input sums below range over \\\\(1,2,3\\\\).\\nLet \\\\(y_1,y_2,y_3\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence of positive steps \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all such triples and all eight label sign patterns. Moreover, the existence time \\\\(T_0\\\\) can be chosen uniformly over these triples and labels: its construction uses only three inputs, \\\\(|G_{ab}|\\\\le1\\\\), and the fixed activation and initialization scales. It does not depend on width, step size, input dimension, or a lower bound on a Gram eigenvalue.\\n\\nPredictions, residuals, loss, and all entries of all three kernel blocks converge in probability uniformly on this interval. The joint three-input hidden path laws converge with their second moments in the uniform path norm. Integrated squared hidden velocities converge. The joint empirical averages of any fixed finite collection of continuous, globally Lipschitz forward/adjoint probes, with bounded products when a product instruction is used, converge to their corresponding population expectations, uniformly in time. Unbounded first-layer backward fields and their quadratic contractions are included by the tail argument below.\\n\\nHere a fixed action probe is a finite calculation from the current fields and constants using fixed globally Lipschitz coordinate functions, linear combinations, and appropriately typed calls to \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) and its adjoint. A product instruction is allowed when both varying factors are uniformly bounded, for example after clipping. Final same-layer joint measurements can be continuous with at most quadratic growth. Probe length and functions do not grow with width or depend on the learning rate.\\n\\nOn a possibly smaller interval \\\\((0,T_*]\\\\), each input's preactivations move in both hidden layers, all three \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) kernel blocks are positive definite, the total kernel is nonconstant, loss strictly decreases, and \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) has positive best-affine-fit error on each hidden preactivation distribution.\\n\\n**Scope:** this is a local-time theorem. A convergence theorem on every finite horizon is not claimed for arbitrary input geometry. \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) is chosen before taking width and learning-rate limits and does not shrink in those limits. The strictly positive feature-learning coefficients and the possibly smaller activity interval \\\\(T_*\\\\) may depend on the fixed input geometry and labels.\\n\\nThe inputs are fixed while taking the width and learning-rate limits. A common existence interval across configurations is not a claim of a convergence rate uniform over configurations that themselves change with width.\\n\\n## The finite network and its exact updates\\n\\nThe dimensions are \\\\(W^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times d}\\\\), \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\). For input \\\\(a=1,2,3\\\\), use\\n\\\\[\\nz_a^{(1)}=\\\\frac{W^{(1)}x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nz_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi=\\\\arctan,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h_a^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad\\nr_{n,a}=f_{n,a}-y_a,\\\\qquad L_n=\\\\sum_{a=1}^3r_{n,a}^2.\\n\\\\]\\nThe summed loss retains the earlier factors of two; using the average of the three losses divides the speed by three.\\n\\nAll initialization entries are independent:\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ij}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently each first-layer root triple is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), independently over neuron index \\\\(i\\\\). The three preactivations for one neuron may be correlated and may satisfy an exact linear relation. This is permitted.\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta_a^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_{n,a}/\\\\partial z_a^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\).\\nIn the displayed rescaled parameterization the layer learning-rate multipliers are \\\\(n,1,n\\\\). The exact first-weight update is\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n=W_k^{(1)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{\\\\sqrt d}\\n\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)}x_b^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\nMultiplication by \\\\(x_a/\\\\sqrt d\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=z_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{n}\\n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)} .\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M1}\\n\\\\]\\nUse physical time \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), linearly interpolate these parameters, and recompute the forward pass at intermediate times.\\n\\nThe whole effect of input geometry on first-layer training is \\\\(G\\\\): training on input \\\\(b\\\\) moves input \\\\(a\\\\)'s preactivation with coefficient \\\\(G_{ab}\\\\). If \\\\(Gc=0\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\sum_a c_a z_a^{(1)}=0\\\\) initially and every update preserves this relation. No inverse of \\\\(G\\\\) is used.\\n\\nThe finite continuous flow has the kernel blocks\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}_{n,ab}\\n=G_{ab}\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(1)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(1)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(2)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(1)})^\\\\top h_b^{(1)}}n\\n \\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(2)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(3)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(2)})^\\\\top h_b^{(2)}}n .\\n\\\\tag{M2}\\n\\\\]\\nDifferentiation gives \\\\(\\\\dot f_n=-2K_n r_n\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\dot L_n=-4r_n^\\\\top K_n r_n\\\\), where \\\\(K_n\\\\) is the sum of the blocks. Each block is positive semidefinite: it is the Gram matrix of the corresponding scaled parameter gradients. Individual residual magnitudes need not decrease; their squared sum does.\\n\\n## Why a new stability estimate is needed\\n\\nWith \\\\(F(s)=s+s^3/3\\\\), the componentwise transformation \\\\(X_a^{(1)}=F(Z_a^{(1)})\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot X_a^{(1)}\\n=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\n\\\\frac{1+(Z_a^{(1)})^2}{1+(Z_b^{(1)})^2}\\n(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{M3}\\n\\\\]\\nIf the inputs are mutually orthogonal, only diagonal terms occur and the ratios cancel. Otherwise nonzero cross-input terms remain and their ratios are unbounded. We therefore work with the original preactivations and prove a uniform tail estimate for the backward fields.\\n\\nThe exact limiting equations will be\\n\\\\[\\nH_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\nf_a=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H_a^{(2)}],\\\\quad r_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot Z_a^{(1)}&=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\\\delta_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_b\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_bH_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M4}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\((u\\\\otimes v)V=u\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[vV]\\\\). This definition displays the normalization that replaces the finite factor \\\\(1/n\\\\). First-layer and second-layer coordinates belong to separate populations. \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) acts from the former to the latter, and its adjoint acts in reverse. Every expectation pairs coordinates of the same population.\\n\\nThe initial root triple is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), the population readout is zero, and the initial action \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) is the joint forward-and-transpose limit of the initial Gaussian matrix, constructed next. The state consists of three first-layer fields and the shared matrix action and readout field. It has finitely many kinds of state objects, not finitely many scalar degrees of freedom.\\n\\n## The fixed-mesh construction and the new tail lemma\\n\\nWe use the following finite Gaussian fact. For a fixed finite calculation from iid jointly Gaussian coordinate tuples, independent of a Gaussian matrix of entry variance \\\\(1/n\\\\), with polynomially bounded coordinate functions and finitely many calls to that matrix and its transpose, every same-layer joint empirical average of a continuous polynomially bounded measurement has a deterministic limit. The limiting action is Gaussian randomness plus the responses forced by earlier opposite-direction calls. The precise response coefficients and their derivative convention are given and used in the appendix below. This is the specialization of Theorem 2.10 and Box 1 of [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685). Its hypotheses hold here: after fixing population scalar coefficients, each instruction is polynomially bounded, and the training instructions whose derivatives are used below are smooth with polynomially bounded derivatives, as functions of finitely many Gaussian variables. The initial tuple covariance may be singular; represent it as a fixed linear image of independent Gaussian roots. No claim of iid finite coordinates after matrix reuse is used.\\n\\nFor completeness, the underlying conditioning mechanism is as follows. If previous calls have revealed \\\\(W_0^{(2)}V=Y\\\\) and \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R\\\\), then the remaining Gaussian matrix is an independent Gaussian projected onto the directions orthogonal to the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) and \\\\(V\\\\). Its conditional mean obeys both recorded equations. A new product is the response fixed by these equations plus that projected Gaussian applied to the unexplored part of the new input. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the response coefficients. The finite Gaussian theorem makes this induction valid also with dependent query directions. We explicitly rely on this stated finite-program result, not on a claim that the infinite-time or growing-program-length theorem already follows from it.\\n\\nThese finite limits can be realized on two common population coordinate spaces. Here are the details relevant to the subsequent continuous-time argument. Take all the programs needed for rational meshes, their finite unions, and a countable closure under rational linear combinations, the coordinate operations in the proof, and bounded Lipschitz approximations. Their finite joint laws are consistent, so they can be realized jointly. Take the mean-square closure of their linear spans on each layer. Exact finite linear identities pass to these laws.\\n\\nA fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\) satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le2\\\\,9^{2n}e^{-nM^2/8}\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{M5}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed two \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets of the unit sphere each have at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points, and every fixed bilinear form of the matrix is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). This finite operator inequality and joint second-moment convergence imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2]\\n\\\\]\\nfor every generated \\\\(V\\\\). Thus the action is well-defined on equal-in-mean-square variables and extends to the closure. The same reasoning applies to the transpose. The exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u)^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\npasses to the limit and identifies the two actions as adjoints. Bounded coordinate multipliers and their required products belong to these spaces by clipping and mean-square approximation. This constructs the common bounded-operator realization used below; it is not an additional assumption.\\n\\nDefine population Euler for (M4) in this common realization, starting from the stated initialization. Its rank expansion is\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}\\n-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\\\sum_b\\nr_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{M6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus each fixed mesh is a finite Gaussian calculation with deterministic population residuals and contractions. Iterative construction is causal: the current fields determine their expectations and then the next fields. Its realization agrees with the finite Gaussian law, because applying the same rank expansion reduces each action to an initial-matrix call and those deterministic contractions.\\n\\nThe appendix proves the essential new estimate:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta:\\\\,\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\\\ \\n\\\\max_{a,k:\\\\,k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\nc\\\\left|(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\right|^2\\n\\\\right)\\\\le C .\\n\\\\tag{M7}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(T_0,c,C>0\\\\) do not depend on the mesh. In particular the squared tail beyond \\\\(R\\\\) is at most \\\\(C e^{-c'R^2}\\\\), after reducing \\\\(c'\\\\).\\n\\nThe appendix is a derivation, not an assumed light-tail condition. It controls the accumulated deterministic response coefficients on both layers. The second-layer derivatives stay bounded; the first-layer derivatives have a Gaussian exponential bound. A short-time induction closes these bounds uniformly in the number of Euler steps.\\n\\n## Population Euler converges to a unique flow\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfcaa3b8087d2b94c66af466a9504\",\"summary\":[\"**Inspecting relevant sections**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-186c5d31-e322-4286-812b-ea005e129fc9\",\"query\":\"'Abstract'\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"findInPage\",\"url\":null,\"pattern\":\"Abstract\"}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-b0892461-e583-44d6-8179-467ece706c8d\",\"query\":\"\\\"mean-field\\\" \\\"maximal update\\\" \\\"nonlinear\\\" rigorous limit ...\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"search\",\"query\":null,\"queries\":[\"\\\"mean-field\\\" \\\"maximal update\\\" \\\"nonlinear\\\" rigorous limit\",\"\\\"operator\\\" \\\"muP\\\" \\\"limit\\\" gradient flow neural networks\",\"\\\"continuous-time\\\" \\\"tensor programs\\\" well-posedness\"]}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfcbcaeb487d2b1327e3edbafce96\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking TPIVb literature**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_JwcfHE6u6FSpPJ7PiMxqPVMq\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-e52ca139-72a3-43af-9bf9-db0cab2613c2\",\"query\":\"\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"other\"}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-0af734b5-c8ed-4ad7-b75e-d11986053cef\",\"query\":\"\\\"muP\\\" \\\"well-posedness\\\" ...\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"search\",\"query\":null,\"queries\":[\"\\\"muP\\\" \\\"well-posedness\\\"\",\"\\\"maximal update\\\" \\\"joint limit\\\" gradient\",\"\\\"nonlinear\\\" \\\"muP\\\" \\\"continuous-time limit\\\"\"]}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfcd036c887d281222407d08d73f7\",\"summary\":[\"**Refining the proof framing**\",\"**Checking growing-time limit gaps**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-bed21dca-daf8-43c1-9969-3c89deddd11d\",\"query\":\"\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"other\"}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-a6b55af2-06ca-400c-b568-02c691f5563d\",\"query\":\"\\\"Infinite-width limit of deep linear neural networks\\\" nonlinear extension ...\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"search\",\"query\":null,\"queries\":[\"\\\"Infinite-width limit of deep linear neural networks\\\" nonlinear extension\",\"\\\"feature learning\\\" \\\"well-posed\\\" \\\"limit\\\" neural networks Gaussian\",\"\\\"muP\\\" \\\"gradient flow\\\" \\\"joint\\\" convergence rigorous nonlinear\"]}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfce9cce487d2b7443c055f543e5e\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying μP scaling assumptions**\",\"**Normalizing rates by time**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfcfbbcf487d2b0db2472e7905507\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfd01445487d2ab3cc1b79579d842\",\"summary\":[\"**Defining the autonomous state**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07152-8897-71b2-9f88-b33f81892225\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-3326-7303-96a6-3fc7a87cd0a4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_limit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfd0e926c87d284f6ed3c4c216299\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking literature novelty**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfd1f0b7c87d28605e485da03d730\",\"summary\":[\"**Rechecking TPIVb wording**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfd225ecc87d2a06f1cd280bfedea\",\"text\":\"I found an important qualification: later tensor-program work already represents the infinite-width weights as operators with closed current-state updates. So that representation is not a new contribution either. The remaining distinction is the control needed to pass to continuous time and prove that finite-width GD approaches the same flow—not the discovery of feature learning or of an operator-valued state.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-00505956-2e2d-4931-8623-e8a02e3eaf84\",\"query\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.01814\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.01814\"}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-a54c342a-42cb-42e9-a458-8465a9979604\",\"query\":\"'Definition 2.6.7'\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"findInPage\",\"url\":null,\"pattern\":\"Definition 2.6.7\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfd3147e487d2b70b354dd589f255\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying μP axiom compatibility**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-2a50465c-4472-42b5-89e9-9eb95357b74d\",\"query\":\"'Remark 2.8.21'\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"findInPage\",\"url\":null,\"pattern\":\"Remark 2.8.21\"}},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07152-6cd0-7192-bedd-74932523e4e9\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfd3e5ff087d2bbb2f214e50382e2\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking classification assumptions**\",\"**Checking scaling distinctions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfd502de487d2b67e524d15913380\",\"summary\":[\"**Refining claim after checks**\",\"**Refining theorem claim**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bfd5dd44887d2b4665e5ef69fef5a\",\"text\":\"No—not in the sense of forcing a new scaling beyond μP. The result is better understood as **a stronger dynamical justification for a particular μP-type model**, rather than a new scaling principle.\\n\\nAnd after checking the later tensor-program papers, I would narrow the novelty claim further: even the representation using current fields and weight operators already exists there.\\n\\n### The scaling is not the new message\\n\\nIn our notation, the three layer learning rates are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_n(n\\\\kappa_1,\\\\;\\\\kappa_2,\\\\;n\\\\kappa_3),\\n\\\\]\\nand one step represents physical time \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\). Consequently, the learning rates **per unit of physical time** are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\eta_n(n\\\\kappa_1,\\\\;\\\\kappa_2,\\\\;n\\\\kappa_3)}{\\\\eta_n}\\n=\\n(n\\\\kappa_1,\\\\;\\\\kappa_2,\\\\;n\\\\kappa_3).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThose are the same relative width powers as μP. Sending the common factor \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\) to zero refines the time discretization; it does not change the relative layer scaling.\\n\\nFor example, choosing \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\) makes the actual learning rates proportional to\\n\\\\[\\n(n^{-1},\\\\;n^{-2},\\\\;n^{-1}).\\n\\\\]\\nThat looks different from \\\\((n,1,n)\\\\) until we remember that we also perform \\\\(n^2\\\\) updates per unit of physical time.\\n\\nOur smaller readout initialization is a separate choice. We have **not** proved that the joint limit forces it. Also, “the same axioms as μP” needs care: μP’s strict maximality characterization includes maximal readout initialization, whereas our population readout starts at zero. The literature already accommodates limits obtained by suppressing initialization or learning rates; this is not a counterexample to its classification. [Tensor Programs IVb, Remarks 2.7.5 and 2.8.21](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.01814).\\n\\nMore conceptually, gradient flow and the NTK regime are different distinctions. Gradient flow means continuous optimization time. NTK means, in this comparison, a particular infinite-width regime with frozen features and a fixed prediction kernel. Continuous-time training can be feature-learning or lazy. μP identifies a feature-learning scaling; it is not merely a correction to NTK dynamics. [Tensor Programs IV](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.14522).\\n\\n### What the extra mathematical obligation actually is\\n\\nThe relevant distinction is between these two guarantees:\\n\\n“After any fixed number of updates, the finite network approaches an infinite-width network.”\\n\\n“Over an entire fixed physical-time interval, increasingly wide networks with increasingly small steps approach one well-defined continuous learning process.”\\n\\nThe second does not follow just by replacing differences with derivatives in the first. To reach time \\\\(T\\\\), we need approximately\\n\\\\[\\nk=\\\\frac{T}{\\\\eta_n}\\n\\\\]\\nupdates, and this number diverges.\\n\\nA simple illustration: an error \\\\(k/n\\\\) vanishes as \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) for every fixed \\\\(k\\\\). But if \\\\(\\\\eta_n=1/n\\\\), reaching physical time \\\\(T\\\\) requires approximately \\\\(nT\\\\) updates, and that error becomes approximately \\\\(T\\\\), not zero. This is only an illustrative error sequence, but it exposes the missing implication.\\n\\nOur proof supplies the additional control needed in the specified model: errors remain controlled as the number of updates grows, the population discretizations approach a unique continuous evolution, and finite-width GD approaches that same evolution. For example,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{0\\\\le t\\\\le T}\\n|L_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t)-L(t)|\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability},\\n\\\\]\\ntogether with the stronger feature, kernel, and matrix-action conclusions we discussed.\\n\\nThis distinction was explicitly recognized in the original μP paper: it takes the width limit while retaining discrete time, and separately identifies existence and uniqueness of continuous-time equations as additional issues. [Tensor Programs IV, “Discrete- vs Continuous-Time Gradient Descent”](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.14522#page=4).\\n\\nHowever, we should not inflate the other ingredients into new discoveries. Tensor Programs IVb already constructs initial weight operators, identifies their transpose with the corresponding adjoint, and writes closed updates for current weights and features. So “we can retain \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) and describe an autonomous infinite-width network” is not, by itself, new relative to the TP literature. [Tensor Programs IVb, §2.6.3 and Theorem 2.7.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.01814#page=22).\\n\\nThe remaining substantive work is proving that these continuous-time equations actually work: a solution exists, it is unique in the relevant class, and the finite systems converge to it over an interval—not merely that the equations can be written down.\\n\\n### What broad message can we reasonably attach to it?\\n\\nThe strongest defensible message is:\\n\\n> In this nonlinear deep-network example, the feature-learning width limit remains a genuine learning process when optimization time becomes continuous. Neither the continuum limit nor the removal of discrete steps forces the hidden layers to freeze.\\n\\nThat is a meaningful mathematical foundation. It allows us to treat the limiting network as a dynamical system whose properties can be investigated, rather than only as the output of a finite-step probabilistic calculation.\\n\\nBut this is **not a newly discovered phenomenon at the level of μP’s feature-learning principle**. Continuous-time μP-equivalent feature-learning equations already appear in Bordelon–Pehlevan’s dynamical mean-field work. [Self-Consistent Dynamical Field Theory](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09653). Rigorous infinite-width dynamics and a subsequent continuous-time limit were also established for deep **linear** networks by Chizat, Colombo, Fernández-Real, and Figalli. [Infinite-width limit of deep linear neural networks](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/cpa.22200).\\n\\nThe potentially distinctive contribution of our result is therefore the **particular rigorous nonlinear joint-limit theorem**, including its explicit nontriviality guarantees—not continuous-time feature learning in general. I have not established that this exact theorem is literature-first.\\n\\nThere is also a substantial difference in breadth. μP provides a classification and a scaling prescription across broad families of networks. Our current result concerns two hidden layers, arctan, a special readout initialization, three fixed inputs, and a short time interval. It establishes a sufficient construction, not a characterization of all possible constructions.\\n\\nTo reach a comparably broad message, we would need something further: a general criterion explaining when feature-learning width limits admit a well-defined continuous flow, or a consequence about learning that the existing descriptions did not reveal. For instance, proving that the evolving representations enable a particular generalization advantage would say more than proving that they move. Likewise, proving a new scaling is *necessary* would say more than showing one scaling works.\\n\\nSo I would frame the present result as:\\n\\n**A rigorous local continuous-time foundation for a restricted nonlinear μP-type model—not a replacement for μP, not a new scaling law, and not yet a new general theory of what deep networks learn.**\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a0713a-3cf6-7203-89db-04c9113f529f\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1788606037,\"completedAt\":1788606506,\"durationMs\":469681,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a0713a-3d59-7741-9de6-07764e171229\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"is the limit non-trivial in the following sense: the regime you obtain is not frozen middle layers, non-lazy, features are moving at a constant speed, as well as the loss, (constant change per unit of time, or their velocity), and also that the activations are in strictly non-linear regime(namely, if we define a limit where pre-activations of arctan are vanishing in value the arctan behaves like a linear activation, we want to avoid this for both hidden layers, is that the case?&#x20;\\n\\nalso, can you more generally define the joint scaling , in terms of what are the key hyper params (layer variances, layerwise learning rates, and what is the scaling in width , and how do we define the GF limit) , also, if you can comment on , if this very particular configuration is the only answer to to the affirmative result, or it can be stated as a more general class of configurations, namely somethign that resemblles more the muP type scaling? \\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf657387087d2acbf4577c1f5348a\",\"text\":\"I’ll distinguish “nonvanishing as width grows” from “constant in time”—the proof establishes the former, not constant velocity throughout training. Then I’ll lay out the initialization and learning-rate scalings, and check how far they extend to a broader, μP-like family.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-3bbb9f03-9837-4a37-8eb7-8dd0968dd57f\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /etc/codex/skills/solve-math-rigorously/SKILL.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: solve-math-rigorously\\ndescription: Produce compact but complete solutions to standalone mathematical questions and proof requests. Use when the user asks to solve, prove, derive, analyze, verify, or correct a mathematical problem that is not primarily an incremental lesson about supplied technical documents. Infer the strongest reasonable interpretation without inventing assumptions, establish a minimal setup, use minimal notation, expose every nontrivial step, state and verify external theorem hypotheses, give proof architecture when useful, and silently audit the draft before answering.\\n---\\n\\n# Solve Mathematics Rigorously\\n\\nProduce a complete mathematical answer rather than a staged document lesson, unless the user explicitly requests incremental pacing. Optimize simultaneously for correctness, rigor, clarity, and economy.\\n\\n## Determine the intended problem\\n\\nInterpret the strongest reasonable version supported by the wording and mathematical context. Do not exploit an omission to answer a trivial or uninteresting straw-man version.\\n\\nHandle ambiguity according to its effect:\\n\\n- If it does not change the substance, adopt the conventional interpretation and state it briefly.\\n- If one reasonable assumption makes the intended problem precise, state that assumption before solving.\\n- If several reasonable interpretations lead to materially different answers, ask for clarification or give a compact case split when that fully resolves the ambiguity.\\n- If a missing assumption is necessary for the claimed conclusion, do not invent it silently.\\n\\nIf the stated claim is false, say so and give a decisive counterexample or contradiction. Present a corrected nearby statement only when it helps, and label it as a correction rather than the original result.\\n\\nAnswer the problem actually asked. Do not weaken a universal claim to a convenient special case, replace an exact question with a numerical approximation, or prove a converse instead of the stated direction.\\n\\n## Establish a minimal setup\\n\\nBegin with a compact setup or preliminaries passage when it reduces ambiguity or supports later reasoning. State only the necessary:\\n\\n- domain and assumptions;\\n- definitions used in the solution;\\n- boundary, regularity, integrability, independence, or nondegeneracy conditions that matter;\\n- notation that must persist through the answer.\\n\\nReuse the problem's notation whenever possible. Introduce a new symbol only when the unabbreviated expression is genuinely cumbersome and recurs often. Define every new symbol before use and state its mathematical type when that is not immediately evident.\\n\\nDo not force a visible setup heading for a short elementary problem. Do not hide substantive assumptions in prose later in the solution.\\n\\n## Give the architecture before a substantial proof\\n\\nBefore a long or conceptually difficult proof, explain the plan in a short paragraph or compact list. Identify the main steps, cases, claims, or lemmas and how they combine to yield the conclusion.\\n\\nKeep the architecture at the level needed to orient the reader. Do not duplicate the detailed proof, and do not substitute a plan for the proof itself. For a routine calculation or one-step argument, omit the architecture.\\n\\n## Execute every nontrivial step\\n\\nDerive each nontrivial algebraic, analytic, probabilistic, geometric, combinatorial, or logical transition. Give enough detail for the user to verify the step without reconstructing missing reasoning.\\n\\nIn particular:\\n\\n- identify the definition, identity, inequality, or prior claim being used;\\n- show substitutions, rearrangements, case transitions, and changes of variables that carry real mathematical content;\\n- track domains, signs, quantifiers, equality conditions, exceptional cases, and possible zero denominators;\\n- justify limit exchanges, differentiation or integration under a sign, convergence claims, and optimization steps;\\n- distinguish exact arguments from heuristics, approximations, and numerical evidence;\\n- show that intermediate claims actually imply the requested conclusion.\\n\\nAvoid words such as “clearly,” “obviously,” “standard,” and “it follows” when they replace reasoning. They are acceptable only after the supporting reason has been supplied.\\n\\nDo not over-explain elementary arithmetic or definitions already established. Compress routine steps while preserving the chain of verification.\\n\\n## Invoke external results completely\\n\\nWhenever the solution relies on a nontrivial theorem or external fact:\\n\\n1. state the exact form needed, including its assumptions and conclusion;\\n2. verify each assumption in the present problem;\\n3. identify the current objects substituted into the theorem;\\n4. state exactly what conclusion the theorem yields here.\\n\\nDo not merely name a theorem or call a fact standard. Prefer the relevant specialization over an unnecessarily general statement.\\n\\nVerify a recent, specialized, source-specific, or uncertain result against a primary or authoritative source before relying on it. Cite the source near the claim. Paraphrase the relevant result unless its exact wording matters; quote only a short compliant excerpt when useful.\\n\\nIf the required theorem is unavailable, uncertain, or does not apply, expose the gap instead of proceeding as though it were valid.\\n\\n## Use examples and checks proportionately\\n\\nInclude an example, boundary case, counterexample, or remark only when it clarifies an abstraction, tests a hypothesis, explains a consequence, or exposes why an assumption is needed.\\n\\nUse low-cost sanity checks when they add confidence, such as:\\n\\n- substituting the result back into the defining relation;\\n- checking dimensions, signs, or units;\\n- testing a boundary or small case;\\n- comparing with a known special case;\\n- deriving the same conclusion by a second short route.\\n\\nDo not add examples mechanically or let them replace the general argument.\\n\\n## Keep the presentation compact\\n\\nUse headings only when they make a substantial solution easier to navigate. Keep notation sparse and stable. Prefer explicit expressions to disposable shorthand.\\n\\nUse equations to carry mathematical content, not decoration. Avoid formula dumps, decorative summaries, and boxed equations. State the conclusion plainly.\\n\\nLet the problem's complexity determine the answer length. Preserve every necessary assumption, theorem check, and logical transition, but remove repetition and exposition that does not improve understanding or verification.\\n\\n## Audit and revise silently\\n\\nDraft the solution, then review it before sending. Check:\\n\\n1. Did the solution address the strongest reasonable interpretation rather than a straw man?\\n2. Were all adopted assumptions stated, and were none invented merely to force the result?\\n3. Was the setup sufficient but minimal?\\n4. Was every persistent symbol necessary and defined?\\n5. Did a substantial proof receive a useful architecture?\\n6. Can the reader verify every nontrivial transition?\\n7. Was every external theorem stated in the needed form, with its hypotheses checked and its conclusion applied explicitly?\\n8. Were recent, specialized, or uncertain results verified from an authoritative source?\\n9. Were edge cases, equality conditions, domains, and quantifiers handled?\\n10. Does the final conclusion answer the exact question?\\n11. Do examples or remarks earn their space?\\n12. Is the answer as compact as rigor allows, with no boxed equations?\\n\\nRevise any failing part and repeat the audit until the answer satisfies the checklist as well as possible. Do not mention the drafting or audit process in the final answer.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-c47043b2-a9ec-47ec-9785-109f6885f476\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /etc/codex/skills/teach-technical-math/SKILL.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: teach-technical-math\\ndescription: Teach the mathematical content of technical documents—papers, reports, notes, appendices, and linked revisions—through rigorous, self-contained, one-concept-at-a-time lessons. Use when the user supplies or references one or more sources and wants to understand their definitions, equations, derivations, proofs, models, limiting constructions, evidence, assumptions, contradictions across versions, or open gaps over multiple turns; also use when they say “next block” or ask to rebuild a confusing part of such a lesson. Reconcile source versions before teaching, preserve stable typed notation, derive rather than paraphrase, and distinguish exact results from formal, heuristic, empirical, conjectural, and open claims.\\n---\\n\\n# Teach Mathematics from Technical Documents\\n\\nAct as a mathematical and theoretical teacher, not a summarizer. Teach a motivated non-specialist at genuine mathematical depth. Expose the simple structure beneath advanced formalism without suppressing difficult steps.\\n\\n## Prepare from the sources\\n\\nRead every source the user identifies in full mathematical detail before teaching source-dependent claims. If a source is inaccessible, incomplete, or missing cited material, state the limitation and do not imply that it was read.\\n\\nBuild an internal source ledger:\\n\\n- record each source's identity, date, and status, such as preliminary, revised, audited, or final;\\n- map notation and assumption changes across versions;\\n- identify the precise objects, problem, model, regime, observables, main claims, constructions, arguments, evidence, and unresolved gaps;\\n- separate compatible claims from incompatible ones;\\n- determine which claims survived later corrections or audits;\\n- retain theorem, equation, section, and page locations when available.\\n\\nDo not dump this ledger as an opening summary. Use it to teach the requested block faithfully. Attribute source claims and distinguish them from your own interpretation.\\n\\nMaintain an internal lesson ledger across turns: stable notation, completed blocks, the last point the user clearly understood, unresolved confusion, and the next logical block. Minimally recall any earlier definition needed in the current response.\\n\\n## Control the pace\\n\\nTeach exactly one natural conceptual block per response. If the user specifies a starting point, begin there. Otherwise begin with the document's objects, assumptions, and concrete setup.\\n\\nUse this domain-neutral progression as a guide:\\n\\n1. objects, assumptions, and setup;\\n2. exact concrete or finite formulation;\\n3. meaning and type of the central variables or maps;\\n4. derivation of the main relations;\\n5. proof or mechanism architecture;\\n6. obstruction, non-closure, or source of difficulty;\\n7. proposed abstraction, approximation, or limiting construction;\\n8. assumptions connecting the construction to the original problem;\\n9. theoretical and empirical evidence;\\n10. remaining gaps and falsifying cases.\\n\\nAdjust the sequence to the sources. Do not jump ahead merely because later material is related. When the user says “next block,” continue with only the next logically necessary idea. When the user is confused, stop advancing and rebuild from the last understood point.\\n\\nWrite each ordinary lesson turn as a compact, seamless blackboard explanation: a few short paragraphs, only the equations needed now, few or no bullets, and normally no headings. Do not begin or end with a broad recap. Stop naturally after the current block and wait for the user before moving deeper.\\n\\n## Construct each block\\n\\nUse this internal order without displaying it as a template:\\n\\n1. **Minimal recall:** restate only the definitions needed now.\\n2. **New object:** define one new object and give its mathematical type.\\n3. **Derivation:** obtain its relation from the preceding setup.\\n4. **Meaning:** explain the role of every term or condition.\\n5. **Analogue or example:** use one structurally faithful simple case when it genuinely helps, then return to the exact mathematics.\\n6. **Role:** connect the object to the document's argument.\\n7. **Status:** state what is established and what remains unproved.\\n\\nMake the block self-contained for its present conceptual burden, not for the entire project.\\n\\n## Enforce notation and type discipline\\n\\nUse a small, stable notation. Prefer the source's symbols unless they collide or obscure meaning. If renaming is necessary, state the mapping explicitly.\\n\\nDefine every symbol before using it. For each object, state as relevant:\\n\\n- its domain, codomain, or ambient space;\\n- whether it is a scalar, vector, matrix, function, operator, random variable, measure, index, or coordinate;\\n- whether it is fixed, sampled, or evolving;\\n- what each index means;\\n- its dependence on other variables or parameters.\\n\\nDistinguish carefully between:\\n\\n- one function of an entire vector and a vector of coordinatewise functions;\\n- an index and a coordinate;\\n- a fixed label or parameter and an evolving state;\\n- a representative element and a probability law over elements;\\n- a mathematical state and a numerical representation of that state.\\n\\nWhen writing an integral, identify the integration variable, its space, the measure, and what the integral averages or accumulates. When a distribution is specified only at initialization, do not imply that an evolved quantity retains that distribution merely because it remains a function of the initial label.\\n\\nDo not introduce shorthand unless the explicit expression is genuinely cumbersome and recurs often. Never reuse one symbol for two roles.\\n\\n## Derive rather than paraphrase\\n\\nFor every central equation or implication:\\n\\n1. state the definition or prior relation from which it follows;\\n2. show the relevant algebraic, differential, variational, probabilistic, logical, or limiting step;\\n3. identify the origin and action of every term;\\n4. explain the closest elementary analogue when useful;\\n5. state why the relation matters to the main result.\\n\\nDo not make the user reverse-engineer a displayed formula. Avoid phrases such as “clearly,” “standard,” or “it follows” when they conceal a nontrivial step.\\n\\nWhen invoking an external theorem, state the exact form needed, list its hypotheses, verify them in the present setting, and explain the resulting conclusion. Check a primary or otherwise authoritative source before relying on a recent, specialized, or uncertain result.\\n\\nWhen teaching a long proof, devote one block to its architecture before teaching the difficult steps. Explain how the claims, cases, or lemmas fit together without pretending that the architecture is itself the proof.\\n\\nUse analogies only when they preserve the relevant mathematical structure. State where the analogy stops and return immediately to the exact object.\\n\\n## Relate levels of description\\n\\nBegin with the document's concrete objects and exact relations. Introduce an abstract, aggregate, continuum, asymptotic, or numerical description only after showing how it arises from the concrete one.\\n\\nWhenever the source changes levels, identify:\\n\\n- the map from the original objects to the new description;\\n- what information is retained and discarded;\\n- which step is an exact identity;\\n- which step uses a theorem and its assumptions;\\n- which step is formal, heuristic, empirical, or conjectural.\\n\\nIf a proposed state or summary evolves autonomously, distinguish internal closure from fidelity to the original system. An autonomous reduced description may still omit information that changes the original system's future.\\n\\nIf the source uses truncation or approximation, distinguish pointwise or static approximation from the stronger control required by an evolving or iterative system. Examine separately:\\n\\n- error that leaves the retained representation;\\n- feedback from omitted components;\\n- amplification or accumulation of error;\\n- the time, parameter, or regime over which stability is claimed.\\n\\nWhen the source calls a construction “finite,” state precisely whether this means finitely many coordinates, finitely many fields, a finite-dimensional state, a finite cutoff, or merely a finite numerical discretization.\\n\\n## Label epistemic status\\n\\nClassify each important source-dependent claim using the most accurate category:\\n\\n- exact identity;\\n- proved theorem under stated assumptions;\\n- rigorous result for a restricted or finite setting;\\n- formal argument;\\n- heuristic mechanism;\\n- empirical or numerical evidence;\\n- conjectural identification;\\n- open implication;\\n- counterexample or negative result.\\n\\nDo not silently promote a claim. Express each important assumption as a missing implication:\\n\\n1. what is assumed;\\n2. what conclusion it would justify;\\n3. what connecting step is not established;\\n4. what observation or counterexample would refute it.\\n\\nInternal consistency of a proposed theory does not prove that it represents the target object.\\n\\n## Evaluate evidence and alternatives\\n\\nFor every numerical comparison, identify:\\n\\n- the systems or quantities compared;\\n- what the reference actually represents;\\n- the metric or norm;\\n- the scale of the predicted effect and the discrepancy;\\n- discretization, optimization, rounding, and sampling uncertainty as applicable;\\n- the alternative explanation ruled out;\\n- what the experiment leaves unresolved.\\n\\nDo not infer convergence or a scaling law from too few resolutions. Treat numerical thresholds as decision conventions unless the source proves a mathematical boundary.\\n\\nWhen the user requests a hostile or devil's-advocate analysis, include only alternatives that engage the declared target and make a distinct mathematical or quantitative prediction. Do not infer global behavior from a local or instantaneous property without proving the connecting step. Do not use possible error cancellation as a complete explanation unless it predicts the observed pattern.\\n\\n## Repair confusion completely\\n\\nWhen the user identifies an undefined symbol, ambiguity, missing derivation, or conceptual gap:\\n\\n- acknowledge the failure directly;\\n- do not defend the prior wording;\\n- do not append a one-sentence patch;\\n- rewrite the entire relevant block self-containedly;\\n- preserve compactness and stable notation.\\n\\nTreat confusion as evidence that the explanation needs rebuilding, not that the mathematics is inherently inaccessible.\\n\\nBefore sending each block, verify silently that it is faithful to the sources, introduces only one conceptual burden, defines every needed symbol, derives rather than paraphrases, states the status of important claims, stays in the user's language, avoids unnecessary notation and formula dumps, and is compact enough to absorb in one sitting.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf65b92a487d2a22e4bbfe590fd89\",\"summary\":[\"**Generalizing the initialization scaling**\",\"**Checking the initial drift**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_nmUurdXxsLm5EJt28L4by67j\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-3326-7303-96a6-3fc7a87cd0a4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_limit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_R6257d7FtdR6kpbkD7IVDqMb\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-099c-7b50-a5c8-f3a360d99bfe\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_ADU24jKGu9Mbp2b8zxeuZucK\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf681bb5487d29439bb0809d2ac5a\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing scaling table**\",\"**Refining rate-scaling conditions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf690416487d2b9389532d1f1c74a\",\"summary\":[\"**Proving middle-weight movement**\",\"**Checking scaling assumptions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf69e4b0487d2890c9762f0a08027\",\"summary\":[\"**Selecting nonlinearity criterion**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf6acd1c887d2969bdd54e11fc1e7\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking Gaussian-tail closure**\",\"**Bounding Volterra growth**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf6bd161c87d293960b0c391b33ba\",\"summary\":[\"**Narrowing the μP claim**\",\"**Checking μP rate conventions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf6d008dc87d2990ebfb0a7d17ce6\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking table 8**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf6d60c3487d2be8baddb9dcbdd8b\",\"text\":\"The nontriviality claims do hold, with one qualification: because the limiting readout starts at zero, hidden-feature velocity is zero at \\\\(t=0\\\\), then grows proportionally to \\\\(t\\\\). At each fixed sufficiently small \\\\(t>0\\\\), it has a positive, width-independent limit. The loss is already decreasing at \\\\(t=0\\\\). The proof also gives a positive error for the best affine fit to arctan on each hidden distribution, which rules out an asymptotically linear activation regime.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-74618213-9ac6-4c58-b350-a552967fa63a\",\"query\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.03466\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.03466\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf6da724087d2ab375edf5c7088e7\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking table 8**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-0847ceec-582f-4ead-b392-152e54811bf4\",\"query\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.03466\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.03466\"}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-f25e1614-b850-4ffc-bf14-80adcf46eb91\",\"query\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/microsoft/mup/main/mup/optim.py\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/microsoft/mup/main/mup/optim.py\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf6eab25887d29e127b7a16959591\",\"summary\":[\"**Selecting p22 screenshot**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-98916863-e74b-4883-92fb-12ac64f65e77\",\"query\":\"\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"other\"}},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0713a-a39a-72f2-86d7-1ba86ce01820\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-099c-7b50-a5c8-f3a360d99bfe\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0713a-e274-7d82-9f4b-97c7e7eb49b8\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0713a-8ea2-7033-85be-51554e4c5110\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-3326-7303-96a6-3fc7a87cd0a4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_limit\"},{\"type\":\"contextCompaction\",\"id\":\"01a0713c-9853-7b83-858a-772b37b0ad33\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf757dd3487d2bf6031c68d2028ea\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking activity-proof details**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf75e2dc087d29bc154b7561f6a58\",\"text\":\"I’m using the teaching and rigorous-math skills to separate what the proof establishes from the comparison with μP. The scaling check gives a broader family: positive, width-independent initialization and learning-rate constants can vary, and the readout can shrink at any positive power of width. The zero-readout initialization remains an important part of the result.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-923af5a7-9059-418c-b833-88c8695f55dd\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' /tmp/THREE_INPUT_SCALED_LIMIT_EXTENSION_CHECK.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Three-input local limit with initialization and learning-rate constants\\n\\nThis extends the existence/convergence portion of `/tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md`. Strict feature-learning expansions must incorporate the constants separately.\\n\\n## A simple sufficient family\\n\\nKeep the three fixed normalized inputs, their Gram matrix \\\\(G\\\\), the forward normalization, output normalization, activation, and summed loss unchanged. Initialize independently by\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ij}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\sigma_1^2),\\\\qquad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\sigma_2^2/n),\\\\qquad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\sigma_3^2n^{-2\\\\beta}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(0<\\\\sigma_1,\\\\sigma_2<\\\\infty\\\\), \\\\(0\\\\le\\\\sigma_3<\\\\infty\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\beta>0\\\\). Exact zero readout is allowed. Use layer learning-rate multipliers \\\\(n\\\\kappa_1,\\\\kappa_2,n\\\\kappa_3\\\\), with \\\\(0<\\\\kappa_\\\\ell<\\\\infty\\\\). The exact updates become\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}&=z_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\kappa_1\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}&=W_k^{(2)}\\n-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n\\\\kappa_2}{n}\\\\sum_b\\nr_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}&=W_k^{(3)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\kappa_3\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor every positive \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\), the same local theorem holds, with population first-layer root \\\\(N(0,\\\\sigma_1^2G)\\\\), population initial readout zero, and initial Gaussian matrix action of variance scale \\\\(\\\\sigma_2^2\\\\). Each population differential equation acquires its respective factor \\\\(\\\\kappa_\\\\ell\\\\). The positive existence interval depends on fixed scale bounds but not on width, actual learning rate, auxiliary mesh, or a lower Gram eigenvalue. For scale parameters in a fixed compact set with finite upper bounds, the existence time can be common. Positivity of the scales is needed for the strict feature-learning claims, not for the estimates themselves.\\n\\nThe weighted prediction kernel is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK=\\\\kappa_1K^{(1)}+\\\\kappa_2K^{(2)}+\\\\kappa_3K^{(3)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus \\\\(\\\\dot f=-2Kr\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\dot L=-4r^TKr\\\\). One can either retain the old unweighted block definitions and display these coefficients, or define weighted blocks explicitly. Omitting the coefficients from the prediction equation would be incorrect.\\n\\n## Every bridge in the proof\\n\\nThe Gaussian root theorem permits \\\\(\\\\sigma_1^2G\\\\), even when singular, by writing the root as \\\\(\\\\sigma_1G^{1/2}\\\\gamma\\\\). Initial matrix operator concentration changes only to\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le2\\\\,9^{2n}\\\\exp[-nM^2/(8\\\\sigma_2^2)].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nChoose \\\\(M\\\\) large compared with \\\\(\\\\sigma_2\\\\). The construction of the common bounded action and its adjoint is unchanged.\\n\\nHere is the exact correction to the response formulas. Keep \\\\(A,C\\\\) defined as expectations of derivatives with respect to the actual Gaussian slots. Their covariances now are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\xi_{b,s}]\\n=\\\\sigma_2^2\\\\mathbb E[H_{a,k}^{(1)}H_{b,s}^{(1)}],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\eta_{b,s}]\\n=\\\\sigma_2^2\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBoth derivative-response sums acquire a factor \\\\(\\\\sigma_2^2\\\\):\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n=\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sigma_2^2\\\\sum_{b,s<k}C_{ak,bs}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\n=\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sigma_2^2\\\\sum_{b,s\\\\le k}A_{ak,bs}H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently, the two complete recursions are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\n\\\\sum_{b,s<k}\\n\\\\left(\\\\sigma_2^2C_{ak,bs}-2\\\\kappa_2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\n\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\\\right)\\n\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_{a,k}=\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\n\\\\sum_{b,s\\\\le k}\\n\\\\left(\\\\sigma_2^2A_{ak,bs}\\n-\\\\mathbf1_{s<k}2\\\\kappa_2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}]\\\\right)\\nH_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe variance factor multiplies the initial-matrix responses, not the trained rank-one memory.\\n\\nThe readout estimate is now\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB_{k+1}\\\\le B_k+6\\\\kappa_3\\\\Delta\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n(1+\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty B_k),\\\\qquad B_0=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus \\\\(B_k\\\\le C_0T\\\\), \\\\(|r_{a,k}|\\\\le C_0\\\\), and \\\\(|\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}|\\\\le C_0T\\\\), with constants depending on \\\\(\\\\kappa_3\\\\). The forward and backward training-memory row sums remain \\\\(O(T)\\\\) and \\\\(O(T^3)\\\\). The derivative recurrences then retain the form\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\le CT\\\\exp[CT(1+\\\\mathcal A_{k-1})+CT^4],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nV_k\\\\le\\\\max\\\\{V_{k-1},1+C_1T(\\\\mathcal C_k+C_0T)V_{k-1}\\\\},\\n\\\\qquad \\\\mathcal A_k\\\\le C_1TV_k,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nafter absorbing fixed factors \\\\(\\\\kappa_1,\\\\kappa_2,\\\\kappa_3,\\\\sigma_2^2\\\\) into constants. The same short-time induction gives \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_k,\\\\mathcal C_k=O(T)\\\\), \\\\(V_k\\\\le2\\\\), and a Gaussian backward tail bound uniform over meshes. No lower eigenvalue of \\\\(G\\\\) enters.\\n\\nThe localization, Euler Cauchy, existence, uniqueness, autonomy, oracle and actual-GD comparison arguments are therefore unchanged. They use finite upper bounds for the readout supremum and matrix operator norm. For the actual initial readout, for every \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\sigma_3>0\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_{n,0}^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty>\\\\varepsilon)\\n\\\\le2n\\\\exp[-\\\\varepsilon^2n^{2\\\\beta}/(2\\\\sigma_3^2)]\\\\to0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\|W_{n,0}^{(3)}\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\right]\\n=\\\\sigma_3^2n^{-2\\\\beta}\\\\to0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe oracle can therefore still start from zero readout. Its initialization discrepancy vanishes, and the actual readout supremum is bounded with probability tending to one. These are precisely the two initialization obligations in the original bridge. There is no special role for \\\\(\\\\beta=1\\\\).\\n\\nThe same kernel, probe, hidden-path and hidden-velocity convergence statements follow. The physical parameter-speed identities include the squared learning multipliers:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot W^{(1)}\\\\|_F^2/n=4\\\\kappa_1^2r^TK^{(1)}r,\\n\\\\quad\\n\\\\|\\\\dot W^{(2)}\\\\|_F^2=4\\\\kappa_2^2r^TK^{(2)}r,\\n\\\\quad\\n\\\\|\\\\dot W^{(3)}\\\\|_2^2/n=4\\\\kappa_3^2r^TK^{(3)}r.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAccordingly \\\\(-\\\\dot L\\\\) is the sum of these three quantities divided by their respective \\\\(\\\\kappa_\\\\ell\\\\).\\n\\n## Converging learning-rate constants\\n\\nThe same theorem allows \\\\(\\\\kappa_{\\\\ell,n}\\\\to\\\\kappa_\\\\ell\\\\in(0,\\\\infty)\\\\). The sequences are eventually uniformly bounded. In the oracle comparison, replacing the actual coefficients by their limits adds a vector-field forcing bounded by\\n\\n\\\\[\\nC\\\\sum_{\\\\ell=1}^3|\\\\kappa_{\\\\ell,n}-\\\\kappa_\\\\ell|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe bound follows from the already proved uniform mean-square bounds on each component velocity; it requires no extra differentiability or finite-coordinate tail estimate. The comparison bracket therefore becomes\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W_{n,0}^{(3)}\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\n+(1+R)(\\\\eta_n+\\\\Delta)\\n+\\\\sum_\\\\ell|\\\\kappa_{\\\\ell,n}-\\\\kappa_\\\\ell|\\n+e^{-cR^2}+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll terms vanish in the existing order of limits. No convergence rate for \\\\(\\\\kappa_{\\\\ell,n}\\\\) is required. The same coupling argument would also permit converging positive \\\\(\\\\sigma_{1,n},\\\\sigma_{2,n}\\\\), with an additional vanishing initialization discrepancy, but fixed scales are already a sufficient family.\\n\\n## Separate bounded, nonvanishing readout extension\\n\\nSuppose instead the initial readout coordinates are iid copies of an independent random variable \\\\(W_0^{(3)}\\\\) with \\\\(|W_0^{(3)}|\\\\le B_0<\\\\infty\\\\). Keep that same initialization in the finite oracle and in the population state. The local existence/convergence argument still closes, but the limiting system and its small-time expansion are different.\\n\\nFor the finite-program step, a bounded scalar root can be represented as a bounded measurable function of an independent Gaussian root, using its quantile function. All response derivatives are taken with respect to forward/backward Gaussian slots, not that initial-root coordinate. Equivalently one can use the finite Gaussian conditioning theorem with an independent bounded root. Fixed-program moments and the needed response derivatives remain valid.\\n\\nThe changed estimates are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W_k^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\ |\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}|\\n\\\\le C(B_0+T),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\eta_{a,k}^2\\\\le C(B_0+T)^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe backward learned-memory row sum is now \\\\(O(T)\\\\), not \\\\(O(T^3)\\\\). Differentiating the readout still contributes \\\\(CTV_k\\\\), since its independent initial root has zero derivative in every forward Gaussian slot. Differentiating the second-layer backpropagated field contributes at most \\\\(C(B_0+T)V_k\\\\). Thus\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\le CT\\n\\\\exp[CT(1+\\\\mathcal A_{k-1})+CT^2],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nV_k\\\\le\\\\max\\\\{V_{k-1},\\n1+C(B_0+T)(\\\\mathcal C_k+CT)V_{k-1}\\\\},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\le C(B_0+T)V_k.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTake an \\\\(O(1+B_0)\\\\) cap for \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\), an \\\\(O(T)\\\\) cap for \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\), and choose \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) so the coefficient of \\\\(V_{k-1}\\\\) in the second alternative is at most \\\\(1/2\\\\). The same causal induction gives \\\\(V_k\\\\le2\\\\), bounded response row sums, and a mesh-uniform Gaussian tail for \\\\(P_{a,k}\\\\). All existence/convergence bridges follow. The initial readout discrepancy is zero because the oracle uses the actual sampled root.\\n\\nThis does **not** retain the earlier \\\\(t^2\\\\) leading hidden displacement or the old proof of nonconstant kernel. With a nonzero initial readout, hidden training usually begins at order \\\\(t\\\\). Those strict conclusions require a separate calculation. Also, unbounded \\\\(O(1)\\\\) Gaussian initial readout is not covered by this bounded-readout proof; its supremum and population essential supremum are unbounded, so additional localization is needed.\\n\\nThe safe primary claim is therefore: arbitrary positive finite first/second initialization scales, any polynomially vanishing Gaussian rescaled readout, and positive finite converging layer learning multipliers preserve the full local-limit proof; bounded nonvanishing iid readout preserves local existence/convergence by the explicitly modified bootstrap, but is a different initial-state theorem.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-4ea6337a-2c71-4b26-b5d5-78c0eefd5fe7\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,320p' /tmp/three_input_activity_lemma.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Three-input activity lemma, including rank-two input Gram matrices\\n\\nAssume the local autonomous population flow and its mean-square continuity have been established with the same equations as the two-input flow, with all input sums now over a=1,2,3. Let x_a/sqrt(d) have norm one, and assume x_a and x_b are never parallel or antiparallel. Labels y_a are arbitrary signs. The input Gram matrix G is positive semidefinite with diagonal one; it need not be invertible.\\n\\nWrite the first-layer Gaussian root as Z_{0,a}^{(1)}=w^T x_a/sqrt(d), w~N(0,I_d), and H_{0,a}^{(1)}=atan(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}). Define Q_ab=E[H_{0,a}^{(1)}H_{0,b}^{(1)}].\\n\\n## Q is positive definite even when G is singular\\n\\nIf a linear combination of these arctangents vanishes almost surely, continuity and the full support of w make it vanish for every w. Choose v outside the finitely many proper hyperplanes defined by v^T x_a=0 and v^T(x_a±x_b)=0. This is possible by pairwise nonparallelity. The numbers b_a=v^T x_a/sqrt(d) are nonzero and have distinct squares. Restrict the identity to w=t v. The coefficients of t,t^3,t^5 in the arctangent Taylor series give\\n\\n    sum_a c_a b_a (b_a^2)^k = 0, k=0,1,2.\\n\\nThe Vandermonde determinant is nonzero, so c_a b_a=0 for every a, hence c=0. Thus Q is positive definite.\\n\\nConsequently Y_a:=Z_{0,a}^{(2)} are a full-support centered Gaussian triple with covariance Q. Define\\n\\n    S = sum_a y_a atan(Y_a),\\n    U_a = S phi'(Y_a),\\n    P_a = (W_0^{(2)})^* U_a,\\n    B_a = phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}) P_a,\\n    V_ab = E[U_a U_b],    D_ab = E[B_a B_b].\\n\\nThe event S=0 has probability zero: conditional on Y_2,Y_3, S is a strictly monotone function of Y_1, and Y_1 has a continuous conditional density. If a linear combination of U_a vanishes, division by S and full support imply sum_a c_a/(1+Y_a^2)=0 everywhere. Varying one coordinate at a time forces each c_a=0. Thus V is positive definite.\\n\\nThe first reused transpose call has the Gaussian conditioning representation\\n\\n    P_a = sum_c H_{0,c}^{(1)} [Q^{-1} E(Y U_a)]_c + Gamma_a,\\n\\nwhere Gamma~N(0,V) is independent of the first-layer Gaussian root. Therefore the conditional covariance of B given the first-layer root is\\n\\n    diag(phi'(Z_0^{(1)})) V diag(phi'(Z_0^{(1)})),\\n\\nwhich is positive definite everywhere. It follows that D is positive definite.\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n    T_a = sum_b G_ab y_b B_b,\\n    A_a = phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}) T_a,\\n    R_a = sum_b y_b Q_ab U_b + W_0^{(2)} A_a.\\n\\nThe first-layer t^2 coefficient T_a is nonzero in mean square for every a. Indeed its coefficient of Gamma_a conditional on the roots includes G_aa y_a phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}), which is nonzero; positive definiteness of V makes its conditional variance positive.\\n\\n## Each second-layer coefficient is nonzero; no permutation symmetry is needed\\n\\nLet alpha_a=Q^{-1}E[H_0^{(1)}A_a], and subtract the projection onto the three initial forward inputs:\\n\\n    A_a^perp = A_a - sum_c alpha_{a,c} H_{0,c}^{(1)},\\n    sigma_a^2 = E[(A_a^perp)^2].\\n\\nConditional on the first-layer roots, A_a is a linear function of Gamma with coefficients\\n\\n    c_b = phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}) G_ab y_b phi'(Z_{0,b}^{(1)}).\\n\\nIn particular c_a=y_a phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)})^2 is nonzero. Thus\\n\\n    sigma_a^2 >= E Var(A_a | first-layer roots) = E[c^T V c] > 0.\\n\\nCondition the initial matrix on all three already revealed forward calls W_0^{(2)}H_{0,c}^{(1)}=Y_c and all three transpose calls (W_0^{(2)})^*U_b=P_b. The next forward call has law\\n\\n    W_0^{(2)}A_a = sum_c alpha_{a,c}Y_c + sum_b beta_{a,b}U_b + sigma_a gamma_a,\\n    beta_a = V^{-1} E[P A_a^perp],\\n\\nwhere gamma_a is standard Gaussian independent of the second-layer history Y (and therefore of U). This follows directly from the Gaussian matrix conditional mean and its independent doubly projected residual; the fixed-rank projection of a fresh Gaussian output disappears in normalized mean square. The Gram matrices Q,V are positive definite, so the displayed inverse formulas are legitimate. A separate such call suffices for each a; joint independence of different gamma_a is not claimed.\\n\\nThe other terms in R_a are functions of Y. Consequently\\n\\n    E[R_a^2] >= sigma_a^2 > 0.\\n\\nThis establishes each input's second-hidden-layer activity without the two-input proof's swap argument.\\n\\n## Small-time expansion and strict conclusions\\n\\nSubstitution in the integral equations, using bounded activation derivatives and the bounded initial action, gives\\n\\n    W^{(3)}(t) = 2t S + o_L2(t),\\n    delta_a^{(2)}(t) = 2t U_a + o_L2(t),\\n    Z_a^{(1)}(t)-Z_{0,a}^{(1)} = 2t^2 T_a + o_L2(t^2),\\n    W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)} = 2t^2 sum_b y_b U_b tensor H_{0,b}^{(1)} + o_op(t^2),\\n    Z_a^{(2)}(t)-Y_a = 2t^2 R_a + o_L2(t^2).\\n\\nThe corresponding velocities are 4t T_a+o_L2(t) and 4t R_a+o_L2(t). Hence each hidden squared displacement has positive leading coefficient 4E[T_a^2] or 4E[R_a^2] times t^4, and each integrated squared hidden speed has positive leading coefficient (16/3)E[T_a^2] or (16/3)E[R_a^2] times t^3.\\n\\nSet A_1=y^T(G entrywise-times D)y and A_2=y^T(Q entrywise-times V)y. Both are strictly positive. In particular the first conclusion does not need G positive definite: if D>=lambda I, diagonalizing G as sum_j v_j v_j^T gives\\n\\n    G entrywise-times D = sum_j diag(v_j)D diag(v_j) >= lambda diag(G) = lambda I.\\n\\nLikewise Q entrywise-times V is positive definite. Adjunction gives\\n\\n    sum_a y_a E[U_a R_a] = A_1+A_2 > 0.\\n\\nThus the first and second kernel blocks are\\n\\n    K^{(1)}(t)=4t^2(G entrywise-times D)+o(t^2),\\n    K^{(2)}(t)=4t^2(Q entrywise-times V)+o(t^2),\\n\\nand both are positive definite for sufficiently small positive t. The initial readout block E[atan(Y)atan(Y)^T] is positive definite by the full support of Y, so it remains positive definite for small t. The total kernel satisfies\\n\\n    y^T K(t)y = y^T K^{(3)}(0)y + 8(A_1+A_2)t^2 + o(t^2),\\n\\nso it is nonconstant. The loss starts at three and has derivative -4y^T K^{(3)}(0)y<0, hence strictly decreases on a sufficiently short interval.\\n\\nEvery initial hidden marginal is a nondegenerate Gaussian. Its variance is positive, and atan cannot agree with an affine function on its full support. Thus its best-affine-fit error is positive. Mean-square continuity, and the variance/covariance formula for this fit, preserve these facts on a sufficiently small common interval.\\n\\nThe same proof works for any fixed finite number of pairwise nonparallel normalized inputs; for m inputs the Vandermonde argument uses the first m odd Taylor coefficients.\\n\\n## Positive initialization and learning-rate constants\\n\\nLet the first-weight entries initially have variance sigma_1^2>0 and the second-weight entries variance sigma_2^2/n, with sigma_2>0. Let the three fixed positive layer multipliers be kappa_1,kappa_2,kappa_3, so the population equations are\\n\\n    dot Z_a^{(1)} = -2 kappa_1 sum_b G_ab r_b delta_b^{(1)},\\n    dot W^{(2)} = -2 kappa_2 sum_b r_b delta_b^{(2)} tensor H_b^{(1)},\\n    dot W^{(3)} = -2 kappa_3 sum_b r_b H_b^{(2)}.\\n\\nThe readout initialization tends to zero. All constants are fixed independently of width and step size.\\n\\nThe first roots now have covariance sigma_1^2 G. Their activation Gram matrix Q remains positive definite by the same Vandermonde argument. The second Gaussian root has covariance sigma_2^2 Q and still has full support. Use the preceding definitions S,U,P,B,T,V,D and A_1,A_2 under this changed initial law. The conditional transpose law becomes\\n\\n    P_a = sum_c H_{0,c}^{(1)}[Q^{-1}E(Y U_a)]_c + Gamma_a,\\n    Cov(Gamma) = sigma_2^2 V.\\n\\nThe conditional mean coefficient is unchanged: the sigma_2^2 in the Gaussian regression covariance cancels the one from the initialization variance. All positivity proofs therefore continue to hold.\\n\\nDefine the weighted second-layer coefficient\\n\\n    R_a^kappa = kappa_2 sum_b y_b Q_ab U_b\\n               + kappa_1 W_0^{(2)}[phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}) T_a].\\n\\nThe expansions, including every multiplier, are\\n\\n    W^{(3)}(t) = 2 kappa_3 t S + o_L2(t),\\n    delta_a^{(2)}(t) = 2 kappa_3 t U_a + o_L2(t),\\n    Z_a^{(1)}(t)-Z_{0,a}^{(1)} = 2 kappa_1 kappa_3 t^2 T_a + o_L2(t^2),\\n    W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)} = 2 kappa_2 kappa_3 t^2 sum_b y_b U_b tensor H_{0,b}^{(1)} + o_op(t^2),\\n    Z_a^{(2)}(t)-Y_a = 2 kappa_3 t^2 R_a^kappa + o_L2(t^2).\\n\\nThe fresh Gaussian part of R_a^kappa has variance\\n\\n    kappa_1^2 sigma_2^2 E[(A_a^perp)^2] > 0.\\n\\nThus every first and second hidden coefficient remains strictly nonzero for positive constants. The physical kernel is the weighted sum\\n\\n    K = kappa_1 K^{(1)} + kappa_2 K^{(2)} + kappa_3 K^{(3)},\\n\\nnot the unweighted sum. Its first two blocks have expansions\\n\\n    kappa_1 K^{(1)}(t) = 4 kappa_1 kappa_3^2 t^2 (G entrywise-times D) + o(t^2),\\n    kappa_2 K^{(2)}(t) = 4 kappa_2 kappa_3^2 t^2 (Q entrywise-times V) + o(t^2).\\n\\nThe readout block has label-direction expansion\\n\\n    y^T K^{(3)}(t)y = E[S^2] + 4 kappa_3 t^2 (kappa_1 A_1+kappa_2 A_2) + o(t^2).\\n\\nConsequently\\n\\n    y^T K(t)y = kappa_3 E[S^2]\\n                 + 8 kappa_3^2 (kappa_1 A_1+kappa_2 A_2)t^2 + o(t^2).\\n\\nAll three weighted blocks are positive definite for sufficiently small positive time, and the total kernel is nonconstant.\\n\\n### Exact size and meaning of nontrivial hidden motion\\n\\nThe mean-square velocity limits are\\n\\n    dot Z_a^{(1)}(t)/t -> 4 kappa_1 kappa_3 T_a,\\n    dot Z_a^{(2)}(t)/t -> 4 kappa_3 R_a^kappa.\\n\\nHence for some positive constants c_{ell,a},C_{ell,a} and common positive T_*,\\n\\n    c_{ell,a} t <= sqrt(E|dot Z_a^{(ell)}(t)|^2) <= C_{ell,a} t,\\n    0<t<=T_*.\\n\\nIn particular the RMS hidden speed is positive for each fixed t>0 in the interval and survives the width/step limits. It is not a time-independent positive speed: it equals zero initially and vanishes linearly as t tends to zero. A positive uniform speed lower bound holds on [tau,T_*] for every fixed tau>0, not on (0,T_*].\\n\\nThe squared first-layer displacement and integrated squared speed are respectively\\n\\n    4 kappa_1^2 kappa_3^2 E[T_a^2] t^4 + o(t^4),\\n    (16/3) kappa_1^2 kappa_3^2 E[T_a^2] t^3 + o(t^3).\\n\\nFor the second layer replace kappa_1^2 E[T_a^2] by E[(R_a^kappa)^2].\\n\\nBy contrast, the loss slope is already nonzero at initialization:\\n\\n    -dot L(0) = 4 kappa_3 E[S^2] > 0.\\n\\nContinuity allows the common interval to be reduced so that\\n\\n    -dot L(t) >= 2 kappa_3 E[S^2] > 0, 0<=t<=T_*.\\n\\nOnly the summed loss is asserted to decrease; the individual residual magnitudes need not all decrease.\\n\\nFor the nonlinear claim define the best affine-fit error\\n\\n    E_aff(Z) = inf_{a,b} E[(phi(Z)-a Z-b)^2]\\n             = Var(phi(Z)) - Cov(Z,phi(Z))^2/Var(Z)\\n\\nwhenever Var(Z)>0. Every initial hidden marginal is a nondegenerate Gaussian, and atan is not affine on its full support, so the six initial values are positive. Mean-square continuity gives a common short interval with\\n\\n    E_aff(Z_a^{(ell)}(t)) >= (1/2) min_{b,j} E_aff(Z_{0,b}^{(j)}) > 0,\\n\\nand with each hidden variance at least half the minimum initial hidden variance. These are genuine bounds uniform over time including zero, but they depend on the fixed geometry and positive hyperparameters.\\n\\n### What fails at zero parameters\\n\\nIf kappa_3=0, the zero limiting readout stays zero, all backpropagated fields remain zero, and the entire system is frozen. If sigma_1=0 or sigma_2=0, the second-layer activation is initially zero; with zero limiting readout there is no seed for its update and the entire population system is again frozen.\\n\\nIf kappa_1=0, the first hidden layer is fixed and its weighted kernel block is zero. If kappa_2=0, the second weight matrix is fixed and its weighted kernel block is zero, although the second hidden preactivation can still move through the moving first-layer activation. With kappa_1=0 but kappa_2,kappa_3>0, each second hidden preactivation still moves: its coefficient is kappa_2 S sum_b y_b Q_ab phi'(Y_b), which cannot vanish almost surely by full support and the linear independence of the coordinate derivative functions. If kappa_1=kappa_2=0, only readout training remains and the total kernel is constant.\\n\\nStrict positivity for every fixed positive choice does not imply a lower bound uniform as a hyperparameter approaches zero or as the allowed input configurations approach a degenerate boundary. The existence time and all quantitative nontriviality constants may depend on those fixed choices, but not on width or step size.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7623be087d2a6c3818d8a54ffb3\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking proof details.**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-8d7bae66-83de-4007-9c15-d8094e18ce2c\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,200p' /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Three inputs: a rigorous local-time joint width and gradient-flow limit\\n\\n## Result and scope\\n\\nFix three inputs \\\\(x_1,x_2,x_3\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a\\\\|_2^2}{d}=1,\\\\qquad\\nx_a\\\\ne x_b,\\\\quad x_a\\\\ne -x_b\\\\quad(a\\\\ne b),\\\\qquad\\nG_{ab}=\\\\frac{x_a^\\\\top x_b}{d}.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause the norms agree, the conditions on \\\\(x_a,x_b\\\\) are exactly pairwise nonparallelity. The \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) matrix \\\\(G\\\\) is positive semidefinite with diagonal one. It may be singular. All input indices and input sums below range over \\\\(1,2,3\\\\).\\nLet \\\\(y_1,y_2,y_3\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence of positive steps \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all such triples and all eight label sign patterns. Moreover, the existence time \\\\(T_0\\\\) can be chosen uniformly over these triples and labels: its construction uses only three inputs, \\\\(|G_{ab}|\\\\le1\\\\), and the fixed activation and initialization scales. It does not depend on width, step size, input dimension, or a lower bound on a Gram eigenvalue.\\n\\nPredictions, residuals, loss, and all entries of all three kernel blocks converge in probability uniformly on this interval. The joint three-input hidden path laws converge with their second moments in the uniform path norm. Integrated squared hidden velocities converge. The joint empirical averages of any fixed finite collection of continuous, globally Lipschitz forward/adjoint probes, with bounded products when a product instruction is used, converge to their corresponding population expectations, uniformly in time. Unbounded first-layer backward fields and their quadratic contractions are included by the tail argument below.\\n\\nHere a fixed action probe is a finite calculation from the current fields and constants using fixed globally Lipschitz coordinate functions, linear combinations, and appropriately typed calls to \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) and its adjoint. A product instruction is allowed when both varying factors are uniformly bounded, for example after clipping. Final same-layer joint measurements can be continuous with at most quadratic growth. Probe length and functions do not grow with width or depend on the learning rate.\\n\\nOn a possibly smaller interval \\\\((0,T_*]\\\\), each input's preactivations move in both hidden layers, all three \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) kernel blocks are positive definite, the total kernel is nonconstant, loss strictly decreases, and \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) has positive best-affine-fit error on each hidden preactivation distribution.\\n\\n**Scope:** this is a local-time theorem. A convergence theorem on every finite horizon is not claimed for arbitrary input geometry. \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) is chosen before taking width and learning-rate limits and does not shrink in those limits. The strictly positive feature-learning coefficients and the possibly smaller activity interval \\\\(T_*\\\\) may depend on the fixed input geometry and labels.\\n\\nThe inputs are fixed while taking the width and learning-rate limits. A common existence interval across configurations is not a claim of a convergence rate uniform over configurations that themselves change with width.\\n\\n## The finite network and its exact updates\\n\\nThe dimensions are \\\\(W^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times d}\\\\), \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\). For input \\\\(a=1,2,3\\\\), use\\n\\\\[\\nz_a^{(1)}=\\\\frac{W^{(1)}x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nz_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi=\\\\arctan,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h_a^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad\\nr_{n,a}=f_{n,a}-y_a,\\\\qquad L_n=\\\\sum_{a=1}^3r_{n,a}^2.\\n\\\\]\\nThe summed loss retains the earlier factors of two; using the average of the three losses divides the speed by three.\\n\\nAll initialization entries are independent:\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ij}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently each first-layer root triple is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), independently over neuron index \\\\(i\\\\). The three preactivations for one neuron may be correlated and may satisfy an exact linear relation. This is permitted.\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta_a^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_{n,a}/\\\\partial z_a^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\).\\nIn the displayed rescaled parameterization the layer learning-rate multipliers are \\\\(n,1,n\\\\). The exact first-weight update is\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n=W_k^{(1)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{\\\\sqrt d}\\n\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)}x_b^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\nMultiplication by \\\\(x_a/\\\\sqrt d\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=z_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{n}\\n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)} .\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M1}\\n\\\\]\\nUse physical time \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), linearly interpolate these parameters, and recompute the forward pass at intermediate times.\\n\\nThe whole effect of input geometry on first-layer training is \\\\(G\\\\): training on input \\\\(b\\\\) moves input \\\\(a\\\\)'s preactivation with coefficient \\\\(G_{ab}\\\\). If \\\\(Gc=0\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\sum_a c_a z_a^{(1)}=0\\\\) initially and every update preserves this relation. No inverse of \\\\(G\\\\) is used.\\n\\nThe finite continuous flow has the kernel blocks\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}_{n,ab}\\n=G_{ab}\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(1)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(1)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(2)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(1)})^\\\\top h_b^{(1)}}n\\n \\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(2)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(3)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(2)})^\\\\top h_b^{(2)}}n .\\n\\\\tag{M2}\\n\\\\]\\nDifferentiation gives \\\\(\\\\dot f_n=-2K_n r_n\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\dot L_n=-4r_n^\\\\top K_n r_n\\\\), where \\\\(K_n\\\\) is the sum of the blocks. Each block is positive semidefinite: it is the Gram matrix of the corresponding scaled parameter gradients. Individual residual magnitudes need not decrease; their squared sum does.\\n\\n## Why a new stability estimate is needed\\n\\nWith \\\\(F(s)=s+s^3/3\\\\), the componentwise transformation \\\\(X_a^{(1)}=F(Z_a^{(1)})\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot X_a^{(1)}\\n=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\n\\\\frac{1+(Z_a^{(1)})^2}{1+(Z_b^{(1)})^2}\\n(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{M3}\\n\\\\]\\nIf the inputs are mutually orthogonal, only diagonal terms occur and the ratios cancel. Otherwise nonzero cross-input terms remain and their ratios are unbounded. We therefore work with the original preactivations and prove a uniform tail estimate for the backward fields.\\n\\nThe exact limiting equations will be\\n\\\\[\\nH_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\nf_a=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H_a^{(2)}],\\\\quad r_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot Z_a^{(1)}&=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\\\delta_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_b\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_bH_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M4}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\((u\\\\otimes v)V=u\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[vV]\\\\). This definition displays the normalization that replaces the finite factor \\\\(1/n\\\\). First-layer and second-layer coordinates belong to separate populations. \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) acts from the former to the latter, and its adjoint acts in reverse. Every expectation pairs coordinates of the same population.\\n\\nThe initial root triple is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), the population readout is zero, and the initial action \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) is the joint forward-and-transpose limit of the initial Gaussian matrix, constructed next. The state consists of three first-layer fields and the shared matrix action and readout field. It has finitely many kinds of state objects, not finitely many scalar degrees of freedom.\\n\\n## The fixed-mesh construction and the new tail lemma\\n\\nWe use the following finite Gaussian fact. For a fixed finite calculation from iid jointly Gaussian coordinate tuples, independent of a Gaussian matrix of entry variance \\\\(1/n\\\\), with polynomially bounded coordinate functions and finitely many calls to that matrix and its transpose, every same-layer joint empirical average of a continuous polynomially bounded measurement has a deterministic limit. The limiting action is Gaussian randomness plus the responses forced by earlier opposite-direction calls. The precise response coefficients and their derivative convention are given and used in the appendix below. This is the specialization of Theorem 2.10 and Box 1 of [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685). Its hypotheses hold here: after fixing population scalar coefficients, each instruction is polynomially bounded, and the training instructions whose derivatives are used below are smooth with polynomially bounded derivatives, as functions of finitely many Gaussian variables. The initial tuple covariance may be singular; represent it as a fixed linear image of independent Gaussian roots. No claim of iid finite coordinates after matrix reuse is used.\\n\\nFor completeness, the underlying conditioning mechanism is as follows. If previous calls have revealed \\\\(W_0^{(2)}V=Y\\\\) and \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R\\\\), then the remaining Gaussian matrix is an independent Gaussian projected onto the directions orthogonal to the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) and \\\\(V\\\\). Its conditional mean obeys both recorded equations. A new product is the response fixed by these equations plus that projected Gaussian applied to the unexplored part of the new input. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the response coefficients. The finite Gaussian theorem makes this induction valid also with dependent query directions. We explicitly rely on this stated finite-program result, not on a claim that the infinite-time or growing-program-length theorem already follows from it.\\n\\nThese finite limits can be realized on two common population coordinate spaces. Here are the details relevant to the subsequent continuous-time argument. Take all the programs needed for rational meshes, their finite unions, and a countable closure under rational linear combinations, the coordinate operations in the proof, and bounded Lipschitz approximations. Their finite joint laws are consistent, so they can be realized jointly. Take the mean-square closure of their linear spans on each layer. Exact finite linear identities pass to these laws.\\n\\nA fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\) satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le2\\\\,9^{2n}e^{-nM^2/8}\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{M5}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed two \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets of the unit sphere each have at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points, and every fixed bilinear form of the matrix is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). This finite operator inequality and joint second-moment convergence imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2]\\n\\\\]\\nfor every generated \\\\(V\\\\). Thus the action is well-defined on equal-in-mean-square variables and extends to the closure. The same reasoning applies to the transpose. The exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u)^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\npasses to the limit and identifies the two actions as adjoints. Bounded coordinate multipliers and their required products belong to these spaces by clipping and mean-square approximation. This constructs the common bounded-operator realization used below; it is not an additional assumption.\\n\\nDefine population Euler for (M4) in this common realization, starting from the stated initialization. Its rank expansion is\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}\\n-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\\\sum_b\\nr_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{M6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus each fixed mesh is a finite Gaussian calculation with deterministic population residuals and contractions. Iterative construction is causal: the current fields determine their expectations and then the next fields. Its realization agrees with the finite Gaussian law, because applying the same rank expansion reduces each action to an initial-matrix call and those deterministic contractions.\\n\\nThe appendix proves the essential new estimate:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta:\\\\,\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\\\ \\n\\\\max_{a,k:\\\\,k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\nc\\\\left|(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\right|^2\\n\\\\right)\\\\le C .\\n\\\\tag{M7}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(T_0,c,C>0\\\\) do not depend on the mesh. In particular the squared tail beyond \\\\(R\\\\) is at most \\\\(C e^{-c'R^2}\\\\), after reducing \\\\(c'\\\\).\\n\\nThe appendix is a derivation, not an assumed light-tail condition. It controls the accumulated deterministic response coefficients on both layers. The second-layer derivatives stay bounded; the first-layer derivatives have a Gaussian exponential bound. A short-time induction closes these bounds uniformly in the number of Euler steps.\\n\\n## Population Euler converges to a unique flow\\n\\nFor comparing two population states use\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\sum_{a=1}^3\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|Z_a^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\tag{M8}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each mean-square norm by the Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\).\\n\\nAll Euler trajectories, and all the actual discrete trajectories with \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\le1\\\\), have bounds independent of width on a fixed bounded time interval, whenever the initial readout supremum and matrix operator norm are bounded. To see this, boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n|r_a|\\\\le 1+\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}_{k+1}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\nC\\\\eta_n(1+\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty).\\n\\\\]\\nThe geometric sum bounds the readout supremum. The rank-one update then bounds the matrix operator norm, and the first-layer update bounds the root-mean-square velocity and displacement. The same calculation applies to population Euler with \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\). All initial bounds hold with probability tending to one for the finite initialization.\\n\\nOn these bounded sets, the forward quantities, residuals, second-layer backward fields, and the maps\\n\\\\[\\n(Z_1^{(1)},Z_2^{(1)},Z_3^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\n\\\\longmapsto (W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\nare Lipschitz in (M8). This follows by adding and subtracting one factor at a time, using boundedness and Lipschitz continuity of \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\), the readout supremum bound, and the operator bound.\\n\\nOnly the first-layer multiplication needs a separate estimate. Write\\n\\\\[\\nP_a=(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-5b1cb9fc-72a7-4327-821f-47579ffb67ea\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '380,565p' /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"\\\\sigma_a^2=\\\\mathbb E[(J_a^\\\\perp)^2].\\n\\\\]\\nThis is ordinary least-squares projection onto the three old \\\\(H\\\\) fields.\\n\\nGiven the first-layer roots, \\\\(J_a\\\\) is a linear function of the Gaussian vector \\\\(\\\\Gamma\\\\), with coefficients\\n\\\\[\\nc_b=\\\\phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)})G_{ab}y_b\\\\phi'(Z_{0,b}^{(1)}).\\n\\\\]\\nIn particular \\\\(c_a=y_a\\\\phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)})^2\\\\ne0\\\\). Subtracting a function of the roots cannot remove this conditional randomness, so\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sigma_a^2\\n\\\\ge\\\\mathbb E[\\\\operatorname{Var}(J_a\\\\mid Z_{0,1}^{(1)},Z_{0,2}^{(1)},Z_{0,3}^{(1)})]\\n=\\\\mathbb E[c^\\\\top Vc]>0.\\n\\\\tag{M18a}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nCondition on the already revealed forward calls\\n\\\\(W_0^{(2)}H_{0,c}^{(1)}=Z_{0,c}^{(2)}\\\\) and transpose calls\\n\\\\((W_0^{(2)})^*U_b=P_b\\\\). The new forward call has the population law\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}J_a\\n=\\\\sum_c\\\\alpha_{a,c}Z_{0,c}^{(2)}\\n+\\\\sum_b\\\\beta_{a,b}U_b+\\\\sigma_a\\\\gamma_a,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\sum_c V_{bc}\\\\beta_{a,c}=\\\\mathbb E[P_bJ_a^\\\\perp].\\n\\\\tag{M18b}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\gamma_a\\\\sim N(0,1)\\\\) is independent of the previous second-layer history. To see the variance, the remaining finite Gaussian matrix is projected orthogonally to the three observed input directions on each side. Applying it to the part of \\\\(J_a\\\\) perpendicular to the old \\\\(H\\\\) inputs gives variance \\\\(\\\\|J_a^\\\\perp\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\). The output projection has fixed rank three, so its removal changes normalized mean square by \\\\(O(1/n)\\\\). Joint empirical convergence then gives (M18b). The response coefficients follow from the two recorded matrix identities. Both Gram inverses are legitimate because \\\\(Q,V\\\\succ0\\\\). One such calculation is made for each \\\\(a\\\\); independence of the three \\\\(\\\\gamma_a\\\\) from one another is neither claimed nor needed.\\n\\nThe learned-matrix contribution \\\\(\\\\sum_b y_bQ_{ab}U_b\\\\) in \\\\(R_a\\\\) depends only on the previous second-layer coordinates. It cannot cancel the independent new Gaussian term. Thus\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[R_a^2]\\\\ge\\\\sigma_a^2>0,\\\\qquad a=1,2,3.\\n\\\\tag{M18c}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSubstitution in the integral equations, beginning with \\\\(W^{(3)}(0)=0\\\\) and \\\\(r_a(0)=-y_a\\\\), gives\\n\\\\[\\nW^{(3)}(t)=2tS+o_{L^2}(t),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}(t)=2tU_a+o_{L^2}(t),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nZ_a^{(1)}(t)-Z_{0,a}^{(1)}=2t^2T_a+o_{L^2}(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nW^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}\\n=2t^2\\\\sum_b y_bU_b\\\\otimes H_{0,b}^{(1)}+o_{\\\\rm op}(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nZ_a^{(2)}(t)-Z_{0,a}^{(2)}=2t^2R_a+o_{L^2}(t^2).\\n\\\\tag{M19}\\n\\\\]\\nFor example the readout integral divided by \\\\(t\\\\) converges to \\\\(2S\\\\), then the second-layer backward field divided by \\\\(t\\\\) converges to \\\\(2U_a\\\\). Integrating the first-layer and matrix velocities therefore gives the factors \\\\(2t^2\\\\). Expanding \\\\(W^{(2)}H_a^{(1)}\\\\) gives the two terms in \\\\(R_a\\\\). Each passage uses bounded \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\), the bounded matrix action, and strong mean-square continuity. Differentiating the activation along these strongly convergent mean-square increments is justified by dominated convergence against their fixed limiting direction; no global differentiability claim for nonlinear maps on the whole mean-square space is used.\\n\\nThe corresponding hidden velocities are \\\\(4tT_a+o_{L^2}(t)\\\\) and \\\\(4tR_a+o_{L^2}(t)\\\\). Consequently each input's squared displacement has positive coefficient \\\\(4\\\\mathbb E[T_a^2]\\\\) or \\\\(4\\\\mathbb E[R_a^2]\\\\) times \\\\(t^4\\\\), and its integrated squared speed has positive coefficient \\\\((16/3)\\\\mathbb E[T_a^2]\\\\) or \\\\((16/3)\\\\mathbb E[R_a^2]\\\\) times \\\\(t^3\\\\).\\n\\nIt remains to check that singular \\\\(G\\\\) does not make a kernel block singular. Let \\\\(\\\\lambda>0\\\\) be the smallest eigenvalue of \\\\(D\\\\). For every \\\\(c\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^3\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\sum_{a,b}c_ac_bG_{ab}D_{ab}\\n&=\\\\frac1d\\\\sum_{j=1}^d\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\left(\\\\sum_a c_a x_{a,j}B_a\\\\right)^2\\\\right]\\\\\\\\\\n&\\\\ge\\\\frac{\\\\lambda}{d}\\\\sum_{j=1}^d\\\\sum_a c_a^2x_{a,j}^2\\n=\\\\lambda\\\\sum_a c_a^2 .\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{M20}\\n\\\\]\\nThis uses normalization, not invertibility of \\\\(G\\\\). Thus the matrix with entries \\\\(G_{ab}D_{ab}\\\\) is positive definite. Likewise the matrix with entries \\\\(Q_{ab}V_{ab}\\\\) is positive definite: if \\\\(\\\\lambda_V>0\\\\) is the smallest eigenvalue of \\\\(V\\\\), its quadratic form is at least \\\\(\\\\lambda_V\\\\sum_aQ_{aa}c_a^2>0\\\\) for \\\\(c\\\\ne0\\\\).\\n\\nPut\\n\\\\[\\nA_1=\\\\sum_{a,b}y_ay_bG_{ab}D_{ab}>0,\\\\qquad\\nA_2=\\\\sum_{a,b}y_ay_bQ_{ab}V_{ab}>0.\\n\\\\]\\nAdjunction and (M17) give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_a y_a\\\\mathbb E[U_aR_a]=A_1+A_2.\\n\\\\]\\nThe kernel expansions are\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}_{ab}(t)=4t^2G_{ab}D_{ab}+o(t^2),\\\\qquad\\nK^{(2)}_{ab}(t)=4t^2Q_{ab}V_{ab}+o(t^2).\\n\\\\]\\nExpanding \\\\(H_a^{(2)}(t)\\\\) using (M19) shows that the readout block contributes \\\\(4(A_1+A_2)t^2\\\\) to its quadratic form in direction \\\\(y\\\\). The other two blocks contribute the same amount. Therefore\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top K(t)y\\n=y^\\\\top K^{(3)}(0)y+8(A_1+A_2)t^2+o(t^2).\\n\\\\tag{M21}\\n\\\\]\\nThe total kernel is nonconstant. The first two blocks are positive definite at sufficiently small positive times by (M20). The initial readout block is positive definite because the second-layer Gaussian triple has full support, and the three coordinate arctangents have no linear relation. It remains positive definite by continuity.\\n\\nFinally,\\n\\\\[\\nL(0)=3,\\\\qquad \\\\dot L(0)=-4y^\\\\top K^{(3)}(0)y<0.\\n\\\\]\\nThe loss strictly decreases on a sufficiently small interval. Every initial hidden marginal is a nondegenerate Gaussian, so it has positive variance and positive best-affine-fit error for \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\). The variance/covariance formula for that fit and mean-square continuity preserve positivity for small time.\\n\\nAll strict conclusions hold on some \\\\((0,T_*]\\\\) with \\\\(0<T_*\\\\le T_0\\\\). These time constants do not depend on \\\\(n\\\\), \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\), or \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\). The existence time \\\\(T_0\\\\) is uniform over normalized triples; the activity interval and positive activity coefficients may depend on the fixed triple and labels. No lower bound uniform near parallel configurations is claimed for those coefficients.\\n\\n## Appendix: the mesh-uniform response calculation\\n\\nThe following calculation supplies (M7). Its equation numbers are local to the appendix.\\n\\nLet the input Gram matrix be\\n\\\\[\\nG_{ab}=x_a^\\\\top x_b/d,\\\\qquad G\\\\succeq0,\\\\quad G_{aa}=1,\\\\quad |G_{ab}|\\\\le1,\\n\\\\]\\nand labels be \\\\(y_1,y_2,y_3\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). Use the summed loss \\\\(\\\\sum_{a=1}^3r_a^2\\\\); averaging the loss only rescales time. The first-layer population root \\\\((Z_{1,0}^{(1)},Z_{2,0}^{(1)},Z_{3,0}^{(1)})\\\\) is centered Gaussian with covariance \\\\(G\\\\). The shared readout starts at \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\).\\n\\nFor fixed mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta>0\\\\), population Euler obeys\\n\\\\[\\nH_{a,k}^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_{a,k}^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(2)}H_{a,k}^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_{a,k}^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\n\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_{a,k}^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nr_{a,k}=\\\\mathbb E[W_k^{(3)}H_{a,k}^{(2)}]-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k+1}^{(1)}=Z_{a,k}^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^3G_{ab}r_{b,k}\\\\phi'(Z_{b,k}^{(1)})P_{b,k},\\n\\\\qquad P_{b,k}:=(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}=W_k^{(2)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_{b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,k}^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_{b,k}H_{b,k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\phi=\\\\arctan\\\\). All response derivatives below hold every deterministic coefficient in these finite recursions fixed, including residuals, contractions, and response coefficients. They do not differentiate through expectations or through the covariance law.\\n\\n## Exact finite-mesh Gaussian response representation\\n\\nIntroduce two centered jointly Gaussian collections \\\\(\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\), independent of one another. The first-layer root is independent of both. Their covariances are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\xi_{b,s}]=\\\\mathbb E[H_{a,k}^{(1)}H_{b,s}^{(1)}],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\eta_{b,s}]=\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}].\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThese are separate-layer probability spaces; taking them independent on a product space is only a convenient representation, not an assertion of a natural pairing of neurons across layers.\\n\\nDefine the deterministic coefficients\\n\\\\[\\nA_{ak,bs}:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}}{\\\\partial\\\\xi_{b,s}}\\\\right],\\\\quad s\\\\le k,\\n\\\\qquad\\nC_{ak,bs}:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial H_{a,k}^{(1)}}{\\\\partial\\\\eta_{b,s}}\\\\right],\\\\quad s<k.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nThe derivatives are symbolic derivatives of the displayed causal recursions, treating Gaussian slots as separate real arguments even when their realized joint covariance is singular. Then the exact limiting initial-matrix actions are\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}=\\n\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s<k}C_{ak,bs}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\n\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}A_{ak,bs}H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nExpanding the learned matrix gives the full equations\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\n\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s<k}\\n\\\\left(C_{ak,bs}-2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\\\right)\\n\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nP_{a,k}=\\n\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}\\n\\\\left(A_{ak,bs}-\\\\mathbf1_{s<k}2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}]\\\\right)\\nH_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nTogether with (1), the readout update in (2), and \\\\(\\\\delta=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)})\\\\), these are causal finite recursions. To construct step \\\\(k\\\\): first construct \\\\(H_{a,k}^{(1)}\\\\) and its derivatives using \\\\(\\\\eta\\\\)-slots through \\\\(k-1\\\\); next construct \\\\(Z_{a,k}^{(2)},\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\) using \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\)-slots through \\\\(k\\\\); finally construct \\\\(P_{a,k}\\\\) and update first-layer coordinates and readout. The Gaussian covariance matrices extend consistently because they are Gram matrices of the constructed source variables.\\n\\nWhy the Gaussian response rule has this form: conditioning on previous forward and transpose products gives the Gaussian matrix regression formula with a projected fresh Gaussian matrix. In the limit, its Gaussian parts in each orientation have covariance equal to the Gram matrix of the sources. Its non-Gaussian part lies in the span of the previous sources in the opposite orientation. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the coefficients of this response as the expectations in (4). The elementary identity used is, for a centered Gaussian vector \\\\(g\\\\) with covariance \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[g_i f(g)]=\\\\sum_j\\\\Sigma_{ij}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\partial_j f(g)].\\n\\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nIt is valid even for singular \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\), by writing \\\\(g=B\\\\gamma\\\\) and integrating by parts in independent standard Gaussian coordinates \\\\(\\\\gamma\\\\). If the conditional regression uses a pseudoinverse, the symbolic derivative coefficients and the pseudoinverse coefficients can differ only by a vector in the covariance nullspace; the corresponding combination of source variables has zero squared norm, so the response itself is unchanged. This is the singular-covariance issue that must be retained in a complete proof.\\n\\nFor a rigorous external formulation of this finite-program identity, see [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685), Box 1, Remarks 2.11–2.12 and Theorem 2.10. The algebraic recursions here are smooth, polynomially bounded functions of finitely many Gaussian slots, and their symbolic derivatives are polynomially bounded for fixed program length. Thus all expectations in (4) exist. The finite-query theorem handles fixed program length; the estimates below are the additional ingredient needed uniformly as length increases. This note does not purport to reprove the entire master theorem in the preceding paragraph.\\n\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf76e616487d2916546d5124bb3d6\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving fixed-input t² bound**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_j3N6pfRbj1ji5XuIdvv0cgmd\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf785e8e087d286196d24640e68ee\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking μP scaling sources**\",\"**Refining local theorem scope**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7989b2887d28fcb7e4bf4e47a1b\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7a72e9c87d290ad06e18e58537d\",\"summary\":[\"**Refining gradient-flow equations**\",\"**Adding rank-one update scaling**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7b40a8c87d2ac6fe1324f76a056\",\"summary\":[\"**Refining the kernel expansion**\",\"**Clarifying kernel variation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0713e-d279-7110-be5c-4a8195edade0\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7bfaf4c87d2b8c8408e736d74aa\",\"summary\":[\"**Reviewing skill requirements**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7c8014087d2b41c8802dcf3c634\",\"summary\":[\"**Updating progress commentary**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7cceff487d28729fbc99042873f\",\"text\":\"The μP comparison is precise: with your explicit \\\\(1/n\\\\) output factor, the SGD learning-rate multipliers \\\\((n,1,n)\\\\) match μP’s width scaling. μP also explicitly permits zero readout initialization. One distinction matters: a nonzero random readout can give initial predictions tending to zero without the readout itself tending to zero—and that changes when hidden-layer motion begins.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-7973d961-28b2-445c-b7cb-30259cbedee3\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' /etc/codex/skills/solve-math-rigorously/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: solve-math-rigorously\\ndescription: Produce compact but complete solutions to standalone mathematical questions and proof requests. Use when the user asks to solve, prove, derive, analyze, verify, or correct a mathematical problem that is not primarily an incremental lesson about supplied technical documents. Infer the strongest reasonable interpretation without inventing assumptions, establish a minimal setup, use minimal notation, expose every nontrivial step, state and verify external theorem hypotheses, give proof architecture when useful, and silently audit the draft before answering.\\n---\\n\\n# Solve Mathematics Rigorously\\n\\nProduce a complete mathematical answer rather than a staged document lesson, unless the user explicitly requests incremental pacing. Optimize simultaneously for correctness, rigor, clarity, and economy.\\n\\n## Determine the intended problem\\n\\nInterpret the strongest reasonable version supported by the wording and mathematical context. Do not exploit an omission to answer a trivial or uninteresting straw-man version.\\n\\nHandle ambiguity according to its effect:\\n\\n- If it does not change the substance, adopt the conventional interpretation and state it briefly.\\n- If one reasonable assumption makes the intended problem precise, state that assumption before solving.\\n- If several reasonable interpretations lead to materially different answers, ask for clarification or give a compact case split when that fully resolves the ambiguity.\\n- If a missing assumption is necessary for the claimed conclusion, do not invent it silently.\\n\\nIf the stated claim is false, say so and give a decisive counterexample or contradiction. Present a corrected nearby statement only when it helps, and label it as a correction rather than the original result.\\n\\nAnswer the problem actually asked. Do not weaken a universal claim to a convenient special case, replace an exact question with a numerical approximation, or prove a converse instead of the stated direction.\\n\\n## Establish a minimal setup\\n\\nBegin with a compact setup or preliminaries passage when it reduces ambiguity or supports later reasoning. State only the necessary:\\n\\n- domain and assumptions;\\n- definitions used in the solution;\\n- boundary, regularity, integrability, independence, or nondegeneracy conditions that matter;\\n- notation that must persist through the answer.\\n\\nReuse the problem's notation whenever possible. Introduce a new symbol only when the unabbreviated expression is genuinely cumbersome and recurs often. Define every new symbol before use and state its mathematical type when that is not immediately evident.\\n\\nDo not force a visible setup heading for a short elementary problem. Do not hide substantive assumptions in prose later in the solution.\\n\\n## Give the architecture before a substantial proof\\n\\nBefore a long or conceptually difficult proof, explain the plan in a short paragraph or compact list. Identify the main steps, cases, claims, or lemmas and how they combine to yield the conclusion.\\n\\nKeep the architecture at the level needed to orient the reader. Do not duplicate the detailed proof, and do not substitute a plan for the proof itself. For a routine calculation or one-step argument, omit the architecture.\\n\\n## Execute every nontrivial step\\n\\nDerive each nontrivial algebraic, analytic, probabilistic, geometric, combinatorial, or logical transition. Give enough detail for the user to verify the step without reconstructing missing reasoning.\\n\\nIn particular:\\n\\n- identify the definition, identity, inequality, or prior claim being used;\\n- show substitutions, rearrangements, case transitions, and changes of variables that carry real mathematical content;\\n- track domains, signs, quantifiers, equality conditions, exceptional cases, and possible zero denominators;\\n- justify limit exchanges, differentiation or integration under a sign, convergence claims, and optimization steps;\\n- distinguish exact arguments from heuristics, approximations, and numerical evidence;\\n- show that intermediate claims actually imply the requested conclusion.\\n\\nAvoid words such as “clearly,” “obviously,” “standard,” and “it follows” when they replace reasoning. They are acceptable only after the supporting reason has been supplied.\\n\\nDo not over-explain elementary arithmetic or definitions already established. Compress routine steps while preserving the chain of verification.\\n\\n## Invoke external results completely\\n\\nWhenever the solution relies on a nontrivial theorem or external fact:\\n\\n1. state the exact form needed, including its assumptions and conclusion;\\n2. verify each assumption in the present problem;\\n3. identify the current objects substituted into the theorem;\\n4. state exactly what conclusion the theorem yields here.\\n\\nDo not merely name a theorem or call a fact standard. Prefer the relevant specialization over an unnecessarily general statement.\\n\\nVerify a recent, specialized, source-specific, or uncertain result against a primary or authoritative source before relying on it. Cite the source near the claim. Paraphrase the relevant result unless its exact wording matters; quote only a short compliant excerpt when useful.\\n\\nIf the required theorem is unavailable, uncertain, or does not apply, expose the gap instead of proceeding as though it were valid.\\n\\n## Use examples and checks proportionately\\n\\nInclude an example, boundary case, counterexample, or remark only when it clarifies an abstraction, tests a hypothesis, explains a consequence, or exposes why an assumption is needed.\\n\\nUse low-cost sanity checks when they add confidence, such as:\\n\\n- substituting the result back into the defining relation;\\n- checking dimensions, signs, or units;\\n- testing a boundary or small case;\\n- comparing with a known special case;\\n- deriving the same conclusion by a second short route.\\n\\nDo not add examples mechanically or let them replace the general argument.\\n\\n## Keep the presentation compact\\n\\nUse headings only when they make a substantial solution easier to navigate. Keep notation sparse and stable. Prefer explicit expressions to disposable shorthand.\\n\\nUse equations to carry mathematical content, not decoration. Avoid formula dumps, decorative summaries, and boxed equations. State the conclusion plainly.\\n\\nLet the problem's complexity determine the answer length. Preserve every necessary assumption, theorem check, and logical transition, but remove repetition and exposition that does not improve understanding or verification.\\n\\n## Audit and revise silently\\n\\nDraft the solution, then review it before sending. Check:\\n\\n1. Did the solution address the strongest reasonable interpretation rather than a straw man?\\n2. Were all adopted assumptions stated, and were none invented merely to force the result?\\n3. Was the setup sufficient but minimal?\\n4. Was every persistent symbol necessary and defined?\\n5. Did a substantial proof receive a useful architecture?\\n6. Can the reader verify every nontrivial transition?\\n7. Was every external theorem stated in the needed form, with its hypotheses checked and its conclusion applied explicitly?\\n8. Were recent, specialized, or uncertain results verified from an authoritative source?\\n9. Were edge cases, equality conditions, domains, and quantifiers handled?\\n10. Does the final conclusion answer the exact question?\\n11. Do examples or remarks earn their space?\\n12. Is the answer as compact as rigor allows, with no boxed equations?\\n\\nRevise any failing part and repeat the audit until the answer satisfies the checklist as well as possible. Do not mention the drafting or audit process in the final answer.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-729356f0-2947-4fb0-892e-3515a053f262\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,320p' /etc/codex/skills/teach-technical-math/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: teach-technical-math\\ndescription: Teach the mathematical content of technical documents—papers, reports, notes, appendices, and linked revisions—through rigorous, self-contained, one-concept-at-a-time lessons. Use when the user supplies or references one or more sources and wants to understand their definitions, equations, derivations, proofs, models, limiting constructions, evidence, assumptions, contradictions across versions, or open gaps over multiple turns; also use when they say “next block” or ask to rebuild a confusing part of such a lesson. Reconcile source versions before teaching, preserve stable typed notation, derive rather than paraphrase, and distinguish exact results from formal, heuristic, empirical, conjectural, and open claims.\\n---\\n\\n# Teach Mathematics from Technical Documents\\n\\nAct as a mathematical and theoretical teacher, not a summarizer. Teach a motivated non-specialist at genuine mathematical depth. Expose the simple structure beneath advanced formalism without suppressing difficult steps.\\n\\n## Prepare from the sources\\n\\nRead every source the user identifies in full mathematical detail before teaching source-dependent claims. If a source is inaccessible, incomplete, or missing cited material, state the limitation and do not imply that it was read.\\n\\nBuild an internal source ledger:\\n\\n- record each source's identity, date, and status, such as preliminary, revised, audited, or final;\\n- map notation and assumption changes across versions;\\n- identify the precise objects, problem, model, regime, observables, main claims, constructions, arguments, evidence, and unresolved gaps;\\n- separate compatible claims from incompatible ones;\\n- determine which claims survived later corrections or audits;\\n- retain theorem, equation, section, and page locations when available.\\n\\nDo not dump this ledger as an opening summary. Use it to teach the requested block faithfully. Attribute source claims and distinguish them from your own interpretation.\\n\\nMaintain an internal lesson ledger across turns: stable notation, completed blocks, the last point the user clearly understood, unresolved confusion, and the next logical block. Minimally recall any earlier definition needed in the current response.\\n\\n## Control the pace\\n\\nTeach exactly one natural conceptual block per response. If the user specifies a starting point, begin there. Otherwise begin with the document's objects, assumptions, and concrete setup.\\n\\nUse this domain-neutral progression as a guide:\\n\\n1. objects, assumptions, and setup;\\n2. exact concrete or finite formulation;\\n3. meaning and type of the central variables or maps;\\n4. derivation of the main relations;\\n5. proof or mechanism architecture;\\n6. obstruction, non-closure, or source of difficulty;\\n7. proposed abstraction, approximation, or limiting construction;\\n8. assumptions connecting the construction to the original problem;\\n9. theoretical and empirical evidence;\\n10. remaining gaps and falsifying cases.\\n\\nAdjust the sequence to the sources. Do not jump ahead merely because later material is related. When the user says “next block,” continue with only the next logically necessary idea. When the user is confused, stop advancing and rebuild from the last understood point.\\n\\nWrite each ordinary lesson turn as a compact, seamless blackboard explanation: a few short paragraphs, only the equations needed now, few or no bullets, and normally no headings. Do not begin or end with a broad recap. Stop naturally after the current block and wait for the user before moving deeper.\\n\\n## Construct each block\\n\\nUse this internal order without displaying it as a template:\\n\\n1. **Minimal recall:** restate only the definitions needed now.\\n2. **New object:** define one new object and give its mathematical type.\\n3. **Derivation:** obtain its relation from the preceding setup.\\n4. **Meaning:** explain the role of every term or condition.\\n5. **Analogue or example:** use one structurally faithful simple case when it genuinely helps, then return to the exact mathematics.\\n6. **Role:** connect the object to the document's argument.\\n7. **Status:** state what is established and what remains unproved.\\n\\nMake the block self-contained for its present conceptual burden, not for the entire project.\\n\\n## Enforce notation and type discipline\\n\\nUse a small, stable notation. Prefer the source's symbols unless they collide or obscure meaning. If renaming is necessary, state the mapping explicitly.\\n\\nDefine every symbol before using it. For each object, state as relevant:\\n\\n- its domain, codomain, or ambient space;\\n- whether it is a scalar, vector, matrix, function, operator, random variable, measure, index, or coordinate;\\n- whether it is fixed, sampled, or evolving;\\n- what each index means;\\n- its dependence on other variables or parameters.\\n\\nDistinguish carefully between:\\n\\n- one function of an entire vector and a vector of coordinatewise functions;\\n- an index and a coordinate;\\n- a fixed label or parameter and an evolving state;\\n- a representative element and a probability law over elements;\\n- a mathematical state and a numerical representation of that state.\\n\\nWhen writing an integral, identify the integration variable, its space, the measure, and what the integral averages or accumulates. When a distribution is specified only at initialization, do not imply that an evolved quantity retains that distribution merely because it remains a function of the initial label.\\n\\nDo not introduce shorthand unless the explicit expression is genuinely cumbersome and recurs often. Never reuse one symbol for two roles.\\n\\n## Derive rather than paraphrase\\n\\nFor every central equation or implication:\\n\\n1. state the definition or prior relation from which it follows;\\n2. show the relevant algebraic, differential, variational, probabilistic, logical, or limiting step;\\n3. identify the origin and action of every term;\\n4. explain the closest elementary analogue when useful;\\n5. state why the relation matters to the main result.\\n\\nDo not make the user reverse-engineer a displayed formula. Avoid phrases such as “clearly,” “standard,” or “it follows” when they conceal a nontrivial step.\\n\\nWhen invoking an external theorem, state the exact form needed, list its hypotheses, verify them in the present setting, and explain the resulting conclusion. Check a primary or otherwise authoritative source before relying on a recent, specialized, or uncertain result.\\n\\nWhen teaching a long proof, devote one block to its architecture before teaching the difficult steps. Explain how the claims, cases, or lemmas fit together without pretending that the architecture is itself the proof.\\n\\nUse analogies only when they preserve the relevant mathematical structure. State where the analogy stops and return immediately to the exact object.\\n\\n## Relate levels of description\\n\\nBegin with the document's concrete objects and exact relations. Introduce an abstract, aggregate, continuum, asymptotic, or numerical description only after showing how it arises from the concrete one.\\n\\nWhenever the source changes levels, identify:\\n\\n- the map from the original objects to the new description;\\n- what information is retained and discarded;\\n- which step is an exact identity;\\n- which step uses a theorem and its assumptions;\\n- which step is formal, heuristic, empirical, or conjectural.\\n\\nIf a proposed state or summary evolves autonomously, distinguish internal closure from fidelity to the original system. An autonomous reduced description may still omit information that changes the original system's future.\\n\\nIf the source uses truncation or approximation, distinguish pointwise or static approximation from the stronger control required by an evolving or iterative system. Examine separately:\\n\\n- error that leaves the retained representation;\\n- feedback from omitted components;\\n- amplification or accumulation of error;\\n- the time, parameter, or regime over which stability is claimed.\\n\\nWhen the source calls a construction “finite,” state precisely whether this means finitely many coordinates, finitely many fields, a finite-dimensional state, a finite cutoff, or merely a finite numerical discretization.\\n\\n## Label epistemic status\\n\\nClassify each important source-dependent claim using the most accurate category:\\n\\n- exact identity;\\n- proved theorem under stated assumptions;\\n- rigorous result for a restricted or finite setting;\\n- formal argument;\\n- heuristic mechanism;\\n- empirical or numerical evidence;\\n- conjectural identification;\\n- open implication;\\n- counterexample or negative result.\\n\\nDo not silently promote a claim. Express each important assumption as a missing implication:\\n\\n1. what is assumed;\\n2. what conclusion it would justify;\\n3. what connecting step is not established;\\n4. what observation or counterexample would refute it.\\n\\nInternal consistency of a proposed theory does not prove that it represents the target object.\\n\\n## Evaluate evidence and alternatives\\n\\nFor every numerical comparison, identify:\\n\\n- the systems or quantities compared;\\n- what the reference actually represents;\\n- the metric or norm;\\n- the scale of the predicted effect and the discrepancy;\\n- discretization, optimization, rounding, and sampling uncertainty as applicable;\\n- the alternative explanation ruled out;\\n- what the experiment leaves unresolved.\\n\\nDo not infer convergence or a scaling law from too few resolutions. Treat numerical thresholds as decision conventions unless the source proves a mathematical boundary.\\n\\nWhen the user requests a hostile or devil's-advocate analysis, include only alternatives that engage the declared target and make a distinct mathematical or quantitative prediction. Do not infer global behavior from a local or instantaneous property without proving the connecting step. Do not use possible error cancellation as a complete explanation unless it predicts the observed pattern.\\n\\n## Repair confusion completely\\n\\nWhen the user identifies an undefined symbol, ambiguity, missing derivation, or conceptual gap:\\n\\n- acknowledge the failure directly;\\n- do not defend the prior wording;\\n- do not append a one-sentence patch;\\n- rewrite the entire relevant block self-containedly;\\n- preserve compactness and stable notation.\\n\\nTreat confusion as evidence that the explanation needs rebuilding, not that the mathematics is inherently inaccessible.\\n\\nBefore sending each block, verify silently that it is faithful to the sources, introduces only one conceptual burden, defines every needed symbol, derives rather than paraphrases, states the status of important claims, stays in the user's language, avoids unnecessary notation and formula dumps, and is compact enough to absorb in one sitting.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7d2555c87d2ae91480d40dd4c1d\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7e3055887d2abf9520fc723ccc1\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying learning-rate scaling**\",\"**Refining loss-positivity proof**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7ee592887d29c1419dfbee7446c\",\"summary\":[\"**Answering after checks**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf7fd084887d29044d4ef1a3312e2\",\"text\":\"Yes—in the sense that the motion survives the width and learning-rate limits. For every fixed allowed three-input configuration, there is a fixed \\\\(T_*>0\\\\) on which both hidden layers learn, the middle matrix changes its action, the loss decreases, and neither activation becomes effectively linear.\\n\\nThere is one important qualification: **the hidden velocities are not constant in time.** They start at zero because the limiting readout starts at zero, and initially grow proportionally to \\\\(t\\\\). At each fixed sufficiently small \\\\(t>0\\\\), their size is strictly positive and independent of width and learning rate.\\n\\nLet me make both the scaling and these conclusions precise.\\n\\n## The scaling family and its physical clock\\n\\nKeep three fixed inputs \\\\(x_a\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a\\\\|_2^2}{d}=1,\\\\qquad\\nG_{ab}=\\\\frac{x_a^\\\\top x_b}{d},\\n\\\\]\\nno two parallel or antiparallel, and labels \\\\(y_a\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\).\\n\\nThe stored parameters have dimensions\\n\\\\[\\nW^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times d},\\\\qquad\\nW^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n},\\\\qquad\\nW^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTheir normalization is part of the model:\\n\\\\[\\nz_{a,k}^{(1)}=\\\\frac{W_k^{(1)}x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\n\\\\qquad\\nz_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(2)}h_{a,k}^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_{a,k}^{(\\\\ell)}=\\\\arctan\\\\!\\\\left(z_{a,k}^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a,k}=\\\\frac{(W_k^{(3)})^\\\\top h_{a,k}^{(2)}}n,\\n\\\\qquad\\nr_{n,a,k}=f_{n,a,k}-y_a,\\n\\\\qquad\\nL_{n,k}=\\\\sum_{a=1}^3r_{n,a,k}^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere is a sufficient family extending the specific configuration we proved. Initialize all entries independently as centered Gaussians:\\n\\n| Stored parameter | Initialization variance per entry | Learning rate multiplying its loss gradient |\\n|---|---:|---:|\\n| \\\\(W^{(1)}\\\\) | \\\\(\\\\sigma_1^2\\\\) | \\\\(\\\\eta_n n\\\\kappa_1\\\\) |\\n| \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) | \\\\(\\\\sigma_2^2/n\\\\) | \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\kappa_2\\\\) |\\n| \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) | \\\\(\\\\sigma_3^2n^{-2\\\\beta}\\\\) | \\\\(\\\\eta_n n\\\\kappa_3\\\\) |\\n\\nThe assumptions are\\n\\\\[\\n0<\\\\sigma_1,\\\\sigma_2,\\\\kappa_1,\\\\kappa_2,\\\\kappa_3<\\\\infty,\\n\\\\qquad\\n0\\\\le \\\\sigma_3<\\\\infty,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\beta>0,\\n\\\\]\\nwith these constants fixed as width grows. Exact zero readout initialization is also allowed.\\n\\nThe earlier configuration was the special choice with all these scale constants equal to \\\\(1\\\\), \\\\(\\\\beta=1\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\).\\n\\nWhy these learning-rate powers? Recall the residual-free backpropagation fields:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\n=W_k^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_{a,k}^{(2)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n=\\\\phi'(z_{a,k}^{(1)})\\n\\\\odot(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThey equal \\\\(n\\\\,\\\\partial f_{n,a,k}/\\\\partial z_{a,k}^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\).\\n\\nApplying the learning rates in the table gives the exact updates\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=z_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\kappa_1\\n\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=W_k^{(2)}\\n-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n\\\\kappa_2}{n}\\n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}\\n(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=W_k^{(3)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\kappa_3\\n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe explicit \\\\(n\\\\)'s in the input and readout learning rates cancel the \\\\(1/n\\\\)'s in their gradients. The resulting field updates have the common step size \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\).\\n\\nFor the middle matrix, its \\\\(1/n\\\\) update factor also does **not** make its action vanish. Holding its current input fixed,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n&(W_{k+1}^{(2)}-W_k^{(2)})h_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&\\\\qquad=\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\kappa_2\\n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top h_{a,k}^{(1)}}n.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\nThe last factor is an empirical average with a finite, generally nonzero limit. Thus entrywise-small matrix updates produce width-independent changes in the matrix’s action.\\n\\nThe physical clock is\\n\\\\[\\nt=k\\\\eta_n.\\n\\\\]\\nFor the **local three-input theorem**, the joint limit requires\\n\\\\[\\nn\\\\longrightarrow\\\\infty,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\nNo additional relation between them is needed in that local argument. For example, \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-p}\\\\) works for every fixed \\\\(p>0\\\\).\\n\\nIn particular, the displayed input-layer learning rate \\\\(n\\\\eta_n\\\\) need not itself tend to zero: its gradient contains the compensating \\\\(1/n\\\\). What matters for the continuous-time approximation is the actual field increment.\\n\\nThe earlier condition \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) remains sufficient, but the newer local multi-input argument does not need it. This is not a claim that we have strengthened the earlier theorem on every finite time horizon.\\n\\nWithin this family, the local convergence proof still closes. The positive scale constants modify its bounds but introduce no growing powers of width. Also,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\\\longrightarrow0,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\max_j|W_{0,j}^{(3)}|\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}\\n\\\\]\\nfor every \\\\(\\\\beta>0\\\\). Consequently, all these readout initializations give the same population initialization \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\).\\n\\n## What is nontrivial in the resulting limit?\\n\\nWrite \\\\(Z_a^{(\\\\ell)}(t)\\\\) for a population preactivation and\\n\\\\[\\nH_a^{(\\\\ell)}(t)=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(\\\\ell)}(t)).\\n\\\\]\\nExpectations below average over neurons in the corresponding population.\\n\\nThe short-time expansion gives positive constants \\\\(c,C,T_*\\\\) such that\\n\\\\[\\nct\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\sqrt{\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left|\\\\frac{dZ_a^{(\\\\ell)}(t)}{dt}\\\\right|^2\\n\\\\right]}\\n\\\\le Ct,\\n\\\\qquad\\n0<t\\\\le T_*,\\n\\\\]\\nfor every input \\\\(a\\\\) and both hidden layers \\\\(\\\\ell=1,2\\\\).\\n\\nThis quantity is the RMS velocity across neurons. The same type of bounds holds for the activation velocity \\\\(dH_a^{(\\\\ell)}/dt\\\\), since\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{dH_a^{(\\\\ell)}}{dt}\\n=\\n\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(\\\\ell)})\\\\frac{dZ_a^{(\\\\ell)}}{dt},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\phi'(z)=\\\\frac1{1+z^2}>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSo the precise picture is:\\n\\n- At \\\\(t=0\\\\), hidden velocity is zero.\\n- At each fixed small \\\\(t>0\\\\), hidden velocity is positive and does not disappear with width.\\n- On any fixed interval \\\\([\\\\tau,T_*]\\\\), with \\\\(\\\\tau>0\\\\), it is bounded below by a positive constant.\\n- The initial hidden displacement is proportional to \\\\(t^2\\\\), not to a vanishing power of width.\\n\\nThe matrix itself is genuinely learning—not merely receiving a moving input. Holding the **initial** first-layer activation fixed, the expansion gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sqrt{\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left|\\n[W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}]H_{0,a}^{(1)}\\n\\\\right|^2\\n\\\\right]}\\n=\\nc_a t^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\qquad c_a>0.\\n\\\\]\\nThus its action changes even in this fixed-input test. The prediction kernel also changes; the limit is neither a frozen-feature model nor a constant-kernel model.\\n\\nThe delay in hidden motion has a simple cause. Initially,\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(3)}=0,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}(0)=\\\\delta_a^{(1)}(0)=0.\\n\\\\]\\nBut the readout immediately starts moving:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}(0)\\n=\\n2\\\\kappa_3\\\\sum_a y_aH_{0,a}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nIt becomes proportional to \\\\(t\\\\), so the backpropagated fields become proportional to \\\\(t\\\\), producing hidden displacement proportional to \\\\(t^2\\\\).\\n\\nThe loss does not have this initial delay. Since the population predictions start at zero,\\n\\\\[\\nL(0)=3,\\n\\\\]\\nand\\n\\\\[\\n-\\\\dot L(0)\\n=\\n4\\\\kappa_3\\\\,\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(\\\\sum_a y_aH_{0,a}^{(2)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n>0.\\n\\\\]\\nStrict positivity uses the initial-feature independence argument proved for nonparallel inputs: the displayed label-weighted combination cannot be the zero random variable. By continuity, after possibly reducing \\\\(T_*\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n-\\\\dot L(t)\\\\ge c_L>0,\\n\\\\qquad 0\\\\le t\\\\le T_*.\\n\\\\]\\nThis concerns the summed loss; individual examples’ squared residuals need not all decrease.\\n\\nFinally, neither hidden activation is being linearized by shrinking preactivations. Already at initialization, with \\\\(g\\\\sim N(0,1)\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(Z_{0,a}^{(1)})=\\\\sigma_1^2>0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(Z_{0,a}^{(2)})\\n=\\n\\\\sigma_2^2\\\\,\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\arctan(\\\\sigma_1g)^2]\\n>0.\\n\\\\]\\nBoth quantities are independent of width.\\n\\nThere is a stronger statement than nonvanishing variance. For both layers and every input, on a fixed short interval,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\inf_{c_0,c_1\\\\in\\\\mathbb R}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\arctan(Z_a^{(\\\\ell)}(t))\\n-c_0-c_1Z_a^{(\\\\ell)}(t)\\n\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\ge c_{\\\\mathrm{nonlinear}}>0.\\n\\\\]\\nEven the **best affine approximation** retains positive mean-squared error.\\n\\nAt initialization, this is positive because each hidden marginal is a nondegenerate Gaussian and arctan is not affine. Mean-square continuity preserves positivity for short time. Thus the claim rules out the activation becoming asymptotically linear under the actual neuron distribution, not merely at a selected preactivation value.\\n\\nAll these positive constants are independent of width and learning rate. They may depend on the fixed input geometry and hyperparameters. In particular, choosing \\\\(\\\\sigma_1,\\\\sigma_2\\\\) extremely small can make the nonlinear effect numerically small; we are not claiming uniform lower bounds as these constants approach zero.\\n\\n## How close is this to μP?\\n\\nQuite close: **the SGD width scaling is μP’s scaling**, in the convention where the readout has an explicit \\\\(1/n\\\\) forward multiplier.\\n\\nWith fixed input dimension, μP uses width-independent input initialization, middle-weight variance proportional to \\\\(1/n\\\\), and the stored-parameter SGD learning-rate powers\\n\\\\[\\n(n,\\\\;1,\\\\;n).\\n\\\\]\\nThese match the table above. Different fixed input-normalization conventions only change constants, not width powers. [Tensor Programs V, Table 8](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.03466#page=23).\\n\\nThe initialization distinction matters. Positive-variance μP commonly gives the stored \\\\(W_0^{(3)}\\\\) order-one coordinates, while our family makes those coordinates vanish. But zero initialization is explicitly permitted by μP’s scaling rules, and zero readout initialization is discussed separately in the paper. [Tensor Programs V, Appendix B and Appendix D.2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.03466#page=27).\\n\\nSo an accurate description is:\\n\\n> μP SGD scaling with a vanishing-readout initialization, together with a proved local joint width–gradient-flow limit.\\n\\nA standard nonvanishing random readout may also produce initial predictions tending to zero, but its population readout field is not zero. It is therefore a different initial state, generally with different initial hidden velocities. The zero-readout proof cannot simply be declared a proof for that entire family.\\n\\nOur affirmative result is consequently **a class of configurations, not one isolated numerical choice**. The positive constants \\\\(\\\\sigma_1,\\\\sigma_2,\\\\kappa_1,\\\\kappa_2,\\\\kappa_3\\\\) can vary. Their values affect the population dynamics; multiplying all three \\\\(\\\\kappa_\\\\ell\\\\) by the same constant merely rescales time. The readout seed parameters \\\\(\\\\sigma_3,\\\\beta>0\\\\) disappear from the limiting initialization.\\n\\nThis is a sufficient class, not a classification of every possible width scaling. And the existence and nontriviality guarantees here remain local in time.\\n\\nThe detailed checks are in the [scaling extension](/tmp/THREE_INPUT_SCALED_LIMIT_EXTENSION_CHECK.md) and [nontriviality calculation](/tmp/three_input_activity_lemma.md).\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a0712b-9b1f-7e01-a3b6-944f30e62dfd\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1788605078,\"completedAt\":1788605675,\"durationMs\":597167,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a0712b-9b65-7f61-98ff-e4d93c6c8120\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"so you prove existence of an absolytely positive time, not dependent on learning rate and widht, as it holds for the limit, and it is also non-vanishing (it's a constant as we take limits)?&#x20;\\n\\ncan you generalize this same result to 3 input configuraiton , also none of them parallel , but otherwise arbitrary, and arbitrary +1 or -1 labels? \\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf2986a3c87d29802916fed196761\",\"text\":\"Yes: \\\\(T_0\\\\) is a fixed positive constant, independent of width and learning rate, so it does not shrink as either limit is taken. I’ll check the three-input extension next. One new distinction matters: three pairwise nonparallel inputs can still be linearly dependent, so their Gram matrix may be singular.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-6900faf1-e44f-41c0-abb1-ae0f35361a83\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /etc/codex/skills/solve-math-rigorously/SKILL.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: solve-math-rigorously\\ndescription: Produce compact but complete solutions to standalone mathematical questions and proof requests. Use when the user asks to solve, prove, derive, analyze, verify, or correct a mathematical problem that is not primarily an incremental lesson about supplied technical documents. Infer the strongest reasonable interpretation without inventing assumptions, establish a minimal setup, use minimal notation, expose every nontrivial step, state and verify external theorem hypotheses, give proof architecture when useful, and silently audit the draft before answering.\\n---\\n\\n# Solve Mathematics Rigorously\\n\\nProduce a complete mathematical answer rather than a staged document lesson, unless the user explicitly requests incremental pacing. Optimize simultaneously for correctness, rigor, clarity, and economy.\\n\\n## Determine the intended problem\\n\\nInterpret the strongest reasonable version supported by the wording and mathematical context. Do not exploit an omission to answer a trivial or uninteresting straw-man version.\\n\\nHandle ambiguity according to its effect:\\n\\n- If it does not change the substance, adopt the conventional interpretation and state it briefly.\\n- If one reasonable assumption makes the intended problem precise, state that assumption before solving.\\n- If several reasonable interpretations lead to materially different answers, ask for clarification or give a compact case split when that fully resolves the ambiguity.\\n- If a missing assumption is necessary for the claimed conclusion, do not invent it silently.\\n\\nIf the stated claim is false, say so and give a decisive counterexample or contradiction. Present a corrected nearby statement only when it helps, and label it as a correction rather than the original result.\\n\\nAnswer the problem actually asked. Do not weaken a universal claim to a convenient special case, replace an exact question with a numerical approximation, or prove a converse instead of the stated direction.\\n\\n## Establish a minimal setup\\n\\nBegin with a compact setup or preliminaries passage when it reduces ambiguity or supports later reasoning. State only the necessary:\\n\\n- domain and assumptions;\\n- definitions used in the solution;\\n- boundary, regularity, integrability, independence, or nondegeneracy conditions that matter;\\n- notation that must persist through the answer.\\n\\nReuse the problem's notation whenever possible. Introduce a new symbol only when the unabbreviated expression is genuinely cumbersome and recurs often. Define every new symbol before use and state its mathematical type when that is not immediately evident.\\n\\nDo not force a visible setup heading for a short elementary problem. Do not hide substantive assumptions in prose later in the solution.\\n\\n## Give the architecture before a substantial proof\\n\\nBefore a long or conceptually difficult proof, explain the plan in a short paragraph or compact list. Identify the main steps, cases, claims, or lemmas and how they combine to yield the conclusion.\\n\\nKeep the architecture at the level needed to orient the reader. Do not duplicate the detailed proof, and do not substitute a plan for the proof itself. For a routine calculation or one-step argument, omit the architecture.\\n\\n## Execute every nontrivial step\\n\\nDerive each nontrivial algebraic, analytic, probabilistic, geometric, combinatorial, or logical transition. Give enough detail for the user to verify the step without reconstructing missing reasoning.\\n\\nIn particular:\\n\\n- identify the definition, identity, inequality, or prior claim being used;\\n- show substitutions, rearrangements, case transitions, and changes of variables that carry real mathematical content;\\n- track domains, signs, quantifiers, equality conditions, exceptional cases, and possible zero denominators;\\n- justify limit exchanges, differentiation or integration under a sign, convergence claims, and optimization steps;\\n- distinguish exact arguments from heuristics, approximations, and numerical evidence;\\n- show that intermediate claims actually imply the requested conclusion.\\n\\nAvoid words such as “clearly,” “obviously,” “standard,” and “it follows” when they replace reasoning. They are acceptable only after the supporting reason has been supplied.\\n\\nDo not over-explain elementary arithmetic or definitions already established. Compress routine steps while preserving the chain of verification.\\n\\n## Invoke external results completely\\n\\nWhenever the solution relies on a nontrivial theorem or external fact:\\n\\n1. state the exact form needed, including its assumptions and conclusion;\\n2. verify each assumption in the present problem;\\n3. identify the current objects substituted into the theorem;\\n4. state exactly what conclusion the theorem yields here.\\n\\nDo not merely name a theorem or call a fact standard. Prefer the relevant specialization over an unnecessarily general statement.\\n\\nVerify a recent, specialized, source-specific, or uncertain result against a primary or authoritative source before relying on it. Cite the source near the claim. Paraphrase the relevant result unless its exact wording matters; quote only a short compliant excerpt when useful.\\n\\nIf the required theorem is unavailable, uncertain, or does not apply, expose the gap instead of proceeding as though it were valid.\\n\\n## Use examples and checks proportionately\\n\\nInclude an example, boundary case, counterexample, or remark only when it clarifies an abstraction, tests a hypothesis, explains a consequence, or exposes why an assumption is needed.\\n\\nUse low-cost sanity checks when they add confidence, such as:\\n\\n- substituting the result back into the defining relation;\\n- checking dimensions, signs, or units;\\n- testing a boundary or small case;\\n- comparing with a known special case;\\n- deriving the same conclusion by a second short route.\\n\\nDo not add examples mechanically or let them replace the general argument.\\n\\n## Keep the presentation compact\\n\\nUse headings only when they make a substantial solution easier to navigate. Keep notation sparse and stable. Prefer explicit expressions to disposable shorthand.\\n\\nUse equations to carry mathematical content, not decoration. Avoid formula dumps, decorative summaries, and boxed equations. State the conclusion plainly.\\n\\nLet the problem's complexity determine the answer length. Preserve every necessary assumption, theorem check, and logical transition, but remove repetition and exposition that does not improve understanding or verification.\\n\\n## Audit and revise silently\\n\\nDraft the solution, then review it before sending. Check:\\n\\n1. Did the solution address the strongest reasonable interpretation rather than a straw man?\\n2. Were all adopted assumptions stated, and were none invented merely to force the result?\\n3. Was the setup sufficient but minimal?\\n4. Was every persistent symbol necessary and defined?\\n5. Did a substantial proof receive a useful architecture?\\n6. Can the reader verify every nontrivial transition?\\n7. Was every external theorem stated in the needed form, with its hypotheses checked and its conclusion applied explicitly?\\n8. Were recent, specialized, or uncertain results verified from an authoritative source?\\n9. Were edge cases, equality conditions, domains, and quantifiers handled?\\n10. Does the final conclusion answer the exact question?\\n11. Do examples or remarks earn their space?\\n12. Is the answer as compact as rigor allows, with no boxed equations?\\n\\nRevise any failing part and repeat the audit until the answer satisfies the checklist as well as possible. Do not mention the drafting or audit process in the final answer.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf29df9e087d2945cd2230b7df897\",\"summary\":[\"**Proving ridge-function independence**\",\"**Proving covariance positivity**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf2b0759087d29cd3969bb0d796d0\",\"text\":\"I’m using the rigorous-math skill to check that the proof covers singular Gram matrices, rather than adding an assumption that the three inputs are linearly independent. I’ll check the feature-learning conclusions separately too: with three inputs, the input-swap symmetry used in the two-input proof is no longer available.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_JedB78hCqoIVwrxS9hlrlCJi\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-099c-7b50-a5c8-f3a360d99bfe\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_yTe0OPJkEhKL6JoO4DI7JCDm\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-3326-7303-96a6-3fc7a87cd0a4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_limit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf2d1531487d28b663e2db50273ac\",\"summary\":[\"**Reusing existing geometry context**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf2daadc087d288336c1152fa7cf0\",\"summary\":[\"**Proving fresh matrix formula**\",\"**Proving conditional variance positivity**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf2ec1ef087d2b4c1b1aec8d98599\",\"summary\":[\"**Proving uniform T0 geometry**\",\"**Separating uniform T0 claims**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf2feb5c887d28cd69ed84b2a85c9\",\"summary\":[\"**Reading proof and appendix**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-6ec1d807-e98e-46fe-81bb-8132ecefde03\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,620p' /tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Two inputs: a rigorous local-time joint width and gradient-flow limit\\n\\n## Result and scope\\n\\nFix two inputs \\\\(x_1,x_2\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a\\\\|_2^2}{d}=1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\rho=\\\\frac{x_1^\\\\top x_2}{d}\\\\in(-1,1),\\\\qquad\\nG=\\\\begin{pmatrix}1&\\\\rho\\\\\\\\\\\\rho&1\\\\end{pmatrix}.\\n\\\\]\\nLet \\\\(y_1,y_2\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence of positive steps \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all the stated angles and both label patterns.\\n\\nPredictions, residuals, loss, and all entries of all three kernel blocks converge in probability uniformly on this interval. The joint two-input hidden path laws converge with their second moments in the uniform path norm. Integrated squared hidden velocities converge. The joint empirical averages of any fixed finite collection of continuous, globally Lipschitz forward/adjoint probes, with bounded products when a product instruction is used, converge to their corresponding population expectations, uniformly in time. Unbounded first-layer backward fields and their quadratic contractions are included by the tail argument below.\\n\\nOn a possibly smaller interval \\\\((0,T_*]\\\\), each input's preactivations move in both hidden layers, all three \\\\(2\\\\times2\\\\) kernel blocks are positive definite, the total kernel is nonconstant, loss strictly decreases, and \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) has positive best-affine-fit error on each hidden preactivation distribution.\\n\\n**Scope:** for nonorthogonal inputs this proof establishes a fixed positive time interval, not convergence on every compact time interval. The latter remains unproved here. When \\\\(\\\\rho=0\\\\), the earlier cubic-coordinate argument does extend to every finite time interval under its original sufficient step condition \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\). No optimizer preconditioning is being introduced.\\n\\n## The finite network and its exact updates\\n\\nThe dimensions are \\\\(W^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times d}\\\\), \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\). For input \\\\(a=1,2\\\\), use\\n\\\\[\\nz_a^{(1)}=\\\\frac{W^{(1)}x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nz_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi=\\\\arctan,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h_a^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad\\nr_{n,a}=f_{n,a}-y_a,\\\\qquad L_n=r_{n,1}^2+r_{n,2}^2.\\n\\\\]\\nThe summed loss retains the earlier factors of two; using the average loss halves the speed.\\n\\nAll initialization entries are independent:\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ij}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently each first-layer root pair is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), independently over neuron index \\\\(i\\\\). The two preactivations for one neuron are not independent unless \\\\(\\\\rho=0\\\\).\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta_a^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_{n,a}/\\\\partial z_a^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\).\\nIn the displayed rescaled parameterization the layer learning-rate multipliers are \\\\(n,1,n\\\\). The exact first-weight update is\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n=W_k^{(1)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{\\\\sqrt d}\\n\\\\sum_{b=1}^2r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)}x_b^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\nMultiplication by \\\\(x_a/\\\\sqrt d\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=z_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{n}\\n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)} .\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M1}\\n\\\\]\\nUse physical time \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), linearly interpolate these parameters, and recompute the forward pass at intermediate times.\\n\\nThe whole effect of the input geometry on first-layer training is the matrix \\\\(G\\\\). For example, the update of \\\\(z_1^{(1)}\\\\) contains its own gradient and \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\) times input 2's gradient.\\n\\nThe finite continuous flow has the kernel blocks\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}_{n,ab}\\n=G_{ab}\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(1)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(1)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(2)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(1)})^\\\\top h_b^{(1)}}n\\n \\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(2)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(3)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(2)})^\\\\top h_b^{(2)}}n .\\n\\\\tag{M2}\\n\\\\]\\nDifferentiation gives \\\\(\\\\dot f_n=-2K_n r_n\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\dot L_n=-4r_n^\\\\top K_n r_n\\\\), where \\\\(K_n\\\\) is the sum of the blocks. Each block is positive semidefinite: it is the Gram matrix of the corresponding scaled parameter gradients. Individual residual magnitudes need not decrease; their squared sum does.\\n\\n## Why a new stability estimate is needed\\n\\nWith \\\\(F(s)=s+s^3/3\\\\), the componentwise transformation \\\\(X_a^{(1)}=F(Z_a^{(1)})\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot X_a^{(1)}\\n=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\n\\\\frac{1+(Z_a^{(1)})^2}{1+(Z_b^{(1)})^2}\\n(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{M3}\\n\\\\]\\nFor \\\\(\\\\rho=0\\\\) the ratios cancel. For \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\ne0\\\\) they do not, and are unbounded. We therefore work with the original preactivations and prove a uniform tail estimate for the backward fields.\\n\\nThe exact limiting equations will be\\n\\\\[\\nH_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\nf_a=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H_a^{(2)}],\\\\quad r_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot Z_a^{(1)}&=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\\\delta_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_b\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_bH_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M4}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\((u\\\\otimes v)V=u\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[vV]\\\\). This definition displays the normalization that replaces the finite factor \\\\(1/n\\\\). First-layer and second-layer coordinates belong to separate populations. \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) acts from the former to the latter, and its adjoint acts in reverse. Every expectation pairs coordinates of the same population.\\n\\nThe initial root pair is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), the population readout is zero, and the initial action \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) is the joint forward-and-transpose limit of the initial Gaussian matrix, constructed next. The state consists of two first-layer fields and the shared matrix action and readout field. It has finitely many kinds of state objects, not finitely many scalar degrees of freedom.\\n\\n## The fixed-mesh construction and the new tail lemma\\n\\nWe use the following finite Gaussian fact. For a fixed finite calculation from independent Gaussian roots and a Gaussian matrix of variance \\\\(1/n\\\\), with smooth polynomially bounded coordinate functions and finitely many calls to that matrix and its transpose, every same-layer joint empirical average of a continuous polynomially bounded measurement has a deterministic limit. The limiting action is Gaussian randomness plus the responses forced by earlier opposite-direction calls. The precise response coefficients and their derivative convention are given and used in the appendix below. This is the smooth specialization of Theorem 2.10 and Box 1 of [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685). Its hypotheses hold here: after fixing population scalar coefficients, each instruction and each derivative needed below is a polynomially bounded function of finitely many Gaussian variables. No claim of iid finite coordinates after matrix reuse is used.\\n\\nFor completeness, the underlying conditioning mechanism is as follows. If previous calls have revealed \\\\(W_0^{(2)}V=Y\\\\) and \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R\\\\), then the remaining Gaussian matrix is an independent Gaussian projected onto the directions orthogonal to the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) and \\\\(V\\\\). Its conditional mean obeys both recorded equations. A new product is the response fixed by these equations plus that projected Gaussian applied to the unexplored part of the new input. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the response coefficients. The finite Gaussian theorem makes this induction valid also with dependent query directions. We explicitly rely on this stated finite-program result, not on a claim that the infinite-time or growing-program-length theorem already follows from it.\\n\\nThese finite limits can be realized on two common population coordinate spaces. Here are the details relevant to the subsequent continuous-time argument. Take all the programs needed for rational meshes, their finite unions, and a countable closure under rational linear combinations, the coordinate operations in the proof, and bounded Lipschitz approximations. Their finite joint laws are consistent, so they can be realized jointly. Take the mean-square closure of their linear spans on each layer. Exact finite linear identities pass to these laws.\\n\\nA fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\) satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le2\\\\,9^{2n}e^{-nM^2/8}\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{M5}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed two \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets of the unit sphere each have at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points, and every fixed bilinear form of the matrix is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). This finite operator inequality and joint second-moment convergence imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2]\\n\\\\]\\nfor every generated \\\\(V\\\\). Thus the action is well-defined on equal-in-mean-square variables and extends to the closure. The same reasoning applies to the transpose. The exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u)^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\npasses to the limit and identifies the two actions as adjoints. Bounded coordinate multipliers and their required products belong to these spaces by clipping and mean-square approximation. This constructs the common bounded-operator realization used below; it is not an additional assumption.\\n\\nDefine population Euler for (M4) in this common realization, starting from the stated initialization. Its rank expansion is\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}\\n-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\\\sum_b\\nr_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{M6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus each fixed mesh is a finite Gaussian calculation with deterministic population residuals and contractions. Iterative construction is causal: the current fields determine their expectations and then the next fields. Its realization agrees with the finite Gaussian law, because applying the same rank expansion reduces each action to an initial-matrix call and those deterministic contractions.\\n\\nThe appendix proves the essential new estimate:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta:\\\\,\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\\\ \\n\\\\max_{a,k:\\\\,k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\nc\\\\left|(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\right|^2\\n\\\\right)\\\\le C .\\n\\\\tag{M7}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(T_0,c,C>0\\\\) do not depend on the mesh. In particular the squared tail beyond \\\\(R\\\\) is at most \\\\(C e^{-c'R^2}\\\\), after reducing \\\\(c'\\\\).\\n\\nThe appendix is a derivation, not an assumed light-tail condition. It controls the accumulated deterministic response coefficients on both layers. The second-layer derivatives stay bounded; the first-layer derivatives have a Gaussian exponential bound. A short-time induction closes these bounds uniformly in the number of Euler steps.\\n\\n## Population Euler converges to a unique flow\\n\\nFor comparing two population states use\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\sum_{a=1}^2\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|Z_a^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\tag{M8}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each mean-square norm by the Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\).\\n\\nAll Euler trajectories, and all the actual discrete trajectories with \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\le1\\\\), have bounds independent of width on a fixed bounded time interval, whenever the initial readout supremum and matrix operator norm are bounded. To see this, boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n|r_a|\\\\le 1+\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}_{k+1}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\nC\\\\eta_n(1+\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty).\\n\\\\]\\nThe geometric sum bounds the readout supremum. The rank-one update then bounds the matrix operator norm, and the first-layer update bounds the root-mean-square velocity and displacement. The same calculation applies to population Euler with \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\). All initial bounds hold with probability tending to one for the finite initialization.\\n\\nOn these bounded sets, the forward quantities, residuals, second-layer backward fields, and the maps\\n\\\\[\\n(Z_1^{(1)},Z_2^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\n\\\\longmapsto (W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\nare Lipschitz in (M8). This follows by adding and subtracting one factor at a time, using boundedness and Lipschitz continuity of \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\), the readout supremum bound, and the operator bound.\\n\\nOnly the first-layer multiplication needs a separate estimate. Write\\n\\\\[\\nP_a=(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\widetilde P_a=(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThen\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})P_a-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)})\\\\widetilde P_a\\n=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(P_a-\\\\widetilde P_a)\\n+[\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)})]\\\\widetilde P_a.\\n\\\\]\\nSplit the last term at \\\\(|\\\\widetilde P_a|=R\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) is bounded and Lipschitz,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|[\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z)]\\\\widetilde P\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le C R\\\\|Z-\\\\widetilde Z\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+C\\\\|\\\\widetilde P\\\\,1_{\\\\{|\\\\widetilde P|>R\\\\}}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{M9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\). Only the reference field needs a tail bound. For a reference Euler grid point, (M7) bounds the final term by \\\\(C e^{-cR^2}\\\\).\\n\\nAn Euler interpolant moves by at most \\\\(C\\\\Delta\\\\) in (M8) between grid points. Its backward field therefore differs from the grid field by at most \\\\(C\\\\Delta\\\\) in mean square, using the Lipschitz map above. Thus, at any intermediate time, the vector field differs from the interpolant's assigned grid velocity by at most\\n\\\\[\\nC(1+R)\\\\Delta+C e^{-cR^2}.\\n\\\\tag{M10}\\n\\\\]\\nThe same localization gives the comparison inequality for two meshes. Integration and the elementary Gronwall inequality yield, for \\\\(0<T\\\\le T_0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d(\\\\theta^\\\\Delta(t),\\\\theta^{\\\\Delta'}(t))\\n\\\\le\\nC e^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\left((1+R)(\\\\Delta+\\\\Delta')+e^{-cR^2}\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{M11}\\n\\\\]\\nFirst send both meshes to zero with \\\\(R\\\\) fixed. Then send \\\\(R\\\\) to infinity. The term \\\\(e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) vanishes, so the Euler paths are Cauchy uniformly in time in (M8).\\n\\nThe state spaces are complete, and the limit keeps the readout supremum and operator bounds. The coordinate product \\\\((Z,P)\\\\mapsto\\\\phi'(Z)P\\\\) is continuous from \\\\(L^2\\\\times L^2\\\\) to \\\\(L^2\\\\): split off the change of \\\\(P\\\\), then use boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) and dominated convergence against the fixed limiting \\\\(P^2\\\\). This allows passage to the integral form of (M4). The limit is therefore a solution, not just a limit of observables. It inherits (M7) by convergence in probability and Fatou's inequality; at intermediate grid times use the \\\\(O(\\\\Delta)\\\\) backward-field difference.\\n\\nThe same estimate compares this solution with any other solution of (M4) in the bounded state class with the same initial state. Only the constructed reference solution requires (M7). Their error is bounded by \\\\(C e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) for every \\\\(R\\\\), hence is zero. This proves uniqueness.\\n\\nThe equations use only the current fields and the current action and adjoint. In particular, their future evolution does not require the stored Euler history. More formally, the closed spaces generated by current fields, bounded coordinate operations, and current forward/adjoint actions contain the future: clipped vector fields and their Euler approximations remain there, and (M9) removes the clipping. Equal current joint action laws identify these generated spaces by a mean-square isometry, which intertwines their actions. Uniqueness therefore identifies their future action laws. This proves autonomy and restartability along the constructed interval.\\n\\n## From the population flow to the actual fine-step GD\\n\\nFix a coarse mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), independently of the actual step \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\). Build a finite oracle by expanding (M6), supplying it with the population residuals and every population contraction in these expansions. It starts from the same finite first-layer initialization and the same \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) as GD, but has \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\). Every oracle instruction now has deterministic scalar coefficients. The fixed finite Gaussian theorem applies jointly to all its nodes and proves convergence of all the needed empirical contractions and second moments.\\n\\nThere is a useful distinction between oracle nodes and an actual finite network state. Construct proxy parameter arrays using its first-layer nodes and readout, and the matrix\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k,\\\\mathrm{proxy}}^{(2)}\\n=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k,b}r_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(2)}\\n(h_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{M12}\\n\\\\]\\nApplying this proxy matrix to a current oracle activation differs from its prescribed oracle preactivation only by sums of empirical-minus-population contractions. For example each forward discrepancy has the form\\n\\\\[\\n-2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(2)}\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\frac{(h_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)})^\\\\top\\nh_{a,k,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)}}{n}\\n-\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{M13}\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose discrepancy has the corresponding contraction of two \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) fields. At a fixed mesh there are finitely many such terms. Their scalar errors tend to zero, and their vector norms stay bounded. The actual network quantities recomputed from the proxy arrays therefore differ from the oracle nodes by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) in root mean square. Bounded readout and the operator bound justify each subsequent forward/backward comparison. In particular its recomputed vector field differs from its prescribed oracle grid velocity by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\).\\n\\nFor each fixed threshold \\\\(R\\\\), the oracle's empirical squared tails converge to their population counterparts. One may use continuous upper cutoffs, equal to one beyond \\\\(R\\\\) and zero below \\\\(R-1\\\\), to avoid discontinuities. The fixed finite Gaussian theorem applies to these quadratic-growth measurements. Consequently the root-mean-square tail of the recomputed proxy backward field has limit superior at most \\\\(C e^{-cR^2}\\\\), after modifying constants. This is proved for the reference oracle, not assumed for actual GD.\\n\\nLinearly interpolate the proxy parameters. Their metric speed is bounded in probability, with limiting bound independent of the mesh, by (M4) and the node consistency just proved. Their between-grid vector-field defect is bounded by (M10), plus a fixed-mesh \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\). Compare the actual GD interpolant to this proxy. Actual GD's derivative is its vector field at the preceding fine grid point; its state changes by \\\\(O(\\\\eta_n)\\\\) inside a fine step. Apply (M9) with the proxy as reference. No tail bound for the actual GD is needed. Gronwall gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t),\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{proxy},\\\\Delta}(t))\\n\\\\le C e^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\bigg(\\n&\\\\frac{\\\\|W_{n,0}^{(3)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n+(1+R)(\\\\eta_n+\\\\Delta)\\\\\\\\\\n&+e^{-cR^2}+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\n\\\\bigg).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) here is at fixed \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\). Bounds on the actual readout supremum are obtained from its update, not from root-mean-square closeness.\\n\\nThe initialization discrepancy vanishes, and \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). First take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\); next take \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\to0\\\\); finally take \\\\(R\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). Together with (M11), the fixed-mesh oracle empirical convergence, and the triangle inequality for observables, this proves the claimed joint limit. Thus this local proof needs no width-dependent learning-rate restriction beyond \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\).\\n\\nFor the first kernel block, use (M9) once more to transfer the first-layer \\\\(\\\\delta^{(1)}\\\\) fields in mean square; the other blocks follow directly from the bounded/Lipschitz maps. Products and contractions then converge by Cauchy–Schwarz. For hidden velocities,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot z_a^{(2)}\\n=\\\\dot W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)}\\n+W^{(2)}\\\\bigl[\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot\\\\dot z_a^{(1)}\\\\bigr].\\n\\\\]\\nThe reference first-layer velocity is a finite linear combination of bounded multiples of the backward fields, hence has the same tail control. Applying the same comparison proves convergence of both hidden velocities in integrated mean square and hence of their integrated squared norms.\\n\\nTo obtain path-law convergence, fir\",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":35194}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf30617b887d29f0539efe2c7b45b\",\"summary\":[\"**Updating three-input proof file**\",\"**Rewriting joint-Gaussian root clause**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf317399c87d28ccab9823dec2d14\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing extension proof**\",\"**Clarifying uniform time quantifiers**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0712c-3373-7072-92a3-d29a27f661f1\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-3326-7303-96a6-3fc7a87cd0a4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_limit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf328d53c87d29661a49fddfcd9a8\",\"summary\":[\"**Locating TP theorem source**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-8fc4e7a1-1a8f-472d-9a14-c1c5bcc85d66\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n 'Setup|positive.semidefinite|positive semidefinite|singular|Jointly|jointly Gaussian|Master Theorem|ZHat|ZDot' /tmp/tp3src/NetsorT2.tex\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"9:    \\\\caption{Graphical summary of \\\\netsort{} and its Master Theorem.\\n119:    \\\\item [\\\\texttt{ZHat}\\\\label{Z:Zhat}] {$\\\\hat{Z}^{Wx}$} is a Gaussian variable with zero mean. Let $\\\\mathcal V_W$ denote the set of all vectors in the program of the form $W y$ for some $y$.\\n120:    Then $\\\\{\\\\hat Z^{W y}: W y \\\\in \\\\mathcal V_W\\\\}$ is defined to be jointly Gaussian with zero mean and covariance\\n125:    \\\\item [\\\\texttt{ZDot}\\\\label{Z:Zdot}]\\n183:The following \\\\emph{Master Theorem} rigorously relates the \\\\emph{symbolically constructed} random variables $Z^h$ to the vectors $h$ in the program and their \\\\emph{analytic limits}.\\n186:\\\\begin{thm}[\\\\netsort{} Master Theorem]\\n201:\\\\cref{thm:NetsorTMasterTheorem} says that each ``coordinate slice'' $(h^1_\\\\alpha, \\\\ldots, h^k_\\\\alpha)$ can be thought of as an iid copy of $(Z^{h^1},\\\\ldots, Z^{h^k})$. Indeed, as consequences of the Master Theorem, \\\\cref{thm:NetsorTConvergenceInDistribution,{thm:NetsorTMultiConvergenceInDistribution}} formalize this intuition into a convergence in distribution.\\n203:\\\\cref{thm:NetsorTMasterTheorem} strictly generalizes the Master Theorem in \\\\citet{yangTP2}.\\n204:The \\\\netsor{} Master Theorem in \\\\citet{yangTP1} allows nonlinearities growing faster than polynomially, but otherwise is also a special case of \\\\cref{thm:NetsorTMasterTheorem}.\\n237:In \\\\cref{appendix:VarDim}, we also describe modification to the Master Theorems if, instead of requiring all matrices in $\\\\mathcal W$ to be square, we allow rectangular matrices.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-8f5a9977-2cb5-4b2e-9438-1875e319d51f\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '18,64p' /tmp/tp3src/NetsorT2.tex\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"This entire section is summarized in \\\\cref{fig:summary}, which we encourage the reader to regularly consult over the course of this section.\\nOriginally, \\\\netsort{}%\\n\\\\footnote{pronounced \\\\emph{netsert} or \\\\emph{netser-T}. The \\\\emph{ts} is pronounced like in \\\\emph{tsar}. The \\\\emph{or} is pronounced as in tens\\\\emph{or}. Another way of thinking is \\\\netsor{} is just \\\\emph{tensor} with \\\\emph{ten} reversed.}\\nwas motivated by a desire to understand the behavior of large (wide) neural networks.\\nFor example, a simple $L$-hidden-layer perceptron can be described by alternating applications of nonlinearities and matrix multiplication: For weight matrices $W^1 \\\\in \\\\R^{n\\\\times d}$ and\\n$W^2, \\\\ldots, W^L \\\\in \\\\R^{n\\\\times n}$, and nonlinearity $\\\\phi:\\\\R \\\\to \\\\R$, such a neural network on input $\\\\xi\\\\in \\\\R^d$ is given by $h^{1}(\\\\xi)=W^{1}\\\\xi\\\\in\\\\R^{n}$, and%\\n\\\\footnote{\\n    Following neural network convention, $\\\\phi(x) = (\\\\phi(x_1), \\\\ldots, \\\\phi(x_n))$.\\n}\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n    x^{l}(\\\\xi)=\\\\phi(h^{l}(\\\\xi)) \\\\in \\\\R^n,\\n    \\\\quad\\n    h^{l+1}(\\\\xi)=W^{l+1}x^{l}(\\\\xi)\\\\in\\\\R^{n},\\n    \\\\quad\\\\text{for $l=1,\\\\ldots, L-1$,}\\n    \\\\label{eqn:MLP}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nand the network output is $f(\\\\xi) = v^\\\\trsp x^L(\\\\xi)$ for some weight vector $v \\\\in \\\\R^n$.%\\n\\\\footnote{\\n    For simplicity, we omit biases and set equal the widths of all layers, but these two simplifications can easily be removed; see \\\\cref{appendix:VarDim}.\\n}\\nThus, intuitively, a language of solely \\\\emph{nonlinearity application} and \\\\emph{matrix multiplication} seems to strike a good balance between 1) simplicity (and ease of analysis), and 2) generality.\\nIndeed, as shown in \\\\citet{yangTP1,yangTP2}, such a language can express \\\\emph{practically all} of modern deep learning, beyond the toy example here.\\n\\\\citet{yangTP2} formalized a version of this language, called \\\\netsort{}, which we recall here.   \\n\\\\begin{defn}\\\\label{defn:netsort}\\nA \\\\netsort{} program is a sequence of $\\\\R^{n}$ vectors (which we will refer to as \\\\emph{vectors in the program}) inductively generated via one of the following ways from an initial set $\\\\mathcal{V}$ of random $\\\\R^{n}$ vectors and a set $\\\\mathcal{W}$ of random $n\\\\times n$ matrices \\n\\\\begin{description}\\n\\\\item [\\\\texttt{Nonlin\\\\label{instr:nonlin}}] Given $\\\\phi:\\\\R^{k}\\\\to\\\\R$ and $x^{1},\\\\ldots,x^{k}\\\\in\\\\R^{n}$, we can generate%\\n\\\\footnote{Again, $\\\\phi$ is applied coordinatewise.\\nHere $\\\\phi$ and $k$ should be thought of as fixed while $n \\\\to \\\\infty$.}\\n$\\\\phi(x^{1},\\\\ldots,x^{k})\\\\in\\\\R^{n}$, where\\n\\\\[\\\\phi(x^{1},\\\\ldots,x^{k})_\\\\alpha \\\\defeq\\\\phi(x^{1}_\\\\alpha,\\\\ldots,x^{k}_\\\\alpha),\\\\quad\\\\text{for each $\\\\alpha \\\\in [n]$.}\\\\] \\n\\\\item [\\\\texttt{MatMul\\\\label{instr:matmul}}] Given $W\\\\in\\\\R^{n\\\\times n}$ and $x\\\\in\\\\R^{n}$, we can generate $Wx\\\\in\\\\R^{n}$ or $W^{\\\\trsp}x\\\\in\\\\R^{n}$.\\n\\\\end{description}\\n\\\\end{defn}\\nFor example, in \\\\cref{eqn:MLP}, $h^{l}(\\\\xi)=W^{l}x^{l-1}(\\\\xi)$ is an instance of \\\\refMatMul{}, while $x^{l}(\\\\xi)=\\\\phi(h^{l}(\\\\xi))$ is an instance of \\\\refNonlin{}.\\nWe are interested in understanding the behavior of \\\\netsort{} programs when $n$ is large, and when $\\\\mathcal{V}$ and $\\\\mathcal{W}$ are sampled as follows:\\n\\\\begin{setup}[\\\\netsort{}]\\\\label{setup:netsort}\\n1) For each initial $W\\\\in\\\\mathcal{W}$, we sample iid $W_{\\\\alpha\\\\beta}\\\\sim\\\\Gaus(0,\\\\sigma_{W}^{2}/n)$ for some variance $\\\\sigma_{W}^{2}$ associated to $W$, independent of other $W' \\\\in \\\\mathcal {W}$; 2) for some multivariate Gaussian $Z^{\\\\mathcal{V}}=\\\\left\\\\{ Z^{h}:h\\\\in\\\\mathcal{V}\\\\right\\\\} \\\\in\\\\R^{\\\\mathcal{V}}$, we sample the initial set of vectors $\\\\mathcal{V}$ like $\\\\left\\\\{ h_{\\\\alpha}:h\\\\in\\\\mathcal{V}\\\\right\\\\} \\\\sim Z^{\\\\mathcal{V}}$ iid for each $\\\\alpha\\\\in[n]$.\\n\\\\end{setup}\\n\\\\begin{exmp}\\\\label{exmp:MLPsetup}\\nFor example, we will often be interested in understanding the behavior of \\\\cref{eqn:MLP} when $W^1,\\\\ldots, W^L$ are sampled randomly.\\nIn this case, $\\\\mathcal W$ consists of $W^2, \\\\ldots, W^L \\\\in \\\\R^{n\\\\times n}$, each of which is sampled like $W^l_{\\\\alpha\\\\beta}\\\\sim\\\\Gaus(0, 1/n)$.\\nOn the other hand, $\\\\mathcal V$ consists of $h^1(\\\\xi)=W^1 \\\\xi$, which is distributed like $h^1(\\\\xi)_\\\\alpha \\\\disteq \\\\Gaus(0, \\\\|\\\\xi\\\\|^2/d)$ if $W^1 \\\\in \\\\R^{n\\\\times d}$ is sampled like $W^1_{\\\\alpha\\\\beta} \\\\sim \\\\Gaus(0,1/d)$.%\\n\\\\footnote{In neural network settings, we are interested in the limit where the intermediate dimensions $n\\\\to\\\\infty$ but the input dimension $d$ stays fixed.\\nThus, we treat the input-to-hidden matrix $W^1$ differently from other $W^l$.}\\nIf we compute the MLP in \\\\cref{eqn:MLP} on other inputs $\\\\xi_1, \\\\ldots, \\\\xi_k$ as well, then $\\\\mathcal V = \\\\{W^1 \\\\xi, W^1 \\\\xi_1, \\\\ldots, W^1 \\\\xi_k\\\\}$ with $Z^{\\\\mathcal V}$ being the multivariate Gaussian with covariance $\\\\Cov(Z^{W^1 \\\\xi_i}, Z^{W^1 \\\\xi_j}) = \\\\xi_i^\\\\trsp \\\\xi_j / m$.\\n\\\\end{exmp}\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-e02e3b5a-c54a-41f9-8d06-4a796f54a4fb\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '113,141p' /tmp/tp3src/NetsorT2.tex\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"    \\\\[\\n    Z^{\\\\phi(x^{1},\\\\ldots,x^{k})}\\\\defeq \\\\phi(Z^{x^{1}},\\\\ldots,Z^{x^{k}}).\\n    \\\\]\\n    \\\\item [\\\\texttt{ZMatMul}\\\\label{Z:MatMul}] $Z^{Wx}\\\\defeq \\\\hat{Z}^{Wx}+\\\\Zdot^{Wx}$\\n    for every $W_{\\\\alpha\\\\beta}\\\\sim\\\\Gaus(0,\\\\sigma_{W}^{2}/n)$ and vector $x$, where\\n    \\\\begin{description}\\n    \\\\item [\\\\texttt{ZHat}\\\\label{Z:Zhat}] {$\\\\hat{Z}^{Wx}$} is a Gaussian variable with zero mean. Let $\\\\mathcal V_W$ denote the set of all vectors in the program of the form $W y$ for some $y$.\\n    Then $\\\\{\\\\hat Z^{W y}: W y \\\\in \\\\mathcal V_W\\\\}$ is defined to be jointly Gaussian with zero mean and covariance\\n    \\\\[\\n    \\\\Cov\\\\left(\\\\hat{Z}^{Wx},\\\\hat{Z}^{W y}\\\\right)\\\\defeq \\\\sigma_{W}^{2}\\\\EV Z^{x}Z^{y},\\\\quad\\\\text{for any \\\\ensuremath{Wx,W y\\\\in\\\\mathcal{V}_W}.}\\n    \\\\]\\n    Furthermore, $\\\\{\\\\hat Z^{W y}: W y \\\\in \\\\mathcal V_W\\\\}$ is mutually independent from $\\\\{\\\\hat Z^{v }: v \\\\in \\\\mathcal V \\\\cup \\\\bigcup_{\\\\bar W \\\\ne W} \\\\mathcal V_{\\\\bar W}\\\\}$, where $\\\\bar{W}$ ranges over $\\\\mathcal{W}\\\\cup\\\\{A^{\\\\trsp}:A\\\\in\\\\mathcal{W}\\\\}$.\\n    \\\\item [\\\\texttt{ZDot}\\\\label{Z:Zdot}]\\n    By the definition in this box, $Z^x$ is always a deterministic function of a set of $\\\\Zhat^{\\\\bullet}$ random variables.\\n    Then the partial derivative $\\\\partial Z^x / \\\\partial \\\\Zhat^\\\\bullet$ can be defined symbolically and is another random variable.\\n    Then we set\\n    \\\\[\\\\Zdot^{Wx}\\\\defeq \\\\sigma_{W}^{2}\\\\sum Z^{y}\\\\EV\\\\frac{\\\\partial Z^{x}}{\\\\partial\\\\hat{Z}^{W^{\\\\trsp}y}},\\\\]\\n    summing over $\\\\hat Z^{W^\\\\trsp y}, W^\\\\trsp y \\\\in \\\\mathcal V_{W^\\\\trsp},$ that $Z^x$ is a function of (where $\\\\mathcal V_{W^\\\\trsp}$ is defined in \\\\refZhat{}).\\n    There is some nuiance in this definition, so see \\\\cref{rem:PartialDer} and \\\\ref{rem:ExpectationPartialDer}.\\n    \\\\end{description}\\n    \\\\end{description}\\n\\\\end{pabox}\\n\\nThe rules above are largely the same as in \\\\cite{yangTP2}, except the definition of $\\\\hat{Z}^{Wx}$ and $\\\\Zdot^{Wx}$. In the restricted \\\\netsort{} programs of \\\\citep{yangTP2}, $\\\\Zdot^{Wx}$ turns out to be 0 (see \\\\cref{thm:GIA}), so $\\\\hat{Z}^{Wx}$ was implicitly identified with $Z^{Wx}$. However, a general \\\\netsort{} program will have $\\\\Zdot^{Wx}\\\\ne0$. \\n\\n\\\\begin{rem}\\nNote that $Z^{x},\\\\hat{Z}^{x},\\\\Zdot^{x}$ only depend on how $x$ is computed in the \\\\netsort{} program (i.e. the program structure), not the specific (random) value of $x$.\\n\\\\end{rem}\\n\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-2e1f099d-1ce5-41a2-b430-b2988ecc13d5\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '183,235p' /tmp/tp3src/NetsorT2.tex\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"The following \\\\emph{Master Theorem} rigorously relates the \\\\emph{symbolically constructed} random variables $Z^h$ to the vectors $h$ in the program and their \\\\emph{analytic limits}.\\nIt is one of our main results and will also be our main workhorse in this paper.\\n\\n\\\\begin{thm}[\\\\netsort{} Master Theorem]\\n\\\\label{thm:NetsorTMasterTheorem} Fix a \\\\netsort{} program. Suppose the initial matrices $\\\\mathcal{W}$ and vectors $\\\\mathcal{V}$ are sampled in the fashion of \\\\cref{setup:netsort}.\\nAssume all nonlinearities $\\\\phi$ used in \\\\refNonlin{} are polynomially bounded. Then for any fixed $k$ and any \\\\emph{polynomially bounded}%\\n\\\\footnote{\\n    $\\\\phi: \\\\R^k \\\\to \\\\R$ is \\\\emph{polynomially-bounded} if $|\\\\phi(x)| \\\\le C\\\\|x\\\\|^p + c$ for some $p, C, c > 0$, for all $x \\\\in \\\\R^k$.\\n}\\n$\\\\psi:\\\\R^{k}\\\\to\\\\R$, as $n\\\\to\\\\infty$,\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n\\\\f 1n\\\\sum_{\\\\alpha=1}^{n}\\\\psi(h_{\\\\alpha}^{1},\\\\ldots,h_{\\\\alpha}^{k})\\\\asto\\\\EV\\\\psi(Z^{h^{1}},\\\\ldots,Z^{h^{k}}),\\n\\\\label{eqn:NetsorTMasterTheorem}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nfor any collection of vectors $h^{1},\\\\ldots,h^{k}$ in the program, where $Z^{h^{i}}$ are defined in \\\\cref{defn:Z}.%\\n\\\\footnote{Difference with \\\\cite[Thm 6.3]{yangScalingLimitsWide2019arXiv.org}: We have gotten rid of the ``rank convergence'' assumption (which we call ``rank stability'' in this paper) by showing that it comes for free.\\nSee \\\\cref{thm:rankstabilitymain} and see \\\\ref{IH:coreSet} and \\\\cref{lemma:rankStability} in \\\\cref{sec:proofMainTheorem}.}\\n\\\\end{thm}\\n\\\\cref{thm:NetsorTMasterTheorem} says that each ``coordinate slice'' $(h^1_\\\\alpha, \\\\ldots, h^k_\\\\alpha)$ can be thought of as an iid copy of $(Z^{h^1},\\\\ldots, Z^{h^k})$. Indeed, as consequences of the Master Theorem, \\\\cref{thm:NetsorTConvergenceInDistribution,{thm:NetsorTMultiConvergenceInDistribution}} formalize this intuition into a convergence in distribution.\\nVersions of \\\\cref{thm:NetsorTMasterTheorem} with convergence in mean (instead of almost sure) are also available (\\\\cref{thm:NetsorTMeanConvergence,thm:NetsorTMeanConvergenceLinearlyBounded}).\\n\\\\cref{thm:NetsorTMasterTheorem} strictly generalizes the Master Theorem in \\\\citet{yangTP2}.\\nThe \\\\netsor{} Master Theorem in \\\\citet{yangTP1} allows nonlinearities growing faster than polynomially, but otherwise is also a special case of \\\\cref{thm:NetsorTMasterTheorem}.\\nWe outline the proof of \\\\cref{thm:NetsorTMasterTheorem} in \\\\cref{sec:proofsketch} and give a full proof in \\\\cref{sec:proofMainTheorem}.\\n\\n\\\\begin{rem}[Partial derivative]\\\\label{rem:PartialDer}\\n    The partial derivative in \\\\refZdot{} should be interpreted as follows.\\n    By a simple inductive argument, $Z^x$ for every vector $x$ in the program is defined \\\\emph{uniquely} as a deterministic function $\\\\varphi(\\\\Zhat^{x^1}, \\\\ldots, \\\\Zhat^{x^k})$ of some $x^1, \\\\ldots, x^k$ in $\\\\mathcal V$ or introduced by \\\\refMatMul{}.%\\n    \\\\footnote{In the context of \\\\netsortplus{} introduced later in \\\\cref{sec:netsortplus}, this is still true, but $\\\\varphi$ here will take the form of a parametrized nonlinearity $\\\\varphi(-;\\\\mathring \\\\theta_1, \\\\ldots, \\\\mathring \\\\theta_l)$ with some deterministic parameters $\\\\mathring \\\\theta_1, \\\\ldots, \\\\mathring \\\\theta_l$.}\\n    For instance, in \\\\cref{exmp:ATAvZdot}, $Z^x = \\\\Zhat^x + \\\\Zhat^v$ if $v \\\\in \\\\mathcal V$, so $\\\\varphi$ is given by $\\\\varphi(a, b) = a + b$.\\n    Then\\n    \\\\begin{equation*}\\n        \\\\partial Z^x / \\\\partial \\\\Zhat^{x^i} \\\\defeq \\\\partial_i \\\\varphi(\\\\Zhat^{x^1}, \\\\ldots, \\\\Zhat^{x^k}), \\\\quad \\\\text{and} \\\\quad \\\\partial Z^x / \\\\partial \\\\Zhat^z \\\\defeq 0 \\\\text{ for any } z \\\\not\\\\in\\\\{x^1, \\\\ldots, x^k\\\\}.\\n    \\\\end{equation*}\\n    Note this definition depends on the precise way the program is written, not just on the underlying mathematics.\\n    For example, if $y,z \\\\in \\\\mathcal V$ and $x = \\\\phi(W(y+z))$, then $Z^x = \\\\phi(\\\\Zhat^{W(y+z)})$ so that $\\\\partial Z^x/\\\\partial \\\\Zhat^{Wy} = \\\\partial Z^x/\\\\partial \\\\Zhat^{Wz} = 0$.\\n    If instead, we have $x = \\\\phi(Wy+Wz)$, then $Z^x = \\\\phi(\\\\Zhat^{Wy} + \\\\Zhat^{Wz})$ so that $\\\\partial Z^x/\\\\partial \\\\Zhat^{W(x+y)} = 0$.\\n    However, in both cases, $\\\\Zdot^{W^\\\\trsp x} = (Z^y + Z^z)\\\\EV \\\\phi'(\\\\Zhat^{W(y+z)})$.\\n\\\\end{rem}\\n\\n\\\\begin{rem}[Partial derivative expectation]\\\\label{rem:ExpectationPartialDer}\\n    The quantity $\\\\EV\\\\frac{\\\\partial Z^{x}}{\\\\partial\\\\hat{Z}^{W^{\\\\trsp}y}}$ is well defined if $Z^{x}$ is differentiable in $\\\\hat{Z}^{W^{\\\\trsp}y}$. However, even if this is not the case, e.g. if $x=\\\\theta(W^\\\\trsp y)$ where $\\\\theta$ is the Heavyside step function, we can still define this expectation by leveraging Stein's lemma (\\\\cref{lemma:stein}):\\n    \\n    In \\\\refZdot{}, suppose $\\\\{W^{\\\\trsp}y^{i}\\\\}_{i=1}^{k}$ are all elements of $\\\\mathcal V_{W^\\\\trsp}$ introduced before $x$.\\n    Define the matrix $C\\\\in\\\\R^{k\\\\times k}$ by $C_{ij}\\\\defeq \\\\EV Z^{y^{i}}Z^{y^{j}}$ and define the vector $b\\\\in\\\\R^{k}$ by $b_{i}\\\\defeq \\\\EV\\\\hat{Z}^{W^{\\\\trsp}y^{i}}Z^{x}$. If $a=C^{+}b$ (where $C^{+}$ denotes the pseudoinverse of $C$), then in \\\\refZdot{} we may set\\n    \\\\begin{equation}\\n    \\\\sigma_{W}^{2}\\\\EV\\\\frac{\\\\partial Z^{x}}{\\\\partial\\\\hat{Z}^{W^{\\\\trsp}y^{i}}}=a_{i}.\\\\label{eq:ExpectationPartialDer}\\n    \\\\end{equation}\\n    This definition agrees with the partial derivative expectation by Stein's lemma (\\\\cref{lemma:stein}) when the latter is well defined.\\n    \\\\cref{thm:NetsorTMasterTheorem} holds with this broader definition of partial derivative expectation.\\n\\\\end{rem}\\n    \\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Extensions}\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf33f3dd087d28c68681c6e549cb7\",\"summary\":[\"**Assessing source needs**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-3ec27f18-1a88-415a-8a1b-bd1769f4d0fa\",\"query\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685\"}},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0712c-09e1-70c2-bb71-7aca8a29068e\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0712c-099c-7b50-a5c8-f3a360d99bfe\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/three_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf348659887d28787e67a905bbc7c\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing source replacements**\",\"**Applying prepared source edits**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf3592a4c87d29f0e048ea60721b1\",\"summary\":[\"**Reading agent section**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-8c5d1789-8f7d-4b1b-aad7-a151a658180b\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,340p' /tmp/three_input_activity_lemma.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Three-input activity lemma, including rank-two input Gram matrices\\n\\nAssume the local autonomous population flow and its mean-square continuity have been established with the same equations as the two-input flow, with all input sums now over a=1,2,3. Let x_a/sqrt(d) have norm one, and assume x_a and x_b are never parallel or antiparallel. Labels y_a are arbitrary signs. The input Gram matrix G is positive semidefinite with diagonal one; it need not be invertible.\\n\\nWrite the first-layer Gaussian root as Z_{0,a}^{(1)}=w^T x_a/sqrt(d), w~N(0,I_d), and H_{0,a}^{(1)}=atan(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}). Define Q_ab=E[H_{0,a}^{(1)}H_{0,b}^{(1)}].\\n\\n## Q is positive definite even when G is singular\\n\\nIf a linear combination of these arctangents vanishes almost surely, continuity and the full support of w make it vanish for every w. Choose v outside the finitely many proper hyperplanes defined by v^T x_a=0 and v^T(x_a±x_b)=0. This is possible by pairwise nonparallelity. The numbers b_a=v^T x_a/sqrt(d) are nonzero and have distinct squares. Restrict the identity to w=t v. The coefficients of t,t^3,t^5 in the arctangent Taylor series give\\n\\n    sum_a c_a b_a (b_a^2)^k = 0, k=0,1,2.\\n\\nThe Vandermonde determinant is nonzero, so c_a b_a=0 for every a, hence c=0. Thus Q is positive definite.\\n\\nConsequently Y_a:=Z_{0,a}^{(2)} are a full-support centered Gaussian triple with covariance Q. Define\\n\\n    S = sum_a y_a atan(Y_a),\\n    U_a = S phi'(Y_a),\\n    P_a = (W_0^{(2)})^* U_a,\\n    B_a = phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}) P_a,\\n    V_ab = E[U_a U_b],    D_ab = E[B_a B_b].\\n\\nThe event S=0 has probability zero: conditional on Y_2,Y_3, S is a strictly monotone function of Y_1, and Y_1 has a continuous conditional density. If a linear combination of U_a vanishes, division by S and full support imply sum_a c_a/(1+Y_a^2)=0 everywhere. Varying one coordinate at a time forces each c_a=0. Thus V is positive definite.\\n\\nThe first reused transpose call has the Gaussian conditioning representation\\n\\n    P_a = sum_c H_{0,c}^{(1)} [Q^{-1} E(Y U_a)]_c + Gamma_a,\\n\\nwhere Gamma~N(0,V) is independent of the first-layer Gaussian root. Therefore the conditional covariance of B given the first-layer root is\\n\\n    diag(phi'(Z_0^{(1)})) V diag(phi'(Z_0^{(1)})),\\n\\nwhich is positive definite everywhere. It follows that D is positive definite.\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n    T_a = sum_b G_ab y_b B_b,\\n    A_a = phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}) T_a,\\n    R_a = sum_b y_b Q_ab U_b + W_0^{(2)} A_a.\\n\\nThe first-layer t^2 coefficient T_a is nonzero in mean square for every a. Indeed its coefficient of Gamma_a conditional on the roots includes G_aa y_a phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}), which is nonzero; positive definiteness of V makes its conditional variance positive.\\n\\n## Each second-layer coefficient is nonzero; no permutation symmetry is needed\\n\\nLet alpha_a=Q^{-1}E[H_0^{(1)}A_a], and subtract the projection onto the three initial forward inputs:\\n\\n    A_a^perp = A_a - sum_c alpha_{a,c} H_{0,c}^{(1)},\\n    sigma_a^2 = E[(A_a^perp)^2].\\n\\nConditional on the first-layer roots, A_a is a linear function of Gamma with coefficients\\n\\n    c_b = phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)}) G_ab y_b phi'(Z_{0,b}^{(1)}).\\n\\nIn particular c_a=y_a phi'(Z_{0,a}^{(1)})^2 is nonzero. Thus\\n\\n    sigma_a^2 >= E Var(A_a | first-layer roots) = E[c^T V c] > 0.\\n\\nCondition the initial matrix on all three already revealed forward calls W_0^{(2)}H_{0,c}^{(1)}=Y_c and all three transpose calls (W_0^{(2)})^*U_b=P_b. The next forward call has law\\n\\n    W_0^{(2)}A_a = sum_c alpha_{a,c}Y_c + sum_b beta_{a,b}U_b + sigma_a gamma_a,\\n    beta_a = V^{-1} E[P A_a^perp],\\n\\nwhere gamma_a is standard Gaussian independent of the second-layer history Y (and therefore of U). This follows directly from the Gaussian matrix conditional mean and its independent doubly projected residual; the fixed-rank projection of a fresh Gaussian output disappears in normalized mean square. The Gram matrices Q,V are positive definite, so the displayed inverse formulas are legitimate. A separate such call suffices for each a; joint independence of different gamma_a is not claimed.\\n\\nThe other terms in R_a are functions of Y. Consequently\\n\\n    E[R_a^2] >= sigma_a^2 > 0.\\n\\nThis establishes each input's second-hidden-layer activity without the two-input proof's swap argument.\\n\\n## Small-time expansion and strict conclusions\\n\\nSubstitution in the integral equations, using bounded activation derivatives and the bounded initial action, gives\\n\\n    W^{(3)}(t) = 2t S + o_L2(t),\\n    delta_a^{(2)}(t) = 2t U_a + o_L2(t),\\n    Z_a^{(1)}(t)-Z_{0,a}^{(1)} = 2t^2 T_a + o_L2(t^2),\\n    W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)} = 2t^2 sum_b y_b U_b tensor H_{0,b}^{(1)} + o_op(t^2),\\n    Z_a^{(2)}(t)-Y_a = 2t^2 R_a + o_L2(t^2).\\n\\nThe corresponding velocities are 4t T_a+o_L2(t) and 4t R_a+o_L2(t). Hence each hidden squared displacement has positive leading coefficient 4E[T_a^2] or 4E[R_a^2] times t^4, and each integrated squared hidden speed has positive leading coefficient (16/3)E[T_a^2] or (16/3)E[R_a^2] times t^3.\\n\\nSet A_1=y^T(G entrywise-times D)y and A_2=y^T(Q entrywise-times V)y. Both are strictly positive. In particular the first conclusion does not need G positive definite: if D>=lambda I, diagonalizing G as sum_j v_j v_j^T gives\\n\\n    G entrywise-times D = sum_j diag(v_j)D diag(v_j) >= lambda diag(G) = lambda I.\\n\\nLikewise Q entrywise-times V is positive definite. Adjunction gives\\n\\n    sum_a y_a E[U_a R_a] = A_1+A_2 > 0.\\n\\nThus the first and second kernel blocks are\\n\\n    K^{(1)}(t)=4t^2(G entrywise-times D)+o(t^2),\\n    K^{(2)}(t)=4t^2(Q entrywise-times V)+o(t^2),\\n\\nand both are positive definite for sufficiently small positive t. The initial readout block E[atan(Y)atan(Y)^T] is positive definite by the full support of Y, so it remains positive definite for small t. The total kernel satisfies\\n\\n    y^T K(t)y = y^T K^{(3)}(0)y + 8(A_1+A_2)t^2 + o(t^2),\\n\\nso it is nonconstant. The loss starts at three and has derivative -4y^T K^{(3)}(0)y<0, hence strictly decreases on a sufficiently short interval.\\n\\nEvery initial hidden marginal is a nondegenerate Gaussian. Its variance is positive, and atan cannot agree with an affine function on its full support. Thus its best-affine-fit error is positive. Mean-square continuity, and the variance/covariance formula for this fit, preserve these facts on a sufficiently small common interval.\\n\\nThe same proof works for any fixed finite number of pairwise nonparallel normalized inputs; for m inputs the Vandermonde argument uses the first m odd Taylor coefficients.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf35f197087d29722982123f3038d\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking conditional Gaussian lemma**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf36d6f8487d2bb8503463cae23a9\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing the proof**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf373559c87d28922a9ced19583c7\",\"text\":\"The three-input case appears to retain all the local conclusions, including when the inputs lie in a plane. The key new facts are that their arctangent features remain linearly independent, and a fresh Gaussian component prevents either hidden layer’s movement from cancelling for any one input. I’m checking the complete statement, including all label choices.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-8ff24b47-7757-4f73-b071-8f0a56cbaed3\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,620p' /tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Two inputs: a rigorous local-time joint width and gradient-flow limit\\n\\n## Result and scope\\n\\nFix two inputs \\\\(x_1,x_2\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a\\\\|_2^2}{d}=1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\rho=\\\\frac{x_1^\\\\top x_2}{d}\\\\in(-1,1),\\\\qquad\\nG=\\\\begin{pmatrix}1&\\\\rho\\\\\\\\\\\\rho&1\\\\end{pmatrix}.\\n\\\\]\\nLet \\\\(y_1,y_2\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence of positive steps \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all the stated angles and both label patterns.\\n\\nPredictions, residuals, loss, and all entries of all three kernel blocks converge in probability uniformly on this interval. The joint two-input hidden path laws converge with their second moments in the uniform path norm. Integrated squared hidden velocities converge. The joint empirical averages of any fixed finite collection of continuous, globally Lipschitz forward/adjoint probes, with bounded products when a product instruction is used, converge to their corresponding population expectations, uniformly in time. Unbounded first-layer backward fields and their quadratic contractions are included by the tail argument below.\\n\\nOn a possibly smaller interval \\\\((0,T_*]\\\\), each input's preactivations move in both hidden layers, all three \\\\(2\\\\times2\\\\) kernel blocks are positive definite, the total kernel is nonconstant, loss strictly decreases, and \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) has positive best-affine-fit error on each hidden preactivation distribution.\\n\\n**Scope:** for nonorthogonal inputs this proof establishes a fixed positive time interval, not convergence on every compact time interval. The latter remains unproved here. When \\\\(\\\\rho=0\\\\), the earlier cubic-coordinate argument does extend to every finite time interval under its original sufficient step condition \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\). No optimizer preconditioning is being introduced.\\n\\n## The finite network and its exact updates\\n\\nThe dimensions are \\\\(W^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times d}\\\\), \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\). For input \\\\(a=1,2\\\\), use\\n\\\\[\\nz_a^{(1)}=\\\\frac{W^{(1)}x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nz_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi=\\\\arctan,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h_a^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad\\nr_{n,a}=f_{n,a}-y_a,\\\\qquad L_n=r_{n,1}^2+r_{n,2}^2.\\n\\\\]\\nThe summed loss retains the earlier factors of two; using the average loss halves the speed.\\n\\nAll initialization entries are independent:\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ij}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently each first-layer root pair is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), independently over neuron index \\\\(i\\\\). The two preactivations for one neuron are not independent unless \\\\(\\\\rho=0\\\\).\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta_a^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_{n,a}/\\\\partial z_a^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\).\\nIn the displayed rescaled parameterization the layer learning-rate multipliers are \\\\(n,1,n\\\\). The exact first-weight update is\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n=W_k^{(1)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{\\\\sqrt d}\\n\\\\sum_{b=1}^2r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)}x_b^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\nMultiplication by \\\\(x_a/\\\\sqrt d\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=z_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{n}\\n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)} .\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M1}\\n\\\\]\\nUse physical time \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), linearly interpolate these parameters, and recompute the forward pass at intermediate times.\\n\\nThe whole effect of the input geometry on first-layer training is the matrix \\\\(G\\\\). For example, the update of \\\\(z_1^{(1)}\\\\) contains its own gradient and \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\) times input 2's gradient.\\n\\nThe finite continuous flow has the kernel blocks\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}_{n,ab}\\n=G_{ab}\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(1)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(1)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(2)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(1)})^\\\\top h_b^{(1)}}n\\n \\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(2)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(3)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(2)})^\\\\top h_b^{(2)}}n .\\n\\\\tag{M2}\\n\\\\]\\nDifferentiation gives \\\\(\\\\dot f_n=-2K_n r_n\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\dot L_n=-4r_n^\\\\top K_n r_n\\\\), where \\\\(K_n\\\\) is the sum of the blocks. Each block is positive semidefinite: it is the Gram matrix of the corresponding scaled parameter gradients. Individual residual magnitudes need not decrease; their squared sum does.\\n\\n## Why a new stability estimate is needed\\n\\nWith \\\\(F(s)=s+s^3/3\\\\), the componentwise transformation \\\\(X_a^{(1)}=F(Z_a^{(1)})\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot X_a^{(1)}\\n=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\n\\\\frac{1+(Z_a^{(1)})^2}{1+(Z_b^{(1)})^2}\\n(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{M3}\\n\\\\]\\nFor \\\\(\\\\rho=0\\\\) the ratios cancel. For \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\ne0\\\\) they do not, and are unbounded. We therefore work with the original preactivations and prove a uniform tail estimate for the backward fields.\\n\\nThe exact limiting equations will be\\n\\\\[\\nH_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\nf_a=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H_a^{(2)}],\\\\quad r_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot Z_a^{(1)}&=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\\\delta_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_b\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_bH_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M4}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\((u\\\\otimes v)V=u\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[vV]\\\\). This definition displays the normalization that replaces the finite factor \\\\(1/n\\\\). First-layer and second-layer coordinates belong to separate populations. \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) acts from the former to the latter, and its adjoint acts in reverse. Every expectation pairs coordinates of the same population.\\n\\nThe initial root pair is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), the population readout is zero, and the initial action \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) is the joint forward-and-transpose limit of the initial Gaussian matrix, constructed next. The state consists of two first-layer fields and the shared matrix action and readout field. It has finitely many kinds of state objects, not finitely many scalar degrees of freedom.\\n\\n## The fixed-mesh construction and the new tail lemma\\n\\nWe use the following finite Gaussian fact. For a fixed finite calculation from independent Gaussian roots and a Gaussian matrix of variance \\\\(1/n\\\\), with smooth polynomially bounded coordinate functions and finitely many calls to that matrix and its transpose, every same-layer joint empirical average of a continuous polynomially bounded measurement has a deterministic limit. The limiting action is Gaussian randomness plus the responses forced by earlier opposite-direction calls. The precise response coefficients and their derivative convention are given and used in the appendix below. This is the smooth specialization of Theorem 2.10 and Box 1 of [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685). Its hypotheses hold here: after fixing population scalar coefficients, each instruction and each derivative needed below is a polynomially bounded function of finitely many Gaussian variables. No claim of iid finite coordinates after matrix reuse is used.\\n\\nFor completeness, the underlying conditioning mechanism is as follows. If previous calls have revealed \\\\(W_0^{(2)}V=Y\\\\) and \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R\\\\), then the remaining Gaussian matrix is an independent Gaussian projected onto the directions orthogonal to the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) and \\\\(V\\\\). Its conditional mean obeys both recorded equations. A new product is the response fixed by these equations plus that projected Gaussian applied to the unexplored part of the new input. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the response coefficients. The finite Gaussian theorem makes this induction valid also with dependent query directions. We explicitly rely on this stated finite-program result, not on a claim that the infinite-time or growing-program-length theorem already follows from it.\\n\\nThese finite limits can be realized on two common population coordinate spaces. Here are the details relevant to the subsequent continuous-time argument. Take all the programs needed for rational meshes, their finite unions, and a countable closure under rational linear combinations, the coordinate operations in the proof, and bounded Lipschitz approximations. Their finite joint laws are consistent, so they can be realized jointly. Take the mean-square closure of their linear spans on each layer. Exact finite linear identities pass to these laws.\\n\\nA fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\) satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le2\\\\,9^{2n}e^{-nM^2/8}\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{M5}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed two \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets of the unit sphere each have at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points, and every fixed bilinear form of the matrix is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). This finite operator inequality and joint second-moment convergence imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2]\\n\\\\]\\nfor every generated \\\\(V\\\\). Thus the action is well-defined on equal-in-mean-square variables and extends to the closure. The same reasoning applies to the transpose. The exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u)^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\npasses to the limit and identifies the two actions as adjoints. Bounded coordinate multipliers and their required products belong to these spaces by clipping and mean-square approximation. This constructs the common bounded-operator realization used below; it is not an additional assumption.\\n\\nDefine population Euler for (M4) in this common realization, starting from the stated initialization. Its rank expansion is\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}\\n-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\\\sum_b\\nr_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{M6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus each fixed mesh is a finite Gaussian calculation with deterministic population residuals and contractions. Iterative construction is causal: the current fields determine their expectations and then the next fields. Its realization agrees with the finite Gaussian law, because applying the same rank expansion reduces each action to an initial-matrix call and those deterministic contractions.\\n\\nThe appendix proves the essential new estimate:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta:\\\\,\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\\\ \\n\\\\max_{a,k:\\\\,k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\nc\\\\left|(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\right|^2\\n\\\\right)\\\\le C .\\n\\\\tag{M7}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(T_0,c,C>0\\\\) do not depend on the mesh. In particular the squared tail beyond \\\\(R\\\\) is at most \\\\(C e^{-c'R^2}\\\\), after reducing \\\\(c'\\\\).\\n\\nThe appendix is a derivation, not an assumed light-tail condition. It controls the accumulated deterministic response coefficients on both layers. The second-layer derivatives stay bounded; the first-layer derivatives have a Gaussian exponential bound. A short-time induction closes these bounds uniformly in the number of Euler steps.\\n\\n## Population Euler converges to a unique flow\\n\\nFor comparing two population states use\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\sum_{a=1}^2\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|Z_a^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\tag{M8}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each mean-square norm by the Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\).\\n\\nAll Euler trajectories, and all the actual discrete trajectories with \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\le1\\\\), have bounds independent of width on a fixed bounded time interval, whenever the initial readout supremum and matrix operator norm are bounded. To see this, boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n|r_a|\\\\le 1+\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}_{k+1}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\nC\\\\eta_n(1+\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty).\\n\\\\]\\nThe geometric sum bounds the readout supremum. The rank-one update then bounds the matrix operator norm, and the first-layer update bounds the root-mean-square velocity and displacement. The same calculation applies to population Euler with \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\). All initial bounds hold with probability tending to one for the finite initialization.\\n\\nOn these bounded sets, the forward quantities, residuals, second-layer backward fields, and the maps\\n\\\\[\\n(Z_1^{(1)},Z_2^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\n\\\\longmapsto (W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\nare Lipschitz in (M8). This follows by adding and subtracting one factor at a time, using boundedness and Lipschitz continuity of \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\), the readout supremum bound, and the operator bound.\\n\\nOnly the first-layer multiplication needs a separate estimate. Write\\n\\\\[\\nP_a=(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\widetilde P_a=(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThen\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})P_a-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)})\\\\widetilde P_a\\n=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(P_a-\\\\widetilde P_a)\\n+[\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)})]\\\\widetilde P_a.\\n\\\\]\\nSplit the last term at \\\\(|\\\\widetilde P_a|=R\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) is bounded and Lipschitz,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|[\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z)]\\\\widetilde P\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le C R\\\\|Z-\\\\widetilde Z\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+C\\\\|\\\\widetilde P\\\\,1_{\\\\{|\\\\widetilde P|>R\\\\}}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{M9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\). Only the reference field needs a tail bound. For a reference Euler grid point, (M7) bounds the final term by \\\\(C e^{-cR^2}\\\\).\\n\\nAn Euler interpolant moves by at most \\\\(C\\\\Delta\\\\) in (M8) between grid points. Its backward field therefore differs from the grid field by at most \\\\(C\\\\Delta\\\\) in mean square, using the Lipschitz map above. Thus, at any intermediate time, the vector field differs from the interpolant's assigned grid velocity by at most\\n\\\\[\\nC(1+R)\\\\Delta+C e^{-cR^2}.\\n\\\\tag{M10}\\n\\\\]\\nThe same localization gives the comparison inequality for two meshes. Integration and the elementary Gronwall inequality yield, for \\\\(0<T\\\\le T_0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d(\\\\theta^\\\\Delta(t),\\\\theta^{\\\\Delta'}(t))\\n\\\\le\\nC e^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\left((1+R)(\\\\Delta+\\\\Delta')+e^{-cR^2}\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{M11}\\n\\\\]\\nFirst send both meshes to zero with \\\\(R\\\\) fixed. Then send \\\\(R\\\\) to infinity. The term \\\\(e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) vanishes, so the Euler paths are Cauchy uniformly in time in (M8).\\n\\nThe state spaces are complete, and the limit keeps the readout supremum and operator bounds. The coordinate product \\\\((Z,P)\\\\mapsto\\\\phi'(Z)P\\\\) is continuous from \\\\(L^2\\\\times L^2\\\\) to \\\\(L^2\\\\): split off the change of \\\\(P\\\\), then use boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) and dominated convergence against the fixed limiting \\\\(P^2\\\\). This allows passage to the integral form of (M4). The limit is therefore a solution, not just a limit of observables. It inherits (M7) by convergence in probability and Fatou's inequality; at intermediate grid times use the \\\\(O(\\\\Delta)\\\\) backward-field difference.\\n\\nThe same estimate compares this solution with any other solution of (M4) in the bounded state class with the same initial state. Only the constructed reference solution requires (M7). Their error is bounded by \\\\(C e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) for every \\\\(R\\\\), hence is zero. This proves uniqueness.\\n\\nThe equations use only the current fields and the current action and adjoint. In particular, their future evolution does not require the stored Euler history. More formally, the closed spaces generated by current fields, bounded coordinate operations, and current forward/adjoint actions contain the future: clipped vector fields and their Euler approximations remain there, and (M9) removes the clipping. Equal current joint action laws identify these generated spaces by a mean-square isometry, which intertwines their actions. Uniqueness therefore identifies their future action laws. This proves autonomy and restartability along the constructed interval.\\n\\n## From the population flow to the actual fine-step GD\\n\\nFix a coarse mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), independently of the actual step \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\). Build a finite oracle by expanding (M6), supplying it with the population residuals and every population contraction in these expansions. It starts from the same finite first-layer initialization and the same \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) as GD, but has \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\). Every oracle instruction now has deterministic scalar coefficients. The fixed finite Gaussian theorem applies jointly to all its nodes and proves convergence of all the needed empirical contractions and second moments.\\n\\nThere is a useful distinction between oracle nodes and an actual finite network state. Construct proxy parameter arrays using its first-layer nodes and readout, and the matrix\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k,\\\\mathrm{proxy}}^{(2)}\\n=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k,b}r_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(2)}\\n(h_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{M12}\\n\\\\]\\nApplying this proxy matrix to a current oracle activation differs from its prescribed oracle preactivation only by sums of empirical-minus-population contractions. For example each forward discrepancy has the form\\n\\\\[\\n-2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(2)}\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\frac{(h_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)})^\\\\top\\nh_{a,k,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)}}{n}\\n-\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{M13}\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose discrepancy has the corresponding contraction of two \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) fields. At a fixed mesh there are finitely many such terms. Their scalar errors tend to zero, and their vector norms stay bounded. The actual network quantities recomputed from the proxy arrays therefore differ from the oracle nodes by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) in root mean square. Bounded readout and the operator bound justify each subsequent forward/backward comparison. In particular its recomputed vector field differs from its prescribed oracle grid velocity by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\).\\n\\nFor each fixed threshold \\\\(R\\\\), the oracle's empirical squared tails converge to their population counterparts. One may use continuous upper cutoffs, equal to one beyond \\\\(R\\\\) and zero below \\\\(R-1\\\\), to avoid discontinuities. The fixed finite Gaussian theorem applies to these quadratic-growth measurements. Consequently the root-mean-square tail of the recomputed proxy backward field has limit superior at most \\\\(C e^{-cR^2}\\\\), after modifying constants. This is proved for the reference oracle, not assumed for actual GD.\\n\\nLinearly interpolate the proxy parameters. Their metric speed is bounded in probability, with limiting bound independent of the mesh, by (M4) and the node consistency just proved. Their between-grid vector-field defect is bounded by (M10), plus a fixed-mesh \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\). Compare the actual GD interpolant to this proxy. Actual GD's derivative is its vector field at the preceding fine grid point; its state changes by \\\\(O(\\\\eta_n)\\\\) inside a fine step. Apply (M9) with the proxy as reference. No tail bound for the actual GD is needed. Gronwall gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t),\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{proxy},\\\\Delta}(t))\\n\\\\le C e^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\bigg(\\n&\\\\frac{\\\\|W_{n,0}^{(3)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n+(1+R)(\\\\eta_n+\\\\Delta)\\\\\\\\\\n&+e^{-cR^2}+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\n\\\\bigg).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) here is at fixed \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\). Bounds on the actual readout supremum are obtained from its update, not from root-mean-square closeness.\\n\\nThe initialization discrepancy vanishes, and \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). First take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\); next take \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\to0\\\\); finally take \\\\(R\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). Together with (M11), the fixed-mesh oracle empirical convergence, and the triangle inequality for observables, this proves the claimed joint limit. Thus this local proof needs no width-dependent learning-rate restriction beyond \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\).\\n\\nFor the first kernel block, use (M9) once more to transfer the first-layer \\\\(\\\\delta^{(1)}\\\\) fields in mean square; the other blocks follow directly from the bounded/Lipschitz maps. Products and contractions then converge by Cauchy–Schwarz. For hidden velocities,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot z_a^{(2)}\\n=\\\\dot W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)}\\n+W^{(2)}\\\\bigl[\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot\\\\dot z_a^{(1)}\\\\bigr].\\n\\\\]\\nThe reference first-layer velocity is a finite linear combination of bounded multiples of the backward fields, hence has the same tail control. Applying the same comparison proves convergence of both hidden velocities in integrated mean square and hence of their integrated squared norms.\\n\\nTo obtain path-law convergence, fir\",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":35194}},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-b904ed25-6dca-4513-b2d5-927cba135567\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Three inputs: a rigorous local-time joint width and gradient-flow limit\\n\\n## Result and scope\\n\\nFix three inputs \\\\(x_1,x_2,x_3\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a\\\\|_2^2}{d}=1,\\\\qquad\\nx_a\\\\ne x_b,\\\\quad x_a\\\\ne -x_b\\\\quad(a\\\\ne b),\\\\qquad\\nG_{ab}=\\\\frac{x_a^\\\\top x_b}{d}.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause the norms agree, the conditions on \\\\(x_a,x_b\\\\) are exactly pairwise nonparallelity. The \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) matrix \\\\(G\\\\) is positive semidefinite with diagonal one. It may be singular. All input indices and input sums below range over \\\\(1,2,3\\\\).\\nLet \\\\(y_1,y_2,y_3\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence of positive steps \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all such triples and all eight label sign patterns. Moreover, the existence time (T_0) can be chosen uniformly over these triples and labels: its construction uses only three inputs, (|G_{ab}|le1), the fixed activation and initialization scales. It does not depend on width, step size, input dimension, or a lower bound on a Gram eigenvalue.\\n\\nPredictions, residuals, loss, and all entries of all three kernel blocks converge in probability uniformly on this interval. The joint three-input hidden path laws converge with their second moments in the uniform path norm. Integrated squared hidden velocities converge. The joint empirical averages of any fixed finite collection of continuous, globally Lipschitz forward/adjoint probes, with bounded products when a product instruction is used, converge to their corresponding population expectations, uniformly in time. Unbounded first-layer backward fields and their quadratic contractions are included by the tail argument below.\\n\\nOn a possibly smaller interval \\\\((0,T_*]\\\\), each input's preactivations move in both hidden layers, all three \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) kernel blocks are positive definite, the total kernel is nonconstant, loss strictly decreases, and \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) has positive best-affine-fit error on each hidden preactivation distribution.\\n\\n**Scope:** this is a local-time theorem. A convergence theorem on every finite horizon is not claimed for arbitrary input geometry. \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) is chosen before taking width and learning-rate limits and does not shrink in those limits. The strictly positive feature-learning coefficients and the possibly smaller activity interval \\\\(T_*\\\\) may depend on the fixed input geometry and labels.\\n\\n## The finite network and its exact updates\\n\\nThe dimensions are \\\\(W^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times d}\\\\), \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\). For input \\\\(a=1,2,3\\\\), use\\n\\\\[\\nz_a^{(1)}=\\\\frac{W^{(1)}x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nz_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi=\\\\arctan,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h_a^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad\\nr_{n,a}=f_{n,a}-y_a,\\\\qquad L_n=\\\\sum_{a=1}^3r_{n,a}^2.\\n\\\\]\\nThe summed loss retains the earlier factors of two; using the average loss halves the speed.\\n\\nAll initialization entries are independent:\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ij}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently each first-layer root triple is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), independently over neuron index \\\\(i\\\\). The three preactivations for one neuron may be correlated and may satisfy an exact linear relation. This is permitted.\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta_a^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_{n,a}/\\\\partial z_a^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\).\\nIn the displayed rescaled parameterization the layer learning-rate multipliers are \\\\(n,1,n\\\\). The exact first-weight update is\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n=W_k^{(1)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{\\\\sqrt d}\\n\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)}x_b^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\nMultiplication by \\\\(x_a/\\\\sqrt d\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=z_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{n}\\n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)} .\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M1}\\n\\\\]\\nUse physical time \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), linearly interpolate these parameters, and recompute the forward pass at intermediate times.\\n\\nThe whole effect of input geometry on first-layer training is (G): training on input (b) moves input (a)'s preactivation with coefficient (G_{ab}). If (Gc=0), then (sum_a c_a z_a^{(1)}=0) initially and every update preserves this relation. No inverse of (G) is used.\\n\\nThe finite continuous flow has the kernel blocks\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}_{n,ab}\\n=G_{ab}\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(1)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(1)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(2)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(1)})^\\\\top h_b^{(1)}}n\\n \\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(2)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(3)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(2)})^\\\\top h_b^{(2)}}n .\\n\\\\tag{M2}\\n\\\\]\\nDifferentiation gives \\\\(\\\\dot f_n=-2K_n r_n\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\dot L_n=-4r_n^\\\\top K_n r_n\\\\), where \\\\(K_n\\\\) is the sum of the blocks. Each block is positive semidefinite: it is the Gram matrix of the corresponding scaled parameter gradients. Individual residual magnitudes need not decrease; their squared sum does.\\n\\n## Why a new stability estimate is needed\\n\\nWith \\\\(F(s)=s+s^3/3\\\\), the componentwise transformation \\\\(X_a^{(1)}=F(Z_a^{(1)})\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot X_a^{(1)}\\n=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\n\\\\frac{1+(Z_a^{(1)})^2}{1+(Z_b^{(1)})^2}\\n(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{M3}\\n\\\\]\\nIf the inputs are mutually orthogonal, only diagonal terms occur and the ratios cancel. Otherwise nonzero cross-input terms remain and their ratios are unbounded. We therefore work with the original preactivations and prove a uniform tail estimate for the backward fields.\\n\\nThe exact limiting equations will be\\n\\\\[\\nH_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\nf_a=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H_a^{(2)}],\\\\quad r_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot Z_a^{(1)}&=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\\\delta_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_b\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_bH_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M4}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\((u\\\\otimes v)V=u\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[vV]\\\\). This definition displays the normalization that replaces the finite factor \\\\(1/n\\\\). First-layer and second-layer coordinates belong to separate populations. \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) acts from the former to the latter, and its adjoint acts in reverse. Every expectation pairs coordinates of the same population.\\n\\nThe initial root triple is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), the population readout is zero, and the initial action \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) is the joint forward-and-transpose limit of the initial Gaussian matrix, constructed next. The state consists of three first-layer fields and the shared matrix action and readout field. It has finitely many kinds of state objects, not finitely many scalar degrees of freedom.\\n\\n## The fixed-mesh construction and the new tail lemma\\n\\nWe use the following finite Gaussian fact. For a fixed finite calculation from iid jointly Gaussian coordinate tuples, independent of a Gaussian matrix of entry variance \\\\(1/n\\\\), with smooth polynomially bounded coordinate functions and finitely many calls to that matrix and its transpose, every same-layer joint empirical average of a continuous polynomially bounded measurement has a deterministic limit. The limiting action is Gaussian randomness plus the responses forced by earlier opposite-direction calls. The precise response coefficients and their derivative convention are given and used in the appendix below. This is the smooth specialization of Theorem 2.10 and Box 1 of [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685). Its hypotheses hold here: after fixing population scalar coefficients, each instruction and each derivative needed below is a polynomially bounded function of finitely many Gaussian variables. The initial tuple covariance may be singular; represent it as a fixed linear image of independent Gaussian roots. No claim of iid finite coordinates after matrix reuse is used.\\n\\nFor completeness, the underlying conditioning mechanism is as follows. If previous calls have revealed \\\\(W_0^{(2)}V=Y\\\\) and \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R\\\\), then the remaining Gaussian matrix is an independent Gaussian projected onto the directions orthogonal to the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) and \\\\(V\\\\). Its conditional mean obeys both recorded equations. A new product is the response fixed by these equations plus that projected Gaussian applied to the unexplored part of the new input. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the response coefficients. The finite Gaussian theorem makes this induction valid also with dependent query directions. We explicitly rely on this stated finite-program result, not on a claim that the infinite-time or growing-program-length theorem already follows from it.\\n\\nThese finite limits can be realized on two common population coordinate spaces. Here are the details relevant to the subsequent continuous-time argument. Take all the programs needed for rational meshes, their finite unions, and a countable closure under rational linear combinations, the coordinate operations in the proof, and bounded Lipschitz approximations. Their finite joint laws are consistent, so they can be realized jointly. Take the mean-square closure of their linear spans on each layer. Exact finite linear identities pass to these laws.\\n\\nA fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\) satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le2\\\\,9^{2n}e^{-nM^2/8}\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{M5}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed two \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets of the unit sphere each have at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points, and every fixed bilinear form of the matrix is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). This finite operator inequality and joint second-moment convergence imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2]\\n\\\\]\\nfor every generated \\\\(V\\\\). Thus the action is well-defined on equal-in-mean-square variables and extends to the closure. The same reasoning applies to the transpose. The exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u)^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\npasses to the limit and identifies the two actions as adjoints. Bounded coordinate multipliers and their required products belong to these spaces by clipping and mean-square approximation. This constructs the common bounded-operator realization used below; it is not an additional assumption.\\n\\nDefine population Euler for (M4) in this common realization, starting from the stated initialization. Its rank expansion is\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}\\n-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\\\sum_b\\nr_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{M6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus each fixed mesh is a finite Gaussian calculation with deterministic population residuals and contractions. Iterative construction is causal: the current fields determine their expectations and then the next fields. Its realization agrees with the finite Gaussian law, because applying the same rank expansion reduces each action to an initial-matrix call and those deterministic contractions.\\n\\nThe appendix proves the essential new estimate:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta:\\\\,\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\\\ \\n\\\\max_{a,k:\\\\,k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\nc\\\\left|(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\right|^2\\n\\\\right)\\\\le C .\\n\\\\tag{M7}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(T_0,c,C>0\\\\) do not depend on the mesh. In particular the squared tail beyond \\\\(R\\\\) is at most \\\\(C e^{-c'R^2}\\\\), after reducing \\\\(c'\\\\).\\n\\nThe appendix is a derivation, not an assumed light-tail condition. It controls the accumulated deterministic response coefficients on both layers. The second-layer derivatives stay bounded; the first-layer derivatives have a Gaussian exponential bound. A short-time induction closes these bounds uniformly in the number of Euler steps.\\n\\n## Population Euler converges to a unique flow\\n\\nFor comparing two population states use\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\sum_{a=1}^3\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|Z_a^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\tag{M8}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each mean-square norm by the Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\).\\n\\nAll Euler trajectories, and all the actual discrete trajectories with \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\le1\\\\), have bounds independent of width on a fixed bounded time interval, whenever the initial readout supremum and matrix operator norm are bounded. To see this, boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n|r_a|\\\\le 1+\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}_{k+1}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\nC\\\\eta_n(1+\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty).\\n\\\\]\\nThe geometric sum bounds the readout supremum. The rank-one update then bounds the matrix operator norm, and the first-layer update bounds the root-mean-square velocity and displacement. The same calculation applies to population Euler with \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\). All initial bounds hold with probability tending to one for the finite initialization.\\n\\nOn these bounded sets, the forward quantities, residuals, second-layer backward fields, and the maps\\n\\\\[\\n(Z_1^{(1)},Z_2^{(1)},Z_3^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\n\\\\longmapsto (W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\nare Lipschitz in (M8). This follows by adding and subtracting one factor at a time, using boundedness and Lipschitz continuity of \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\), the readout supremum bound, and the operator bound.\\n\\nOnly the first-layer multiplication needs a separate estimate. Write\\n\\\\[\\nP_a=(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\widetilde P_a=(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThen\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})P_a-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)})\\\\widetilde P_a\\n=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(P_a-\\\\widetilde P_a)\\n+[\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)})]\\\\widetilde P_a.\\n\\\\]\\nSplit the last term at \\\\(|\\\\widetilde P_a|=R\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) is bounded and Lipschitz,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|[\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z)]\\\\widetilde P\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le C R\\\\|Z-\\\\widetilde Z\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+C\\\\|\\\\widetilde P\\\\,1_{\\\\{|\\\\widetilde P|>R\\\\}}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{M9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\). Only the reference field needs a tail bound. For a reference Euler grid point, (M7) bounds the final term by \\\\(C e^{-cR^2}\\\\).\\n\\nAn Euler interpolant moves by at most \\\\(C\\\\Delta\\\\) in (M8) between grid points. Its backward field therefore differs from the grid field by at most \\\\(C\\\\Delta\\\\) in mean square, using the Lipschitz map above. Thus, at any intermediate time, the vector field differs from the interpolant's assigned grid velocity by at most\\n\\\\[\\nC(1+R)\\\\Delta+C e^{-cR^2}.\\n\\\\tag{M10}\\n\\\\]\\nThe same localization gives the comparison inequality for two meshes. Integration and the elementary Gronwall inequality yield, for \\\\(0<T\\\\le T_0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d(\\\\theta^\\\\Delta(t),\\\\theta^{\\\\Delta'}(t))\\n\\\\le\\nC e^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\left((1+R)(\\\\Delta+\\\\Delta')+e^{-cR^2}\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{M11}\\n\\\\]\\nFirst send both meshes to zero with \\\\(R\\\\) fixed. Then send \\\\(R\\\\) to infinity. The term \\\\(e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) vanishes, so the Euler paths are Cauchy uniformly in time in (M8).\\n\\nThe state spaces are complete, and the limit keeps the readout supremum and operator bounds. The coordinate product \\\\((Z,P)\\\\mapsto\\\\phi'(Z)P\\\\) is continuous from \\\\(L^2\\\\times L^2\\\\) to \\\\(L^2\\\\): split off the change of \\\\(P\\\\), then use boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) and dominated convergence against the fixed limiting \\\\(P^2\\\\). This allows passage to the integral form of (M4). The limit is therefore a solution, not just a limit of observables. It inherits (M7) by convergence in probability and Fatou's inequality; at intermediate grid times use the \\\\(O(\\\\Delta)\\\\) backward-field difference.\\n\\nThe same estimate compares this solution with any other solution of (M4) in the bounded state class with the same initial state. Only the constructed reference solution requires (M7). Their error is bounded by \\\\(C e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) for every \\\\(R\\\\), hence is zero. This proves uniqueness.\\n\\nThe equations use only the current fields and the current action and adjoint. In particular, their future evolution does not require the stored Euler history. More formally, the closed spaces generated by current fields, bounded coordinate operations, and current forward/adjoint actions contain the future: clipped vector fields and their Euler approximations remain there, and (M9) removes the clipping. Equal current joint action laws identify these generated spaces by a mean-square isometry, which intertwines their actions. Uniqueness therefore identifies their future action laws. This proves autonomy and restartability along the constructed interval.\\n\\n## From the population flow to the actual fine-step GD\\n\\nFix a coarse mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), independently of the actual step \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\). Build a finite oracle by expanding (M6), supplying it with the population residuals and every population contraction in these expansions. It starts from the same finite first-layer initialization and the same \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) as GD, but has \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\). Every oracle instruction now has deterministic scalar coefficients. The fixed finite Gaussian theorem applies jointly to all its nodes and proves convergence of all the needed empirical contractions and second moments.\\n\\nThere is a useful distinction between oracle nodes and an actual finite network state. Construct proxy parameter arrays using its first-layer nodes and readout, and the matrix\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k,\\\\mathrm{proxy}}^{(2)}\\n=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k,b}r_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(2)}\\n(h_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{M12}\\n\\\\]\\nApplying this proxy matrix to a current oracle activation differs from its prescribed oracle preactivation only by sums of empirical-minus-population contractions. For example each forward discrepancy has the form\\n\\\\[\\n-2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(2)}\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\frac{(h_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)})^\\\\top\\nh_{a,k,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)}}{n}\\n-\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{M13}\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose discrepancy has the corresponding contraction of two \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) fields. At a fixed mesh there are finitely many such terms. Their scalar errors tend to zero, and their vector norms stay bounded. The actual network quantities recomputed from the proxy arrays therefore differ from the oracle nodes by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) in root mean square. Bounded readout and the operator bound justify each subsequent forward/backward comparison. In particular its recomputed vector field differs from its prescribed oracle grid velocity by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\).\\n\\nFor each fixed threshold \\\\(R\\\\), the oracle's empirical squared tails converge to their population counterparts. One may use continuous upper cutoffs, equal to one beyond \\\\(R\\\\) and zero below \\\\(R-1\\\\), to avoid discontinuities. The fixed finite Gaussian theorem applies to these quadratic-growth measurements. Consequently the root-mean-square tail of the recomputed proxy backward field has limit superior at most \\\\(C e^{-cR^2}\\\\), after modifying constants. This is proved for the reference oracle, not assumed for actual GD.\\n\\nLinearly interpolate the proxy parameters. Their metric speed is bounded in probability, with limiting bound independent of the mesh, by (M4) and the node consistency just proved. Their between-grid vector-field defect is bounded by (M10), plus a fixed-mesh \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\). Compare the actual GD interpolant to this proxy. Actual GD's derivative is its vector field at the preceding fine grid point; its state changes by \\\\(O(\\\\eta_n)\\\\) inside a fine step. Apply (M9) with the proxy as reference. No tail bound for the actual GD is needed. Gronwall gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t),\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{proxy},\\\\Delta}(t))\\n\\\\le C e^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\bigg(\\n&\\\\frac{\\\\|W_{n,0}^{(3)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n+(1+R)(\\\\eta_n+\\\\Delta)\\\\\\\\\\n&+e^{-cR^2}+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\n\\\\bigg).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) here is at fixed \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\). Bounds on the actual readout supremum are obtained from its update, not from root-mean-square closeness.\\n\\nThe initialization discrepancy vanishes, and \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). First take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\); next take \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\to0\\\\); finally take \\\\(R\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). Together with (M11), the \",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":40833}}]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-9f9282f0-866b-43ac-ad12-f418d5543551\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n 'rho|two|both|pair|G\\\\\\\\\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\{-1\\\\\\\\}|2k|4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Delta|sum_\\\\\\\\{[ab]=1\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\\\\"'^2|Let y|Scope'\\\"' /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"11:Because the norms agree, the conditions on \\\\(x_a,x_b\\\\) are exactly pairwise nonparallelity. The \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) matrix \\\\(G\\\\) is positive semidefinite with diagonal one. It may be singular. All input indices and input sums below range over \\\\(1,2,3\\\\).\\n16:On a possibly smaller interval \\\\((0,T_*]\\\\), each input's preactivations move in both hidden layers, all three \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) kernel blocks are positive definite, the total kernel is nonconstant, loss strictly decreases, and \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) has positive best-affine-fit error on each hidden preactivation distribution.\\n18:**Scope:** this is a local-time theorem. A convergence theorem on every finite horizon is not claimed for arbitrary input geometry. \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) is chosen before taking width and learning-rate limits and does not shrink in those limits. The strictly positive feature-learning coefficients and the possibly smaller activity interval \\\\(T_*\\\\) may depend on the fixed input geometry and labels.\\n20:## The finite network and its exact updates\\n33:The summed loss retains the earlier factors of two; using the average loss halves the speed.\\n115:Here \\\\((u\\\\otimes v)V=u\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[vV]\\\\). This definition displays the normalization that replaces the finite factor \\\\(1/n\\\\). First-layer and second-layer coordinates belong to separate populations. \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) acts from the former to the latter, and its adjoint acts in reverse. Every expectation pairs coordinates of the same population.\\n123:For completeness, the underlying conditioning mechanism is as follows. If previous calls have revealed \\\\(W_0^{(2)}V=Y\\\\) and \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R\\\\), then the remaining Gaussian matrix is an independent Gaussian projected onto the directions orthogonal to the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) and \\\\(V\\\\). Its conditional mean obeys both recorded equations. A new product is the response fixed by these equations plus that projected Gaussian applied to the unexplored part of the new input. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the response coefficients. The finite Gaussian theorem makes this induction valid also with dependent query directions. We explicitly rely on this stated finite-program result, not on a claim that the infinite-time or growing-program-length theorem already follows from it.\\n125:These finite limits can be realized on two common population coordinate spaces. Here are the details relevant to the subsequent continuous-time argument. Take all the programs needed for rational meshes, their finite unions, and a countable closure under rational linear combinations, the coordinate operations in the proof, and bounded Lipschitz approximations. Their finite joint laws are consistent, so they can be realized jointly. Take the mean-square closure of their linear spans on each layer. Exact finite linear identities pass to these laws.\\n133:Indeed two \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets of the unit sphere each have at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points, and every fixed bilinear form of the matrix is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). This finite operator inequality and joint second-moment convergence imply\\n142:passes to the limit and identifies the two actions as adjoints. Bounded coordinate multipliers and their required products belong to these spaces by clipping and mean-square approximation. This constructs the common bounded-operator realization used below; it is not an additional assumption.\\n164:The appendix is a derivation, not an assumed light-tail condition. It controls the accumulated deterministic response coefficients on both layers. The second-layer derivatives stay bounded; the first-layer derivatives have a Gaussian exponential bound. A short-time induction closes these bounds uniformly in the number of Euler steps.\\n168:For comparing two population states use\\n219:The same localization gives the comparison inequality for two meshes. Integration and the elementary Gronwall inequality yield, for \\\\(0<T\\\\le T_0\\\\),\\n227:First send both meshes to zero with \\\\(R\\\\) fixed. Then send \\\\(R\\\\) to infinity. The term \\\\(e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) vanishes, so the Euler paths are Cauchy uniformly in time in (M8).\\n239:There is a useful distinction between oracle nodes and an actual finite network state. Construct proxy parameter arrays using its first-layer nodes and readout, and the matrix\\n257:The transpose discrepancy has the corresponding contraction of two \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) fields. At a fixed mesh there are finitely many such terms. Their scalar errors tend to zero, and their vector norms stay bounded. The actual network quantities recomputed from the proxy arrays therefore differ from the oracle nodes by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) in root mean square. Bounded readout and the operator bound justify each subsequent forward/backward comparison. In particular its recomputed vector field differs from its prescribed oracle grid velocity by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\).\\n283:The reference first-layer velocity is a finite linear combination of bounded multiples of the backward fields, hence has the same tail control. Applying the same comparison proves convergence of both hidden velocities in integrated mean square and hence of their integrated squared norms.\\n285:To obtain path-law convergence, first use the finite Gaussian theorem on any fixed finite time grid. Between two times separated by at most \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\), any absolutely continuous coordinate path satisfies\\n294:We have proved existence, uniqueness, and convergence without needing \\\\(G\\\\) positive definite. For strict feature learning, two additional arguments replace the two-input proof's full-support and exchange-symmetry shortcuts.\\n301:We first prove \\\\(Q\\\\) positive definite even if \\\\(G\\\\) has rank two. If \\\\(c^\\\\top Qc=0\\\\), then\\n312:Each is a proper hyperplane because the inputs are nonzero and no two equal up to sign. A finite union of proper hyperplanes cannot cover \\\\(\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\). Hence the numbers \\\\(b_a=v^\\\\top x_a/\\\\sqrt d\\\\) are nonzero and their squares are distinct. Restrict the identity to \\\\(w=tv\\\\). The coefficients of \\\\(t,t^3,t^5\\\\) in\\n335:Conditional on the last two second-layer coordinates, \\\\(S\\\\) is a strictly monotone function of the first coordinate. That first coordinate has a continuous conditional Gaussian density, so \\\\(\\\\mathbb P(S=0)=0\\\\). If \\\\(\\\\sum_ac_aU_a=0\\\\), division by \\\\(S\\\\), full support, and continuity imply\\n336:\\\\(\\\\sum_ac_a\\\\phi'(s_a)=0\\\\) for every \\\\(s\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^3\\\\). Varying one coordinate while fixing the other two forces each \\\\(c_a=0\\\\). Therefore \\\\(V\\\\succ0\\\\).\\n403:Here \\\\(\\\\gamma_a\\\\sim N(0,1)\\\\) is independent of the previous second-layer history. To see the variance, the remaining finite Gaussian matrix is projected orthogonally to the three observed input directions on each side. Applying it to the part of \\\\(J_a\\\\) perpendicular to the old \\\\(H\\\\) inputs gives variance \\\\(\\\\|J_a^\\\\perp\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\). The output projection has fixed rank three, so its removal changes normalized mean square by \\\\(O(1/n)\\\\). Joint empirical convergence then gives (M18b). The response coefficients follow from the two recorded matrix identities. Both Gram inverses are legitimate because \\\\(Q,V\\\\succ0\\\\). One such calculation is made for each \\\\(a\\\\); independence of the three \\\\(\\\\gamma_a\\\\) from one another is neither claimed nor needed.\\n427:For example the readout integral divided by \\\\(t\\\\) converges to \\\\(2S\\\\), then the second-layer backward field divided by \\\\(t\\\\) converges to \\\\(2U_a\\\\). Integrating the first-layer and matrix velocities therefore gives the factors \\\\(2t^2\\\\). Expanding \\\\(W^{(2)}H_a^{(1)}\\\\) gives the two terms in \\\\(R_a\\\\). Each passage uses bounded \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\), the bounded matrix action, and strong mean-square continuity. Differentiating the activation along these strongly convergent mean-square increments is justified by dominated convergence against their fixed limiting direction; no global differentiability claim for nonlinear maps on the whole mean-square space is used.\\n458:Expanding \\\\(H_a^{(2)}(t)\\\\) using (M19) shows that the readout block contributes \\\\(4(A_1+A_2)t^2\\\\) to its quadratic form in direction \\\\(y\\\\). The other two blocks contribute the same amount. Therefore\\n464:The total kernel is nonconstant. The first two blocks are positive definite at sufficiently small positive times by (M20). The initial readout block is positive definite because the second-layer Gaussian triple has full support, and the three coordinate arctangents have no linear relation. It remains positive definite by continuity.\\n509:Introduce two centered jointly Gaussian collections \\\\(\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\), independent of one another. The first-layer root is independent of both. Their covariances are\\n516:These are separate-layer probability spaces; taking them independent on a product space is only a convenient representation, not an assertion of a natural pairing of neurons across layers.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf3bfde1887d29ca5174f318085e6\",\"summary\":[\"**Fixing average-loss division**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-c775d8a4-d486-4691-ae8f-b6313e99a6ab\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,130p' /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Three inputs: a rigorous local-time joint width and gradient-flow limit\\n\\n## Result and scope\\n\\nFix three inputs \\\\(x_1,x_2,x_3\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a\\\\|_2^2}{d}=1,\\\\qquad\\nx_a\\\\ne x_b,\\\\quad x_a\\\\ne -x_b\\\\quad(a\\\\ne b),\\\\qquad\\nG_{ab}=\\\\frac{x_a^\\\\top x_b}{d}.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause the norms agree, the conditions on \\\\(x_a,x_b\\\\) are exactly pairwise nonparallelity. The \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) matrix \\\\(G\\\\) is positive semidefinite with diagonal one. It may be singular. All input indices and input sums below range over \\\\(1,2,3\\\\).\\nLet \\\\(y_1,y_2,y_3\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence of positive steps \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all such triples and all eight label sign patterns. Moreover, the existence time (T_0) can be chosen uniformly over these triples and labels: its construction uses only three inputs, (|G_{ab}|le1), the fixed activation and initialization scales. It does not depend on width, step size, input dimension, or a lower bound on a Gram eigenvalue.\\n\\nPredictions, residuals, loss, and all entries of all three kernel blocks converge in probability uniformly on this interval. The joint three-input hidden path laws converge with their second moments in the uniform path norm. Integrated squared hidden velocities converge. The joint empirical averages of any fixed finite collection of continuous, globally Lipschitz forward/adjoint probes, with bounded products when a product instruction is used, converge to their corresponding population expectations, uniformly in time. Unbounded first-layer backward fields and their quadratic contractions are included by the tail argument below.\\n\\nOn a possibly smaller interval \\\\((0,T_*]\\\\), each input's preactivations move in both hidden layers, all three \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) kernel blocks are positive definite, the total kernel is nonconstant, loss strictly decreases, and \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) has positive best-affine-fit error on each hidden preactivation distribution.\\n\\n**Scope:** this is a local-time theorem. A convergence theorem on every finite horizon is not claimed for arbitrary input geometry. \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) is chosen before taking width and learning-rate limits and does not shrink in those limits. The strictly positive feature-learning coefficients and the possibly smaller activity interval \\\\(T_*\\\\) may depend on the fixed input geometry and labels.\\n\\n## The finite network and its exact updates\\n\\nThe dimensions are \\\\(W^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times d}\\\\), \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\). For input \\\\(a=1,2,3\\\\), use\\n\\\\[\\nz_a^{(1)}=\\\\frac{W^{(1)}x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nz_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi=\\\\arctan,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h_a^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad\\nr_{n,a}=f_{n,a}-y_a,\\\\qquad L_n=\\\\sum_{a=1}^3r_{n,a}^2.\\n\\\\]\\nThe summed loss retains the earlier factors of two; using the average loss halves the speed.\\n\\nAll initialization entries are independent:\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ij}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently each first-layer root triple is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), independently over neuron index \\\\(i\\\\). The three preactivations for one neuron may be correlated and may satisfy an exact linear relation. This is permitted.\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta_a^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_{n,a}/\\\\partial z_a^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\).\\nIn the displayed rescaled parameterization the layer learning-rate multipliers are \\\\(n,1,n\\\\). The exact first-weight update is\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n=W_k^{(1)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{\\\\sqrt d}\\n\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)}x_b^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\nMultiplication by \\\\(x_a/\\\\sqrt d\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=z_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{n}\\n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)} .\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M1}\\n\\\\]\\nUse physical time \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), linearly interpolate these parameters, and recompute the forward pass at intermediate times.\\n\\nThe whole effect of input geometry on first-layer training is (G): training on input (b) moves input (a)'s preactivation with coefficient (G_{ab}). If (Gc=0), then (sum_a c_a z_a^{(1)}=0) initially and every update preserves this relation. No inverse of (G) is used.\\n\\nThe finite continuous flow has the kernel blocks\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}_{n,ab}\\n=G_{ab}\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(1)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(1)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(2)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(1)})^\\\\top h_b^{(1)}}n\\n \\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(2)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(3)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(2)})^\\\\top h_b^{(2)}}n .\\n\\\\tag{M2}\\n\\\\]\\nDifferentiation gives \\\\(\\\\dot f_n=-2K_n r_n\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\dot L_n=-4r_n^\\\\top K_n r_n\\\\), where \\\\(K_n\\\\) is the sum of the blocks. Each block is positive semidefinite: it is the Gram matrix of the corresponding scaled parameter gradients. Individual residual magnitudes need not decrease; their squared sum does.\\n\\n## Why a new stability estimate is needed\\n\\nWith \\\\(F(s)=s+s^3/3\\\\), the componentwise transformation \\\\(X_a^{(1)}=F(Z_a^{(1)})\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot X_a^{(1)}\\n=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\n\\\\frac{1+(Z_a^{(1)})^2}{1+(Z_b^{(1)})^2}\\n(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{M3}\\n\\\\]\\nIf the inputs are mutually orthogonal, only diagonal terms occur and the ratios cancel. Otherwise nonzero cross-input terms remain and their ratios are unbounded. We therefore work with the original preactivations and prove a uniform tail estimate for the backward fields.\\n\\nThe exact limiting equations will be\\n\\\\[\\nH_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\nf_a=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H_a^{(2)}],\\\\quad r_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot Z_a^{(1)}&=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\\\delta_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_b\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_bH_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M4}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\((u\\\\otimes v)V=u\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[vV]\\\\). This definition displays the normalization that replaces the finite factor \\\\(1/n\\\\). First-layer and second-layer coordinates belong to separate populations. \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) acts from the former to the latter, and its adjoint acts in reverse. Every expectation pairs coordinates of the same population.\\n\\nThe initial root triple is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), the population readout is zero, and the initial action \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) is the joint forward-and-transpose limit of the initial Gaussian matrix, constructed next. The state consists of three first-layer fields and the shared matrix action and readout field. It has finitely many kinds of state objects, not finitely many scalar degrees of freedom.\\n\\n## The fixed-mesh construction and the new tail lemma\\n\\nWe use the following finite Gaussian fact. For a fixed finite calculation from iid jointly Gaussian coordinate tuples, independent of a Gaussian matrix of entry variance \\\\(1/n\\\\), with smooth polynomially bounded coordinate functions and finitely many calls to that matrix and its transpose, every same-layer joint empirical average of a continuous polynomially bounded measurement has a deterministic limit. The limiting action is Gaussian randomness plus the responses forced by earlier opposite-direction calls. The precise response coefficients and their derivative convention are given and used in the appendix below. This is the smooth specialization of Theorem 2.10 and Box 1 of [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685). Its hypotheses hold here: after fixing population scalar coefficients, each instruction and each derivative needed below is a polynomially bounded function of finitely many Gaussian variables. The initial tuple covariance may be singular; represent it as a fixed linear image of independent Gaussian roots. No claim of iid finite coordinates after matrix reuse is used.\\n\\nFor completeness, the underlying conditioning mechanism is as follows. If previous calls have revealed \\\\(W_0^{(2)}V=Y\\\\) and \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R\\\\), then the remaining Gaussian matrix is an independent Gaussian projected onto the directions orthogonal to the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) and \\\\(V\\\\). Its conditional mean obeys both recorded equations. A new product is the response fixed by these equations plus that projected Gaussian applied to the unexplored part of the new input. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the response coefficients. The finite Gaussian theorem makes this induction valid also with dependent query directions. We explicitly rely on this stated finite-program result, not on a claim that the infinite-time or growing-program-length theorem already follows from it.\\n\\nThese finite limits can be realized on two common population coordinate spaces. Here are the details relevant to the subsequent continuous-time argument. Take all the programs needed for rational meshes, their finite unions, and a countable closure under rational linear combinations, the coordinate operations in the proof, and bounded Lipschitz approximations. Their finite joint laws are consistent, so they can be realized jointly. Take the mean-square closure of their linear spans on each layer. Exact finite linear identities pass to these laws.\\n\\nA fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\) satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le2\\\\,9^{2n}e^{-nM^2/8}\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-136302b9-ae12-43ef-ac31-6534f6695ce6\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '475,685p' /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"\\nThe following calculation supplies (M7). Its equation numbers are local to the appendix.\\n\\nLet the input Gram matrix be\\n\\\\[\\nG_{ab}=x_a^\\\\top x_b/d,\\\\qquad G\\\\succeq0,\\\\quad G_{aa}=1,\\\\quad |G_{ab}|\\\\le1,\\n\\\\]\\nand labels be \\\\(y_1,y_2,y_3\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). Use the summed loss \\\\(\\\\sum_{a=1}^3r_a^2\\\\); averaging the loss only rescales time. The first-layer population root \\\\((Z_{1,0}^{(1)},Z_{2,0}^{(1)},Z_{3,0}^{(1)})\\\\) is centered Gaussian with covariance \\\\(G\\\\). The shared readout starts at \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\).\\n\\nFor fixed mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta>0\\\\), population Euler obeys\\n\\\\[\\nH_{a,k}^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_{a,k}^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(2)}H_{a,k}^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_{a,k}^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\n\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_{a,k}^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nr_{a,k}=\\\\mathbb E[W_k^{(3)}H_{a,k}^{(2)}]-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k+1}^{(1)}=Z_{a,k}^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^3G_{ab}r_{b,k}\\\\phi'(Z_{b,k}^{(1)})P_{b,k},\\n\\\\qquad P_{b,k}:=(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}=W_k^{(2)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_{b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,k}^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_{b,k}H_{b,k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\phi=\\\\arctan\\\\). All response derivatives below hold every deterministic coefficient in these finite recursions fixed, including residuals, contractions, and response coefficients. They do not differentiate through expectations or through the covariance law.\\n\\n## Exact finite-mesh Gaussian response representation\\n\\nIntroduce two centered jointly Gaussian collections \\\\(\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\), independent of one another. The first-layer root is independent of both. Their covariances are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\xi_{b,s}]=\\\\mathbb E[H_{a,k}^{(1)}H_{b,s}^{(1)}],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\eta_{b,s}]=\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}].\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThese are separate-layer probability spaces; taking them independent on a product space is only a convenient representation, not an assertion of a natural pairing of neurons across layers.\\n\\nDefine the deterministic coefficients\\n\\\\[\\nA_{ak,bs}:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}}{\\\\partial\\\\xi_{b,s}}\\\\right],\\\\quad s\\\\le k,\\n\\\\qquad\\nC_{ak,bs}:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial H_{a,k}^{(1)}}{\\\\partial\\\\eta_{b,s}}\\\\right],\\\\quad s<k.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nThe derivatives are symbolic derivatives of the displayed causal recursions, treating Gaussian slots as separate real arguments even when their realized joint covariance is singular. Then the exact limiting initial-matrix actions are\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}=\\n\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s<k}C_{ak,bs}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\n\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}A_{ak,bs}H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nExpanding the learned matrix gives the full equations\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\n\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s<k}\\n\\\\left(C_{ak,bs}-2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\\\right)\\n\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nP_{a,k}=\\n\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}\\n\\\\left(A_{ak,bs}-\\\\mathbf1_{s<k}2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}]\\\\right)\\nH_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nTogether with (1), the readout update in (2), and \\\\(\\\\delta=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)})\\\\), these are causal finite recursions. To construct step \\\\(k\\\\): first construct \\\\(H_{a,k}^{(1)}\\\\) and its derivatives using \\\\(\\\\eta\\\\)-slots through \\\\(k-1\\\\); next construct \\\\(Z_{a,k}^{(2)},\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\) using \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\)-slots through \\\\(k\\\\); finally construct \\\\(P_{a,k}\\\\) and update first-layer coordinates and readout. The Gaussian covariance matrices extend consistently because they are Gram matrices of the constructed source variables.\\n\\nWhy the Gaussian response rule has this form: conditioning on previous forward and transpose products gives the Gaussian matrix regression formula with a projected fresh Gaussian matrix. In the limit, its Gaussian parts in each orientation have covariance equal to the Gram matrix of the sources. Its non-Gaussian part lies in the span of the previous sources in the opposite orientation. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the coefficients of this response as the expectations in (4). The elementary identity used is, for a centered Gaussian vector \\\\(g\\\\) with covariance \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[g_i f(g)]=\\\\sum_j\\\\Sigma_{ij}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\partial_j f(g)].\\n\\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nIt is valid even for singular \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\), by writing \\\\(g=B\\\\gamma\\\\) and integrating by parts in independent standard Gaussian coordinates \\\\(\\\\gamma\\\\). If the conditional regression uses a pseudoinverse, the symbolic derivative coefficients and the pseudoinverse coefficients can differ only by a vector in the covariance nullspace; the corresponding combination of source variables has zero squared norm, so the response itself is unchanged. This is the singular-covariance issue that must be retained in a complete proof.\\n\\nFor a rigorous external formulation of this finite-program identity, see [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685), Box 1, Remarks 2.11–2.12 and Theorem 2.10. The algebraic recursions here are smooth, polynomially bounded functions of finitely many Gaussian slots, and their symbolic derivatives are polynomially bounded for fixed program length. Thus all expectations in (4) exist. The finite-query theorem handles fixed program length; the estimates below are the additional ingredient needed uniformly as length increases. This note does not purport to reprove the entire master theorem in the preceding paragraph.\\n\\n## Uniform short-time response bounds\\n\\nFix \\\\(T\\\\le1\\\\) and \\\\(k\\\\Delta\\\\le T\\\\). Constants in this section depend only on bounds for \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi',\\\\phi''\\\\), on the bounds \\\\(|G_{ab}|\\\\le1\\\\) and the fixed number three of inputs; they do not depend on \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), \\\\(k\\\\), or the Gaussian covariance ranks.\\n\\nPut \\\\(B_k=\\\\|W_k^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\). Since \\\\(|r_{a,k}|\\\\le1+\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty B_k\\\\), (2) gives\\n\\\\[\\nB_{k+1}\\\\le B_k+6\\\\Delta\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty(1+\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty B_k).\\n\\\\]\\nThe resulting geometric sum gives, for a fixed \\\\(C_0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nB_k\\\\le C_0T,\\\\qquad |r_{a,k}|\\\\le C_0,\\\\qquad |\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}|\\\\le C_0T.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\nThe absolute row sums of the training-memory coefficients in (7) and (8) are therefore at most \\\\(C_0T\\\\) and \\\\(C_0T^3\\\\), respectively.\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A_k=\\\\max_{a,\\\\,r\\\\le k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s\\\\le r}|A_{ar,bs}|,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathcal C_k=\\\\max_{a,\\\\,r\\\\le k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\\\sum_{s<r}|C_{ar,bs}|.\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (8) already gives the pathwise bound\\n\\\\[\\n|P_{a,k}-\\\\eta_{a,k}|\\\\le C_0(\\\\mathcal A_k+T^3),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\eta_{a,k}^2]\\\\le C_0^2T^2.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFirst estimate \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\) in terms of \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\). Let \\\\(J_k\\\\) be the maximum, over the first-layer coordinates at times through \\\\(k\\\\), of the sum of absolute derivatives with respect to all backward Gaussian slots \\\\(\\\\eta_{b,s}\\\\). Initially \\\\(J_0=0\\\\). In (8), the derivative of \\\\(P_{a,k}\\\\) has absolute sum at most\\n\\\\[\\n1+(\\\\mathcal A_k+C_0T^3)J_k,\\n\\\\]\\nbecause \\\\(|\\\\phi'|\\\\le1\\\\). Differentiating (1) and using bounded \\\\(G,r,\\\\phi',\\\\phi''\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\nJ_{k+1}\\\\le\\n\\\\left(1+C\\\\Delta\\\\left[1+\\\\mathcal A_k+\\\\sum_{b=1}^3|\\\\eta_{b,k}|\\\\right]\\\\right)J_k+C\\\\Delta.\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently,\\n\\\\[\\nJ_k\\\\le CT\\\\exp\\\\left(CT(1+\\\\mathcal A_{k-1})+C\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^3|\\\\eta_{b,r}|\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\nNo independence over time is used. Jensen's inequality applied to the average of the \\\\(3k\\\\) terms, followed by the scalar Gaussian exponential bound, gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\left(C\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^3|\\\\eta_{b,r}|\\\\right)\\n\\\\le 2\\\\exp\\\\left(\\\\frac92 C^2C_0^2T^4\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\nSince absolute expectation is bounded by expectation of absolute value, (4), (14), and (15) imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\le CT\\\\exp\\\\left(CT(1+\\\\mathcal A_{k-1})+CT^4\\\\right),\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\nafter increasing \\\\(C\\\\).\\n\\nNow estimate \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\) through the second-layer recursion. Let \\\\(V_k\\\\) be the maximum, over \\\\(Z_{a,r}^{(2)}\\\\) for \\\\(r\\\\le k\\\\), of the sum of absolute derivatives with respect to all forward Gaussian slots \\\\(\\\\xi_{b,s}\\\\). It is a nonnegative random variable, but the bound below is deterministic.\\n\\nThe readout is\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(3)}=-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{a=1}^3r_{a,r}\\\\phi(Z_{a,r}^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nTherefore its derivative absolute sum is at most \\\\(C_0T V_k\\\\). Differentiating \\\\(\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_{a,k}^{(2)})\\\\), using (10), yields\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{b,s}\\\\left|\\\\frac{\\\\partial\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}}{\\\\partial\\\\xi_{b,s}}\\\\right|\\\\le C_1T V_k.\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (7), whose memory uses only \\\\(s<k\\\\), yields\\n\\\\[\\nV_k\\\\le \\\\max\\\\{V_{k-1},\\\\,1+C_1T(\\\\mathcal C_k+C_0T)V_{k-1}\\\\}.\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\nInitially \\\\(V_0=1\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_0=0\\\\).\\n\\nChoose fixed constants \\\\(M_A=2C_1\\\\) and \\\\(M_C\\\\) large enough that the right side of (16) is at most \\\\(M_CT\\\\) whenever \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\le M_AT\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\le T_0\\\\). All these constants can be chosen independently of the input geometry because only \\\\(|G_{ab}|\\\\le1\\\\) was used. Then choose \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) small enough that\\n\\\\[\\nC_1T_0^2(M_C+C_0)\\\\le\\\\tfrac12.\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\nAn induction over \\\\(k\\\\) now proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\le M_AT,\\\\qquad \\\\mathcal C_k\\\\le M_CT,\\\\qquad V_k\\\\le2,\\n\\\\qquad k\\\\Delta\\\\le T\\\\le T_0.\\n\\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed the bound for \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\) supplies the bound for \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\) in (16); (18)-(19) then give \\\\(V_k\\\\le2\\\\); (17) gives \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\le2C_1T\\\\).\\n\\nCombining (12) and (20), every backward coordinate is a centered Gaussian of variance at most \\\\(C T^2\\\\), plus a bounded (possibly dependent) remainder of absolute value at most \\\\(CT\\\\). Thus there are fixed \\\\(c,C>0\\\\) such that\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta>0}\\\\sup_{k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\\\max_a\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\left(c|P_{a,k}|^2/T_0^2\\\\right)\\\\le C.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is the desired mesh-uniform Gaussian-tail bound. It was proved from bounded second-layer coordinate derivatives and a short-time response bootstrap. It was not assumed merely from fixed-program moment convergence.\\n\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf3c9fdd887d288c65c5a377007a0\",\"summary\":[\"**Fixing escaped JS paragraphs**\",\"**Patching probe closure scope**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-42307a6d-9be2-49c0-af93-e905336fb236\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -11,3 +11,3 @@\\n Because the norms agree, the conditions on \\\\(x_a,x_b\\\\) are exactly pairwise nonparallelity. The \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) matrix \\\\(G\\\\) is positive semidefinite with diagonal one. It may be singular. All input indices and input sums below range over \\\\(1,2,3\\\\).\\n-Let \\\\(y_1,y_2,y_3\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence of positive steps \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all such triples and all eight label sign patterns. Moreover, the existence time (T_0) can be chosen uniformly over these triples and labels: its construction uses only three inputs, (|G_{ab}|le1), the fixed activation and initialization scales. It does not depend on width, step size, input dimension, or a lower bound on a Gram eigenvalue.\\n+Let \\\\(y_1,y_2,y_3\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence of positive steps \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all such triples and all eight label sign patterns. Moreover, the existence time \\\\(T_0\\\\) can be chosen uniformly over these triples and labels: its construction uses only three inputs, \\\\(|G_{ab}|\\\\le1\\\\), and the fixed activation and initialization scales. It does not depend on width, step size, input dimension, or a lower bound on a Gram eigenvalue.\\n \\n@@ -15,2 +15,4 @@\\n \\n+Here a fixed action probe is a finite calculation from the current fields and constants using fixed globally Lipschitz coordinate functions, linear combinations, and appropriately typed calls to \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) and its adjoint. A product instruction is allowed when both varying factors are uniformly bounded, for example after clipping. Final same-layer joint measurements can be continuous with at most quadratic growth. Probe length and functions do not grow with width or depend on the learning rate.\\n+\\n On a possibly smaller interval \\\\((0,T_*]\\\\), each input's preactivations move in both hidden layers, all three \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) kernel blocks are positive definite, the total kernel is nonconstant, loss strictly decreases, and \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) has positive best-affine-fit error on each hidden preactivation distribution.\\n@@ -32,3 +34,3 @@\\n \\\\]\\n-The summed loss retains the earlier factors of two; using the average loss halves the speed.\\n+The summed loss retains the earlier factors of two; using the average of the three losses divides the speed by three.\\n \\n@@ -69,3 +71,3 @@\\n \\n-The whole effect of input geometry on first-layer training is (G): training on input (b) moves input (a)'s preactivation with coefficient (G_{ab}). If (Gc=0), then (sum_a c_a z_a^{(1)}=0) initially and every update preserves this relation. No inverse of (G) is used.\\n+The whole effect of input geometry on first-layer training is \\\\(G\\\\): training on input \\\\(b\\\\) moves input \\\\(a\\\\)'s preactivation with coefficient \\\\(G_{ab}\\\\). If \\\\(Gc=0\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\sum_a c_a z_a^{(1)}=0\\\\) initially and every update preserves this relation. No inverse of \\\\(G\\\\) is used.\\n \\n@@ -657,2 +659 @@\\n This is the desired mesh-uniform Gaussian-tail bound. It was proved from bounded second-layer coordinate derivatives and a short-time response bootstrap. It was not assumed merely from fixed-program moment convergence.\\n-\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-8d635236-bf42-41d2-8067-a81a8f9fcaa0\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'wc -l -w /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"  659  5072 /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf3e1c3ac87d284286906eedcd35a\",\"summary\":[\"**Patching theorem wording**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-9db1cd6d-68cb-49e2-b8d4-f5c0a212142b\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '120,125p' /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"\\n## The fixed-mesh construction and the new tail lemma\\n\\nWe use the following finite Gaussian fact. For a fixed finite calculation from iid jointly Gaussian coordinate tuples, independent of a Gaussian matrix of entry variance \\\\(1/n\\\\), with smooth polynomially bounded coordinate functions and finitely many calls to that matrix and its transpose, every same-layer joint empirical average of a continuous polynomially bounded measurement has a deterministic limit. The limiting action is Gaussian randomness plus the responses forced by earlier opposite-direction calls. The precise response coefficients and their derivative convention are given and used in the appendix below. This is the smooth specialization of Theorem 2.10 and Box 1 of [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685). Its hypotheses hold here: after fixing population scalar coefficients, each instruction and each derivative needed below is a polynomially bounded function of finitely many Gaussian variables. The initial tuple covariance may be singular; represent it as a fixed linear image of independent Gaussian roots. No claim of iid finite coordinates after matrix reuse is used.\\n\\nFor completeness, the underlying conditioning mechanism is as follows. If previous calls have revealed \\\\(W_0^{(2)}V=Y\\\\) and \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R\\\\), then the remaining Gaussian matrix is an independent Gaussian projected onto the directions orthogonal to the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) and \\\\(V\\\\). Its conditional mean obeys both recorded equations. A new product is the response fixed by these equations plus that projected Gaussian applied to the unexplored part of the new input. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the response coefficients. The finite Gaussian theorem makes this induction valid also with dependent query directions. We explicitly rely on this stated finite-program result, not on a claim that the infinite-time or growing-program-length theorem already follows from it.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-72fe2546-6117-472f-ad6b-33fb726d5f2b\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -122,3 +122,3 @@\\n \\n-We use the following finite Gaussian fact. For a fixed finite calculation from iid jointly Gaussian coordinate tuples, independent of a Gaussian matrix of entry variance \\\\(1/n\\\\), with smooth polynomially bounded coordinate functions and finitely many calls to that matrix and its transpose, every same-layer joint empirical average of a continuous polynomially bounded measurement has a deterministic limit. The limiting action is Gaussian randomness plus the responses forced by earlier opposite-direction calls. The precise response coefficients and their derivative convention are given and used in the appendix below. This is the smooth specialization of Theorem 2.10 and Box 1 of [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685). Its hypotheses hold here: after fixing population scalar coefficients, each instruction and each derivative needed below is a polynomially bounded function of finitely many Gaussian variables. The initial tuple covariance may be singular; represent it as a fixed linear image of independent Gaussian roots. No claim of iid finite coordinates after matrix reuse is used.\\n+We use the following finite Gaussian fact. For a fixed finite calculation from iid jointly Gaussian coordinate tuples, independent of a Gaussian matrix of entry variance \\\\(1/n\\\\), with polynomially bounded coordinate functions and finitely many calls to that matrix and its transpose, every same-layer joint empirical average of a continuous polynomially bounded measurement has a deterministic limit. The limiting action is Gaussian randomness plus the responses forced by earlier opposite-direction calls. The precise response coefficients and their derivative convention are given and used in the appendix below. This is the specialization of Theorem 2.10 and Box 1 of [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685). Its hypotheses hold here: after fixing population scalar coefficients, each instruction is polynomially bounded, and the training instructions whose derivatives are used below are smooth with polynomially bounded derivatives, as functions of finitely many Gaussian variables. The initial tuple covariance may be singular; represent it as a fixed linear image of independent Gaussian roots. No claim of iid finite coordinates after matrix reuse is used.\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf3e9ad3487d2a0ffa13fc4635830\",\"summary\":[\"**Auditing follow-up work**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_dp0YstdibZhBJk7qjSXZndhI\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-5f05-7041-af97-2caadcc2099b\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_a\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_ce3pep7VD7uP3gB8Lqzhj1Oz\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_QAiDzzBPndMjdJUuUF6XTLmq\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-9cde-7bd2-a31d-1f5d3d5717c2\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_c\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf40025e887d2942ef2f3f381ae91\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying uniform derivative bounds**\",\"**Checking theorem conditions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf410a9c887d2bba4101713e897e7\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying the kernel-rank proof**\",\"**Verifying fresh-noise independence**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf41e91e887d2b3a35f0c51f94c24\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking variance lower bounds**\",\"**Checking generic projection v**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf430e37c87d28aa705a068385d63\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking Gaussian residual proof**\",\"**Verifying uniform tail control**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07131-113d-7da2-90c5-b2ac2b61db23\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-9cde-7bd2-a31d-1f5d3d5717c2\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_c\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf4406fd087d2ae22bad7924f40de\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying gradient-flow equations**\",\"**Checking the T0 bound**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf452de5087d2832802cc25f12791\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing feature-dependence update**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf457b24887d2a564f9d6a0aa8097\",\"text\":\"The singular case checks out: a linear relation among the inputs remains a linear relation among their first preactivations, but generally not among their arctangent activations. That lets the proof retain all three training directions. The existence-time bound also uses no minimum angle or Gram eigenvalue, so one common positive \\\\(T_0\\\\) can cover all normalized triples; the amount of feature movement need not have a uniform positive lower bound near parallel configurations.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf457ecec87d28da8afa81510290f\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing three-layer setup**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07130-fcd2-7651-8ad9-04113ba2aee2\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf469ce5487d29c8e3eb497de9779\",\"summary\":[\"**Reading file metadata**\",\"**Checking activity bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf47bba1c87d2941cf522b3a3df3e\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking pairwise angle assumptions**\",\"**Deriving Taylor triple identity**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07130-de36-7a60-9959-088ed5631931\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-5f05-7041-af97-2caadcc2099b\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_a\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf48c55cc87d2a0e3a756f19735af\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving the three-equation determinant**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf49b94cc87d2bbf46651d9fda436\",\"summary\":[\"**Recording audit details**\",\"**Clarifying finite-vector notation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-48b1028c-01bd-4af1-b58b-b81c8a3b1143\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -21,2 +21,4 @@\\n \\n+The inputs are fixed while taking the width and learning-rate limits. A common existence interval across configurations is not a claim of a convergence rate uniform over configurations that themselves change with width.\\n+\\n ## The finite network and its exact updates\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-55037834-d00b-4a62-97c5-e896bcfdff39\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_REVIEW.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Three-input proof review record\\n\\nDate: 2026-09-05.\\n\\nReviewed artifact: /tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md.\\n\\nClaim: a local autonomous joint width/GD-step limit for three fixed normalized pairwise nonparallel inputs, arbitrary labels in {-1,1}, and any positive eta_n tending to zero. Singular input Gram matrices are explicitly included. The existence interval can be chosen uniformly over normalized triples, but no uniform convergence rate over width-dependent geometries is claimed. Strict feature-learning coefficients can depend on the fixed geometry.\\n\\nThree independent adversarial reviewers were supplied the complete three-input proof and its cited primary reference. They were not supplied the exploratory derivations or one another's conclusions. All three returned PASS:\\n\\n- Reviewer A checked singular geometry, the common population operator construction, uniform time bootstrap, actual-GD transfer, and the new per-input Gaussian-innovation argument.\\n- Reviewer B checked the Vandermonde feature-independence argument, the 3k-term Gaussian bound, probe conditions, ordered limits, and per-input activity.\\n- Reviewer C checked the conditional Gaussian variance and response coefficients in M18a–M18c, positive kernel blocks with singular G, all leading time coefficients, and uniform existence time.\\n\\nAll explicitly verified that the proof does not rely on a two-input exchange symmetry or on invertibility of the input Gram matrix. All found the factors in the three-input updates and activity expansions consistent.\\n\\nNo material fixes were requested. A final scope sentence clarifies that a common existence interval does not assert a convergence rate uniform over geometries changing with width.\\n\\nThese mathematical reviews are not formal proof-assistant verification. They apply to the local theorem; a global-in-time theorem for arbitrary input geometry is not established.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf4b24e8487d2a299920f6b372569\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing the proof setup**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf4c223ec87d280b97d554bda63d9\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf4cb2aa087d2b750039c7154a92a\",\"text\":\"Yes—exactly. **\\\\(T_0\\\\) is a fixed positive amount of physical time**, chosen before taking either limit. It is not a sequence of intervals shrinking with \\\\(n\\\\) or \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\). Indeed, the number of GD updates covered by that interval grows:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\lfloor\\\\frac{T_0}{\\\\eta_n}\\\\right\\\\rfloor\\\\longrightarrow\\\\infty.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAnd **the same local result extends to three inputs under precisely your assumptions**, including configurations where the three inputs are linearly dependent.\\n\\nThe [complete three-input proof is here](/tmp/THREE_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md). Below are the statement and the substantive changes.\\n\\n## The three-input result\\n\\nFix \\\\(x_1,x_2,x_3\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), with\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a\\\\|_2^2}{d}=1,\\n\\\\qquad\\nx_a\\\\ne \\\\pm x_b\\\\quad(a\\\\ne b),\\n\\\\qquad\\ny_a\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDefine their normalized dot-product matrix by\\n\\n\\\\[\\nG_{ab}=\\\\frac{x_a^\\\\top x_b}{d},\\n\\\\qquad a,b\\\\in\\\\{1,2,3\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nKeep the same network and initialization:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_a^{(1)}=\\\\frac{W^{(1)}x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(1)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\nz_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi=\\\\arctan,\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{0,ij}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith independent initialization entries. The weights have dimensions \\\\(n\\\\times d\\\\), \\\\(n\\\\times n\\\\), and \\\\(n\\\\), respectively.\\n\\nUse the summed loss\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}=\\\\frac1n(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h_a^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nr_{n,a}=f_{n,a}-y_a,\\n\\\\qquad\\nL_n=\\\\sum_{a=1}^3r_{n,a}^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAveraging these three losses instead would divide the speed by three.\\n\\nWith the same residual-free backpropagation quantities,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_a^{(2)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe exact GD updates are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=\\nz_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\nG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=\\nW_k^{(2)}\\n-\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{n}\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\nr_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}\\n(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n-\\n2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_{b=1}^3\\nr_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThere exists a fixed \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that, for every sequence of positive learning rates satisfying\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\longrightarrow0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthese networks converge on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\) to a unique autonomous population gradient flow.\\n\\nThe convergence includes predictions, residuals, loss, all three kernel blocks, joint hidden trajectories, integrated squared hidden velocities, and the forward-and-adjoint measurements of \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\).\\n\\nFor example, for either hidden layer and every bounded Lipschitz measurement \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T_0}\\n\\\\left|\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\n\\\\psi\\\\!\\\\left(\\nz_{1,i}^{(\\\\ell)}(t),\\nz_{2,i}^{(\\\\ell)}(t),\\nz_{3,i}^{(\\\\ell)}(t)\\n\\\\right)\\n-\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\,\\n\\\\psi\\\\!\\\\left(\\nZ_1^{(\\\\ell)}(t),\\nZ_2^{(\\\\ell)}(t),\\nZ_3^{(\\\\ell)}(t)\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\right|\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nin probability. Thus the limit retains how **all three inputs behave jointly at a neuron**.\\n\\nThe population state now has five objects:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ_1^{(1)},\\\\quad Z_2^{(1)},\\\\quad Z_3^{(1)},\\n\\\\quad W^{(2)},\\\\quad W^{(3)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUsing the same forward and backward formulas with population coordinates, expectations, and \\\\((W^{(2)})^*\\\\), its equations are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot Z_a^{(1)}\\n&=-2\\\\sum_{b=1}^3G_{ab}r_b\\\\delta_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}\\n&=-2\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_b\\\\,\\n\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}\\n&=-2\\\\sum_{b=1}^3r_bH_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_a=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H_a^{(2)}],\\n\\\\qquad r_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)})V\\n=\\n\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[H_b^{(1)}V].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe current state determines every term in these equations; no stored training history is needed to continue the evolution.\\n\\n## Why three inputs require an additional check\\n\\nPairwise nonparallel inputs need not be linearly independent. For example, if \\\\(x_1,x_2\\\\) are orthogonal and normalized as above, then\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_3=\\\\frac{x_1+x_2}{\\\\sqrt2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis normalized and parallel to neither, but \\\\(G\\\\) is singular.\\n\\nThe existence proof accommodates this. Its estimates use\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|G_{ab}|\\\\le1,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nnot \\\\(G^{-1}\\\\) or a positive lower bound on its eigenvalues. The finite Gaussian construction also allows correlated, singular Gaussian root distributions. [Tensor Programs III, Setup 2.2 and Theorem 2.10](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685)\\n\\nThe backward-field estimate therefore extends with sums over three inputs:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta}\\n\\\\max_{a,k:\\\\,k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\nc\\\\left|(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\right|^2\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\le C.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOnly numerical constants change. The same cutoff comparison then proves existence, uniqueness, and convergence from actual GD.\\n\\nIn fact, **one common existence time \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) can be chosen for all normalized three-input configurations and all eight label patterns**. It does not deteriorate merely because \\\\(G\\\\) becomes singular. This is a common time interval, not a claim of convergence rates uniform over changing configurations.\\n\\nThe subtler question is whether feature learning remains strictly nonzero for every input.\\n\\nFirst, a linear relation among the inputs does not force the same relation among their arctangent features. More precisely,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nQ_{ab}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[H_{0,a}^{(1)}H_{0,b}^{(1)}]\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis positive definite even when \\\\(G\\\\) is singular.\\n\\nHere is the core reason. Suppose\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{a=1}^3 c_a\\\\arctan(w^\\\\top x_a/\\\\sqrt d)=0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nfor Gaussian \\\\(w\\\\). Continuity makes this hold for every \\\\(w\\\\). Choose a direction \\\\(v\\\\) whose three projections \\\\(b_a=v^\\\\top x_a/\\\\sqrt d\\\\) are nonzero and have distinct absolute values; pairwise nonparallelity permits this choice. Setting \\\\(w=tv\\\\) and comparing the coefficients of \\\\(t,t^3,t^5\\\\) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_a c_ab_a=0,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\sum_a c_ab_a^3=0,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\sum_a c_ab_a^5=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe coefficient matrix has nonzero determinant because the \\\\(b_a^2\\\\) are distinct. Therefore all \\\\(c_a=0\\\\).\\n\\nSo the three activation fields provide three genuinely distinct directions, despite any linear dependence among the inputs.\\n\\nSecond, the two-input proof’s exchange symmetry is unavailable for a general triple. To prove that **each** second-layer preactivation moves, the new proof examines its next reused forward call. That call contains a fresh Gaussian component of strictly positive variance, independent of the previously observed second-layer coordinates. The learned rank-one contributions depend on those previous coordinates and therefore cannot cancel this new component.\\n\\nThis establishes movement for every input, not merely movement after summing over inputs.\\n\\n## What survives substantively\\n\\nFor each \\\\(a\\\\in\\\\{1,2,3\\\\}\\\\) and each hidden layer,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\n\\\\bigl(Z_a^{(\\\\ell)}(t)-Z_a^{(\\\\ell)}(0)\\\\bigr)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n=\\nc_{\\\\ell,a}t^4+o(t^4),\\n\\\\qquad c_{\\\\ell,a}>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll three \\\\(3\\\\times3\\\\) kernel blocks are positive definite for sufficiently small positive time, and\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top K(t)y\\n=\\ny^\\\\top K(0)y+ct^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\qquad c>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus both hidden layers learn features for every input, and the kernel changes. Moreover,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nL(0)=3,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot L(0)=-4y^\\\\top K^{(3)}(0)y<0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese conclusions hold for arbitrary label signs.\\n\\nThe distinction to retain is: the **existence interval is a fixed, nonvanishing interval**, while the amount of feature movement—and the smaller interval on which all strict activity conclusions are guaranteed—may depend on the particular input geometry. Neither interval shrinks with width or learning rate. The arbitrary-geometry theorem remains local in time; global-time convergence is not claimed.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a07116-336d-7dd3-81d4-3e34bdef252c\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1788603675,\"completedAt\":1788604821,\"durationMs\":1146319,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a07116-35a6-7731-925c-ad6bb5d46552\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Can you extend this result to two inputs? for simplicity assume that they have normalized RMS norm (=1), and they have any angle other than parallel (not 0 or 180 degrees), and their labels are either +1 or -1, (same or oppositing), can you extend the result proven above to this two input case? \\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bed20c2c887d2baba038ffd82ade4\",\"text\":\"I’ll work out the two-input dynamics and check which parts of the proof extend. A shared first-layer weight now updates both preactivations, with their angle determining the coupling; that is the main change to resolve.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-6ddc79d8-4bda-4ec6-b5f7-781f3e0ff1f2\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /etc/codex/skills/solve-math-rigorously/SKILL.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: solve-math-rigorously\\ndescription: Produce compact but complete solutions to standalone mathematical questions and proof requests. Use when the user asks to solve, prove, derive, analyze, verify, or correct a mathematical problem that is not primarily an incremental lesson about supplied technical documents. Infer the strongest reasonable interpretation without inventing assumptions, establish a minimal setup, use minimal notation, expose every nontrivial step, state and verify external theorem hypotheses, give proof architecture when useful, and silently audit the draft before answering.\\n---\\n\\n# Solve Mathematics Rigorously\\n\\nProduce a complete mathematical answer rather than a staged document lesson, unless the user explicitly requests incremental pacing. Optimize simultaneously for correctness, rigor, clarity, and economy.\\n\\n## Determine the intended problem\\n\\nInterpret the strongest reasonable version supported by the wording and mathematical context. Do not exploit an omission to answer a trivial or uninteresting straw-man version.\\n\\nHandle ambiguity according to its effect:\\n\\n- If it does not change the substance, adopt the conventional interpretation and state it briefly.\\n- If one reasonable assumption makes the intended problem precise, state that assumption before solving.\\n- If several reasonable interpretations lead to materially different answers, ask for clarification or give a compact case split when that fully resolves the ambiguity.\\n- If a missing assumption is necessary for the claimed conclusion, do not invent it silently.\\n\\nIf the stated claim is false, say so and give a decisive counterexample or contradiction. Present a corrected nearby statement only when it helps, and label it as a correction rather than the original result.\\n\\nAnswer the problem actually asked. Do not weaken a universal claim to a convenient special case, replace an exact question with a numerical approximation, or prove a converse instead of the stated direction.\\n\\n## Establish a minimal setup\\n\\nBegin with a compact setup or preliminaries passage when it reduces ambiguity or supports later reasoning. State only the necessary:\\n\\n- domain and assumptions;\\n- definitions used in the solution;\\n- boundary, regularity, integrability, independence, or nondegeneracy conditions that matter;\\n- notation that must persist through the answer.\\n\\nReuse the problem's notation whenever possible. Introduce a new symbol only when the unabbreviated expression is genuinely cumbersome and recurs often. Define every new symbol before use and state its mathematical type when that is not immediately evident.\\n\\nDo not force a visible setup heading for a short elementary problem. Do not hide substantive assumptions in prose later in the solution.\\n\\n## Give the architecture before a substantial proof\\n\\nBefore a long or conceptually difficult proof, explain the plan in a short paragraph or compact list. Identify the main steps, cases, claims, or lemmas and how they combine to yield the conclusion.\\n\\nKeep the architecture at the level needed to orient the reader. Do not duplicate the detailed proof, and do not substitute a plan for the proof itself. For a routine calculation or one-step argument, omit the architecture.\\n\\n## Execute every nontrivial step\\n\\nDerive each nontrivial algebraic, analytic, probabilistic, geometric, combinatorial, or logical transition. Give enough detail for the user to verify the step without reconstructing missing reasoning.\\n\\nIn particular:\\n\\n- identify the definition, identity, inequality, or prior claim being used;\\n- show substitutions, rearrangements, case transitions, and changes of variables that carry real mathematical content;\\n- track domains, signs, quantifiers, equality conditions, exceptional cases, and possible zero denominators;\\n- justify limit exchanges, differentiation or integration under a sign, convergence claims, and optimization steps;\\n- distinguish exact arguments from heuristics, approximations, and numerical evidence;\\n- show that intermediate claims actually imply the requested conclusion.\\n\\nAvoid words such as “clearly,” “obviously,” “standard,” and “it follows” when they replace reasoning. They are acceptable only after the supporting reason has been supplied.\\n\\nDo not over-explain elementary arithmetic or definitions already established. Compress routine steps while preserving the chain of verification.\\n\\n## Invoke external results completely\\n\\nWhenever the solution relies on a nontrivial theorem or external fact:\\n\\n1. state the exact form needed, including its assumptions and conclusion;\\n2. verify each assumption in the present problem;\\n3. identify the current objects substituted into the theorem;\\n4. state exactly what conclusion the theorem yields here.\\n\\nDo not merely name a theorem or call a fact standard. Prefer the relevant specialization over an unnecessarily general statement.\\n\\nVerify a recent, specialized, source-specific, or uncertain result against a primary or authoritative source before relying on it. Cite the source near the claim. Paraphrase the relevant result unless its exact wording matters; quote only a short compliant excerpt when useful.\\n\\nIf the required theorem is unavailable, uncertain, or does not apply, expose the gap instead of proceeding as though it were valid.\\n\\n## Use examples and checks proportionately\\n\\nInclude an example, boundary case, counterexample, or remark only when it clarifies an abstraction, tests a hypothesis, explains a consequence, or exposes why an assumption is needed.\\n\\nUse low-cost sanity checks when they add confidence, such as:\\n\\n- substituting the result back into the defining relation;\\n- checking dimensions, signs, or units;\\n- testing a boundary or small case;\\n- comparing with a known special case;\\n- deriving the same conclusion by a second short route.\\n\\nDo not add examples mechanically or let them replace the general argument.\\n\\n## Keep the presentation compact\\n\\nUse headings only when they make a substantial solution easier to navigate. Keep notation sparse and stable. Prefer explicit expressions to disposable shorthand.\\n\\nUse equations to carry mathematical content, not decoration. Avoid formula dumps, decorative summaries, and boxed equations. State the conclusion plainly.\\n\\nLet the problem's complexity determine the answer length. Preserve every necessary assumption, theorem check, and logical transition, but remove repetition and exposition that does not improve understanding or verification.\\n\\n## Audit and revise silently\\n\\nDraft the solution, then review it before sending. Check:\\n\\n1. Did the solution address the strongest reasonable interpretation rather than a straw man?\\n2. Were all adopted assumptions stated, and were none invented merely to force the result?\\n3. Was the setup sufficient but minimal?\\n4. Was every persistent symbol necessary and defined?\\n5. Did a substantial proof receive a useful architecture?\\n6. Can the reader verify every nontrivial transition?\\n7. Was every external theorem stated in the needed form, with its hypotheses checked and its conclusion applied explicitly?\\n8. Were recent, specialized, or uncertain results verified from an authoritative source?\\n9. Were edge cases, equality conditions, domains, and quantifiers handled?\\n10. Does the final conclusion answer the exact question?\\n11. Do examples or remarks earn their space?\\n12. Is the answer as compact as rigor allows, with no boxed equations?\\n\\nRevise any failing part and repeat the audit until the answer satisfies the checklist as well as possible. Do not mention the drafting or audit process in the final answer.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-bac0ea15-5fe4-4816-980a-26b9f923faff\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/TWO_HIDDEN_LAYER_PROOF.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Two hidden layers: a complete proof of the joint width and learning-rate limit\\n\\nWe consider one input, equal to \\\\(1\\\\), and target \\\\(1\\\\). At width \\\\(n\\\\), the first preactivation \\\\(z^{(1)}=W^{(1)}\\\\) and output weight \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) are vectors in \\\\(\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\). Throughout, \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) means the rescaled output weight. Put\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z^{(1)}),\\\\quad z^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_n=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad r_n=f_n-1,\\\\qquad L_n=r_n^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll initial entries are independent, with\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,i}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad W_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nDefine the derivatives without the loss residual by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_n/\\\\partial z^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\). The exact GD being analyzed is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{k+1}^{(1)}&=z_k^{(1)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}\\\\delta_k^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}}n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}(h_k^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}h_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nWe use the clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), interpolate these three parameters linearly, and recompute the other network quantities from them. The proof applies whenever \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\), in particular to \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\).\\n\\nWe will construct one deterministic population evolution, prove that every fixed finite collection of its forward and backward measurements is the limit of the corresponding finite-network measurements, and prove uniform convergence of predictions, losses, and all three kernel blocks on every finite time interval. We also prove convergence of the two hidden trajectory laws and their integrated squared speeds. At small positive times both hidden layers move, the activation remains non-affine on their distributions, and the kernel changes.\\n\\nCapital letters \\\\(Z^{(\\\\ell)},H^{(\\\\ell)},X^{(1)}\\\\) denote individual population coordinates. We keep \\\\(W^{(2)},W^{(3)},\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\) for their population counterparts, explicitly identifying when we have moved to population quantities. An expectation always concerns coordinates belonging to the same layer. For a scalar population variable \\\\(V\\\\), write \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\); finite vectors always use \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\), with the width normalization visible.\\n\\nFirst consider the continuous finite-width flow associated with (1). Set\\n\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+s^3/3,\\\\qquad x^{(1)}=F(z^{(1)}).\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(F'(s)\\\\phi'(s)=1\\\\), this flow becomes\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot x^{(1)}&=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-\\\\frac{2r_n}{n}\\\\delta^{(2)}(h^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2r_nh^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nThe change of coordinates is exact for the flow. Euler with proof mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) means replacing each derivative in (2) by its forward difference. In particular,\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}^{(1)}=x_k^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is Euler for the transformed flow; transforming the GD iterates in (1) produces an additional defect, estimated below.\\n\\nIterating the Euler matrix update gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently, for any appropriate vectors \\\\(v,u\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}v=W_0^{(2)}v-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top v}{n},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top u=(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}h_s^{(1)}\\n\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_s^{(2)})^\\\\top u}{n}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus training adds a finite sum of rank-one matrices. At fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\), only finitely many initial-matrix calls and scalar contractions need to be understood.\\n\\nThere is a logical detail here: the empirical coefficients in (5)–(6) are random. We first analyze an auxiliary finite system, the oracle, using deterministic population residuals and contractions in their place. Those numbers will be constructed below from the initial population action; they are not assumptions about the empirical network. We then prove that its empirical contractions approach the supplied numbers, and compare it with the empirical-feedback Euler system.\\n\\nThe matrix calculation needed for this construction is elementary Gaussian conditioning. For example, the first forward call is\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditional on \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), its coordinates are independent centered Gaussians of variance \\\\(\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\). Hence the initial population coordinates satisfy\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad H_0^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_0^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]).\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\nIn the oracle, \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\) and \\\\(r_0=-1\\\\). Until the oracle comparison below, the vectors in these first-call examples are oracle vectors. Its first update leaves \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) unchanged and gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(3)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)})\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_0^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nThe next transpose call depends on the matrix already used in the forward pass. Define, for nonzero \\\\(b\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nP_{b^\\\\perp}=I-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditioning each Gaussian row on its observed dot product with \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), and then summing the rows with coefficients \\\\(\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)}\\\\), gives\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d=\\n\\\\frac{(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}h_0^{(1)}\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nHere equality is conditional in distribution, and \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the preceding history. The first term supplies precisely the overlap required by\\n\\\\[\\n(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)};\\n\\\\]\\nthe second is perpendicular to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\).\\n\\nThe next update is\\n\\\\[\\nx_2^{(1)}=x_0^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_1(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\nh_2^{(1)}=\\\\phi(F^{-1}(x_2^{(1)})),\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(r_1=2\\\\Delta\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi(Z_0^{(2)})^2]-1\\\\).\\nThus the next initial-matrix call is \\\\(W_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\). Condition on both previous observations. Multiplying the resulting conditional matrix by \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}z_0^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{[P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}]^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2^2}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}h_2^{(1)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the previous history. The denominators in (8)–(9) are positive almost surely for \\\\(\\\\Delta>0\\\\), because the initial Gaussians are nondegenerate and \\\\(\\\\phi'(s)>0\\\\). The first response retains the old forward answer. The second supplies the remaining overlap required by the old transpose answer. The last term disturbs neither observation. Formula (5), with population coefficients for the oracle, then adds the learned rank-one correction to obtain its actual preactivation node.\\n\\nWe now prove the finite Gaussian fact needed to repeat this argument, including the case of dependent query directions. The proof below requires only empirical convergence against continuous measurements with at most quadratic growth. It does not claim convergence against arbitrary discontinuous functions.\\n\\nConsider a fixed finite calculation starting from iid coordinate tuples with finite second moments, independent of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\). Its instructions are fixed globally Lipschitz coordinate functions, linear combinations with deterministic coefficients, and calls to \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) or its transpose. Root tuples may include both \\\\(z_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(F(z_0^{(1)})\\\\). Then all same-layer joint empirical laws have deterministic limits, including their second moments.\\n\\nFor precision, the distance between two laws used here is the smallest root-mean-square distance between random vectors having those laws, usually denoted \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\). For empirical laws, matching coordinates gives the bound\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{v_i},\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{\\\\widetilde v_i}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\le\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\|v_i-\\\\widetilde v_i\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\nConvergence in this distance means weak convergence together with convergence of second moments. Thus it includes all dot products of a fixed joint tuple.\\n\\nFirst, the Gaussian matrix has a uniform operator bound with exponentially high probability. A \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net of the Euclidean unit sphere has at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) elements. Using two such nets,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le 2\\\\,9^{2n}\\\\exp(-nM^2/8).\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed the operator norm is at most twice the largest bilinear form on these nets, and each bilinear form is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). Choose a fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\).\\n\\nTo avoid inverting almost-dependent query directions, temporarily add \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) to the input of each matrix call, where each \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\) is a new independent standard Gaussian vector. Fix \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\). This is only a device for the proof; we will remove it.\\n\\nSuppose previous calls have revealed\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}V=Y,\\\\qquad (W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R.\\n\\\\]\\nThe columns of \\\\(V\\\\) are old first-layer input vectors, the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) are old second-layer input vectors, and \\\\(Y,R\\\\) contain the respective answers. Their column counts are fixed. All coordinates derived from these answers give no further information once the answers and roots are fixed. Gaussian conditioning therefore leaves\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}\\\\overset d=\\nY(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\n+U(U^\\\\top U)^{-1}R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}\\n+P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}P_{V^\\\\perp}.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(P_{V^\\\\perp}=I-V(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\\\), and similarly for \\\\(U\\\\); terms for an empty column list are zero. The fresh matrix has independent \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\) entries. The first two terms obey both recorded equations; the remaining Gaussian matrix lies in exactly the directions that leave those equations unchanged. Orthogonal components of a centered isotropic Gaussian are independent, which proves (12), including for adaptive queries: each input is chosen from the earlier answers before its new answer is observed.\\n\\nFor a new right-side input \\\\(v\\\\), multiplication gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}v\\\\overset d=Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma,\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_n=(V^\\\\top V/n)^{-1}(V^\\\\top v/n),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\beta_n=(U^\\\\top U/n)^{-1}(R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}v/n).\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose case exchanges the two layer populations.\\n\\nThe induction statement is joint \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence of every finite tuple of previously constructed vectors on each layer. Equivalently, the average of any continuous measurement \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\) with\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\psi(s)|\\\\le C(1+\\\\|s\\\\|_2^2)\\n\\\\]\\nconverges to its population expectation. The choices \\\\(\\\\psi(s)=s^2\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\psi(s,t)=st\\\\) give the required squared norms and contractions. Revealing each fresh input noise first extends the old tuple by independent Gaussian coordinates; conditional averaging proves this extension exactly as for the fresh output noise below. Thus all the normalized products in (13) converge. The perturbation guarantees invertibility of their limiting input Gram matrices: when a new column \\\\(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) is added to any old input list, conditional Gaussian second-moment calculations give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi)\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}a\\\\|_2^2}{n}+\\\\varepsilon^2+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe cross term has conditional variance at most \\\\(4\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\|a\\\\|_2^2/n^2\\\\), and only a fixed number of Gaussian directions are removed. Its limiting squared distance is at least \\\\(\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\). Successive Schur complements therefore stay positive; all the finite Gram inverses in (13) converge.\\n\\nConsequently the response coefficients and Gaussian variance converge. Write \\\\(P_U=I-P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\) for projection onto the column span of \\\\(U\\\\). This projection removes finitely many directions, and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{U}\\\\gamma\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\,\\\\middle|\\\\,\\\\text{history}\\\\right]\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{rank}(U)}n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\nAfter removing this negligible projection and replacing coefficients by their limits, the new coordinates are an old coordinate function plus independent scalar Gaussians. For a bounded Lipschitz measurement, conditional empirical variance is at most a constant divided by \\\\(n\\\\). Its conditional mean is the old empirical average of the measurement averaged over one scalar Gaussian, which converges by the previous induction step. The conditional variance of the new squared norm is also \\\\(O((1+\\\\|Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n\\\\|_2^2/n)/n)\\\\). Thus second moments converge as well. This proves the enlarged joint \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence. Lipschitz coordinate operations preserve it, closing the induction at fixed \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\).\\n\\nFinally couple the perturbed and unperturbed calculations using the same roots and matrix. On the event in (11) and the event that the finitely many \\\\(\\\\|\\\\xi\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\) are bounded, induction through the finite instructions gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{\\\\text{constructed }v}\\\\frac{\\\\|v^{\\\\varepsilon}-v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n\\\\le C\\\\varepsilon,\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(C\\\\) depends on the fixed calculation but not on \\\\(n\\\\) or \\\\(0<\\\\varepsilon\\\\le1\\\\). Each coordinate map multiplies a difference by its fixed Lipschitz constant, and each matrix call by at most \\\\(M\\\\), plus the new perturbation. Both events have probability tending to one. By (10), the deterministic limiting laws for \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\) form a Cauchy family in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\); its limit is also the limit of the unperturbed empirical laws, first taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\to0\\\\). This proves the finite Gaussian fact without assuming stability of a singular unperturbed Gram matrix.\\n\\nNow we turn these compatible finite calculations into one population matrix action. Take a countable collection of initial calculations, closed under rational linear combinations, initial-matrix calls in both directions, the Lipschitz maps used above, and a countable family of bounded Lipschitz functions of finite coordinate lists that is dense among continuous functions on compact sets. Include constants, clipping at every integer level, and products of bounded globally Lipschitz factors. In every such product, clip each factor that is not already bounded; all coordinate instructions in this collection must be globally Lipschitz. Finite joint limiting laws are consistent because every union of finitely many calculations is another such calculation. They therefore define two countable random coordinate collections, one per layer. Let their probability spaces contain exactly the information generated by these coordinates.\\n\\nIdentify two first-layer probes whenever\\n\\\\[\\nv\\\\sim\\\\widetilde v\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability},\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\nand do the same separately on the second layer. The limiting mean-square distance is \\\\(\\\\mathbb E[(V-\\\\widetilde V)^2]\\\\), so these classes are precisely the corresponding population random variables, identified when equal almost surely. Define\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}[v]=[W_0^{(2)}v].\\n\\\\]\\nThe definition is independent of representatives because (11) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|W_0^{(2)}v-W_0^{(2)}\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\le M^2\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\]\\non an event of probability tending to one. Linearity passes from the finite matrix, as does\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2].\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\nTaking all mean-square limits extends this action uniquely to every square-integrable variable on the first-layer probability space. The density needed here follows from the included bounded coordinate functions: finite-coordinate measurable functions approximate any variable on the generated space, and bounded continuous functions approximate them in mean square. The transpose action extends the same way in the reverse direction. Passing the exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{[(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u]^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\nto population expectations shows that this reverse action is precisely the adjoint:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,W_0^{(2)}V]=\\\\mathbb E[((W_0^{(2)})^*U)V].\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\nArbitrary fixed real coefficients are obtained by rational approximation; the same stability estimates identify any finite computation using them with this population construction.\\n\\nOn these fixed spaces the population state is \\\\((X^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\\\). Its derived variables and dynamics are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{gathered}\\nZ^{(1)}=F^{-1}(X^{(1)}),\\\\quad H^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H^{(1)},\\\\quad H^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(2)}),\\\\\\\\\\nf=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H^{(2)}],\\\\quad r=f-1,\\\\quad\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)}),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot X^{(1)}=-2r(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}=-2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}=-2rH^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{gathered} \\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\nThe rank-one action means\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)})V\\n=\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H^{(1)}V].\\n\\\\]\\nInitially \\\\(X_0^{(1)}=F(Z_0^{(1)})\\\\), the matrix action is (18), and \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\). Only three evolving objects are used; the population coordinate spaces themselves are infinite dimensional.\\n\\nWe next prove that (20) has a unique solution at every finite time and that Euler approximates it uniformly. The same estimates will apply at finite width. Measure two population states by\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each variable's \\\\(L^2\\\\) norm by its Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\); call this distance \\\\(d_n\\\\). The operator norm is the largest amplification of root-mean-square size.\\n\\nBoth \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\circ F^{-1}\\\\) are 1-Lipschitz. The functions \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\) are bounded and Lipschitz. If both operator norms are at most \\\\(M\\\\) and both output weights are pointwise bounded by \\\\(B\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le (\\\\pi/2)\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+M\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+B\\\\operatorname{Lip}(\\\\phi')\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}-(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+M\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\nThe output difference obeys Cauchy–Schwarz with \\\\(|H^{(2)}|\\\\le\\\\pi/2\\\\), and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|a\\\\otimes b-\\\\widetilde a\\\\otimes\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n\\\\le\\\\|a-\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nHence the vector field is Lipschitz in (21) on these bounded sets, with a constant independent of width.\\n\\nExistence can be proved directly by iteration of the integral equations. On a sufficiently short time interval, the map \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\mapsto\\\\Theta(0)+\\\\int_0^t\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))\\\\,ds\\\\) preserves a closed set of continuous paths with the indicated bounds and contracts the supremum of (21). This set is complete: mean-square limits preserve a common pointwise bound on \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\). Taking the time interval shorter than the reciprocal of the Lipschitz constant proves existence and uniqueness.\\n\\nTo continue the solution, differentiation of the output, using (19), gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f=-2rK,\\\\quad \\\\dot L=-4r^2K,\\\\qquad\\nK=K^{(1)}+K^{(2)}+K^{(3)},\\n\\\\tag{23}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nK^{(1)}&=\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[(\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)})^2\\\\right],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(2)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\delta^{(2)})^2],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(3)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(2)})^2].\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\nThese formulas also follow by differentiating along square-integrable parameter directions: the gradients of \\\\(L\\\\) in \\\\(Z^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)}\\\\) are \\\\(2r\\\\delta^{(1)},2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},2rH^{(2)}\\\\). For matrix perturbations the relevant inner product is defined first on rank-one changes by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle a\\\\otimes b,c\\\\otimes d\\\\rangle\\n=\\\\mathbb E[ac]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[bd],\\n\\\\]\\nand extended by linearity and completion. This is the population version o\",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":37279}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-d27f304c-ca79-4650-9a4e-638e591ffb25\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g '*TWO_INPUT*' -g '*two_input*' -g '*MULTI*INPUT*' -g '*multi_input*' -g '*two_sample*' .\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"./studies/stieltjes_conjecture/numerics/hybrid_mean_field_campaign/breadth_panel/two_input/run_two_input_point.py\\n./studies/stieltjes_conjecture/numerics/hybrid_mean_field_campaign/breadth_panel/two_input/two_input_simulator.py\\n./studies/stieltjes_conjecture/numerics/hybrid_mean_field_campaign/breadth_panel/two_input/two_input_engine.py\\n./studies/stieltjes_conjecture/numerics/hybrid_mean_field_campaign/breadth_panel/two_input/run_capped_two_input.sh\\n./studies/stieltjes_conjecture/numerics/hybrid_mean_field_campaign/breadth_panel/FROZEN_TWO_INPUT_POINTS.json\\n./studies/stieltjes_conjecture/numerics/hybrid_mean_field_campaign/breadth_panel/two_input/tests/test_two_input_simulator.py\\n./studies/stieltjes_conjecture/numerics/hybrid_mean_field_campaign/breadth_panel/two_input/tests/test_two_input_engine.py\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/cubic_compiler/two_input_plus_gaussian_program/test_two_input_cubic_plus.py\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/cubic_compiler/two_input_plus_gaussian_program/two_input_cubic_connected_fixed.cpp\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/cubic_compiler/two_input_plus_gaussian_program/test_two_input_cubic_stieltjes_order5.py\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/cubic_compiler/two_input_plus_gaussian_program/two_input_cubic_plus_jet.py\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/cubic_compiler/two_input_plus_gaussian_program/two_input_cubic_plus_fixed_rho_jet.py\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/cubic_compiler/two_input_plus_gaussian_program/two_input_cubic_connected.cpp\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/cubic_compiler/two_input_plus_gaussian_program/two_input_cubic_stieltjes_order5.py\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/cubic_compiler/two_input_plus_gaussian_program/audit_two_input_cubic_plus.py\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/quadratic_compiler/campaign2/test_two_input_reference.py\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/quadratic_compiler/campaign2/two_input_reference.py\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/quadratic_compiler/campaign2/two_input_connected.cpp\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/quadratic_compiler/campaign2/test_two_input_connected.py\\n./studies/mean_field_peeling/quadratic_compiler/campaign2/frozen/two_input_connected_vp\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bed26760887d29f57e8f7fbe84ab9\",\"summary\":[\"**Exploring matrix coordinate transforms**\",\"**Exploring cutoff uniqueness estimates**\",\"**Testing scalar residual symmetry**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_R7Ng4l66a2AWOxH2yI7np6FB\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-a75b-78b0-9e9c-692aa8f7cf22\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_coordinates\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_hoRLtb8pmr8bW9pXA8o3rLkE\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-c07d-7941-8a3f-09f3b2fcb988\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_2MvG0Srr2b3iItL9JBTvDEvv\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-dae2-7ec3-ab96-7da423abb3e5\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_limit_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bed4a834487d29cd40e6ca6fe0628\",\"summary\":[\"**Setting up coordinate transforms**\",\"**Seeking a cancellation metric**\",\"**Testing energy-norm uniqueness**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bed59b70487d281de6c770956d43e\",\"summary\":[\"**Assessing Hessian boundedness**\",\"**Testing Gaussian operator bounds**\",\"**Assessing rare-direction responses**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bed6673a487d2a3c1e5ce74d231c0\",\"summary\":[\"**Testing a mirror-coordinate map**\",\"**Exploring per-sample path lifts**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bed786dfc87d2bef3252336ec2ace\",\"summary\":[\"**Reviewing extension assumptions**\",\"**Checking Gaussian tail control**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bed86ee8c87d2b402ec141229667b\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking cavity response**\",\"**Testing metric distortion bounds**\",\"**Bounding metric distortion**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bed99935087d2a7261e49123ba272\",\"summary\":[\"**Bounding solution sensitivity**\",\"**Bounding q-ratio differences**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07116-c0df-7b72-933a-de6e1f07ace7\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-c07d-7941-8a3f-09f3b2fcb988\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"contextCompaction\",\"id\":\"01a07118-4f98-7493-9a87-fd6c253d0029\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07116-db34-7f91-b700-fa8415248bfb\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-dae2-7ec3-ab96-7da423abb3e5\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_limit_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bedfb8aac87d2be702a9d19989e1f\",\"summary\":[\"**Exploring coordinate regularization**\",\"**Developing Gaussian field bounds**\",\"**Closing small-time bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07116-a7b0-7750-aed5-25b1ed088805\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-a75b-78b0-9e9c-692aa8f7cf22\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_coordinates\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bee11090c87d2945492f0fbcd15ad\",\"summary\":[\"**Considering monotonicity delegation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bee18eec487d29b15bc5e525c401a\",\"summary\":[\"**Integrating path transport stability**\",\"**Analyzing stability geometry**\",\"**Decomposing finite-time transpose**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bee29560887d2be372772ccf40bd2\",\"text\":\"There is a real new issue for nonorthogonal inputs: updating the shared first-layer weights couples the two preactivations, so the cubic change of coordinates no longer cancels the activation derivatives. I’m checking whether a different stability argument closes that gap; the two-input equations alone would not prove the limit exists.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_lh7gMnnIUypx8zTSD1qR4HTg\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-dae2-7ec3-ab96-7da423abb3e5\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_limit_route\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_csV6e9n502JkNhvV5IiIJRX3\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-a75b-78b0-9e9c-692aa8f7cf22\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_coordinates\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_knU4tpUiC2RH8hTG48UgX3d4\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-c07d-7941-8a3f-09f3b2fcb988\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bee3fc1e887d29cf2f3d576f64cea\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving response coefficients**\",\"**Bounding response derivatives**\",\"**Bounding forward responses**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bee4d761887d2bb430f0ed47dcec8\",\"summary\":[\"**Calculating first corrections**\",\"**Checking response bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bee5a16d887d2a5cd350edd66a8e7\",\"summary\":[\"**Exploring deterministic tail bounds**\",\"**Testing response-map Lipschitzness**\",\"**Clarifying the parameterization**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bee6a5f1087d2bc9f4513bb329b8e\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking theorem time horizon**\",\"**Inspecting source beginning**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-6cf3936b-c081-4481-af29-24d8586b3156\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,180p' /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/TWO_HIDDEN_LAYER_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Two hidden layers: a complete proof of the joint width and learning-rate limit\\n\\nWe consider one input, equal to \\\\(1\\\\), and target \\\\(1\\\\). At width \\\\(n\\\\), the first preactivation \\\\(z^{(1)}=W^{(1)}\\\\) and output weight \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) are vectors in \\\\(\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\). Throughout, \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) means the rescaled output weight. Put\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z^{(1)}),\\\\quad z^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_n=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad r_n=f_n-1,\\\\qquad L_n=r_n^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll initial entries are independent, with\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,i}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad W_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nDefine the derivatives without the loss residual by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_n/\\\\partial z^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\). The exact GD being analyzed is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{k+1}^{(1)}&=z_k^{(1)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}\\\\delta_k^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}}n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}(h_k^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}h_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nWe use the clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), interpolate these three parameters linearly, and recompute the other network quantities from them. The proof applies whenever \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\), in particular to \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\).\\n\\nWe will construct one deterministic population evolution, prove that every fixed finite collection of its forward and backward measurements is the limit of the corresponding finite-network measurements, and prove uniform convergence of predictions, losses, and all three kernel blocks on every finite time interval. We also prove convergence of the two hidden trajectory laws and their integrated squared speeds. At small positive times both hidden layers move, the activation remains non-affine on their distributions, and the kernel changes.\\n\\nCapital letters \\\\(Z^{(\\\\ell)},H^{(\\\\ell)},X^{(1)}\\\\) denote individual population coordinates. We keep \\\\(W^{(2)},W^{(3)},\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\) for their population counterparts, explicitly identifying when we have moved to population quantities. An expectation always concerns coordinates belonging to the same layer. For a scalar population variable \\\\(V\\\\), write \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\); finite vectors always use \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\), with the width normalization visible.\\n\\nFirst consider the continuous finite-width flow associated with (1). Set\\n\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+s^3/3,\\\\qquad x^{(1)}=F(z^{(1)}).\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(F'(s)\\\\phi'(s)=1\\\\), this flow becomes\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot x^{(1)}&=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-\\\\frac{2r_n}{n}\\\\delta^{(2)}(h^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2r_nh^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nThe change of coordinates is exact for the flow. Euler with proof mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) means replacing each derivative in (2) by its forward difference. In particular,\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}^{(1)}=x_k^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is Euler for the transformed flow; transforming the GD iterates in (1) produces an additional defect, estimated below.\\n\\nIterating the Euler matrix update gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently, for any appropriate vectors \\\\(v,u\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}v=W_0^{(2)}v-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top v}{n},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top u=(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}h_s^{(1)}\\n\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_s^{(2)})^\\\\top u}{n}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus training adds a finite sum of rank-one matrices. At fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\), only finitely many initial-matrix calls and scalar contractions need to be understood.\\n\\nThere is a logical detail here: the empirical coefficients in (5)–(6) are random. We first analyze an auxiliary finite system, the oracle, using deterministic population residuals and contractions in their place. Those numbers will be constructed below from the initial population action; they are not assumptions about the empirical network. We then prove that its empirical contractions approach the supplied numbers, and compare it with the empirical-feedback Euler system.\\n\\nThe matrix calculation needed for this construction is elementary Gaussian conditioning. For example, the first forward call is\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditional on \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), its coordinates are independent centered Gaussians of variance \\\\(\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\). Hence the initial population coordinates satisfy\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad H_0^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_0^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]).\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\nIn the oracle, \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\) and \\\\(r_0=-1\\\\). Until the oracle comparison below, the vectors in these first-call examples are oracle vectors. Its first update leaves \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) unchanged and gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(3)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)})\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_0^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nThe next transpose call depends on the matrix already used in the forward pass. Define, for nonzero \\\\(b\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nP_{b^\\\\perp}=I-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditioning each Gaussian row on its observed dot product with \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), and then summing the rows with coefficients \\\\(\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)}\\\\), gives\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d=\\n\\\\frac{(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}h_0^{(1)}\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nHere equality is conditional in distribution, and \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the preceding history. The first term supplies precisely the overlap required by\\n\\\\[\\n(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)};\\n\\\\]\\nthe second is perpendicular to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\).\\n\\nThe next update is\\n\\\\[\\nx_2^{(1)}=x_0^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_1(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\nh_2^{(1)}=\\\\phi(F^{-1}(x_2^{(1)})),\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(r_1=2\\\\Delta\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi(Z_0^{(2)})^2]-1\\\\).\\nThus the next initial-matrix call is \\\\(W_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\). Condition on both previous observations. Multiplying the resulting conditional matrix by \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}z_0^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{[P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}]^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2^2}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}h_2^{(1)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the previous history. The denominators in (8)–(9) are positive almost surely for \\\\(\\\\Delta>0\\\\), because the initial Gaussians are nondegenerate and \\\\(\\\\phi'(s)>0\\\\). The first response retains the old forward answer. The second supplies the remaining overlap required by the old transpose answer. The last term disturbs neither observation. Formula (5), with population coefficients for the oracle, then adds the learned rank-one correction to obtain its actual preactivation node.\\n\\nWe now prove the finite Gaussian fact needed to repeat this argument, including the case of dependent query directions. The proof below requires only empirical convergence against continuous measurements with at most quadratic growth. It does not claim convergence against arbitrary discontinuous functions.\\n\\nConsider a fixed finite calculation starting from iid coordinate tuples with finite second moments, independent of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\). Its instructions are fixed globally Lipschitz coordinate functions, linear combinations with deterministic coefficients, and calls to \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) or its transpose. Root tuples may include both \\\\(z_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(F(z_0^{(1)})\\\\). Then all same-layer joint empirical laws have deterministic limits, including their second moments.\\n\\nFor precision, the distance between two laws used here is the smallest root-mean-square distance between random vectors having those laws, usually denoted \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\). For empirical laws, matching coordinates gives the bound\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{v_i},\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{\\\\widetilde v_i}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\le\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\|v_i-\\\\widetilde v_i\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\nConvergence in this distance means weak convergence together with convergence of second moments. Thus it includes all dot products of a fixed joint tuple.\\n\\nFirst, the Gaussian matrix has a uniform operator bound with exponentially high probability. A \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net of the Euclidean unit sphere has at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) elements. Using two such nets,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le 2\\\\,9^{2n}\\\\exp(-nM^2/8).\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed the operator norm is at most twice the largest bilinear form on these nets, and each bilinear form is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). Choose a fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\).\\n\\nTo avoid inverting almost-dependent query directions, temporarily add \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) to the input of each matrix call, where each \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\) is a new independent standard Gaussian vector. Fix \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\). This is only a device for the proof; we will remove it.\\n\\nSuppose previous calls have revealed\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}V=Y,\\\\qquad (W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R.\\n\\\\]\\nThe columns of \\\\(V\\\\) are old first-layer input vectors, the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) are old second-layer input vectors, and \\\\(Y,R\\\\) contain the respective answers. Their column counts are fixed. All coordinates derived from these answers give no further information once the answers and roots are fixed. Gaussian conditioning therefore leaves\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}\\\\overset d=\\nY(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\n+U(U^\\\\top U)^{-1}R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}\\n+P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}P_{V^\\\\perp}.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(P_{V^\\\\perp}=I-V(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\\\), and similarly for \\\\(U\\\\); terms for an empty column list are zero. The fresh matrix has independent \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\) entries. The first two terms obey both recorded equations; the remaining Gaussian matrix lies in exactly the directions that leave those equations unchanged. Orthogonal components of a centered isotropic Gaussian are independent, which proves (12), including for adaptive queries: each input is chosen from the earlier answers before its new answer is observed.\\n\\nFor a new right-side input \\\\(v\\\\), multiplication gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}v\\\\overset d=Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma,\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_n=(V^\\\\top V/n)^{-1}(V^\\\\top v/n),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\beta_n=(U^\\\\top U/n)^{-1}(R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}v/n).\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose case exchanges the two layer populations.\\n\\nThe induction statement is joint \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence of every finite tuple of previously constructed vectors on each layer. Equivalently, the average of any continuous measurement \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\) with\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\psi(s)|\\\\le C(1+\\\\|s\\\\|_2^2)\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bee743a4087d29153290b529fb1cf\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing a finite-dimensional result**\",\"**Drafting finite-width kernel results**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bee84655487d2b13461eaa67c04af\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing the response lemma**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_jmfe88iyX1sVhJZl64dSXhvm\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-dae2-7ec3-ab96-7da423abb3e5\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_limit_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bee8e296c87d2a9e379bb0ba8a155\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking short-time continuation**\",\"**Checking continuation bounds**\",\"**Reviewing theorem assumptions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-8d595d41-837a-4947-bf3a-c4cf602a8ab7\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,190p' /tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# A rigorous joint mean-field/gradient-flow limit for the two-hidden-layer arctangent network\\n\\n## Theorem\\n\\nFor every width \\\\(n\\\\), consider the one-sample network\\n\\\\[\\nA_i=W_i^{(1)},\\\\qquad H_i=\\\\phi(A_i),\\\\qquad\\nS_j=\\\\sum_{i=1}^nB_{ji}H_i,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nG_j=\\\\phi(S_j),\\\\qquad f_n=\\\\sum_{j=1}^nc_jG_j,\\n\\\\qquad L_n=(f_n-1)^2,                              \\\\tag{T1}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\phi(x)=\\\\arctan x\\\\).  Initialize all variables independently by\\n\\\\[\\nA_i(0)\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\qquad B_{ji}(0)\\\\sim N(0,n^{-1}),\\n\\\\qquad c_j(0)\\\\sim N(0,n^{-4}).                     \\\\tag{T2}\\n\\\\]\\nUse exact full-batch GD with base step and raw layer multipliers\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_n=n^{-2},\\\\qquad\\n(\\\\lambda_A,\\\\lambda_B,\\\\lambda_c)=(n,1,n^{-1}),       \\\\tag{T3}\\n\\\\]\\nLinearly interpolate the three parameter arrays on the clock\\n\\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), and at intermediate times recompute \\\\(H,S,G,f,L\\\\) from\\nthose interpolated parameters.\\n\\nThen, along the full sequence \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), the exact GD processes converge in probability, uniformly on every compact time interval, to a unique autonomous and restartable action-law gradient flow.  The empirical path laws of \\\\(A\\\\) and \\\\(S\\\\) converge in \\\\(W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\).  Outputs, losses, all three scaled kernel blocks, and both hidden velocity energies converge in the modes stated below.\\n\\nThere exists \\\\(T_*>0\\\\) such that, for every \\\\(0<T\\\\le T_*\\\\):\\n\\n1. both hidden marginal laws have finite strictly positive variance;\\n2. both hidden layers have finite positive integrated mean-square velocity and nonzero mean-square displacement;\\n3. all three parameter blocks have finite positive integrated scaled kernel;\\n4. the affine-regression residual of \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) is positive in both hidden layers;\\n5. \\\\(K(t)\\\\) is nonconstant and \\\\(L(T)<L(0)=1\\\\).\\n\\nIn particular, this is a genuinely nonlinear feature-learning limit, not a frozen-feature or affine limit.  Moreover\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\lambda_A=n^{-1},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\lambda_B=n^{-2},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\lambda_c=n^{-3},                            \\\\tag{T4}\\n\\\\]\\nso every raw parameter step tends to zero.\\n\\nThe proof has five bridges: exact normalization; a dimension-free regularized flow; fixed-mesh width identification by a finite population-oracle tensor program; a same-width Euler sandwich yielding the continuous action law; and a direct comparison of exact GD with that flow.  The final section proves strict activity and nonlinearity.\\n\\n## 1. Exact finite-width equations\\n\\nPut\\n\\\\[\\nC=nc,\\\\qquad \\\\langle u,v\\\\rangle_n=n^{-1}u^\\\\top v,\\n\\\\qquad (u\\\\otimes_n v)z=u\\\\langle v,z\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\nand write\\n\\\\[\\nq(x)=\\\\phi'(x)=\\\\frac1{1+x^2},\\\\qquad\\nD=C\\\\odot q(S),\\\\qquad P=B^\\\\top D,qquad e=f_n-1.     \\\\tag{1.1}\\n\\\\]\\nThen \\\\(f_n=\\\\langle C,G\\\\rangle_n\\\\).  Direct differentiation gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_n}{\\\\partial A_i}=\\\\frac1nq(A_i)P_i,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_n}{\\\\partial B_{ji}}=\\\\frac1nD_jH_i,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_n}{\\\\partial c_j}=G_j.             \\\\tag{1.2}\\n\\\\]\\nMultiplying the loss gradients by (T3) proves that the associated finite-width gradient flow is exactly\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot A=-2e\\\\,q(A)\\\\odot P,qquad\\n\\\\dot B=-2e\\\\,D\\\\otimes_nH,qquad\\n\\\\dot C=-2e\\\\,G.                                     \\\\tag{1.3}\\n\\\\]\\nThe three scaled tangent-kernel blocks are\\n\\\\[\\nK^A_n=\\\\|q(A)P\\\\|_n^2,qquad\\nK^B_n=\\\\|H\\\\|_n^2\\\\|D\\\\|_n^2,qquad\\nK^C_n=\\\\|G\\\\|_n^2,                                  \\\\tag{1.4}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_n^2=\\\\langle v,v\\\\rangle_n\\\\).  Thus, for \\\\(K_n=K_n^A+K_n^B+K_n^C\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f_n=-2eK_n,qquad \\\\dot L_n=-4e^2K_n.          \\\\tag{1.5}\\n\\\\]\\nIn particular, \\\\(|e(t)|\\\\le |e(0)|\\\\) along the flow.\\n\\nDifferentiating \\\\(S=BH\\\\) will also be useful:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot S=-2e\\\\left\\\\{\\\\|H\\\\|_n^2D+B[q(A)^2\\\\odot P]\\\\right\\\\}. \\\\tag{1.6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n## 2. The regularizing coordinate and dimension-free flow estimates\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\nF(a)=a+\\\\frac{a^3}{3},\\\\qquad X=F(A),\\\\qquad J=F^{-1},\\n\\\\qquad h(x)=\\\\arctan J(x).                           \\\\tag{2.1}\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(F'(a)=q(a)^{-1}\\\\), equation (1.3) becomes\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nA&=J(X),& H&=h(X),& S&=BH,\\\\\\\\\\nG&=\\\\arctan S,&D&=Cq(S),&e&=\\\\langle C,G\\\\rangle_n-1,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot X&=-2eB^\\\\top D,&\\n\\\\dot B&=-2eD\\\\otimes_nH,&\\n\\\\dot C&=-2eG.\\n\\\\end{aligned}}                                    \\\\tag{2.2}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is exact, not a linearization.  Also\\n\\\\[\\nJ'(x)=q(J(x)),\\\\qquad h'(x)=q(J(x))^2,               \\\\tag{2.3}\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(J,h,q\\\\circ J\\\\) are globally Lipschitz; \\\\(|H|,|G|\\\\le a_0:=\\\\pi/2\\\\).\\n\\nFor two states define\\n\\\\[\\nd_n(\\\\theta,\\\\widetilde\\\\theta)\\n=\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_n+\\n \\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}+\\n \\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n.                            \\\\tag{2.4}\\n\\\\]\\nOn a set on which \\\\(\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}},\\\\|\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le M_B\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\|C\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le M_C\\\\), the vector field in (2.2) is Lipschitz in (2.4), with a constant independent of \\\\(n\\\\).  Indeed,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|H-\\\\widetilde H\\\\|_n\\\\le\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_n,     \\\\tag{2.5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le a_0\\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n  +M_B\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_n,                       \\\\tag{2.6}\\n\\\\]\\nand\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|D-\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le \\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n\\n +M_C\\\\operatorname{Lip}(q)\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_n.  \\\\tag{2.7}\\n\\\\]\\nFurthermore\\n\\\\[\\n|e-\\\\widetilde e|\\n\\\\le a_0\\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n\\n +\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_n,        \\\\tag{2.8}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|B^\\\\top D-\\\\widetilde B^\\\\top\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le \\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\|D\\\\|_n\\n +M_B\\\\|D-\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_n.                        \\\\tag{2.9}\\n\\\\]\\nFinally \\\\(\\\\|u\\\\otimes_nv\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}=\\\\|u\\\\|_n\\\\|v\\\\|_n\\\\).  Substitution of (2.5)--(2.9) into (2.2) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|V_n(\\\\theta)-V_n(\\\\widetilde\\\\theta)\\\\|_{d_n}\\n\\\\le L(M_B,M_C,\\\\|C\\\\|_n,\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n)d_n(\\\\theta,\\\\widetilde\\\\theta). \\\\tag{2.10}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe required bounded set is invariant on each compact interval, uniformly in width on a high-probability initialization event.  From (1.5), \\\\(|e(t)|\\\\le |e(0)|\\\\), and hence\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|C(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\|C(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty+2a_0|e(0)|t.  \\\\tag{2.11}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (2.2) then bounds \\\\(\\\\|B(t)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\), followed by \\\\(\\\\|X(t)\\\\|_n\\\\).  Explicitly,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\le2a_0|e(0)|\\\\|C\\\\|_n,\\n\\\\quad\\n\\\\|\\\\dot X\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le2|e(0)|\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\|C\\\\|_n.             \\\\tag{2.12}\\n\\\\]\\nNow \\\\(\\\\|C_n(0)\\\\|_n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\), \\\\(\\\\|C_n(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty=o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\|X_n(0)\\\\|_n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\).  A self-contained \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net argument gives a constant \\\\(M_0\\\\) with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|B_n(0)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}>M_0)\\\\le2e^{-cn}. \\\\tag{2.13}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed, two unit-sphere \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets have at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points each,\\n\\\\(\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le2\\\\max_{u,v}|v^\\\\top Bu|\\\\), and every fixed bilinear form is \\\\(N(0,n^{-1})\\\\); a union bound proves (2.13) for any sufficiently large fixed \\\\(M_0\\\\).\\n\\nConsequently (2.2) has a unique global finite-width solution.  On every \\\\([0,T]\\\\), explicit Euler with mesh \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\), polygonally interpolated, satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^\\\\delta(t),\\\\theta_n(t))\\n\\\\le C_T\\\\delta                                             \\\\tag{2.14}\\n\\\\]\\non an event of probability tending to one.  To verify (2.14), the local defect is at most \\\\(\\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\delta^2\\\\), where \\\\(M_T\\\\) bounds the vector field; the recurrence\\n\\\\(r_{k+1}\\\\le(1+L_T\\\\delta)r_k+\\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\delta^2\\\\) and discrete Gronwall give the assertion.  A stopped bootstrap keeps the Euler iterates in the slightly enlarged bounded set.\\n\\n## 3. The only nonclassical external theorem\\n\\nWe use the following exact specialization of Greg Yang, *Tensor Programs III: Neural Matrix Laws*, arXiv:2009.10685v3, Setup 2.2 and Theorem 2.10.\\n\\n**Fixed-program reused-Gaussian theorem.**  Fix a finite program whose\\ninitial matrix has iid \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\) entries, independent of all initial\\nvectors, whose initial vector coordinates are iid copies of a fixed jointly\\nGaussian vector (the covariance may be singular), and whose instructions\\nare coordinatewise polynomially bounded maps and finitely many applications\\nof \\\\(B_0\\\\) and \\\\(B_0^\\\\top\\\\).  For every fixed finite list of program vectors\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0711a-817f-7f42-bd1a-a36a8b3f81c0\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-a75b-78b0-9e9c-692aa8f7cf22\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_coordinates\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0711a-9ab8-77a2-973b-304cd34bcb07\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-c07d-7941-8a3f-09f3b2fcb988\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_activity\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9beea5f5f487d2bd1c8b3d37599bda\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing finite-program proof**\",\"**Handling rank degeneracy**\",\"**Assessing damped cutoff**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9beeb865a487d2b6ae7cc65f193294\",\"summary\":[\"**Comparing cutoff-truncated fields**\",\"**Reviewing the response lemma**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-dc1a9bf2-3bcd-4d88-aab5-13c633d02eea\",\"query\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10685\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10685\"}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-b2a3f6b2-bc77-48a9-bec1-d17246f8b789\",\"query\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2009.10685v3\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2009.10685v3\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9beecee1b887d28b2e4cf3fdcc51cb\",\"summary\":[\"**Reading box 1 text**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-7bc24ef6-e9db-48a4-abff-80592faa3a08\",\"query\":\"'ZDot'\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"findInPage\",\"url\":null,\"pattern\":\"ZDot\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9beed5894487d2813f20b54f8eb010\",\"summary\":[\"**Sending agent source info**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_N4ajzfnFIP8OabYFZMUvM0l3\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-dae2-7ec3-ab96-7da423abb3e5\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_limit_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9beedeccd087d29912f895a3e82fe9\",\"summary\":[\"**Constructing bounded W0**\",\"**Estimating Euler defects**\",\"**Comparing L2 solutions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9beeebd1b887d2b94631a746c4ee78\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking sub-G extension bounds**\",\"**Checking cutoff Gronwall bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9beef8d1a087d2be2e966b91fc6a01\",\"summary\":[\"**Proving rawGD bounds**\",\"**Controlling Volterra response**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bef05f02087d2b5a2123ec92db8e6\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking the stability lemma**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bef0a4d1887d2a40bb7cbe57bff4a\",\"text\":\"The orthogonal case does extend with the existing proof. For other angles, a promising route is to control the tails of the backward fields and use those bounds in the stability argument. I’m checking whether that gives a rigorous result on a short time interval before claiming the arbitrary-angle extension.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_fPyNYgrpSH4c6bMxa4wxsQ4y\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-dae2-7ec3-ab96-7da423abb3e5\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_limit_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bef13ea8c87d2b2dc50b95967e964\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking finite-mesh tail control**\",\"**Formalizing joint consistency**\",\"**Refining local-extension lemma**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-d3c8b6db-1980-4ae1-b1a4-e9bf3376e13f\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,290p' /tmp/two-input-shorttime-route.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# A proposed short-time extension through Gaussian response bounds\\n\\nThis note concerns ordinary GD with two inputs, not preconditioned GD. Its purpose is to supply the missing mesh-uniform bound on backward fields. The exact Gaussian response identity below is the specialization of the smooth finite-program Gaussian conditioning rule. The response estimates themselves are derived here. They give a local-in-time route; no global continuation claim is made.\\n\\nLet the input Gram matrix be\\n\\\\[\\nG=\\\\begin{pmatrix}1&\\\\rho\\\\\\\\\\\\rho&1\\\\end{pmatrix},\\\\qquad |\\\\rho|<1,\\n\\\\]\\nand labels be \\\\(y_1,y_2\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). Use the summed loss \\\\(\\\\sum_{a=1}^2r_a^2\\\\); averaging the loss only rescales time. The first-layer population root \\\\((Z_{1,0}^{(1)},Z_{2,0}^{(1)})\\\\) is centered Gaussian with covariance \\\\(G\\\\). The shared readout starts at \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\).\\n\\nFor fixed mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta>0\\\\), population Euler obeys\\n\\\\[\\nH_{a,k}^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_{a,k}^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(2)}H_{a,k}^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_{a,k}^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\n\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_{a,k}^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nr_{a,k}=\\\\mathbb E[W_k^{(3)}H_{a,k}^{(2)}]-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k+1}^{(1)}=Z_{a,k}^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^2G_{ab}r_{b,k}\\\\phi'(Z_{b,k}^{(1)})P_{b,k},\\n\\\\qquad P_{b,k}:=(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}=W_k^{(2)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^2r_{b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,k}^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^2r_{b,k}H_{b,k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\phi=\\\\arctan\\\\). All response derivatives below hold every deterministic coefficient in these finite recursions fixed, including residuals, contractions, and response coefficients. They do not differentiate through expectations or through the covariance law.\\n\\n## Exact finite-mesh Gaussian response representation\\n\\nIntroduce two centered jointly Gaussian collections \\\\(\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\), independent of one another. The first-layer root is independent of both. Their covariances are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\xi_{b,s}]=\\\\mathbb E[H_{a,k}^{(1)}H_{b,s}^{(1)}],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\eta_{b,s}]=\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}].\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThese are separate-layer probability spaces; taking them independent on a product space is only a convenient representation, not an assertion of a natural pairing of neurons across layers.\\n\\nDefine the deterministic coefficients\\n\\\\[\\nA_{ak,bs}:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}}{\\\\partial\\\\xi_{b,s}}\\\\right],\\\\quad s\\\\le k,\\n\\\\qquad\\nC_{ak,bs}:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial H_{a,k}^{(1)}}{\\\\partial\\\\eta_{b,s}}\\\\right],\\\\quad s<k.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nThe derivatives are symbolic derivatives of the displayed causal recursions, treating Gaussian slots as separate real arguments even when their realized joint covariance is singular. Then the exact limiting initial-matrix actions are\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}=\\n\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s<k}C_{ak,bs}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\n\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}A_{ak,bs}H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nExpanding the learned matrix gives the full equations\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\n\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s<k}\\n\\\\left(C_{ak,bs}-2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\\\right)\\n\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nP_{a,k}=\\n\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}\\n\\\\left(A_{ak,bs}-\\\\mathbf1_{s<k}2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}]\\\\right)\\nH_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nTogether with (1), the readout update in (2), and \\\\(\\\\delta=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)})\\\\), these are causal finite recursions. To construct step \\\\(k\\\\): first construct \\\\(H_{a,k}^{(1)}\\\\) and its derivatives using \\\\(\\\\eta\\\\)-slots through \\\\(k-1\\\\); next construct \\\\(Z_{a,k}^{(2)},\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\) using \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\)-slots through \\\\(k\\\\); finally construct \\\\(P_{a,k}\\\\) and update first-layer coordinates and readout. The Gaussian covariance matrices extend consistently because they are Gram matrices of the constructed source variables.\\n\\nWhy the Gaussian response rule has this form: conditioning on previous forward and transpose products gives the Gaussian matrix regression formula with a projected fresh Gaussian matrix. In the limit, its Gaussian parts in each orientation have covariance equal to the Gram matrix of the sources. Its non-Gaussian part lies in the span of the previous sources in the opposite orientation. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the coefficients of this response as the expectations in (4). The elementary identity used is, for a centered Gaussian vector \\\\(g\\\\) with covariance \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[g_i f(g)]=\\\\sum_j\\\\Sigma_{ij}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\partial_j f(g)].\\n\\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nIt is valid even for singular \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\), by writing \\\\(g=B\\\\gamma\\\\) and integrating by parts in independent standard Gaussian coordinates \\\\(\\\\gamma\\\\). If the conditional regression uses a pseudoinverse, the symbolic derivative coefficients and the pseudoinverse coefficients can differ only by a vector in the covariance nullspace; the corresponding combination of source variables has zero squared norm, so the response itself is unchanged. This is the singular-covariance issue that must be retained in a complete proof.\\n\\nFor a rigorous external formulation of this finite-program identity, the locally available primary source is Tensor Programs III, `/tmp/tp3src/NetsorT2.tex`, definitions ZHat/ZDot and the partial-derivative convention, together with its master theorem. The algebraic recursions here are smooth, polynomially bounded functions of finitely many Gaussian slots, and their symbolic derivatives are polynomially bounded for fixed program length. Thus all expectations in (4) exist. The finite-query theorem handles fixed program length; the estimates below are the additional ingredient needed uniformly as length increases. This note does not purport to reprove the entire master theorem in the preceding paragraph.\\n\\n## Uniform short-time response bounds\\n\\nFix \\\\(T\\\\le1\\\\) and \\\\(k\\\\Delta\\\\le T\\\\). Constants in this section depend only on bounds for \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi',\\\\phi''\\\\), on \\\\(G\\\\), and on the number of inputs; they do not depend on \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), \\\\(k\\\\), or the Gaussian covariance ranks.\\n\\nPut \\\\(B_k=\\\\|W_k^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\). Since \\\\(|r_{a,k}|\\\\le1+\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty B_k\\\\), (2) gives\\n\\\\[\\nB_{k+1}\\\\le B_k+4\\\\Delta\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty(1+\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty B_k).\\n\\\\]\\nThe resulting geometric sum gives, for a fixed \\\\(C_0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nB_k\\\\le C_0T,\\\\qquad |r_{a,k}|\\\\le C_0,\\\\qquad |\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}|\\\\le C_0T.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\nThe absolute row sums of the training-memory coefficients in (7) and (8) are therefore at most \\\\(C_0T\\\\) and \\\\(C_0T^3\\\\), respectively.\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A_k=\\\\max_{a,\\\\,r\\\\le k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s\\\\le r}|A_{ar,bs}|,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathcal C_k=\\\\max_{a,\\\\,r\\\\le k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s<r}|C_{ar,bs}|.\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (8) already gives the pathwise bound\\n\\\\[\\n|P_{a,k}-\\\\eta_{a,k}|\\\\le C_0(\\\\mathcal A_k+T^3),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\eta_{a,k}^2]\\\\le C_0^2T^2.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFirst estimate \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\) in terms of \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\). Let \\\\(J_k\\\\) be the maximum, over the first-layer coordinates at times through \\\\(k\\\\), of the sum of absolute derivatives with respect to all backward Gaussian slots \\\\(\\\\eta_{b,s}\\\\). Initially \\\\(J_0=0\\\\). In (8), the derivative of \\\\(P_{a,k}\\\\) has absolute sum at most\\n\\\\[\\n1+(\\\\mathcal A_k+C_0T^3)J_k,\\n\\\\]\\nbecause \\\\(|\\\\phi'|\\\\le1\\\\). Differentiating (1) and using bounded \\\\(G,r,\\\\phi',\\\\phi''\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\nJ_{k+1}\\\\le\\n\\\\left(1+C\\\\Delta\\\\left[1+\\\\mathcal A_k+|\\\\eta_{1,k}|+|\\\\eta_{2,k}|\\\\right]\\\\right)J_k+C\\\\Delta.\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently,\\n\\\\[\\nJ_k\\\\le CT\\\\exp\\\\left(CT(1+\\\\mathcal A_{k-1})+C\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^2|\\\\eta_{b,r}|\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\nNo independence over time is used. Jensen's inequality applied to the average of the \\\\(2k\\\\) terms, followed by the scalar Gaussian exponential bound, gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\left(C\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^2|\\\\eta_{b,r}|\\\\right)\\n\\\\le 2\\\\exp(2C^2T^2C_0^2T^2).\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\nSince absolute expectation is bounded by expectation of absolute value, (4), (14), and (15) imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\le CT\\\\exp\\\\left(CT(1+\\\\mathcal A_{k-1})+CT^4\\\\right),\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\nafter increasing \\\\(C\\\\).\\n\\nNow estimate \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\) through the second-layer recursion. Let \\\\(V_k\\\\) be the maximum, over \\\\(Z_{a,r}^{(2)}\\\\) for \\\\(r\\\\le k\\\\), of the sum of absolute derivatives with respect to all forward Gaussian slots \\\\(\\\\xi_{b,s}\\\\). It is a nonnegative random variable, but the bound below is deterministic.\\n\\nThe readout is\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(3)}=-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{a=1}^2r_{a,r}\\\\phi(Z_{a,r}^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nTherefore its derivative absolute sum is at most \\\\(C_0T V_k\\\\). Differentiating \\\\(\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_{a,k}^{(2)})\\\\), using (10), yields\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{b,s}\\\\left|\\\\frac{\\\\partial\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}}{\\\\partial\\\\xi_{b,s}}\\\\right|\\\\le C_1T V_k.\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (7), whose memory uses only \\\\(s<k\\\\), yields\\n\\\\[\\nV_k\\\\le \\\\max\\\\{V_{k-1},\\\\,1+C_1T(\\\\mathcal C_k+C_0T)V_{k-1}\\\\}.\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\nInitially \\\\(V_0=1\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_0=0\\\\).\\n\\nChoose fixed constants \\\\(M_A=2C_1\\\\) and \\\\(M_C\\\\) large enough that the right side of (16) is at most \\\\(M_CT\\\\) whenever \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\le M_AT\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\le T_0\\\\). Then choose \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) small enough that\\n\\\\[\\nC_1T_0^2(M_C+C_0)\\\\le\\\\tfrac12.\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\nAn induction over \\\\(k\\\\) now proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\le M_AT,\\\\qquad \\\\mathcal C_k\\\\le M_CT,\\\\qquad V_k\\\\le2,\\n\\\\qquad k\\\\Delta\\\\le T\\\\le T_0.\\n\\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed the bound for \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\) supplies the bound for \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\) in (16); (18)-(19) then give \\\\(V_k\\\\le2\\\\); (17) gives \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\le2C_1T\\\\).\\n\\nCombining (12) and (20), every backward coordinate is a centered Gaussian of variance at most \\\\(C T^2\\\\), plus a bounded (possibly dependent) remainder of absolute value at most \\\\(CT\\\\). Thus there are fixed \\\\(c,C>0\\\\) such that\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta>0}\\\\sup_{k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\\\max_a\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\left(c|P_{a,k}|^2/T_0^2\\\\right)\\\\le C.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is the desired mesh-uniform Gaussian-tail bound. It was proved from bounded second-layer coordinate derivatives and a short-time response bootstrap. It was not assumed merely from fixed-program moment convergence.\\n\\n## Consequence for the missing stability estimate\\n\\nFor any first-layer field difference \\\\(D\\\\), boundedness and Lipschitz continuity of \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) imply\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z)|\\\\le C\\\\min(|D|,1).\\n\\\\]\\nIf the reference backward field \\\\(P\\\\) obeys (21), split at \\\\(|P|=R\\\\):\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[P^2\\\\min(D^2,1)]\\\\le R^2\\\\mathbb E[D^2]+C e^{-cR^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nChoosing \\\\(R^2\\\\) proportional to \\\\(1+\\\\log(1/\\\\|D\\\\|_{L^2})\\\\) gives, for sufficiently small \\\\(d=\\\\|D\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|P(\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z))\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le C d\\\\sqrt{1+\\\\log(1/d)}.\\n\\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\nThe other parts of the vector field remain Lipschitz in the original mean-square/operator state distance, because activations and readout are bounded. Only the reference field needs (21): split the product as \\\\(\\\\phi'(Z)(P-\\\\widetilde P)+(\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z))\\\\widetilde P\\\\).\\n\\nThus two population Euler paths on the common population operator space satisfy an integral stability inequality with modulus\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\omega(d)=Cd\\\\sqrt{1+\\\\log(1/d)}\\n\\\\]\\nnear zero, plus their vanishing local Euler defects. The integral \\\\(\\\\int_{0+}du/\\\\omega(u)\\\\) diverges. This is the elementary uniqueness mechanism: integrate the differential inequality for \\\\(\\\\int du/\\\\omega(u)\\\\), or use a positive additive error and let it decrease to zero.\\n\\nPopulation Euler paths have uniformly bounded velocities in mean square/operator norm. Their displacement within a step is \\\\(O(\\\\Delta)\\\\). In the nonlinear first-layer term, use the grid-point backward field in (22); the difference of the current and grid-point backward fields is already \\\\(O(\\\\Delta)\\\\) in mean square by boundedness of the operator and readout. The integrated Euler defect is therefore at most\\n\\\\[\\nC\\\\Delta\\\\sqrt{1+\\\\log(1/\\\\Delta)},\\n\\\\]\\nand tends to zero. This gives a Cauchy family of population Euler paths on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), hence a solution there. The same estimate gives uniqueness against any other solution on a bounded state set, because it requires Gaussian tails only for one reference solution; the constructed solution inherits (21) by convergence and lower semicontinuity.\\n\\nTransferring this local flow to finite-width GD still requires writing the reference-oracle localization argument carefully. A useful formulation is that for fixed mesh and threshold \\\\(R\\\\), finite-query convergence transfers each reference oracle's empirical truncated squared tail to its population value. Stability against an arbitrary actual finite trajectory then uses the reference oracle's tail only. No sub-Gaussian estimate for the actual finite trajectory is needed. One first takes width to infinity at fixed mesh and fixed threshold, then removes the mesh and threshold together using (21)-(22). The root agent should verify the exact recurrence before claiming this final transfer.\\n\\nNo global-in-time bound has been established here. The bootstrap (19) explicitly uses small total time and zero initial readout. Restarting would require controlling the existing response memory or a separate continuation argument. A statement for every fixed \\\\(T\\\\) is not justified by this note alone.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bef2c941087d296fb00d1c6ff98f4\",\"summary\":[\"**Running blind audits**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_JjSML2mCC1DX0bg3Y2E6j3V2\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0711e-554f-7be0-b1cc-532fee72f352\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_tail_audit_a\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_KT5i2xjqnrQGtcE9PWsVb7Rz\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0711e-71c6-7653-af61-4da8d667443c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_tail_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bef3c754c87d2acce012299582b29\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing full extension draft**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bef47774c87d2bee61649d5fcb999\",\"summary\":[\"**Drafting short-time proof**\",\"**Proving kernel convergence**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0711a-60a0-7f13-b290-ec53248355fe\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07116-dae2-7ec3-ab96-7da423abb3e5\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_limit_route\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bef58d33887d2ab8f0cff60bbf2f7\",\"summary\":[\"**Bounding W2 interpolation error**\",\"**Assembling kernel proof**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bef6cbef487d28aca433d22e9785f\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing extended TP source**\",\"**Checking finite-program consistency**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bef7c740c87d29e6f92cc288e7522\",\"summary\":[\"**Bounding actual–oracle error**\",\"**Constructing dense program algebra**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0711e-558f-7670-946b-9495c6224458\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0711e-554f-7be0-b1cc-532fee72f352\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_tail_audit_a\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bef8d79cc87d2b55806f45a4b7cd9\",\"summary\":[\"**Establishing L2 product continuity**\",\"**Justifying velocity convergence**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9befa0244887d289636eeebc205fbf\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking appendix proof details**\",\"**Verifying exchange symmetry**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9befacf65887d2be95f64e99abab05\",\"summary\":[\"**Assembling master file**\",\"**Preparing master proof**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0711e-7206-7ac2-8961-ea848c658bf3\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0711e-71c6-7653-af61-4da8d667443c\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_tail_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-f08dbf92-0df7-4518-a3e7-17f86a1e5473\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,245p' /tmp/two-input-shorttime-route.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# A proposed short-time extension through Gaussian response bounds\\n\\nThis note concerns ordinary GD with two inputs, not preconditioned GD. Its purpose is to supply the missing mesh-uniform bound on backward fields. The exact Gaussian response identity below is the specialization of the smooth finite-program Gaussian conditioning rule. The response estimates themselves are derived here. They give a local-in-time route; no global continuation claim is made.\\n\\nLet the input Gram matrix be\\n\\\\[\\nG=\\\\begin{pmatrix}1&\\\\rho\\\\\\\\\\\\rho&1\\\\end{pmatrix},\\\\qquad |\\\\rho|<1,\\n\\\\]\\nand labels be \\\\(y_1,y_2\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). Use the summed loss \\\\(\\\\sum_{a=1}^2r_a^2\\\\); averaging the loss only rescales time. The first-layer population root \\\\((Z_{1,0}^{(1)},Z_{2,0}^{(1)})\\\\) is centered Gaussian with covariance \\\\(G\\\\). The shared readout starts at \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\).\\n\\nFor fixed mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta>0\\\\), population Euler obeys\\n\\\\[\\nH_{a,k}^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_{a,k}^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(2)}H_{a,k}^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_{a,k}^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\n\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_{a,k}^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nr_{a,k}=\\\\mathbb E[W_k^{(3)}H_{a,k}^{(2)}]-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k+1}^{(1)}=Z_{a,k}^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^2G_{ab}r_{b,k}\\\\phi'(Z_{b,k}^{(1)})P_{b,k},\\n\\\\qquad P_{b,k}:=(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}=W_k^{(2)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^2r_{b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,k}^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{b=1}^2r_{b,k}H_{b,k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\phi=\\\\arctan\\\\). All response derivatives below hold every deterministic coefficient in these finite recursions fixed, including residuals, contractions, and response coefficients. They do not differentiate through expectations or through the covariance law.\\n\\n## Exact finite-mesh Gaussian response representation\\n\\nIntroduce two centered jointly Gaussian collections \\\\(\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\), independent of one another. The first-layer root is independent of both. Their covariances are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\xi_{a,k}\\\\xi_{b,s}]=\\\\mathbb E[H_{a,k}^{(1)}H_{b,s}^{(1)}],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\eta_{a,k}\\\\eta_{b,s}]=\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}].\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThese are separate-layer probability spaces; taking them independent on a product space is only a convenient representation, not an assertion of a natural pairing of neurons across layers.\\n\\nDefine the deterministic coefficients\\n\\\\[\\nA_{ak,bs}:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}}{\\\\partial\\\\xi_{b,s}}\\\\right],\\\\quad s\\\\le k,\\n\\\\qquad\\nC_{ak,bs}:=\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial H_{a,k}^{(1)}}{\\\\partial\\\\eta_{b,s}}\\\\right],\\\\quad s<k.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nThe derivatives are symbolic derivatives of the displayed causal recursions, treating Gaussian slots as separate real arguments even when their realized joint covariance is singular. Then the exact limiting initial-matrix actions are\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}=\\n\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s<k}C_{ak,bs}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\n\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}A_{ak,bs}H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nExpanding the learned matrix gives the full equations\\n\\\\[\\nZ_{a,k}^{(2)}=\\n\\\\xi_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s<k}\\n\\\\left(C_{ak,bs}-2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\\\right)\\n\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)},\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nP_{a,k}=\\n\\\\eta_{a,k}+\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}\\n\\\\left(A_{ak,bs}-\\\\mathbf1_{s<k}2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}]\\\\right)\\nH_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nTogether with (1), the readout update in (2), and \\\\(\\\\delta=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)})\\\\), these are causal finite recursions. To construct step \\\\(k\\\\): first construct \\\\(H_{a,k}^{(1)}\\\\) and its derivatives using \\\\(\\\\eta\\\\)-slots through \\\\(k-1\\\\); next construct \\\\(Z_{a,k}^{(2)},\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\) using \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\)-slots through \\\\(k\\\\); finally construct \\\\(P_{a,k}\\\\) and update first-layer coordinates and readout. The Gaussian covariance matrices extend consistently because they are Gram matrices of the constructed source variables.\\n\\nWhy the Gaussian response rule has this form: conditioning on previous forward and transpose products gives the Gaussian matrix regression formula with a projected fresh Gaussian matrix. In the limit, its Gaussian parts in each orientation have covariance equal to the Gram matrix of the sources. Its non-Gaussian part lies in the span of the previous sources in the opposite orientation. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the coefficients of this response as the expectations in (4). The elementary identity used is, for a centered Gaussian vector \\\\(g\\\\) with covariance \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[g_i f(g)]=\\\\sum_j\\\\Sigma_{ij}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\partial_j f(g)].\\n\\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nIt is valid even for singular \\\\(\\\\Sigma\\\\), by writing \\\\(g=B\\\\gamma\\\\) and integrating by parts in independent standard Gaussian coordinates \\\\(\\\\gamma\\\\). If the conditional regression uses a pseudoinverse, the symbolic derivative coefficients and the pseudoinverse coefficients can differ only by a vector in the covariance nullspace; the corresponding combination of source variables has zero squared norm, so the response itself is unchanged. This is the singular-covariance issue that must be retained in a complete proof.\\n\\nFor a rigorous external formulation of this finite-program identity, the locally available primary source is Tensor Programs III, `/tmp/tp3src/NetsorT2.tex`, definitions ZHat/ZDot and the partial-derivative convention, together with its master theorem. The algebraic recursions here are smooth, polynomially bounded functions of finitely many Gaussian slots, and their symbolic derivatives are polynomially bounded for fixed program length. Thus all expectations in (4) exist. The finite-query theorem handles fixed program length; the estimates below are the additional ingredient needed uniformly as length increases. This note does not purport to reprove the entire master theorem in the preceding paragraph.\\n\\n## Uniform short-time response bounds\\n\\nFix \\\\(T\\\\le1\\\\) and \\\\(k\\\\Delta\\\\le T\\\\). Constants in this section depend only on bounds for \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi',\\\\phi''\\\\), on \\\\(G\\\\), and on the number of inputs; they do not depend on \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), \\\\(k\\\\), or the Gaussian covariance ranks.\\n\\nPut \\\\(B_k=\\\\|W_k^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\). Since \\\\(|r_{a,k}|\\\\le1+\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty B_k\\\\), (2) gives\\n\\\\[\\nB_{k+1}\\\\le B_k+4\\\\Delta\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty(1+\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty B_k).\\n\\\\]\\nThe resulting geometric sum gives, for a fixed \\\\(C_0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nB_k\\\\le C_0T,\\\\qquad |r_{a,k}|\\\\le C_0,\\\\qquad |\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}|\\\\le C_0T.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\nThe absolute row sums of the training-memory coefficients in (7) and (8) are therefore at most \\\\(C_0T\\\\) and \\\\(C_0T^3\\\\), respectively.\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A_k=\\\\max_{a,\\\\,r\\\\le k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s\\\\le r}|A_{ar,bs}|,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathcal C_k=\\\\max_{a,\\\\,r\\\\le k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s<r}|C_{ar,bs}|.\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (8) already gives the pathwise bound\\n\\\\[\\n|P_{a,k}-\\\\eta_{a,k}|\\\\le C_0(\\\\mathcal A_k+T^3),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\eta_{a,k}^2]\\\\le C_0^2T^2.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFirst estimate \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\) in terms of \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\). Let \\\\(J_k\\\\) be the maximum, over the first-layer coordinates at times through \\\\(k\\\\), of the sum of absolute derivatives with respect to all backward Gaussian slots \\\\(\\\\eta_{b,s}\\\\). Initially \\\\(J_0=0\\\\). In (8), the derivative of \\\\(P_{a,k}\\\\) has absolute sum at most\\n\\\\[\\n1+(\\\\mathcal A_k+C_0T^3)J_k,\\n\\\\]\\nbecause \\\\(|\\\\phi'|\\\\le1\\\\). Differentiating (1) and using bounded \\\\(G,r,\\\\phi',\\\\phi''\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\nJ_{k+1}\\\\le\\n\\\\left(1+C\\\\Delta\\\\left[1+\\\\mathcal A_k+|\\\\eta_{1,k}|+|\\\\eta_{2,k}|\\\\right]\\\\right)J_k+C\\\\Delta.\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently,\\n\\\\[\\nJ_k\\\\le CT\\\\exp\\\\left(CT(1+\\\\mathcal A_{k-1})+C\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^2|\\\\eta_{b,r}|\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\nNo independence over time is used. Jensen's inequality applied to the average of the \\\\(2k\\\\) terms, followed by the scalar Gaussian exponential bound, gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\left(C\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^2|\\\\eta_{b,r}|\\\\right)\\n\\\\le 2\\\\exp(2C^2T^2C_0^2T^2).\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\nSince absolute expectation is bounded by expectation of absolute value, (4), (14), and (15) imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\le CT\\\\exp\\\\left(CT(1+\\\\mathcal A_{k-1})+CT^4\\\\right),\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\nafter increasing \\\\(C\\\\).\\n\\nNow estimate \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\) through the second-layer recursion. Let \\\\(V_k\\\\) be the maximum, over \\\\(Z_{a,r}^{(2)}\\\\) for \\\\(r\\\\le k\\\\), of the sum of absolute derivatives with respect to all forward Gaussian slots \\\\(\\\\xi_{b,s}\\\\). It is a nonnegative random variable, but the bound below is deterministic.\\n\\nThe readout is\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(3)}=-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{a=1}^2r_{a,r}\\\\phi(Z_{a,r}^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nTherefore its derivative absolute sum is at most \\\\(C_0T V_k\\\\). Differentiating \\\\(\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}=W_k^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_{a,k}^{(2)})\\\\), using (10), yields\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{b,s}\\\\left|\\\\frac{\\\\partial\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}}{\\\\partial\\\\xi_{b,s}}\\\\right|\\\\le C_1T V_k.\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (7), whose memory uses only \\\\(s<k\\\\), yields\\n\\\\[\\nV_k\\\\le \\\\max\\\\{V_{k-1},\\\\,1+C_1T(\\\\mathcal C_k+C_0T)V_{k-1}\\\\}.\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\nInitially \\\\(V_0=1\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_0=0\\\\).\\n\\nChoose fixed constants \\\\(M_A=2C_1\\\\) and \\\\(M_C\\\\) large enough that the right side of (16) is at most \\\\(M_CT\\\\) whenever \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\le M_AT\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\le T_0\\\\). Then choose \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) small enough that\\n\\\\[\\nC_1T_0^2(M_C+C_0)\\\\le\\\\tfrac12.\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\nAn induction over \\\\(k\\\\) now proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\le M_AT,\\\\qquad \\\\mathcal C_k\\\\le M_CT,\\\\qquad V_k\\\\le2,\\n\\\\qquad k\\\\Delta\\\\le T\\\\le T_0.\\n\\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed the bound for \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_{k-1}\\\\) supplies the bound for \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_k\\\\) in (16); (18)-(19) then give \\\\(V_k\\\\le2\\\\); (17) gives \\\\(\\\\mathcal A_k\\\\le2C_1T\\\\).\\n\\nCombining (12) and (20), every backward coordinate is a centered Gaussian of variance at most \\\\(C T^2\\\\), plus a bounded (possibly dependent) remainder of absolute value at most \\\\(CT\\\\). Thus there are fixed \\\\(c,C>0\\\\) such that\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta>0}\\\\sup_{k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\\\max_a\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\left(c|P_{a,k}|^2/T_0^2\\\\right)\\\\le C.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is the desired mesh-uniform Gaussian-tail bound. It was proved from bounded second-layer coordinate derivatives and a short-time response bootstrap. It was not assumed merely from fixed-program moment convergence.\\n\\n## Consequence for the missing stability estimate\\n\\nFor any first-layer field difference \\\\(D\\\\), boundedness and Lipschitz continuity of \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) imply\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z)|\\\\le C\\\\min(|D|,1).\\n\\\\]\\nIf the reference backward field \\\\(P\\\\) obeys (21), split at \\\\(|P|=R\\\\):\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[P^2\\\\min(D^2,1)]\\\\le R^2\\\\mathbb E[D^2]+C e^{-cR^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nChoosing \\\\(R^2\\\\) proportional to \\\\(1+\\\\log(1/\\\\|D\\\\|_{L^2})\\\\) gives, for sufficiently small \\\\(d=\\\\|D\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|P(\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z))\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le C d\\\\sqrt{1+\\\\log(1/d)}.\\n\\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\nThe other parts of the vector field remain Lipschitz in the original mean-square/operator state distance, because activations and readout are bounded. Only the reference field needs (21): split the product as \\\\(\\\\phi'(Z)(P-\\\\widetilde P)+(\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z))\\\\widetilde P\\\\).\\n\\nThus two population Euler paths on the common population operator space satisfy an integral stability inequality with modulus\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\omega(d)=Cd\\\\sqrt{1+\\\\log(1/d)}\\n\\\\]\\nnear zero, plus their vanishing local Euler defects. The integral \\\\(\\\\int_{0+}du/\\\\omega(u)\\\\) diverges. This is the elementary uniqueness mechanism: integrate the differential inequality for \\\\(\\\\int du/\\\\omega(u)\\\\), or use a positive additive error and let it decrease to zero.\\n\\nPopulation Euler paths have uniformly bounded velocities in mean square/operator norm. Their displacement within a step is \\\\(O(\\\\Delta)\\\\). In the nonlinear first-layer term, use the grid-point backward field in (22); the difference of the current and grid-point backward fields is already \\\\(O(\\\\Delta)\\\\) in mean square by boundedness of the operator and readout. The integrated Euler defect is therefore at most\\n\\\\[\\nC\\\\Delta\\\\sqrt{1+\\\\log(1/\\\\Delta)},\\n\\\\]\\nand tends to zero. This gives a Cauchy family of population Euler paths on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), hence a solution there. The same estimate gives uniqueness against any other solution on a bounded state set, because it requires Gaussian tails only for one reference solution; the constructed solution inherits (21) by convergence and lower semicontinuity.\\n\\nTransferring this local flow to finite-width GD still requires writing the reference-oracle localization argument carefully. A useful formulation is that for fixed mesh and threshold \\\\(R\\\\), finite-query convergence transfers each reference oracle's empirical truncated squared tail to its population value. Stability against an arbitrary actual finite trajectory then uses the reference oracle's tail only. No sub-Gaussian estimate for the actual finite trajectory is needed. One first takes width to infinity at fixed mesh and fixed threshold, then removes the mesh and threshold together using (21)-(22). The root agent should verify the exact recurrence before claiming this final transfer.\\n\\nNo global-in-time bound has been established here. The bootstrap (19) explicitly uses small total time and zero initial readout. Restarting would require controlling the existing response memory or a separate continuation argument. A statement for every fixed \\\\(T\\\\) is not justified by this note alone.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-4ef13028-cc0a-4aa1-8fa3-64420f5a6a91\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Two inputs: a rigorous local-time joint width and gradient-flow limit\\n\\n## Result and scope\\n\\nFix two inputs \\\\(x_1,x_2\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a\\\\|_2^2}{d}=1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\rho=\\\\frac{x_1^\\\\top x_2}{d}\\\\in(-1,1),\\\\qquad\\nG=\\\\begin{pmatrix}1&\\\\rho\\\\\\\\\\\\rho&1\\\\end{pmatrix}.\\n\\\\]\\nLet \\\\(y_1,y_2\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all the stated angles and both label patterns.\\n\\nPredictions, residuals, loss, and all entries of all three kernel blocks converge in probability uniformly on this interval. The joint two-input hidden path laws converge with their second moments in the uniform path norm. Integrated squared hidden velocities converge. The joint empirical averages of any fixed finite collection of continuous, globally Lipschitz forward/adjoint probes, with bounded products when a product instruction is used, converge to their corresponding population expectations, uniformly in time. Unbounded first-layer backward fields and their quadratic contractions are included by the tail argument below.\\n\\nOn a possibly smaller interval \\\\((0,T_*]\\\\), each input's preactivations move in both hidden layers, all three \\\\(2\\\\times2\\\\) kernel blocks are positive definite, the total kernel is nonconstant, loss strictly decreases, and \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) has positive best-affine-fit error on each hidden preactivation distribution.\\n\\n**Scope:** for nonorthogonal inputs this proof establishes a fixed positive time interval, not convergence on every compact time interval. The latter remains unproved here. When \\\\(\\\\rho=0\\\\), the earlier cubic-coordinate argument does extend to every finite time interval. No optimizer preconditioning is being introduced.\\n\\n## The finite network and its exact updates\\n\\nThe dimensions are \\\\(W^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times d}\\\\), \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\). For input \\\\(a=1,2\\\\), use\\n\\\\[\\nz_a^{(1)}=\\\\frac{W^{(1)}x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nz_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi=\\\\arctan,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h_a^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad\\nr_{n,a}=f_{n,a}-y_a,\\\\qquad L_n=r_{n,1}^2+r_{n,2}^2.\\n\\\\]\\nThe summed loss retains the earlier factors of two; using the average loss halves the speed.\\n\\nAll initialization entries are independent:\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ij}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently each first-layer root pair is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), independently over neuron index \\\\(i\\\\). The two preactivations for one neuron are not independent unless \\\\(\\\\rho=0\\\\).\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta_a^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_{n,a}/\\\\partial z_a^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\).\\nIn the displayed rescaled parameterization the layer learning-rate multipliers are \\\\(n,1,n\\\\). The exact first-weight update is\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n=W_k^{(1)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{\\\\sqrt d}\\n\\\\sum_{b=1}^2r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)}x_b^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\nMultiplication by \\\\(x_a/\\\\sqrt d\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=z_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{n}\\n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_b r_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)} .\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M1}\\n\\\\]\\nUse physical time \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), linearly interpolate these parameters, and recompute the forward pass at intermediate times.\\n\\nThe whole effect of the input geometry on first-layer training is the matrix \\\\(G\\\\). For example, the update of \\\\(z_1^{(1)}\\\\) contains its own gradient and \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\) times input 2's gradient.\\n\\nThe finite continuous flow has the kernel blocks\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}_{n,ab}\\n=G_{ab}\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(1)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(1)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(2)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(1)})^\\\\top h_b^{(1)}}n\\n \\\\frac{(\\\\delta_a^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_b^{(2)}}n,\\\\quad\\nK^{(3)}_{n,ab}\\n=\\\\frac{(h_a^{(2)})^\\\\top h_b^{(2)}}n .\\n\\\\tag{M2}\\n\\\\]\\nDifferentiation gives \\\\(\\\\dot f_n=-2K_n r_n\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\dot L_n=-4r_n^\\\\top K_n r_n\\\\), where \\\\(K_n\\\\) is the sum of the blocks. Each block is positive semidefinite: it is the Gram matrix of the corresponding scaled parameter gradients. Individual residual magnitudes need not decrease; their squared sum does.\\n\\n## Why a new stability estimate is needed\\n\\nWith \\\\(F(s)=s+s^3/3\\\\), the componentwise transformation \\\\(X_a^{(1)}=F(Z_a^{(1)})\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot X_a^{(1)}\\n=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\n\\\\frac{1+(Z_a^{(1)})^2}{1+(Z_b^{(1)})^2}\\n(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{M3}\\n\\\\]\\nFor \\\\(\\\\rho=0\\\\) the ratios cancel. For \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\ne0\\\\) they do not, and are unbounded. We therefore work with the original preactivations and prove a uniform tail estimate for the backward fields.\\n\\nThe exact limiting equations will be\\n\\\\[\\nH_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H_a^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nH_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\quad\\nf_a=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H_a^{(2)}],\\\\quad r_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot Z_a^{(1)}&=-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\\\delta_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_b\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2\\\\sum_b r_bH_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M4}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\((u\\\\otimes v)V=u\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[vV]\\\\). This definition displays the normalization that replaces the finite factor \\\\(1/n\\\\). First-layer and second-layer coordinates belong to separate populations. \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) acts from the former to the latter, and its adjoint acts in reverse. Every expectation pairs coordinates of the same population.\\n\\nThe initial root pair is \\\\(N(0,G)\\\\), the population readout is zero, and the initial action \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) is the joint forward-and-transpose limit of the initial Gaussian matrix, constructed next. The state consists of two first-layer fields and the shared matrix action and readout field. It has finitely many kinds of state objects, not finitely many scalar degrees of freedom.\\n\\n## The fixed-mesh construction and the new tail lemma\\n\\nWe use the following finite Gaussian fact. For a fixed finite calculation from independent Gaussian roots and a Gaussian matrix of variance \\\\(1/n\\\\), with smooth polynomially bounded coordinate functions and finitely many calls to that matrix and its transpose, every same-layer joint empirical average of a continuous polynomially bounded measurement has a deterministic limit. The limiting action is Gaussian randomness plus the responses forced by earlier opposite-direction calls. The precise response coefficients and their derivative convention are given and used in the appendix below. This is the smooth specialization of Theorem 2.10 and Box 1 of [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685). Its hypotheses hold here: after fixing population scalar coefficients, each instruction and each derivative needed below is a polynomially bounded function of finitely many Gaussian variables. No claim of iid finite coordinates after matrix reuse is used.\\n\\nFor completeness, the underlying conditioning mechanism is as follows. If previous calls have revealed \\\\(W_0^{(2)}V=Y\\\\) and \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R\\\\), then the remaining Gaussian matrix is an independent Gaussian projected onto the directions orthogonal to the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) and \\\\(V\\\\). Its conditional mean obeys both recorded equations. A new product is the response fixed by these equations plus that projected Gaussian applied to the unexplored part of the new input. Gaussian integration by parts identifies the response coefficients. The finite Gaussian theorem makes this induction valid also with dependent query directions. We explicitly rely on this stated finite-program result, not on a claim that the infinite-time or growing-program-length theorem already follows from it.\\n\\nThese finite limits can be realized on two common population coordinate spaces. Here are the details relevant to the subsequent continuous-time argument. Take all the programs needed for rational meshes, their finite unions, and a countable closure under rational linear combinations, the coordinate operations in the proof, and bounded Lipschitz approximations. Their finite joint laws are consistent, so they can be realized jointly. Take the mean-square closure of their linear spans on each layer. Exact finite linear identities pass to these laws.\\n\\nA fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\) satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le2\\\\,9^{2n}e^{-nM^2/8}\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{M5}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed two \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets of the unit sphere each have at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points, and every fixed bilinear form of the matrix is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). This finite operator inequality and joint second-moment convergence imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2]\\n\\\\]\\nfor every generated \\\\(V\\\\). Thus the action is well-defined on equal-in-mean-square variables and extends to the closure. The same reasoning applies to the transpose. The exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u)^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\npasses to the limit and identifies the two actions as adjoints. Bounded coordinate multipliers and their required products belong to these spaces by clipping and mean-square approximation. This constructs the common bounded-operator realization used below; it is not an additional assumption.\\n\\nDefine population Euler for (M4) in this common realization, starting from the stated initialization. Its rank expansion is\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}\\n-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\\\sum_b\\nr_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s}^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{M6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus each fixed mesh is a finite Gaussian calculation with deterministic population residuals and contractions. Iterative construction is causal: the current fields determine their expectations and then the next fields. Its realization agrees with the finite Gaussian law, because applying the same rank expansion reduces each action to an initial-matrix call and those deterministic contractions.\\n\\nThe appendix proves the essential new estimate:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta:\\\\,\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\\\ \\n\\\\max_{a,k:\\\\,k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\nc\\\\left|(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\\\right|^2\\n\\\\right)\\\\le C .\\n\\\\tag{M7}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(T_0,c,C>0\\\\) do not depend on the mesh. In particular the squared tail beyond \\\\(R\\\\) is at most \\\\(C e^{-c'R^2}\\\\), after reducing \\\\(c'\\\\).\\n\\nThe appendix is a derivation, not an assumed light-tail condition. It controls the accumulated deterministic response coefficients on both layers. The second-layer derivatives stay bounded; the first-layer derivatives have a Gaussian exponential bound. A short-time induction closes these bounds uniformly in the number of Euler steps.\\n\\n## Population Euler converges to a unique flow\\n\\nFor comparing two population states use\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\sum_{a=1}^2\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|Z_a^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb E|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}|^2\\\\bigr)^{1/2}.\\n\\\\tag{M8}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each mean-square norm by the Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\).\\n\\nAll Euler trajectories, and all the actual discrete trajectories with \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\le1\\\\), have bounds independent of width on a fixed bounded time interval, whenever the initial readout supremum and matrix operator norm are bounded. To see this, boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n|r_a|\\\\le 1+\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}_{k+1}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\nC\\\\eta_n(1+\\\\|W^{(3)}_k\\\\|_\\\\infty).\\n\\\\]\\nThe geometric sum bounds the readout supremum. The rank-one update then bounds the matrix operator norm, and the first-layer update bounds the root-mean-square velocity and displacement. The same calculation applies to population Euler with \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\). All initial bounds hold with probability tending to one for the finite initialization.\\n\\nOn these bounded sets, the forward quantities, residuals, second-layer backward fields, and the maps\\n\\\\[\\n(Z_1^{(1)},Z_2^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\n\\\\longmapsto (W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\nare Lipschitz in (M8). This follows by adding and subtracting one factor at a time, using boundedness and Lipschitz continuity of \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\), the readout supremum bound, and the operator bound.\\n\\nOnly the first-layer multiplication needs a separate estimate. Write\\n\\\\[\\nP_a=(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\widetilde P_a=(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThen\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})P_a-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)})\\\\widetilde P_a\\n=\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(P_a-\\\\widetilde P_a)\\n+[\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z_a^{(1)})]\\\\widetilde P_a.\\n\\\\]\\nSplit the last term at \\\\(|\\\\widetilde P_a|=R\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) is bounded and Lipschitz,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|[\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z)]\\\\widetilde P\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le C R\\\\|Z-\\\\widetilde Z\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+C\\\\|\\\\widetilde P\\\\,1_{\\\\{|\\\\widetilde P|>R\\\\}}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{M9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\). Only the reference field needs a tail bound. For a reference Euler grid point, (M7) bounds the final term by \\\\(C e^{-cR^2}\\\\).\\n\\nAn Euler interpolant moves by at most \\\\(C\\\\Delta\\\\) in (M8) between grid points. Its backward field therefore differs from the grid field by at most \\\\(C\\\\Delta\\\\) in mean square, using the Lipschitz map above. Thus, at any intermediate time, the vector field differs from the interpolant's assigned grid velocity by at most\\n\\\\[\\nC(1+R)\\\\Delta+C e^{-cR^2}.\\n\\\\tag{M10}\\n\\\\]\\nThe same localization gives the comparison inequality for two meshes. Integration and the elementary Gronwall inequality yield, for \\\\(0<T\\\\le T_0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d(\\\\theta^\\\\Delta(t),\\\\theta^{\\\\Delta'}(t))\\n\\\\le\\nC e^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\left((1+R)(\\\\Delta+\\\\Delta')+e^{-cR^2}\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{M11}\\n\\\\]\\nFirst send both meshes to zero with \\\\(R\\\\) fixed. Then send \\\\(R\\\\) to infinity. The term \\\\(e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) vanishes, so the Euler paths are Cauchy uniformly in time in (M8).\\n\\nThe state spaces are complete, and the limit keeps the readout supremum and operator bounds. The coordinate product \\\\((Z,P)\\\\mapsto\\\\phi'(Z)P\\\\) is continuous from \\\\(L^2\\\\times L^2\\\\) to \\\\(L^2\\\\): split off the change of \\\\(P\\\\), then use boundedness of \\\\(\\\\phi'\\\\) and dominated convergence against the fixed limiting \\\\(P^2\\\\). This allows passage to the integral form of (M4). The limit is therefore a solution, not just a limit of observables. It inherits (M7) by convergence in probability and Fatou's inequality; at intermediate grid times use the \\\\(O(\\\\Delta)\\\\) backward-field difference.\\n\\nThe same estimate compares this solution with any other solution of (M4) in the bounded state class with the same initial state. Only the constructed reference solution requires (M7). Their error is bounded by \\\\(C e^{C(1+R)T-cR^2}\\\\) for every \\\\(R\\\\), hence is zero. This proves uniqueness.\\n\\nThe equations use only the current fields and the current action and adjoint. In particular, their future evolution does not require the stored Euler history. More formally, the closed spaces generated by current fields, bounded coordinate operations, and current forward/adjoint actions contain the future: clipped vector fields and their Euler approximations remain there, and (M9) removes the clipping. Equal current joint action laws identify these generated spaces by a mean-square isometry, which intertwines their actions. Uniqueness therefore identifies their future action laws. This proves autonomy and restartability along the constructed interval.\\n\\n## From the population flow to the actual fine-step GD\\n\\nFix a coarse mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), independently of the actual step \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\). Build a finite oracle by expanding (M6), supplying it with the population residuals and every population contraction in these expansions. It starts from the same finite first-layer initialization and the same \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) as GD, but has \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\). Every oracle instruction now has deterministic scalar coefficients. The fixed finite Gaussian theorem applies jointly to all its nodes and proves convergence of all the needed empirical contractions and second moments.\\n\\nThere is a useful distinction between oracle nodes and an actual finite network state. Construct proxy parameter arrays using its first-layer nodes and readout, and the matrix\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k,\\\\mathrm{proxy}}^{(2)}\\n=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k,b}r_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(2)}\\n(h_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{M12}\\n\\\\]\\nApplying this proxy matrix to a current oracle activation differs from its prescribed oracle preactivation only by sums of empirical-minus-population contractions. For example each forward discrepancy has the form\\n\\\\[\\n-2\\\\Delta r_{b,s}\\\\delta_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(2)}\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\frac{(h_{b,s,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)})^\\\\top\\nh_{a,k,\\\\mathrm{oracle}}^{(1)}}{n}\\n-\\\\mathbb E[H_{b,s}^{(1)}H_{a,k}^{(1)}]\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{M13}\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose discrepancy has the corresponding contraction of two \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) fields. At a fixed mesh there are finitely many such terms. Their scalar errors tend to zero, and their vector norms stay bounded. The actual network quantities recomputed from the proxy arrays therefore differ from the oracle nodes by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) in root mean square. Bounded readout and the operator bound justify each subsequent forward/backward comparison. In particular its recomputed vector field differs from its prescribed oracle grid velocity by \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\).\\n\\nFor each fixed threshold \\\\(R\\\\), the oracle's empirical squared tails converge to their population counterparts. One may use continuous upper cutoffs, equal to one beyond \\\\(R\\\\) and zero below \\\\(R-1\\\\), to avoid discontinuities. The fixed finite Gaussian theorem applies to these quadratic-growth measurements. Consequently the root-mean-square tail of the recomputed proxy backward field has limit superior at most \\\\(C e^{-cR^2}\\\\), after modifying constants. This is proved for the reference oracle, not assumed for actual GD.\\n\\nLinearly interpolate the proxy parameters. Their metric speed is bounded in probability, with limiting bound independent of the mesh, by (M4) and the node consistency just proved. Their between-grid vector-field defect is bounded by (M10), plus a fixed-mesh \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\). Compare the actual GD interpolant to this proxy. Actual GD's derivative is its vector field at the preceding fine grid point; its state changes by \\\\(O(\\\\eta_n)\\\\) inside a fine step. Apply (M9) with the proxy as reference. No tail bound for the actual GD is needed. Gronwall gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t),\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{proxy},\\\\Delta}(t))\\n\\\\le C e^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\bigg(\\n&\\\\frac{\\\\|W_{n,0}^{(3)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n+(1+R)(\\\\eta_n+\\\\Delta)\\\\\\\\\\n&+e^{-cR^2}+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\n\\\\bigg).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\\\tag{M14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe \\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\) here is at fixed \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\). Bounds on the actual readout supremum are obtained from its update, not from root-mean-square closeness.\\n\\nThe initialization discrepancy vanishes, and \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). First take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\); next take \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\to0\\\\); finally take \\\\(R\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). Together with (M11), the fixed-mesh oracle empirical convergence, and the triangle inequality for observables, this proves the claimed joint limit. Thus this local proof needs no width-dependent learning-rate restriction beyond \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\).\\n\\nFor the first kernel block, use (M9) once more to transfer the first-layer \\\\(\\\\delta^{(1)}\\\\) fields in mean square; the other blocks follow directly from the bounded/Lipschitz maps. Products and contractions then converge by Cauchy–Schwarz. For hidden velocities,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot z_a^{(2)}\\n=\\\\dot W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)}\\n+W^{(2)}\\\\bigl[\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\\\odot\\\\dot z_a^{(1)}\\\\bigr].\\n\\\\]\\nThe reference first-layer velocity is a finite linear combination of bounded multiples of the backward fields, hence has the same tail control. Applying the same comparison proves convergence of both hidden velocities in integrated mean square and hence of their integrated squared norms.\\n\\nTo obtain path-law convergence, first use the finite Gaussian theorem on any fixed finite time grid. Between two times s\",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":35111}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":