e}}]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_tdD2xwhVnAIKOhLfevWnKQIP\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-5f05-7041-af97-2caadcc2099b\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_a\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_BsfMqYRjGZw1JQ6bQ6FIaszQ\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07122-9d1b-74c0-b969-867a8fdf2ab3\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-9cde-7bd2-a31d-1f5d3d5717c2\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_c\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_th6l9wRzbe7hpDxJdBeBEop8\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-9cde-7bd2-a31d-1f5d3d5717c2\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_c\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf0e0103c87d282fedc6982af71ab\",\"summary\":[\"**Waiting on proof checks**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07122-8318-71e0-a382-64eb9e0520e1\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-82d4-74a0-92c8-b1ee79e07427\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf0f171bc87d29e795075da39e443\",\"summary\":[\"**Creating five-pass audit**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a07122-5f46-7512-a2c3-e50def46bda8\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a07122-5f05-7041-af97-2caadcc2099b\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/two_input_full_audit_a\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf103dd0c87d2b7ca587e95110dc6\",\"summary\":[\"**Refining probe instructions**\",\"**Refining bounded probe rules**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf117b33c87d28fffa88d487e30c5\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing the review file**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf11efc2487d2b41fadd5c577bc7e\",\"text\":\"The complete local theorem passed three fresh, isolated adversarial reviews, including the arbitrary-angle dynamics, the GD comparison, and feature learning for both label patterns. The local proof also needs only \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\), so it covers the earlier learning-rate regime. I’ll state the short-time restriction prominently; the arbitrary-angle global result remains unproved here.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf12aa2a887d2b39abf193a268a55\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-2131d856-aca4-40a2-a352-110677865716\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -10,3 +10,3 @@\\n \\\\]\\n-Let \\\\(y_1,y_2\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all the stated angles and both label patterns.\\n+Let \\\\(y_1,y_2\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). There is \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that the ordinary, layer-scaled full-batch GD specified below has a unique autonomous population limit on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\), for every sequence of positive steps \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\). In particular this includes the earlier regime \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\) and the original choice \\\\(n^{-2}\\\\). The proof works for all the stated angles and both label patterns.\\n \\n@@ -579,2 +579 @@\\n This is the desired mesh-uniform Gaussian-tail bound. It was proved from bounded second-layer coordinate derivatives and a short-time response bootstrap. It was not assumed merely from fixed-program moment convergence.\\n-\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-bf25f7fc-3b85-4e64-a1d2-10abcc0b771e\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_REVIEW.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Independent review record\\n\\nDate: 2026-09-05.\\n\\nReviewed theorem: the local-time two-input ordinary-GD limit in `/tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md`, for every fixed input correlation in (-1,1), either label pattern, and positive steps eta_n tending to zero. An arbitrary-angle global-in-time theorem is explicitly not claimed.\\n\\nThe new mesh-uniform Gaussian response/tail lemma first received two independent isolated reviews. Both reviewers read only its standalone proof and its explicitly cited primary theorem source. Both returned PASS. They correctly required the common bounded-operator realization and the actual-GD transfer to be supplied separately.\\n\\nThe complete proof then received three fresh, independent, isolated adversarial reviews. Reviewers received only the complete proof and permission to verify its explicit primary theorem reference. They did not receive the discussion, earlier drafts, or other reviewers' conclusions.\\n\\n- `two_input_full_audit_a`: PASS. Checked normalization, common operator construction, tail lemma, ordered-limit actual-GD comparison, uniqueness, path/velocity convergence, and activity coefficients.\\n- `two_input_full_audit_b`: PASS. Checked the response recursion and mesh bootstrap, oracle consistency, one-reference-tail comparison, arbitrary vanishing learning rate, path laws, and kernel changes.\\n- `two_input_full_audit_c`: PASS. Checked both label patterns, all nonparallel angles, first reused-transpose covariance, motion for each input and hidden layer, positive kernel blocks, coefficient 8(A1+A2), and convergence architecture.\\n\\nNo material corrections were requested in the complete-proof round. The scope sentence was clarified during review to retain the earlier learning-rate condition for the separate contextual orthogonal all-time claim. After review, the word 'positive' was added explicitly to the learning-rate hypothesis, as suggested by a reviewer. It makes explicit the convention already used in all calculations.\\n\\nThese are independent mathematical reviews, not formal proof-assistant verification. Their PASS verdicts apply to the stated local theorem. They do not establish the arbitrary-angle all-compact-time extension, which remains unproved here.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-18a084bc-9c5d-4abd-9739-ca568fffa70c\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'wc -l -w /tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md /tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_REVIEW.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"  579  4293 /tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md\\n   17   278 /tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_REVIEW.md\\n  596  4571 total\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf13a56d487d292fc3d427e1d08bf\",\"summary\":[\"**Updating rank-two dynamics**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf14a34dc87d28008ea7fb5567520\",\"summary\":[\"**Setting up bootstrap bounds**\",\"**Defining the L2 norm**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf155ed6c87d2b13755c2769d4959\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking kernel growth terms**\",\"**Verifying feature-growth derivation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf165e2e487d294ecf287aac375a5\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9bf16b134487d2ae2fa03fa360548d\",\"text\":\"**Yes, on a fixed nonzero time interval, for every angle you specified and for either label pattern.** There is one important qualification: I have not extended the earlier *every finite time horizon* conclusion to nonorthogonal inputs. The result proved here is local in time.\\n\\nThe [complete proof](/tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_PROOF.md) passed three fresh, isolated adversarial reviews, following two independent checks of its main new lemma. The [review record](/tmp/TWO_INPUT_LOCAL_LIMIT_REVIEW.md) states their scope.\\n\\nHere is the result and the mathematical argument that makes the extension work.\\n\\n## The two-input system\\n\\nLet the inputs be \\\\(x_1,x_2\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d\\\\), with\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_1\\\\|_2^2}{d}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|x_2\\\\|_2^2}{d}\\n=1,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\rho=\\\\frac{x_1^\\\\top x_2}{d}\\\\in(-1,1).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTheir normalized dot products form the matrix\\n\\n\\\\[\\nG=\\n\\\\begin{pmatrix}\\n1&\\\\rho\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\rho&1\\n\\\\end{pmatrix}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet \\\\(y_1,y_2\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\). The two inputs use the **same weights**, but produce different preactivations:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_a^{(1)}=\\\\frac{W^{(1)}x_a}{\\\\sqrt d},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(1)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\nz_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h_a^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_a^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(a\\\\in\\\\{1,2\\\\}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\phi=\\\\arctan\\\\). Here \\\\(W^{(1)}\\\\) is \\\\(n\\\\times d\\\\), \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) is \\\\(n\\\\times n\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) has \\\\(n\\\\) coordinates.\\n\\nUse\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}\\n=\\n\\\\frac1n(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h_a^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nr_{n,a}=f_{n,a}-y_a,\\n\\\\qquad\\nL_n=r_{n,1}^2+r_{n,2}^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nI use the **sum** of the two squared errors to retain the previous factors of \\\\(2\\\\). Averaging the loss would simply halve the speed.\\n\\nInitialize independently by\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ij}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently, each first-layer neuron begins with a jointly Gaussian pair:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{pmatrix}\\nz_{1,0,i}^{(1)}\\\\\\\\\\nz_{2,0,i}^{(1)}\\n\\\\end{pmatrix}\\n\\\\sim N(0,G).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese pairs are independent across neurons. Within one pair, the correlation is precisely the input correlation \\\\(\\\\rho\\\\).\\n\\nAs before, define backpropagation without the residual:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}\\n=\\nW^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_a^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi'(z_a^{(1)})\\n\\\\odot\\n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWith the same layer scaling as the one-input system, exact GD becomes\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{a,k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=\\nz_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\nG_{ab}r_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=\\nW_k^{(2)}\\n-\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n}{n}\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\nr_{n,b,k}\\\\delta_{b,k}^{(2)}\\n(h_{b,k}^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n-\\n2\\\\eta_n\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\nr_{n,b,k}h_{b,k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first equation is the important change. For input 1, it reads\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_{1,k+1}^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_{1,k}^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\eta_n\\n\\\\left(\\nr_{n,1,k}\\\\delta_{1,k}^{(1)}\\n+\\n\\\\rho\\\\,r_{n,2,k}\\\\delta_{2,k}^{(1)}\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTraining on input 2 moves input 1’s preactivation because their first-layer weights are shared. Their angle determines the amount of this coupling.\\n\\n## The limiting result\\n\\nThere exists \\\\(T_0>0\\\\) such that, whenever\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_n>0,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\longrightarrow0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe discrete networks, on the clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), converge to a unique autonomous population evolution on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\).\\n\\nThus the local result includes—and actually requires less than—the previous condition \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\).\\n\\nThe population state consists of\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ_1^{(1)},\\\\qquad Z_2^{(1)},\\\\qquad W^{(2)},\\\\qquad W^{(3)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese are two first-layer fields, one shared matrix action, and one shared readout field. Everything else is computed from them:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nH_a^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(1)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\nZ_a^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H_a^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nH_a^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z_a^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_a=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H_a^{(2)}],\\n\\\\qquad\\nr_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_a^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(2)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_a^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi'(Z_a^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_a^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTheir evolution is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot Z_a^{(1)}\\n&=-2\\\\sum_{b=1}^2G_{ab}r_b\\\\delta_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}\\n&=-2\\\\sum_{b=1}^2r_b\\\\,\\n\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}\\n&=-2\\\\sum_{b=1}^2r_bH_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe rank-one notation means\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\otimes H_b^{(1)})V\\n=\\n\\\\delta_b^{(2)}\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[H_b^{(1)}V].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt is exactly the population counterpart of multiplying by\\n\\\\(\\\\delta_b^{(2)}(h_b^{(1)})^\\\\top/n\\\\).\\n\\nThe first-layer and second-layer populations remain separate. The operator \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) connects them; its adjoint connects them in reverse.\\n\\nFor example, for either hidden layer and every bounded Lipschitz measurement \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T_0}\\n\\\\left|\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\n\\\\psi\\\\!\\\\left(\\nz_{1,i}^{(\\\\ell)}(t),\\nz_{2,i}^{(\\\\ell)}(t)\\n\\\\right)\\n-\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\,\\n\\\\psi\\\\!\\\\left(\\nZ_1^{(\\\\ell)}(t),\\nZ_2^{(\\\\ell)}(t)\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\right|\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nin probability. This retains the **joint behavior of both inputs at the same neuron**, not merely their separate distributions.\\n\\nThe proof also establishes convergence of the forward and adjoint measurements, predictions, losses, kernel blocks, hidden path laws, and integrated squared hidden speeds.\\n\\n## What makes the proof work\\n\\nThe rank-one expansion and finite-mesh oracle construction survive the addition of a second input. The new difficulty is continuous-time stability.\\n\\nFor one input, setting \\\\(X^{(1)}=F(Z^{(1)})\\\\), where \\\\(F(s)=s+s^3/3\\\\), cancelled the activation derivative. With two inputs, the same calculation gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot X_a^{(1)}\\n=\\n-2\\\\sum_bG_{ab}r_b\\n\\\\frac{1+(Z_a^{(1)})^2}{1+(Z_b^{(1)})^2}\\n(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_b^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe diagonal ratios cancel. The cross-input ratios do not, and they are unbounded. So merely adding an input index to the old stability proof would leave a gap.\\n\\nThe new proof closes that gap by controlling the backward fields\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_{a,k}:=(W_k^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nuniformly as the auxiliary Euler mesh becomes finer.\\n\\nHere is the mechanism. At a fixed mesh, the initial-matrix transpose response has the form\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}\\n=\\ng_{a,k}^{(1)}\\n+\\n\\\\sum_{b=1}^2\\\\sum_{s\\\\le k}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial\\\\delta_{a,k}^{(2)}}\\n{\\\\partial g_{b,s}^{(2)}}\\n\\\\right]\\nH_{b,s}^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe \\\\(g^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(g^{(2)}\\\\) variables denote the Gaussian parts of backward and forward matrix calls. The derivatives track the dependence of the current coordinate on earlier Gaussian inputs, holding the supplied population coefficients fixed. This is the fixed-program Gaussian response rule; it does not itself supply a continuous-time limit. [Tensor Programs III, Box 1 and Theorem 2.10](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685)\\n\\nThe new calculation bounds the **total absolute size of these response coefficients**, uniformly in the number of Euler steps.\\n\\nThere are two mutually supporting bounds:\\n\\n- The second-layer coordinate derivatives are controlled because the activation and its derivatives are bounded, and \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) starts at zero.\\n- The first-layer derivatives involve Gaussian backward fields. Their accumulated growth is controlled using Gaussian exponential moments, without assuming independence across time.\\n\\nA short-time induction closes both bounds. Including the learned rank-one corrections, it proves\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_{a,k}\\n=\\n\\\\text{centered Gaussian}\\n+\\n\\\\text{bounded remainder},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith Gaussian variance at most \\\\(CT^2\\\\) and remainder bounded by \\\\(CT\\\\), uniformly over meshes with \\\\(k\\\\Delta\\\\le T\\\\le T_0\\\\). The remainder need not be independent of the Gaussian.\\n\\nIn particular,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{\\\\Delta}\\\\max_{a,k:\\\\,k\\\\Delta\\\\le T_0}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\exp(c|P_{a,k}|^2)\\n\\\\le C.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is a **proved property of the population Euler construction**, not an extra initialization assumption.\\n\\nWhy is this enough? In comparing two first-layer updates, the troublesome difference is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n[\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z)]\\\\,\\\\widetilde P.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSplit coordinates according to whether \\\\(|\\\\widetilde P|\\\\le R\\\\). On that set, ordinary Lipschitz continuity gives a factor \\\\(R\\\\). Outside it, the Gaussian tail gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sqrt{\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left|\\n[\\\\phi'(Z)-\\\\phi'(\\\\widetilde Z)]\\\\widetilde P\\n\\\\right|^2\\n}\\n\\\\le\\nCR\\\\sqrt{\\\\mathbb E|Z-\\\\widetilde Z|^2}\\n+\\nCe^{-cR^2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus a small fraction of large backward coordinates cannot spoil the comparison.\\n\\nAfter integrating the difference equations, the resulting error has the form\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{error}\\n\\\\le\\nCe^{C(1+R)T}\\n\\\\left[\\n(1+R)(\\\\eta_n+\\\\Delta)\\n+\\n\\\\text{oracle sampling error}\\n+\\ne^{-cR^2}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\napart from the vanishing initialization discrepancy.\\n\\nNow take the limits in this order:\\n\\n1. Fix \\\\(R,\\\\Delta\\\\) and let \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). The finite-mesh oracle sampling error vanishes, and \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\).\\n2. Let \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\to0\\\\).\\n3. Let \\\\(R\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\).\\n\\nThe last step works because\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne^{C(1+R)T}e^{-cR^2}\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe same estimate proves that population Euler paths approach a solution of the displayed differential equations, and that two solutions starting from the same state coincide. This establishes both sides of your original objective: **the finite system converges, and the limiting system genuinely determines its own evolution**.\\n\\nCrucially, the comparison needs the tail bound only for the reference oracle. It does not assume that the actual finite-network coordinates remain iid or have independently established Gaussian tails.\\n\\nFinally, this limit is genuinely feature-learning. Its kernel is now a \\\\(2\\\\times2\\\\) matrix, with blocks\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK_{ab}^{(1)}\\n=\\nG_{ab}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_a^{(1)}\\\\delta_b^{(1)}],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK_{ab}^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[H_a^{(1)}H_b^{(1)}]\\\\,\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_a^{(2)}\\\\delta_b^{(2)}],\\n\\\\qquad\\nK_{ab}^{(3)}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[H_a^{(2)}H_b^{(2)}].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWriting \\\\(K=K^{(1)}+K^{(2)}+K^{(3)}\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot r=-2Kr,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot L=-4r^\\\\top Kr.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe proof establishes, for each input and each hidden layer,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\n\\\\bigl(Z_a^{(\\\\ell)}(t)-Z_a^{(\\\\ell)}(0)\\\\bigr)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n=\\nc_{\\\\ell,a}t^4+o(t^4),\\n\\\\qquad c_{\\\\ell,a}>0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top K(t)y\\n=\\ny^\\\\top K(0)y+ct^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\qquad c>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSo both hidden layers move for both inputs, and the kernel changes. These strict inequalities hold for every \\\\(|\\\\rho|<1\\\\), whether the labels agree or oppose.\\n\\nThe remaining unproved extension is **global time for nonorthogonal inputs**. The argument above proves the complete autonomous joint limit and nonlinear feature learning on a positive time interval; it does not silently promote that local theorem to every finite horizon.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a06e39-7c55-7022-bbb0-02cf6116ca01\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1788555656,\"completedAt\":1788556614,\"durationMs\":957793,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a06e39-7e71-7723-bc30-0c316f3029ca\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"\\n# Files pasted by the user:\\n\\n## \\\"Yes. I’ll keep the same structure, but make it read like one proof rather than …\\\": /home/codex-b/.codex/attachments/690fe483-345a-48ce-a7db-b625c472070f/pasted-text.txt\\n## My request:\\nSo I think I understand the following proof, which is simpler because it's technically only one hidden layer not the two hidden layer proof you have above, but I much much more prefer the proof notaiton and style of argument, it's cleaner and to me more intuitive to understand, can you re-state the entire proof of the two layer result , but in a similar way to the attached proof? Make sure your output and proof are fully riogorous and valid, verifying the proof using independent and isolated and adversarial agents checking for mistakes and gaps (agents only see your proof to avoid biases), and do enough of these rounds until all agents pass cleanly. then output the resulting proof very cleanly and clearly and self contained, in a similar notation and style of writing as the notes attached\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9b318bb13087d28e6d1ecf2334bdd1\",\"text\":\"I’ll read the attached proof for its notation and style, then rebuild the two-hidden-layer argument. I’ll give independent reviewers only the candidate proof, revise any gaps they find, and repeat the review before presenting it.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-f4335556-a715-42a7-a364-d85dbe415957\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /etc/codex/skills/teach-technical-math/SKILL.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: teach-technical-math\\ndescription: Teach the mathematical content of technical documents—papers, reports, notes, appendices, and linked revisions—through rigorous, self-contained, one-concept-at-a-time lessons. Use when the user supplies or references one or more sources and wants to understand their definitions, equations, derivations, proofs, models, limiting constructions, evidence, assumptions, contradictions across versions, or open gaps over multiple turns; also use when they say “next block” or ask to rebuild a confusing part of such a lesson. Reconcile source versions before teaching, preserve stable typed notation, derive rather than paraphrase, and distinguish exact results from formal, heuristic, empirical, conjectural, and open claims.\\n---\\n\\n# Teach Mathematics from Technical Documents\\n\\nAct as a mathematical and theoretical teacher, not a summarizer. Teach a motivated non-specialist at genuine mathematical depth. Expose the simple structure beneath advanced formalism without suppressing difficult steps.\\n\\n## Prepare from the sources\\n\\nRead every source the user identifies in full mathematical detail before teaching source-dependent claims. If a source is inaccessible, incomplete, or missing cited material, state the limitation and do not imply that it was read.\\n\\nBuild an internal source ledger:\\n\\n- record each source's identity, date, and status, such as preliminary, revised, audited, or final;\\n- map notation and assumption changes across versions;\\n- identify the precise objects, problem, model, regime, observables, main claims, constructions, arguments, evidence, and unresolved gaps;\\n- separate compatible claims from incompatible ones;\\n- determine which claims survived later corrections or audits;\\n- retain theorem, equation, section, and page locations when available.\\n\\nDo not dump this ledger as an opening summary. Use it to teach the requested block faithfully. Attribute source claims and distinguish them from your own interpretation.\\n\\nMaintain an internal lesson ledger across turns: stable notation, completed blocks, the last point the user clearly understood, unresolved confusion, and the next logical block. Minimally recall any earlier definition needed in the current response.\\n\\n## Control the pace\\n\\nTeach exactly one natural conceptual block per response. If the user specifies a starting point, begin there. Otherwise begin with the document's objects, assumptions, and concrete setup.\\n\\nUse this domain-neutral progression as a guide:\\n\\n1. objects, assumptions, and setup;\\n2. exact concrete or finite formulation;\\n3. meaning and type of the central variables or maps;\\n4. derivation of the main relations;\\n5. proof or mechanism architecture;\\n6. obstruction, non-closure, or source of difficulty;\\n7. proposed abstraction, approximation, or limiting construction;\\n8. assumptions connecting the construction to the original problem;\\n9. theoretical and empirical evidence;\\n10. remaining gaps and falsifying cases.\\n\\nAdjust the sequence to the sources. Do not jump ahead merely because later material is related. When the user says “next block,” continue with only the next logically necessary idea. When the user is confused, stop advancing and rebuild from the last understood point.\\n\\nWrite each ordinary lesson turn as a compact, seamless blackboard explanation: a few short paragraphs, only the equations needed now, few or no bullets, and normally no headings. Do not begin or end with a broad recap. Stop naturally after the current block and wait for the user before moving deeper.\\n\\n## Construct each block\\n\\nUse this internal order without displaying it as a template:\\n\\n1. **Minimal recall:** restate only the definitions needed now.\\n2. **New object:** define one new object and give its mathematical type.\\n3. **Derivation:** obtain its relation from the preceding setup.\\n4. **Meaning:** explain the role of every term or condition.\\n5. **Analogue or example:** use one structurally faithful simple case when it genuinely helps, then return to the exact mathematics.\\n6. **Role:** connect the object to the document's argument.\\n7. **Status:** state what is established and what remains unproved.\\n\\nMake the block self-contained for its present conceptual burden, not for the entire project.\\n\\n## Enforce notation and type discipline\\n\\nUse a small, stable notation. Prefer the source's symbols unless they collide or obscure meaning. If renaming is necessary, state the mapping explicitly.\\n\\nDefine every symbol before using it. For each object, state as relevant:\\n\\n- its domain, codomain, or ambient space;\\n- whether it is a scalar, vector, matrix, function, operator, random variable, measure, index, or coordinate;\\n- whether it is fixed, sampled, or evolving;\\n- what each index means;\\n- its dependence on other variables or parameters.\\n\\nDistinguish carefully between:\\n\\n- one function of an entire vector and a vector of coordinatewise functions;\\n- an index and a coordinate;\\n- a fixed label or parameter and an evolving state;\\n- a representative element and a probability law over elements;\\n- a mathematical state and a numerical representation of that state.\\n\\nWhen writing an integral, identify the integration variable, its space, the measure, and what the integral averages or accumulates. When a distribution is specified only at initialization, do not imply that an evolved quantity retains that distribution merely because it remains a function of the initial label.\\n\\nDo not introduce shorthand unless the explicit expression is genuinely cumbersome and recurs often. Never reuse one symbol for two roles.\\n\\n## Derive rather than paraphrase\\n\\nFor every central equation or implication:\\n\\n1. state the definition or prior relation from which it follows;\\n2. show the relevant algebraic, differential, variational, probabilistic, logical, or limiting step;\\n3. identify the origin and action of every term;\\n4. explain the closest elementary analogue when useful;\\n5. state why the relation matters to the main result.\\n\\nDo not make the user reverse-engineer a displayed formula. Avoid phrases such as “clearly,” “standard,” or “it follows” when they conceal a nontrivial step.\\n\\nWhen invoking an external theorem, state the exact form needed, list its hypotheses, verify them in the present setting, and explain the resulting conclusion. Check a primary or otherwise authoritative source before relying on a recent, specialized, or uncertain result.\\n\\nWhen teaching a long proof, devote one block to its architecture before teaching the difficult steps. Explain how the claims, cases, or lemmas fit together without pretending that the architecture is itself the proof.\\n\\nUse analogies only when they preserve the relevant mathematical structure. State where the analogy stops and return immediately to the exact object.\\n\\n## Relate levels of description\\n\\nBegin with the document's concrete objects and exact relations. Introduce an abstract, aggregate, continuum, asymptotic, or numerical description only after showing how it arises from the concrete one.\\n\\nWhenever the source changes levels, identify:\\n\\n- the map from the original objects to the new description;\\n- what information is retained and discarded;\\n- which step is an exact identity;\\n- which step uses a theorem and its assumptions;\\n- which step is formal, heuristic, empirical, or conjectural.\\n\\nIf a proposed state or summary evolves autonomously, distinguish internal closure from fidelity to the original system. An autonomous reduced description may still omit information that changes the original system's future.\\n\\nIf the source uses truncation or approximation, distinguish pointwise or static approximation from the stronger control required by an evolving or iterative system. Examine separately:\\n\\n- error that leaves the retained representation;\\n- feedback from omitted components;\\n- amplification or accumulation of error;\\n- the time, parameter, or regime over which stability is claimed.\\n\\nWhen the source calls a construction “finite,” state precisely whether this means finitely many coordinates, finitely many fields, a finite-dimensional state, a finite cutoff, or merely a finite numerical discretization.\\n\\n## Label epistemic status\\n\\nClassify each important source-dependent claim using the most accurate category:\\n\\n- exact identity;\\n- proved theorem under stated assumptions;\\n- rigorous result for a restricted or finite setting;\\n- formal argument;\\n- heuristic mechanism;\\n- empirical or numerical evidence;\\n- conjectural identification;\\n- open implication;\\n- counterexample or negative result.\\n\\nDo not silently promote a claim. Express each important assumption as a missing implication:\\n\\n1. what is assumed;\\n2. what conclusion it would justify;\\n3. what connecting step is not established;\\n4. what observation or counterexample would refute it.\\n\\nInternal consistency of a proposed theory does not prove that it represents the target object.\\n\\n## Evaluate evidence and alternatives\\n\\nFor every numerical comparison, identify:\\n\\n- the systems or quantities compared;\\n- what the reference actually represents;\\n- the metric or norm;\\n- the scale of the predicted effect and the discrepancy;\\n- discretization, optimization, rounding, and sampling uncertainty as applicable;\\n- the alternative explanation ruled out;\\n- what the experiment leaves unresolved.\\n\\nDo not infer convergence or a scaling law from too few resolutions. Treat numerical thresholds as decision conventions unless the source proves a mathematical boundary.\\n\\nWhen the user requests a hostile or devil's-advocate analysis, include only alternatives that engage the declared target and make a distinct mathematical or quantitative prediction. Do not infer global behavior from a local or instantaneous property without proving the connecting step. Do not use possible error cancellation as a complete explanation unless it predicts the observed pattern.\\n\\n## Repair confusion completely\\n\\nWhen the user identifies an undefined symbol, ambiguity, missing derivation, or conceptual gap:\\n\\n- acknowledge the failure directly;\\n- do not defend the prior wording;\\n- do not append a one-sentence patch;\\n- rewrite the entire relevant block self-containedly;\\n- preserve compactness and stable notation.\\n\\nTreat confusion as evidence that the explanation needs rebuilding, not that the mathematics is inherently inaccessible.\\n\\nBefore sending each block, verify silently that it is faithful to the sources, introduces only one conceptual burden, defines every needed symbol, derives rather than paraphrases, states the status of important claims, stays in the user's language, avoids unnecessary notation and formula dumps, and is compact enough to absorb in one sitting.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-a98f901c-3b70-46e9-9292-f026e6aab857\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /home/codex-b/.codex/attachments/690fe483-345a-48ce-a7db-b625c472070f/pasted-text.txt'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"Yes. I’ll keep the same structure, but make it read like one proof rather than a sequence of emphasized slogans.\\n\\nWe work at width \\\\(n\\\\), but keep the \\\\(n\\\\)-dependence implicit. Every normalization by width is written explicitly.\\n\\nAt Euler step \\\\(k\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_k=W_kx_k,\\\\qquad D_k=\\\\tanh(z_k),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}=x_k-hW_k^\\\\top D_k,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}=W_k-\\\\frac hn D_kx_k^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe initialization is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_{i,0}\\\\overset{\\\\mathrm{iid}}{\\\\sim}N(0,1),\\n\\\\qquad\\n(W_0)_{ij}\\\\overset{\\\\mathrm{iid}}{\\\\sim}N\\\\!\\\\left(0,\\\\frac1n\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFix \\\\(h>0\\\\) and a time horizon \\\\(T\\\\). Then there are only finitely many Euler steps.\\n\\nFirst, iterate the matrix update:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k\\n=\\nW_0-\\\\frac hn\\\\sum_{r<k}D_rx_r^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore every forward call can be rewritten as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_kv\\n=\\nW_0v\\n-h\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nD_r\\\\left(\\\\frac1n x_r^\\\\top v\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand every backward call as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^\\\\top u\\n=\\nW_0^\\\\top u\\n-h\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nx_r\\\\left(\\\\frac1nD_r^\\\\top u\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus the only genuinely random matrix object that needs analysis is the initial Gaussian matrix \\\\(W_0\\\\). Everything training adds is a finite sum of previously constructed vectors multiplied by normalized scalar products.\\n\\nThe first obligation is therefore proved by induction over the finite sequence of matrix calls. Assume all vectors constructed so far have a joint population limit and that all required quantities such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n a^\\\\top b\\n\\\\]\\n\\nconverge. For the next use of \\\\(W_0\\\\) or \\\\(W_0^\\\\top\\\\), Gaussian conditioning decomposes the new output into a part forced by previous matrix calls plus a fresh Gaussian part in unexplored directions. The coefficients of the forced part are functions of finite Gram matrices such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1nV^\\\\top V,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac1nV^\\\\top v,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the variance of the fresh part is likewise determined by quantities such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}v\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll of those converge by the induction hypothesis. Hence the next matrix output has a joint limit with everything already constructed. Applying \\\\(\\\\tanh\\\\) then produces the next nonlinear vector, and the required normalized inner products also converge. Since there are only finitely many steps, the induction proves joint convergence of every quantity needed up to time \\\\(T\\\\).\\n\\nThe second obligation is to turn these limiting matrix responses into one actual population operator. Define an equivalence relation on finite-width probe sequences by\\n\\n\\\\[\\nv\\\\sim\\\\widetilde v\\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2\\\\to0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe population vector represented by \\\\(v\\\\) is its equivalence class \\\\([v]\\\\).\\n\\nWe then define the limiting initial operator by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathsf W_0[v]:=[W_0v].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis must be independent of the chosen representative. If \\\\(v\\\\sim\\\\widetilde v\\\\), then\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2\\\\to0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor the Gaussian initialization, the operator norm is bounded by a constant independent of width with probability tending to one:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W_0\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le C.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHence\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\n\\\\|W_0v-W_0\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2\\n\\\\le\\nC^2\\n\\\\frac1n\\n\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2\\n\\\\to0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSo \\\\([W_0v]=[W_0\\\\widetilde v]\\\\), and the operator is well-defined.\\n\\nLinearity is then purely algebraic:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\mathsf W_0(a[v]+b[q])\\n&=\\\\mathsf W_0[av+bq]\\\\\\\\\\n&=[W_0(av+bq)]\\\\\\\\\\n&=[aW_0v+bW_0q]\\\\\\\\\\n&=a\\\\mathsf W_0[v]+b\\\\mathsf W_0[q].\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe same operator-norm estimate also gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\|W_0v\\\\|_2^2\\n\\\\le\\nC^2\\\\frac1n\\\\|v\\\\|_2^2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhich becomes at population level\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\mathsf W_0V)^2]\\n\\\\le\\nC^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSo \\\\(\\\\mathsf W_0\\\\) is bounded and therefore extends uniquely to the \\\\(L^2\\\\)-closure of the generated population vectors.\\n\\nThe third obligation is to prove that the limiting backward action is the adjoint of this same operator. Define temporarily\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB[u]:=[W_0^\\\\top u].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe same norm argument makes \\\\(B\\\\) well-defined. At finite width,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n u^\\\\top W_0v\\n=\\n\\\\frac1n(W_0^\\\\top u)^\\\\top v\\n\\\\]\\n\\nexactly. Obligation 1 gives the corresponding population limits:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n u^\\\\top W_0v\\n\\\\to\\n\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,\\\\mathsf W_0V],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n(W_0^\\\\top u)^\\\\top v\\n\\\\to\\n\\\\mathbb E[(BU)V].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,\\\\mathsf W_0V]\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[(BU)V]\\n\\\\]\\n\\nfor all relevant \\\\(U,V\\\\). But the adjoint \\\\(\\\\mathsf W_0^*\\\\) is the unique map satisfying\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,\\\\mathsf W_0V]\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\mathsf W_0^*U)V].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHence \\\\(B=\\\\mathsf W_0^*\\\\).\\n\\nAt this point the fixed-\\\\(h\\\\) width limit is essentially complete. The trained population operator is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathsf W_k\\n=\\n\\\\mathsf W_0\\n-\\nh\\\\sum_{r<k}D_r\\\\otimes X_r,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(D\\\\otimes X)V\\n=\\nD\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[XV].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe corresponding population Euler recursion is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ_k=\\\\mathsf W_kX_k,\\n\\\\qquad\\nD_k=\\\\tanh(Z_k),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nX_{k+1}\\n=\\nX_k-h\\\\mathsf W_k^*D_k,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathsf W_{k+1}\\n=\\n\\\\mathsf W_k-hD_k\\\\otimes X_k.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nObligation 1 already proves that the finite-width Euler trajectory converges to this population recursion for fixed \\\\(h\\\\).\\n\\nThe final step is \\\\(h\\\\to0\\\\). Write the state as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nY=(X,\\\\mathsf W)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the population dynamics as\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot Y=F(Y),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF(X,\\\\mathsf W)\\n=\\n\\\\left(\\n-\\\\mathsf W^*\\\\tanh(\\\\mathsf WX),\\n\\\\;\\n-\\\\tanh(\\\\mathsf WX)\\\\otimes X\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFirst, both the Euler scheme and the continuous flow remain bounded on every finite time interval. Since \\\\(|\\\\tanh|\\\\le1\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|D\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\le1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor the flow,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{d}{dt}\\\\|X\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\|\\\\mathsf W\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{d}{dt}\\\\|\\\\mathsf W\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\|X\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS(t):=\\\\|X(t)\\\\|_{L^2}+\\\\|\\\\mathsf W(t)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nsatisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS'(t)\\\\le S(t),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS(t)\\\\le S(0)e^t.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor Euler, similarly,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS_{k+1}\\\\le(1+h)S_k,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand hence\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS_k\\\\le e^{kh}S_0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOn this bounded region, \\\\(F\\\\) is Lipschitz. The key estimate is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\tanh(\\\\mathsf WX)-\\\\tanh(\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathsf W}\\\\widetilde X)\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\|\\\\mathsf WX-\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathsf W}\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_{L^2},\\n\\\\]\\n\\ntogether with\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathsf WX-\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathsf W}\\\\widetilde X\\n=\\n\\\\mathsf W(X-\\\\widetilde X)\\n+\\n(\\\\mathsf W-\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathsf W})\\\\widetilde X.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe uniform bounds then give\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|F(Y)-F(\\\\widetilde Y)\\\\|\\n\\\\le\\nL_T\\\\|Y-\\\\widetilde Y\\\\|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nNow compare one exact flow step with one Euler step. The exact solution satisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\nY(t+h)\\n=\\nY(t)+\\\\int_t^{t+h}F(Y(s))\\\\,ds.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWrite this as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nY(t+h)\\n=\\nY(t)+hF(Y(t))+\\\\rho.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\rho\\n=\\n\\\\int_t^{t+h}\\n\\\\bigl(F(Y(s))-F(Y(t))\\\\bigr)\\\\,ds.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince \\\\(F\\\\) is bounded by some \\\\(M_T\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|Y(s)-Y(t)\\\\|\\n\\\\le\\nM_T(s-t),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand Lipschitz continuity gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|F(Y(s))-F(Y(t))\\\\|\\n\\\\le\\nL_TM_T(s-t).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\rho\\\\|\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\frac{L_TM_T}{2}h^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSo the local Euler error is \\\\(O(h^2)\\\\).\\n\\nFinally, let\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne_k=\\\\|Y_k-Y(kh)\\\\|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nComparing the exact and Euler updates gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne_{k+1}\\n\\\\le\\n(1+L_Th)e_k+C_Th^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince \\\\(e_0=0\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne_k\\n\\\\le\\nC_Th^2\\n\\\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}(1+L_Th)^j.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor \\\\(kh\\\\le T\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(1+L_Th)^k\\\\le e^{L_TT},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne_k\\\\le C_T' h.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus the population Euler recursion converges uniformly on finite time intervals to the continuous population flow as \\\\(h\\\\to0\\\\).\\n\\nThe whole argument is therefore: Gaussian conditioning proves the fixed-mesh joint width limit; equivalence classes plus the uniform operator-norm bound produce one genuine bounded population operator; the finite transpose identity identifies its adjoint; the trained matrix becomes a finite sum of rank-one population updates; and standard Euler stability then sends the mesh size to zero.\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-f282573f-37d8-4628-a81d-5071bffcd99f\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'wc -l /tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"1077 /tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3194962c87d2a110e5de50c0e424\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-5cb07002-7470-4b6c-8c01-d6c449aba92b\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,360p' /tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# A rigorous joint mean-field/gradient-flow limit for the two-hidden-layer arctangent network\\n\\n## Theorem\\n\\nFor every width \\\\(n\\\\), consider the one-sample network\\n\\\\[\\nA_i=W_i^{(1)},\\\\qquad H_i=\\\\phi(A_i),\\\\qquad\\nS_j=\\\\sum_{i=1}^nB_{ji}H_i,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nG_j=\\\\phi(S_j),\\\\qquad f_n=\\\\sum_{j=1}^nc_jG_j,\\n\\\\qquad L_n=(f_n-1)^2,                              \\\\tag{T1}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\phi(x)=\\\\arctan x\\\\).  Initialize all variables independently by\\n\\\\[\\nA_i(0)\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\qquad B_{ji}(0)\\\\sim N(0,n^{-1}),\\n\\\\qquad c_j(0)\\\\sim N(0,n^{-4}).                     \\\\tag{T2}\\n\\\\]\\nUse exact full-batch GD with base step and raw layer multipliers\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_n=n^{-2},\\\\qquad\\n(\\\\lambda_A,\\\\lambda_B,\\\\lambda_c)=(n,1,n^{-1}),       \\\\tag{T3}\\n\\\\]\\nLinearly interpolate the three parameter arrays on the clock\\n\\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), and at intermediate times recompute \\\\(H,S,G,f,L\\\\) from\\nthose interpolated parameters.\\n\\nThen, along the full sequence \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), the exact GD processes converge in probability, uniformly on every compact time interval, to a unique autonomous and restartable action-law gradient flow.  The empirical path laws of \\\\(A\\\\) and \\\\(S\\\\) converge in \\\\(W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\).  Outputs, losses, all three scaled kernel blocks, and both hidden velocity energies converge in the modes stated below.\\n\\nThere exists \\\\(T_*>0\\\\) such that, for every \\\\(0<T\\\\le T_*\\\\):\\n\\n1. both hidden marginal laws have finite strictly positive variance;\\n2. both hidden layers have finite positive integrated mean-square velocity and nonzero mean-square displacement;\\n3. all three parameter blocks have finite positive integrated scaled kernel;\\n4. the affine-regression residual of \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) is positive in both hidden layers;\\n5. \\\\(K(t)\\\\) is nonconstant and \\\\(L(T)<L(0)=1\\\\).\\n\\nIn particular, this is a genuinely nonlinear feature-learning limit, not a frozen-feature or affine limit.  Moreover\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\lambda_A=n^{-1},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\lambda_B=n^{-2},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\lambda_c=n^{-3},                            \\\\tag{T4}\\n\\\\]\\nso every raw parameter step tends to zero.\\n\\nThe proof has five bridges: exact normalization; a dimension-free regularized flow; fixed-mesh width identification by a finite population-oracle tensor program; a same-width Euler sandwich yielding the continuous action law; and a direct comparison of exact GD with that flow.  The final section proves strict activity and nonlinearity.\\n\\n## 1. Exact finite-width equations\\n\\nPut\\n\\\\[\\nC=nc,\\\\qquad \\\\langle u,v\\\\rangle_n=n^{-1}u^\\\\top v,\\n\\\\qquad (u\\\\otimes_n v)z=u\\\\langle v,z\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\nand write\\n\\\\[\\nq(x)=\\\\phi'(x)=\\\\frac1{1+x^2},\\\\qquad\\nD=C\\\\odot q(S),\\\\qquad P=B^\\\\top D,qquad e=f_n-1.     \\\\tag{1.1}\\n\\\\]\\nThen \\\\(f_n=\\\\langle C,G\\\\rangle_n\\\\).  Direct differentiation gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_n}{\\\\partial A_i}=\\\\frac1nq(A_i)P_i,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_n}{\\\\partial B_{ji}}=\\\\frac1nD_jH_i,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_n}{\\\\partial c_j}=G_j.             \\\\tag{1.2}\\n\\\\]\\nMultiplying the loss gradients by (T3) proves that the associated finite-width gradient flow is exactly\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot A=-2e\\\\,q(A)\\\\odot P,qquad\\n\\\\dot B=-2e\\\\,D\\\\otimes_nH,qquad\\n\\\\dot C=-2e\\\\,G.                                     \\\\tag{1.3}\\n\\\\]\\nThe three scaled tangent-kernel blocks are\\n\\\\[\\nK^A_n=\\\\|q(A)P\\\\|_n^2,qquad\\nK^B_n=\\\\|H\\\\|_n^2\\\\|D\\\\|_n^2,qquad\\nK^C_n=\\\\|G\\\\|_n^2,                                  \\\\tag{1.4}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_n^2=\\\\langle v,v\\\\rangle_n\\\\).  Thus, for \\\\(K_n=K_n^A+K_n^B+K_n^C\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f_n=-2eK_n,qquad \\\\dot L_n=-4e^2K_n.          \\\\tag{1.5}\\n\\\\]\\nIn particular, \\\\(|e(t)|\\\\le |e(0)|\\\\) along the flow.\\n\\nDifferentiating \\\\(S=BH\\\\) will also be useful:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot S=-2e\\\\left\\\\{\\\\|H\\\\|_n^2D+B[q(A)^2\\\\odot P]\\\\right\\\\}. \\\\tag{1.6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n## 2. The regularizing coordinate and dimension-free flow estimates\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\nF(a)=a+\\\\frac{a^3}{3},\\\\qquad X=F(A),\\\\qquad J=F^{-1},\\n\\\\qquad h(x)=\\\\arctan J(x).                           \\\\tag{2.1}\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(F'(a)=q(a)^{-1}\\\\), equation (1.3) becomes\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nA&=J(X),& H&=h(X),& S&=BH,\\\\\\\\\\nG&=\\\\arctan S,&D&=Cq(S),&e&=\\\\langle C,G\\\\rangle_n-1,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot X&=-2eB^\\\\top D,&\\n\\\\dot B&=-2eD\\\\otimes_nH,&\\n\\\\dot C&=-2eG.\\n\\\\end{aligned}}                                    \\\\tag{2.2}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is exact, not a linearization.  Also\\n\\\\[\\nJ'(x)=q(J(x)),\\\\qquad h'(x)=q(J(x))^2,               \\\\tag{2.3}\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(J,h,q\\\\circ J\\\\) are globally Lipschitz; \\\\(|H|,|G|\\\\le a_0:=\\\\pi/2\\\\).\\n\\nFor two states define\\n\\\\[\\nd_n(\\\\theta,\\\\widetilde\\\\theta)\\n=\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_n+\\n \\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}+\\n \\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n.                            \\\\tag{2.4}\\n\\\\]\\nOn a set on which \\\\(\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}},\\\\|\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le M_B\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\|C\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le M_C\\\\), the vector field in (2.2) is Lipschitz in (2.4), with a constant independent of \\\\(n\\\\).  Indeed,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|H-\\\\widetilde H\\\\|_n\\\\le\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_n,     \\\\tag{2.5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le a_0\\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n  +M_B\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_n,                       \\\\tag{2.6}\\n\\\\]\\nand\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|D-\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le \\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n\\n +M_C\\\\operatorname{Lip}(q)\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_n.  \\\\tag{2.7}\\n\\\\]\\nFurthermore\\n\\\\[\\n|e-\\\\widetilde e|\\n\\\\le a_0\\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n\\n +\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_n,        \\\\tag{2.8}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|B^\\\\top D-\\\\widetilde B^\\\\top\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le \\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\|D\\\\|_n\\n +M_B\\\\|D-\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_n.                        \\\\tag{2.9}\\n\\\\]\\nFinally \\\\(\\\\|u\\\\otimes_nv\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}=\\\\|u\\\\|_n\\\\|v\\\\|_n\\\\).  Substitution of (2.5)--(2.9) into (2.2) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|V_n(\\\\theta)-V_n(\\\\widetilde\\\\theta)\\\\|_{d_n}\\n\\\\le L(M_B,M_C,\\\\|C\\\\|_n,\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_n)d_n(\\\\theta,\\\\widetilde\\\\theta). \\\\tag{2.10}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe required bounded set is invariant on each compact interval, uniformly in width on a high-probability initialization event.  From (1.5), \\\\(|e(t)|\\\\le |e(0)|\\\\), and hence\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|C(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\|C(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty+2a_0|e(0)|t.  \\\\tag{2.11}\\n\\\\]\\nEquation (2.2) then bounds \\\\(\\\\|B(t)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\), followed by \\\\(\\\\|X(t)\\\\|_n\\\\).  Explicitly,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\le2a_0|e(0)|\\\\|C\\\\|_n,\\n\\\\quad\\n\\\\|\\\\dot X\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le2|e(0)|\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\|C\\\\|_n.             \\\\tag{2.12}\\n\\\\]\\nNow \\\\(\\\\|C_n(0)\\\\|_n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\), \\\\(\\\\|C_n(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty=o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\|X_n(0)\\\\|_n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\).  A self-contained \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net argument gives a constant \\\\(M_0\\\\) with\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|B_n(0)\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}>M_0)\\\\le2e^{-cn}. \\\\tag{2.13}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed, two unit-sphere \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets have at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points each,\\n\\\\(\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\\\le2\\\\max_{u,v}|v^\\\\top Bu|\\\\), and every fixed bilinear form is \\\\(N(0,n^{-1})\\\\); a union bound proves (2.13) for any sufficiently large fixed \\\\(M_0\\\\).\\n\\nConsequently (2.2) has a unique global finite-width solution.  On every \\\\([0,T]\\\\), explicit Euler with mesh \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\), polygonally interpolated, satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^\\\\delta(t),\\\\theta_n(t))\\n\\\\le C_T\\\\delta                                             \\\\tag{2.14}\\n\\\\]\\non an event of probability tending to one.  To verify (2.14), the local defect is at most \\\\(\\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\delta^2\\\\), where \\\\(M_T\\\\) bounds the vector field; the recurrence\\n\\\\(r_{k+1}\\\\le(1+L_T\\\\delta)r_k+\\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\delta^2\\\\) and discrete Gronwall give the assertion.  A stopped bootstrap keeps the Euler iterates in the slightly enlarged bounded set.\\n\\n## 3. The only nonclassical external theorem\\n\\nWe use the following exact specialization of Greg Yang, *Tensor Programs III: Neural Matrix Laws*, arXiv:2009.10685v3, Setup 2.2 and Theorem 2.10.\\n\\n**Fixed-program reused-Gaussian theorem.**  Fix a finite program whose\\ninitial matrix has iid \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\) entries, independent of all initial\\nvectors, whose initial vector coordinates are iid copies of a fixed jointly\\nGaussian vector (the covariance may be singular), and whose instructions\\nare coordinatewise polynomially bounded maps and finitely many applications\\nof \\\\(B_0\\\\) and \\\\(B_0^\\\\top\\\\).  For every fixed finite list of program vectors\\nand every polynomially bounded test \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_{i=1}^n\\\\psi(Z_{n,i}^1,\\\\ldots,Z_{n,i}^r)\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\psi(Z^1,\\\\ldots,Z^r)\\\\quad\\\\text{almost surely}. \\\\tag{3.1}\\n\\\\]\\nThe limiting variables retain every reused-transpose response term; a transpose call is not replaced by an independent multiplication.\\n\\nThe full primary proof was checked.  Its Gaussian-conditioning step conditions the matrix simultaneously on all preceding \\\\(BY\\\\) and \\\\(B^\\\\top V\\\\) calls.  A simultaneous induction proves empirical-moment convergence and constructs a full-support core set.  At singular covariance, rank stability and zero stability show that a zero limiting innovation is eventually an exact finite-width linear dependence; a positive innovation is a high-rank projected Gaussian for which the proof establishes the required strong law.  This closes the singular-covariance case without assuming rank stability.  The theorem is only for one fixed finite parameterless program.\\n\\nWe do **not** use the paper's Appendix A.1 conditional almost-sure strengthening or Appendix A.2 mean-convergence proof.  Independent inspection found that the former is false for arbitrary varying conditioning sigma-fields and that the printed proof of the latter omits a uniform-integrability step.  Neither is needed here.  Nor do we import the paper's operator-norm citation; (2.13) was proved directly.\\n\\n## 4. One canonical action space and its autonomous flow\\n\\nThe continuum state must live on fixed spaces before a time integral or a\\nrestart map can be discussed.  We construct those spaces once, from the\\ninitial Gaussian action, rather than constructing unrelated GNS spaces at\\nindividual times.\\n\\n### 4.1  Simultaneous cyclic realization of the initial action\\n\\nUse two sorts, right and left.  Let \\\\(\\\\mathscr P_R,\\\\mathscr P_L\\\\) be the\\ncountable formal probe grammar generated by the right Gaussian coordinate\\n\\\\(A_0\\\\), the left zero coordinate, rational linear combinations, formal\\napplications of \\\\({\\\\bf B}_0,{\\\\bf B}_0^*\\\\), and coordinate maps from the\\nfollowing countable library:\\n\\n* the exact maps \\\\(F,J,h,\\\\arctan,q\\\\),\\n  \\\\((c,s)\\\\mapsto c q(s)\\\\), and\\n  \\\\((a,p)\\\\mapsto q(a)^rp\\\\), \\\\(r=1,2\\\\);\\n* clipping at every positive integer level; and\\n* a countable algebra of bounded Lipschitz cylinder functions with rational\\n  data which generates every finite-dimensional Borel sigma-field.\\n\\nThe grammar is closed again after every new coordinate map or matrix call.\\nIt is countable, and every finite part is one fixed finite \\\\(\\\\mathrm{NETSOR}^T\\\\)\\nprogram.  Apply (3.1) to every finite part and intersect the resulting\\ncountably many probability-one events.  The limiting same-side finite joint\\nlaws are consistent.  Kolmogorov extension therefore gives probability\\nspaces \\\\((\\\\Omega_R,\\\\mu_R)\\\\), \\\\((\\\\Omega_L,\\\\mu_L)\\\\) carrying all formal\\nright and left coordinates.  Because the bounded Lipschitz cylinder algebra\\nis present, their spans are dense in\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal H_R=L^2(\\\\Omega_R,\\\\mu_R),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathcal H_L=L^2(\\\\Omega_L,\\\\mu_L).                  \\\\tag{4.1}\\n\\\\]\\nRational coefficients make the grammar countable; multiplication by an\\narbitrary fixed real is obtained in the \\\\(L^2\\\\) closure by rational\\napproximation.  A particular finite tensor program may equivalently use\\nthat real directly as part of its fixed coordinate map.\\n\\nFor a formal right probe \\\\(v\\\\), define \\\\(B_0v\\\\) to be the coordinate named\\nby \\\\({\\\\bf B}_0v\\\\), and similarly on the left.  On the product coupling of\\nall widths, (2.13) and Borel--Cantelli imply\\n\\\\(\\\\|B_{0,n}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\le M_0\\\\) eventually almost surely.  Passing to the\\nlimiting squared norms gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|B_0v\\\\|_{\\\\mathcal H_L}\\\\le M_0\\\\|v\\\\|_{\\\\mathcal H_R}. \\\\tag{4.2}\\n\\\\]\\nThus zero-norm relations are respected and \\\\(B_0\\\\) extends uniquely to all\\nof \\\\(\\\\mathcal H_R\\\\).  Passing to the limit in\\n\\\\(\\\\langle u,B_{0,n}v\\\\rangle_n=\\\\langle B_{0,n}^\\\\top u,v\\\\rangle_n\\\\)\\nproves that the named transpose action is its Hilbert adjoint.  The\\nZHat/ZDot rule in (3.1) is what determines these actions; in particular the\\nadjoint is not an independent Gaussian operator.\\n\\nPut \\\\(X_0=F(A_0)\\\\in\\\\mathcal H_R\\\\), \\\\(C_0=0\\\\in\\\\mathcal H_L\\\\).  On the fixed\\nspaces (4.1), for a triple \\\\(\\\\Theta=(X,B,C)\\\\), define\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nA&=J(X),&H&=h(X),&S&=BH,\\\\\\\\\\nG&=\\\\arctan S,&D&=Cq(S),&e&=\\\\langle C,G\\\\rangle_L-1,\\n\\\\end{aligned}                                      \\\\tag{4.3}\\n\\\\]\\nand\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot X=-2eB^*D,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot B=-2eD\\\\otimes H,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot C=-2eG.                                      \\\\tag{4.4}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\((D\\\\otimes H)v=D\\\\langle H,v\\\\rangle_R\\\\).  Lipschitz Nemytskii\\nmaps act on \\\\(L^2\\\\); the product \\\\(Cq(S)\\\\) is in \\\\(L^2\\\\) whenever\\n\\\\(C\\\\in L^\\\\infty\\\\); and \\\\(D\\\\otimes H\\\\) is a bounded operator of norm\\n\\\\(\\\\|D\\\\|_2\\\\|H\\\\|_2\\\\).  Thus every term in (4.4) is defined.\\n\\n### 4.2  Existence, uniqueness, and restartability\\n\\nWe give the functional-analytic existence argument explicitly.  On a path\\ninterval \\\\([0,\\\\tau]\\\\), use the complete metric\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le\\\\tau}\\\\{\\\\|X-\\\\widetilde X\\\\|_2+\\n\\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}+\\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_2\\\\} \\\\tag{4.5}\\n\\\\]\\non the closed class having prescribed bounds on \\\\(\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\) and\\n\\\\(\\\\|C\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\).  This class is complete: an \\\\(L^2\\\\) limit of functions\\nbounded by the same \\\\(L^\\\\infty\\\\) constant has a representative with that\\nbound.  The integral map associated with (4.4) preserves the class for\\nsmall \\\\(\\\\tau\\\\), because \\\\(|G|\\\\le a_0\\\\), and (2.5)--(2.10), with ordinary\\nHilbert norms, show that it has Lipschitz constant \\\\(L\\\\tau\\\\).  Taking\\n\\\\(L\\\\tau<1\\\\) proves existence and uniqueness by the contraction argument.\\n\\nThe Hilbert-space chain rule and the adjoint identity give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot e=-2eK,\\\\qquad\\nK=\\\\|q(A)B^*D\\\\|_2^2+\\n  \\\\|H\\\\|_2^2\\\\|D\\\\|_2^2+\\\\|G\\\\|_2^2\\\\ge0.              \\\\tag{4.6}\\n\\\\]\\nHence \\\\(|e(t)|\\\\le|e(0)|\\\\).  Exactly as in (2.11)--(2.12),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|C(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\|C_0\\\\|_\\\\infty+2a_0|e(0)|t,\\\\quad\\n\\\\|\\\\dot B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\le2a_0|e(0)|\\\\|C\\\\|_2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot X\\\\|_2\\\\le2|e(0)|\\\\|B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|C\\\\|_2. \\\\tag{4.7}\\n\\\\]\\nThese bounds prevent finite-time blow-up and continue the solution for all\\nfinite times.  They also put every coordinate product used above in the\\nclosure of the formal cylinder grammar (approximate first by uniformly\\nbounded cylinder functions and then use dominated \\\\(L^2\\\\) convergence).\\n\\nEquation (4.4) is autonomous.  At any current state\\n\\\\(\\\\Theta=(X,B,C)\\\\), root the saturated typed grammar of Section 4.1 at\\n\\\\(X,C,B,B^*\\\\), and let\\n\\\\(\\\\mathcal K_R(\\\\Theta),\\\\mathcal K_L(\\\\Theta)\\\\) be the closures of its\\nrational probe spans.  These are the **current cyclic spaces**.  By closure\\nof the grammar, \\\\(B\\\\mathcal K_R\\\\subseteq\\\\mathcal K_L\\\\),\\n\\\\(B^*\\\\mathcal K_L\\\\subseteq\\\\mathcal K_R\\\\), and every coordinate map in\\n(4.3) preserves the appropriate cyclic closure.  Picard iteration of\\n(4.4), started at \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\), uses only those operations; hence its future\\ntrajectory remains on these cyclic spaces.  The action of \\\\(B\\\\) outside\\nthem is therefore neither observed nor retained by the law-level state.\\n\\nIf two complete current-rooted action laws agree, mapping each named probe\\nto its counterpart is an isometry with dense range in the two cyclic\\nspaces.  It extends to surjective unitaries\\n\\\\(U_R:\\\\mathcal K_R\\\\to\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathcal K}_R\\\\) and\\n\\\\(U_L:\\\\mathcal K_L\\\\to\\\\widetilde{\\\\mathcal K}_L\\\\).  Saturation gives\\n\\\\(U_LB=\\\\widetilde B U_R\\\\), \\\\(U_RB^*=\\\\widetilde B^*U_L\\\\), and commutation\\nwith all coordinate maps.  It also sends \\\\(D\\\\otimes H\\\\) to\\n\\\\((U_LD)\\\\otimes(U_RH)\\\\).  Thus (4.4) is equivariant on precisely the\\nfuture-relevant spaces, and uniqueness descends to action-law equivalence\\nclasses without any unproved density claim in the larger initial\\nrealization.  Restarting the same integral equation at time \\\\(s\\\\) proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Phi_t(\\\\Phi_s[\\\\Theta_0])=\\\\Phi_{t+s}[\\\\Theta_0].     \\\\tag{4.8}\\n\\\\]\\nNo absolute-time tags, initialization array, or history belong to the\\ncurrent state.\\n\\n## 5. Fixed meshes, the population oracle, and the joint limit\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\Theta_k^\\\\delta=(x_k,b_k,c_k)\\\\) be explicit Euler applied to (4.4)\\non the canonical spaces, and use lower-case letters for all its derived\\nnodes.  For \\\\(N=\\\\lceil T/\\\\delta\\\\rceil<\\\\infty\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nb_k=B_0-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r d_r\\\\otimes h_r.       \\\\tag{5.1}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently\\n\\\\[\\ns_k=B_0h_k-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_rd_r\\n                 \\\\langle h_r,h_k\\\\rangle_R,\\n\\\\quad\\np_k=B_0^*d_k-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_rh_r\\n                 \\\\langle d_r,d_k\\\\rangle_L.        \\\\tag{5.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n### 5.1  A genuinely fixed finite tensor program\\n\\nDefine finite-width oracle vectors using the deterministic scalars in\\n(5.2): \\\\(\\\\bar X_{n,0}=F(A_{n,0})\\\\), \\\\(\\\\bar C_{n,0}=0\\\\), and\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-232d64fe-d2c4-4b59-8528-376fa75bdd29\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '361,720p' /tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\bar H_{n,k}&=h(\\\\bar X_{n,k}),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar S_{n,k}&=B_{0,n}\\\\bar H_{n,k}\\n-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r\\\\bar D_{n,r}\\n                    \\\\langle h_r,h_k\\\\rangle_R,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar G_{n,k}&=\\\\arctan\\\\bar S_{n,k},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\bar D_{n,k}=\\\\bar C_{n,k}q(\\\\bar S_{n,k}),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar P_{n,k}&=B_{0,n}^\\\\top\\\\bar D_{n,k}\\n-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r\\\\bar H_{n,r}\\n                    \\\\langle d_r,d_k\\\\rangle_L,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar X_{n,k+1}&=\\\\bar X_{n,k}-2\\\\delta e_k\\\\bar P_{n,k},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar C_{n,k+1}&=\\\\bar C_{n,k}-2\\\\delta e_k\\\\bar G_{n,k}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}                                      \\\\tag{5.3}\\n\\\\]\\nAll coefficients in (5.3) are deterministic and independent of \\\\(n\\\\), and\\nthere are only finitely many \\\\(B_{0,n},B_{0,n}^\\\\top\\\\) calls.  It is therefore\\none fixed parameterless program: the language permits arbitrary fixed real\\ncoordinate maps, so the possibly irrational numbers \\\\(e_r\\\\) and the\\ncanonical Grams cause no parameter line or empirical feedback.  Induction\\non \\\\(k\\\\), using the canonical\\nconstruction of Section 4 and (5.2), identifies its symbolic limiting\\nvariables with the canonical Euler nodes.  Theorem (3.1) then gives, almost\\nsurely, every finite joint law and empirical moment needed below.\\n\\nDefine the finite oracle rank memory\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bar B_{n,k}=B_{0,n}-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r\\n                   \\\\bar D_{n,r}\\\\otimes_n\\\\bar H_{n,r}. \\\\tag{5.4}\\n\\\\]\\nThe program nodes in (5.3) are not asserted to be exact actions of (5.4).\\nTheir two exact residuals are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\bar B_{n,k}\\\\bar H_{n,k}-\\\\bar S_{n,k}\\n&=-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r\\\\bar D_{n,r}\\n \\\\bigl(\\\\langle\\\\bar H_{n,r},\\\\bar H_{n,k}\\\\rangle_n\\n       -\\\\langle h_r,h_k\\\\rangle_R\\\\bigr),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar B_{n,k}^*\\\\bar D_{n,k}-\\\\bar P_{n,k}\\n&=-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r\\\\bar H_{n,r}\\n \\\\bigl(\\\\langle\\\\bar D_{n,r},\\\\bar D_{n,k}\\\\rangle_n\\n       -\\\\langle d_r,d_k\\\\rangle_L\\\\bigr).\\n\\\\end{aligned}                                      \\\\tag{5.5}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is the point at which empirical Gram errors enter; they are not\\nsilently replaced by expectations.\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\mathscr M_N\\\\) be the finite list consisting of the output moment,\\nall \\\\(H\\\\)- and \\\\(D\\\\)-Grams in (5.5), and the squared norms of every oracle\\nvector in (5.3).  Set\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\zeta_n=\\\\max_{M\\\\in\\\\mathscr M_N}|M_n-M|.            \\\\tag{5.6}\\n\\\\]\\nTheorem (3.1) gives \\\\(\\\\zeta_n\\\\to0\\\\) almost surely.  In particular all\\noracle vector norms are bounded on that event for large \\\\(n\\\\), and (5.5)\\nis bounded by \\\\(C_{N,\\\\delta}\\\\zeta_n\\\\) in normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\).\\n\\n### 5.2  Coupling to the actual scalar-feedback Euler scheme\\n\\nUse the same \\\\(A_{n,0},B_{0,n}\\\\) for the actual and oracle schemes.  Let\\n\\\\(B_{n,k}\\\\) be the actual learned matrix, and define\\n\\\\[\\nE_{n,k}=\\\\|X_{n,k}-\\\\bar X_{n,k}\\\\|_n+\\n        \\\\|C_{n,k}-\\\\bar C_{n,k}\\\\|_n+\\n        \\\\|B_{n,k}-\\\\bar B_{n,k}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}.        \\\\tag{5.7}\\n\\\\]\\nOn the common bounded event, (2.5)--(2.9) and the residuals (5.5) give,\\nnode by node,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n&\\\\|H_{n,k}-\\\\bar H_{n,k}\\\\|_n+\\n \\\\|S_{n,k}-\\\\bar S_{n,k}\\\\|_n+\\n \\\\|G_{n,k}-\\\\bar G_{n,k}\\\\|_n\\\\\\\\\\n&\\\\quad+\\\\|D_{n,k}-\\\\bar D_{n,k}\\\\|_n+\\n \\\\|P_{n,k}-\\\\bar P_{n,k}\\\\|_n+|e_{n,k}-e_k|\\n \\\\le C_{N,\\\\delta}(E_{n,k}+\\\\zeta_n).               \\\\tag{5.8}\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\nFor clarity, the scalar step in (5.8) is\\n\\\\[\\n|e_{n,k}-e_k|\\\\le\\n|\\\\langle C_{n,k},G_{n,k}\\\\rangle_n-\\n  \\\\langle\\\\bar C_{n,k},\\\\bar G_{n,k}\\\\rangle_n|\\n+|\\\\langle\\\\bar C_{n,k},\\\\bar G_{n,k}\\\\rangle_n-\\n  \\\\langle c_k,g_k\\\\rangle_L|,\\n\\\\]\\nand the second term is one of (5.6).  The first is bounded by the preceding\\nvector differences.  Subtracting the three Euler updates and using\\n\\\\(\\\\|u\\\\otimes v-u'\\\\otimes v'\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\le\\n\\\\|u-u'\\\\|_2\\\\|v\\\\|_2+\\\\|u'\\\\|_2\\\\|v-v'\\\\|_2\\\\) yields\\n\\\\[\\nE_{n,k+1}\\\\le(1+C_{N,\\\\delta}\\\\delta)E_{n,k}\\n             +C_{N,\\\\delta}\\\\delta\\\\zeta_n.          \\\\tag{5.9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(E_{n,0}=\\\\|C_{n,0}\\\\|_n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\).  Since \\\\(N\\\\) is\\nfixed, discrete Gronwall proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{k\\\\le N}E_{n,k}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.                       \\\\tag{5.10}\\n\\\\]\\nThis proves fixed-mesh identification of the actual Euler network.  It\\ndoes not assert, or require, a tensor theorem with \\\\(N=N_n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\).\\n\\n### 5.3  Removing the mesh\\n\\nThe estimates of Section 2 hold verbatim on the canonical spaces.  Thus\\ncanonical Euler and the unique solution of (4.4) satisfy\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\\\{\\\\|x^\\\\delta-X\\\\|_2+\\n\\\\|b^\\\\delta-B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}+\\\\|c^\\\\delta-C\\\\|_2\\\\}\\n\\\\le C_T\\\\delta.                                     \\\\tag{5.11}\\n\\\\]\\nThe finite-width Euler estimate is (2.14).  We now define the topology and\\nprove the assertion for arbitrary nested operator probes.\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\mathscr L_R,\\\\mathscr L_L\\\\) be the countable current-rooted typed\\nlanguage with roots \\\\(x,c\\\\), rational linear combinations, current\\n\\\\({\\\\bf b},{\\\\bf b}^*\\\\) calls, the exact globally Lipschitz or bounded maps\\n\\\\(J,h,\\\\arctan,q\\\\), the controlled map \\\\((c,s)\\\\mapsto cq(s)\\\\), every\\nrational bounded-Lipschitz cylinder map, and\\n\\\\[\\n(a,p)\\\\mapsto q(a)^r\\\\rho_M(p),\\\\qquad r=1,2,\\\\quad M\\\\in\\\\mathbb N. \\\\tag{5.12}\\n\\\\]\\nIt is closed after every operation.  An \\\\(R\\\\)-**record** is a finite list\\nof same-side probe tuples from this language together with their\\ninner-products.  Its distance is the sum of the \\\\(W_2\\\\) distances of the\\ntuple laws and the absolute differences of the listed inner-products.\\nChoose an enumeration \\\\((R_j)\\\\) and define\\n\\\\[\\nd_{\\\\rm act}(\\\\Theta,\\\\widetilde\\\\Theta)\\n=\\\\sum_{j\\\\ge1}2^{-j}\\\\{1\\\\wedge d_{R_j}(\\\\Theta,\\\\widetilde\\\\Theta)\\\\}. \\\\tag{5.13}\\n\\\\]\\nWe work in the category carrying the common operator and \\\\(C\\\\)-supremum\\nbounds proved in Sections 2 and 4; finite records alone would not encode\\nsuch a bound.\\n\\nHere is the required structural estimate.  If two states live on the same\\nfinite normalized spaces, or on the same canonical spaces, and\\n\\\\[\\nd_0=\\\\|x-\\\\widetilde x\\\\|_2+\\n    \\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}+\\\\|c-\\\\widetilde c\\\\|_2,\\n\\\\]\\nthen for every fixed probe \\\\(v\\\\) in (5.12)\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|v(\\\\Theta)-v(\\\\widetilde\\\\Theta)\\\\|_2\\\\le C_vd_0.     \\\\tag{5.14}\\n\\\\]\\nThis follows by induction on the syntax tree.  The coordinate-map steps\\nuse their Lipschitz bounds; \\\\(cq(s)\\\\) uses the common \\\\(C\\\\)-supremum bound;\\nand the operator step is exactly\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|bv-\\\\widetilde b\\\\,\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|v\\\\|_2+\\n   \\\\|\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2, \\\\tag{5.15}\\n\\\\]\\nwith the identical adjoint estimate.  Rational combinations and\\ninner-products follow from the triangle and Cauchy--Schwarz inequalities.\\nThis proves (5.14) for arbitrarily nested \\\\(b,b^*\\\\) calls, and coupling\\ncoordinates gives the corresponding \\\\(d_R\\\\) estimate.\\n\\nAt a fixed mesh, a finite oracle record is again one fixed tensor program.\\nTo see this without hiding empirical feedback, recursively expand every\\nnew \\\\(b_kv\\\\) call as\\n\\\\[\\nB_0v-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_rd_r\\\\langle h_r,v\\\\rangle_R, \\\\tag{5.16}\\n\\\\]\\nand every adjoint call analogously, using the deterministic canonical\\ninner-product.  Only finitely many calls occur.  Let\\n\\\\(\\\\zeta_{n,R}\\\\) be the maximum absolute empirical-minus-canonical\\ndifference of the finite list of Grams introduced by this recursion,\\ntogether with (5.6).  Theorem (3.1) gives\\n\\\\(\\\\zeta_{n,R}\\\\to0\\\\) almost surely.  Induction on probe depth, using the\\nexact finite-rank action and (5.15), gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{v\\\\in R}\\\\|v(\\\\bar\\\\Theta_{n,k})-\\\\bar v_{n,k}\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le C_{R,N,\\\\delta}\\\\zeta_{n,R},                    \\\\tag{5.17}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\bar v_{n,k}\\\\) is the deterministic-coefficient tensor-program\\nprobe from (5.16).  Equations (5.7)--(5.10) and (5.14) then identify the\\nactual Euler record with the canonical Euler record.\\n\\nFinally compare finite-width flow to its Euler scheme using (2.14) and\\n(5.14), take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\) using (5.17), and compare\\ncanonical Euler to canonical flow using (5.11) and (5.14).  Uniform time\\nmoduli replace the finite grid by all of \\\\([0,T]\\\\).  Hence, for every fixed\\nrecord and every \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_R(\\\\theta_n(t),\\\\Theta(t))\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.       \\\\tag{5.18}\\n\\\\]\\nFor the metric (5.13), first choose a finite prefix whose remaining weight\\nis below \\\\(\\\\varepsilon/2\\\\), and apply a union bound to that prefix.  This\\nproves uniform compact-time convergence in \\\\(d_{\\\\rm act}\\\\).\\nThe state carries the common operator bound furnished by (2.13) and the\\nfinite rank integral in (4.4); mere finite-record convergence without this\\nbound would not suffice.  Equations (4.4), (4.8), and (5.18) prove the\\nclaimed well-posed, autonomous, restartable law-level limit.\\n\\n## 6. Path laws, kernels, and velocities\\n\\nThe raw preactivations are \\\\(A=J(X)\\\\) and \\\\(S=Bh(X)\\\\).  From (1.3), (1.6), and the bounds of Section 2,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\\\bigl(\\\\|\\\\dot A(t)\\\\|_n+\\n\\\\|\\\\dot S(t)\\\\|_n\\\\bigr)<\\\\infty                       \\\\tag{6.1}\\n\\\\]\\non the high-probability initialization event.  For either \\\\(Z=A\\\\) or \\\\(Z=S\\\\), and an interval \\\\(I\\\\) of length \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\sup_{s,t\\\\in I}|Z_i(t)-Z_i(s)|^2\\n\\\\le\\\\delta\\\\int_I\\\\|\\\\dot Z(r)\\\\|_n^2\\\\,dr.            \\\\tag{6.2}\\n\\\\]\\nHence each empirical path law is within \\\\(C_T\\\\sqrt\\\\delta\\\\) in\\n\\\\(W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\) of its grid projection.  At a fixed grid, (5.18)\\nreduces the assertion to a fixed Euler program; (3.1) then gives weak\\nconvergence and convergence of the squared grid maximum, since that maximum\\nis a polynomially bounded test of finitely many program vectors.  First let\\n\\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then let the grid mesh tend to zero in (6.2).  This proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{A_i(\\\\cdot)}\\\\to\\\\mu_A,qquad\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_j\\\\delta_{S_j(\\\\cdot)}\\\\to\\\\mu_S\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in }W_2(C([0,T]))                       \\\\tag{6.3}\\n\\\\]\\nin probability, as well as uniform integrability of the squared path suprema.\\n\\nOne further argument is necessary for the gated backpropagated fields; they are not naively Lipschitz as maps \\\\(L^2\\\\times L^2\\\\to L^2\\\\).  Put again \\\\(P=B^*D\\\\).  Equations (2.5)--(2.9) show that\\n\\\\[\\n(X,B,C)\\\\longmapsto(A,S,D,P)                         \\\\tag{6.4}\\n\\\\]\\nis Lipschitz from the bounded regularized state into normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\).  Also\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot P=\\\\dot B^*D+B^*\\\\dot D,qquad\\n\\\\dot D=\\\\dot Cq(S)+Cq'(S)\\\\dot S,                    \\\\tag{6.5}\\n\\\\]\\nand (1.6) implies a uniform \\\\(L^2\\\\) bound on the right sides.  Consequently the joint empirical laws of \\\\((A,P)\\\\) converge uniformly in time in \\\\(W_2(\\\\mathbb R^2)\\\\), and the resulting family is compact in that topology.\\n\\nWe use the following elementary lemma.\\n\\n**Dominated pushforward lemma.**  If \\\\(\\\\mu_r\\\\to\\\\mu\\\\) in \\\\(W_2(\\\\mathbb R^2)\\\\), with coordinates \\\\((a,p)\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\Psi\\\\) is continuous with \\\\(|\\\\Psi(a,p)|\\\\le |p|\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\Psi_\\\\#\\\\mu_r\\\\to\\\\Psi_\\\\#\\\\mu\\\\) in \\\\(W_2(\\\\mathbb R)\\\\).  The assertion is uniform over a compact \\\\(W_2\\\\)-family.\\n\\n**Proof.**  \\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence is weak convergence plus uniform integrability of \\\\(a^2+p^2\\\\).  On \\\\(|p|\\\\le M\\\\), truncate further to a compact set and approximate \\\\(\\\\Psi\\\\) by bounded continuous functions.  Outside it,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int|\\\\Psi|^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\mu_r\\n\\\\le\\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\mu_r,              \\\\tag{6.6}\\n\\\\]\\nwhich tends to zero uniformly in \\\\(r\\\\) as \\\\(M\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\).  This proves weak convergence and convergence of second moments of the pushforwards, hence \\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence.  Compactness gives the uniform form. \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n\\nApply the lemma to\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Psi_1(a,p)=q(a)p,qquad \\\\Psi_2(a,p)=q(a)^2p.       \\\\tag{6.7}\\n\\\\]\\nBoth are bounded in magnitude by \\\\(|p|\\\\).  The required uniform truncation is as follows.\\nLet\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mu_{n,t}=\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{(A_{n,i}(t),P_{n,i}(t))},\\n\\\\qquad \\\\mu_t=\\\\mathcal L(A(t),P(t)).                 \\\\tag{6.8}\\n\\\\]\\nThe fixed-record convergence (5.18), the Lipschitz map (6.4), and the time\\nmodulus from (6.5) imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}W_2(\\\\mu_{n,t},\\\\mu_t)\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.                       \\\\tag{6.9}\\n\\\\]\\nThe image \\\\(\\\\{\\\\mu_t:0\\\\le t\\\\le T\\\\}\\\\) is compact in \\\\(W_2\\\\).  For any\\n\\\\(\\\\gamma,\\\\mu\\\\in\\\\mathcal P_2(\\\\mathbb R^2)\\\\), an optimal coupling of their\\nsecond coordinates gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\gamma\\n\\\\le4W_2(\\\\gamma,\\\\mu)^2\\n +2\\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M/2}\\\\,d\\\\mu.              \\\\tag{6.10}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed, if \\\\(|p_\\\\mu|\\\\le M/2<|p_\\\\gamma|\\\\), then\\n\\\\(|p_\\\\gamma|\\\\le2|p_\\\\gamma-p_\\\\mu|\\\\); otherwise use\\n\\\\(|p_\\\\gamma|^2\\\\le2|p_\\\\gamma-p_\\\\mu|^2+2|p_\\\\mu|^2\\\\).\\nA finite \\\\(W_2\\\\)-cover of the compact limiting family, followed by (6.10),\\ngives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lim_{M\\\\to\\\\infty}\\\\limsup_{n\\\\to\\\\infty}\\n\\\\mathbb P\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\n \\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\mu_{n,t}>\\\\varepsilon\\\\right)=0. \\\\tag{6.11}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\rho_M(p)=(-M)\\\\vee(p\\\\wedge M)\\\\) and\\n\\\\[\\nQ=q(A)^2P,\\\\qquad Q_M=q(A)^2\\\\rho_M(P),              \\\\tag{6.12}\\n\\\\]\\nwith identical finite-width notation.  Then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|Q-Q_M\\\\|_2^2\\\\le\\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\mu_t, \\\\tag{6.13}\\n\\\\]\\nand the same empirical inequality holds.  At fixed \\\\(M\\\\), the map in\\n(6.12) is bounded and globally Lipschitz, so \\\\(Q_M,BQ_M\\\\), and all their\\njoint Grams are fixed action probes and converge uniformly by (5.18).\\nPut\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_n=\\\\|H_n\\\\|_n^2,\\\\quad\\nY_n=\\\\alpha_nD_n+B_nQ_n,\\\\quad\\nY_{n,M}=\\\\alpha_nD_n+B_nQ_{n,M},                   \\\\tag{6.14}\\n\\\\]\\nand define \\\\(Y,Y_M\\\\) canonically.  Uniform operator bounds give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|Y_n-Y_{n,M}\\\\|_n\\\\le M_B\\\\|Q_n-Q_{n,M}\\\\|_n,\\n\\\\quad\\n\\\\|Y-Y_M\\\\|_2\\\\le M_B\\\\|Q-Q_M\\\\|_2.                 \\\\tag{6.15}\\n\\\\]\\nUsing\\n\\\\(|\\\\|u\\\\|^2-\\\\|v\\\\|^2|\\\\le(\\\\|u\\\\|+\\\\|v\\\\|)\\\\|u-v\\\\|\\\\), first take\\n\\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(M\\\\), and only then \\\\(M\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\).  Equations\\n(6.11)--(6.15) prove\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}|\\\\|Y_n(t)\\\\|_n^2-\\\\|Y(t)\\\\|_2^2|\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.      \\\\tag{6.16}\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(\\\\dot S_n=-2e_nY_n\\\\), (6.16) gives uniform convergence of its\\nsquared velocity.  The same argument applied to \\\\(q(A)P\\\\), whose magnitude\\nis at most \\\\(|P|\\\\), handles \\\\(\\\\dot A\\\\).  Hence\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\dot Z_n(t)\\\\|_n^2dt\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\dot Z(t)\\\\|_2^2dt,\\n\\\\qquad Z=A,S.                                     \\\\tag{6.17}\\n\\\\]\\nThe blocks \\\\(K^B,K^C\\\\) use bounded activations and \\\\(D\\\\), while \\\\(K^A\\\\)\\nuses the same dominated truncation.  Consequently\\n\\\\[\\nK_n^r\\\\to K^r\\\\quad(r=A,B,C),\\\\qquad K_n\\\\to K         \\\\tag{6.18}\\n\\\\]\\nuniformly on \\\\([0,T]\\\\), and \\\\(f_n,L_n\\\\) converge uniformly as well.\\n\\n## 7. Exact GD versus the finite-width flow\\n\\nIt remains to replace the auxiliary Euler schemes by the exact GD specified in the theorem.  Let\\n\\\\[\\nR=-2eB^*D.                                          \\\\tag{7.1}\\n\\\\]\\nOne raw GD step is \\\\(A^+=A+\\\\eta q(A)R\\\\); the \\\\(B,C\\\\) updates are exactly the Euler updates of (2.2).  Since \\\\(F\\\\) is cubic,\\n\\\\[\\nF(A+\\\\eta q(A)R)=F(A)+\\\\eta R\\n +\\\\eta^2Aq(A)^2R^2+\\\\frac{\\\\eta^3}{3}q(A)^3R^3       \\\\tag{7.2}\\n\\\\]\\ncoordinatewise, with no remainder.  Now\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_a|a|q(a)^2<\\\\infty,qquad\\n\\\\|R\\\\|_n\\\\le C_T,qquad\\n\\\\|R\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\sqrt n\\\\|R\\\\|_n.                  \\\\tag{7.3}\\n\\\\]\\nTherefore the one-step defect relative to Euler in the \\\\(X\\\\)-coordinate is at most\\n\\\\[\\nC_T(\\\\eta^2\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta^3n).                         \\\\tag{7.4}\\n\\\\]\\nThe ordinary ODE local defect is \\\\(O_T(\\\\eta^2)\\\\).  Applying the dimension-free stability estimate (2.10) step by step gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t),\\\\theta_n(t))\\n\\\\le C_T\\\\bigl(\\\\eta+\\\\eta\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta^2n\\\\bigr).       \\\\tag{7.5}\\n\\\\]\\nThe proof is first stopped on the enlarged bounded set from Section 2; the right side tends to zero, so the first-exit event is impossible for large \\\\(n\\\\).  With \\\\(\\\\eta=\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\), (7.5) tends to zero.\\n\\nWe linearly interpolate the parameter iterates and recompute all network\\nnodes from those interpolated parameters.  Let \\\\(V_{A,k},V_{B,k}\\\\) be the\\nconstant parameter slopes on step \\\\(k\\\\).  Then\\n\\\\[\\nV_{A,k}=-2e_kq(A_k)P_k,\\\\qquad\\nV_{B,k}=-2e_kD_k\\\\otimes_nH_k,                     \\\\tag{7.6}\\n\\\\]\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-99cbaf4c-603c-450a-8d9a-c82e95c0e346\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'ls -l /tmp/tp3src/proofs.tex /tmp/2009.10685v3.txt /tmp/L2_CONTINUUM_REPAIRS.md /tmp/L2_SECTIONS_4_5_REPAIR.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"-rw-rw-r-- 1 codex-b codex-b 423216 Aug 26 16:50 /tmp/2009.10685v3.txt\\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 codex-b codex-b  15872 Aug 28 17:13 /tmp/L2_CONTINUUM_REPAIRS.md\\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 codex-b codex-b  19247 Aug 28 17:16 /tmp/L2_SECTIONS_4_5_REPAIR.md\\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 codex-b codex-b 168962 May  8  2021 /tmp/tp3src/proofs.tex\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-ab146363-4e17-4ab4-a95c-2b2d532792f3\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '721,1100p' /tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"and, at \\\\(t=k\\\\eta+s\\\\), \\\\(0\\\\le s\\\\le\\\\eta\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\partial_tS(t)=V_{B,k}\\\\arctan(A_k+sV_{A,k})\\n+(B_k+sV_{B,k})q(A_k+sV_{A,k})V_{A,k}.            \\\\tag{7.7}\\n\\\\]\\nThe bounded-state estimates give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{k\\\\eta\\\\le T}\\\\{\\\\|V_{A,k}\\\\|_n+\\n\\\\|V_{B,k}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}+\\\\|\\\\partial_tS\\\\|_n\\\\}\\\\le C_T  \\\\tag{7.8}\\n\\\\]\\non the stopped event; (7.5) closes the stop.\\n\\nIt remains to pass the squared slopes, where a fourth-moment shortcut would\\nbe invalid.  Let \\\\(\\\\widehat\\\\mu_{n,k}\\\\) be the empirical law of\\n\\\\((A_k^{\\\\rm GD},P_k^{\\\\rm GD})\\\\).  Equations (7.5), (6.4), and (6.9) give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{k\\\\eta_n\\\\le T}\\nW_2(\\\\widehat\\\\mu_{n,k},\\\\mu_{k\\\\eta_n})\\\\longrightarrow0. \\\\tag{7.9}\\n\\\\]\\nThus the uniform square-tail estimate (6.11) also holds at all GD nodes.\\nSince \\\\(q'\\\\) is bounded, on \\\\(|P_k|\\\\le M\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|[q(A_k+sV_{A,k})-q(A_k)]V_{A,k}\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le C_T\\\\eta M^2,                                  \\\\tag{7.10}\\n\\\\]\\nwhile on the complement both terms are bounded pointwise by\\n\\\\(C_T|P_k|\\\\).  Take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(M\\\\), then \\\\(M\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\).\\nDefine\\n\\\\(\\\\mathcal V^S_{n,k}=-2e_k\\\\{\\\\|H_k\\\\|_n^2D_k+\\nB_k[q(A_k)^2P_k]\\\\}\\\\).  Equations (7.7)--(7.10), including the easy\\n\\\\(\\\\|V_{B,k}[H(A_k+sV_{A,k})-H_k]\\\\|_n\\\\le C_T\\\\eta\\\\)\\nterm, show\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int_0^T\\\\left\\\\|\\\\partial_tS_n^{\\\\rm GD}(t)-\\n\\\\mathcal V^S_{n,\\\\lfloor t/\\\\eta\\\\rfloor}\\\\right\\\\|_n^2dt\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.       \\\\tag{7.11}\\n\\\\]\\nThe same fixed-\\\\(M\\\\) action-probe argument as (6.12)--(6.16), followed by\\nRiemann-sum convergence of the strongly continuous limiting velocities,\\ntherefore yields\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\partial_tA_n^{\\\\rm GD}\\\\|_n^2dt\\n\\\\to\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\dot A\\\\|_2^2dt,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\partial_tS_n^{\\\\rm GD}\\\\|_n^2dt\\n\\\\to\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\dot S\\\\|_2^2dt.                     \\\\tag{7.12}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWe finally prove, rather than infer from same-time \\\\(L^2\\\\) convergence, the\\npath-space assertion for exact GD.  For \\\\(Z=A,S\\\\), let\\n\\\\(\\\\widehat\\\\Lambda_n^Z=n^{-1}\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{Z_{n,i}^{\\\\rm GD}(\\\\cdot)}\\\\).\\nFor a deterministic partition \\\\(\\\\pi=\\\\{t_j\\\\}\\\\) of mesh at most \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\),\\nlet \\\\(I_\\\\pi z\\\\) interpolate its grid values.  Every absolutely continuous\\nscalar path satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|z-I_\\\\pi z\\\\|_\\\\infty^2\\n\\\\le4\\\\sum_j\\\\sup_{s,t\\\\in[t_j,t_{j+1}]}|z(t)-z(s)|^2.\\n\\\\]\\nCauchy--Schwarz, coordinate averaging, and (7.12) give\\n\\\\[\\nW_2(\\\\widehat\\\\Lambda_n^Z,(I_\\\\pi)_\\\\#\\\\widehat\\\\Lambda_n^Z)^2\\n\\\\le4\\\\Delta\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\partial_tZ_n^{\\\\rm GD}(t)\\\\|_n^2dt\\n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(\\\\Delta).                            \\\\tag{7.13}\\n\\\\]\\nAt the finitely many coarse-grid times, (7.5), the Lipschitz property of\\n\\\\(J\\\\), and (2.6) imply convergence of the GD grid-vector empirical law to\\nthe canonical grid law.  Hence, for fixed \\\\(\\\\pi\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n(I_\\\\pi)_\\\\#\\\\widehat\\\\Lambda_n^Z\\n\\\\longrightarrow (I_\\\\pi)_\\\\#\\\\mu_Z\\n\\\\quad\\\\hbox{in }W_2(C([0,T])).                     \\\\tag{7.14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe limiting version of (6.2) gives\\n\\\\(W_2(\\\\mu_Z,(I_\\\\pi)_\\\\#\\\\mu_Z)\\\\le C_T\\\\sqrt\\\\Delta\\\\).\\nThe triangle inequality in (7.13)--(7.14), first \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed\\n\\\\(\\\\pi\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\downarrow0\\\\), proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\widehat\\\\Lambda_n^A\\\\to\\\\mu_A,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\widehat\\\\Lambda_n^S\\\\to\\\\mu_S\\n\\\\quad\\\\hbox{in }W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\text{ in probability}. \\\\tag{7.15}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe uniform kernel/output/loss assertion for exact GD requires one final\\ntail transfer.  Define at every interpolated time\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\widehat\\\\mu_{n,t}\\n=\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{(A_{n,i}^{\\\\rm GD}(t),\\n                       P_{n,i}^{\\\\rm GD}(t))}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe state estimate (7.5), the Lipschitz estimate (6.4), and (6.9) imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}W_2(\\\\widehat\\\\mu_{n,t},\\\\mu_t)\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.       \\\\tag{7.16}\\n\\\\]\\nApplying (6.10) gives (6.11) with \\\\(\\\\widehat\\\\mu_{n,t}\\\\), uniformly over\\nthe whole interpolation rather than only its nodes.  At fixed \\\\(M\\\\), the\\nmap \\\\(q(a)\\\\rho_M(p)\\\\) is bounded Lipschitz, so (7.5), (5.14), and the\\nflow convergence identify its squared empirical norm uniformly in time.\\nThe difference from \\\\(q(A)P\\\\) has squared norm bounded by the tail in\\n(6.11).  Taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(M\\\\), then \\\\(M\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}|K_{n,\\\\rm GD}^A(t)-K^A(t)|\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.                                 \\\\tag{7.17}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\(K^B,K^C\\\\), the output, and the loss contain only bounded activations and\\nthe Lipschitz field \\\\(D=Cq(S)\\\\), so (7.5) passes them directly.  Therefore\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\\\left(\\n |K_{n,\\\\rm GD}(t)-K(t)|+|f_{n,\\\\rm GD}(t)-f(t)|\\n +|L_{n,\\\\rm GD}(t)-L(t)|\\\\right)\\\\longrightarrow0   \\\\tag{7.18}\\n\\\\]\\nin probability, and each of the three kernel blocks converges separately.\\n\\nThe actual \\\\(C_n(0)\\\\) was already included in the coupling: its normalized\\n\\\\(L^2\\\\) norm is \\\\(O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\), its sup norm is\\n\\\\(o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1)\\\\), and (5.7)--(5.10) transfer it to the zero canonical\\nmark.  Sections 4--7 now prove the full joint diagonal convergence.\\n\\n## 8. Exact initial action law and reused-adjoint response\\n\\nLet\\n\\\\[\\nA_0\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\qquad H_0=\\\\arctan A_0,qquad\\nm=\\\\mathbb E H_0^2>0.                               \\\\tag{8.1}\\n\\\\]\\nThe initial second preactivation has law\\n\\\\[\\nZ\\\\sim N(0,m).                                       \\\\tag{8.2}\\n\\\\]\\nPut\\n\\\\[\\nG_0=\\\\arctan Z,qquad\\nu(Z)=G_0q(Z)=\\\\frac{\\\\arctan Z}{1+Z^2},qquad\\n\\\\nu=\\\\mathbb Eu(Z)^2>0,qquad\\n\\\\kappa=\\\\mathbb Eu'(Z).                              \\\\tag{8.3}\\n\\\\]\\nThe reused adjoint \\\\(\\\\Xi=B_0^*u(Z)\\\\) contains both an innovation and a response.  We prove its law directly.\\n\\nCondition on \\\\(H=(H_i)\\\\).  For row \\\\(b_j\\\\) of \\\\(B_0\\\\), let \\\\(s_j=b_j^\\\\top H\\\\) and \\\\(m_n=\\\\|H\\\\|_n^2\\\\).  Gaussian regression gives\\n\\\\[\\nb_j=\\\\frac{s_j}{nm_n}H+\\\\widetilde b_j,qquad\\n\\\\widetilde b_j\\\\mid(H,s_j)\\\\sim\\nN\\\\left(0,\\\\frac1nP_H^\\\\perp\\\\right),                 \\\\tag{8.4}\\n\\\\]\\nindependently over \\\\(j\\\\).  Consequently, conditionally on \\\\((H,s)\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nB_0^\\\\top u(s)=a_nH+\\\\sqrt{\\\\nu_n}\\\\,P_H^\\\\perp\\\\Gamma, \\\\tag{8.5}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\na_n=\\\\frac1{nm_n}\\\\sum_js_ju(s_j),qquad\\n\\\\nu_n=\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_ju(s_j)^2,qquad\\n\\\\Gamma\\\\sim N(0,I_n).                               \\\\tag{8.6}\\n\\\\]\\nThe laws of large numbers give \\\\(\\\\nu_n\\\\to\\\\nu\\\\), and Gaussian integration by parts for \\\\(Z\\\\sim N(0,m)\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\na_n\\\\to\\\\frac{\\\\mathbb E[Zu(Z)]}{m}=\\\\mathbb Eu'(Z)=\\\\kappa. \\\\tag{8.7}\\n\\\\]\\nMoreover \\\\(\\\\|P_H^\\\\perp\\\\Gamma-\\\\Gamma\\\\|_n\\\\to0\\\\) in probability.  Hence the joint empirical limit is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Xi=\\\\sqrt\\\\nu\\\\,\\\\Gamma+\\\\kappa H_0,qquad\\n\\\\Gamma\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad \\\\Gamma\\\\perp A_0.          \\\\tag{8.8}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is precisely the innovation plus the Onsager/response term; omitting the second summand would be wrong.\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\mathbb E[q(A_0)^2\\\\Xi^2]\\n=\\\\nu\\\\mathbb E q(A_0)^2\\n+\\\\kappa^2\\\\mathbb E[q(A_0)^2H_0^2]>0.               \\\\tag{8.9}\\n\\\\]\\nOn the left Hilbert space define the positive operator\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal R=mI+B_0M_{q(A_0)^2}B_0^*,                \\\\tag{8.10}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(M_{q(A_0)^2}\\\\) is coordinate multiplication.  Then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle u,\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\rangle\\n=m\\\\nu+\\\\langle B_0^*u,q(A_0)^2B_0^*u\\\\rangle\\n=m\\\\nu+d>0.                                         \\\\tag{8.11}\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\ne0\\\\).\\n\\n## 9. Small-time motion and completion of the theorem\\n\\nAt \\\\(t=0\\\\), \\\\(C=0\\\\), \\\\(e=-1\\\\), and hence \\\\(D=P=0\\\\).  The integral equations, in the strong \\\\(L^2\\\\) and operator topologies constructed above, give node by node\\n\\\\[\\nC(t)=2tG_0+O_{L^2}(t^2),qquad\\nD(t)=2t\\\\,u+O_{L^2}(t^2),                            \\\\tag{9.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nA(t)-A_0=2t^2q(A_0)\\\\Xi+o_{L^2}(t^2),               \\\\tag{9.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nB(t)-B_0=2t^2u\\\\otimes H_0+o_{\\\\mathrm{op}}(t^2),   \\\\tag{9.3}\\n\\\\]\\nand\\n\\\\[\\nS(t)-Z=2t^2\\\\mathcal Ru+o_{L^2}(t^2).               \\\\tag{9.4}\\n\\\\]\\nFor example, \\\\(\\\\dot D(0)=2u\\\\), so\\n\\\\(A''(0)=4q(A_0)B_0^*u\\\\), \\\\(B''(0)=4u\\\\otimes H_0\\\\); differentiating \\\\(S=BH\\\\) then gives\\n\\\\[\\nS''(0)=4\\\\{m u+B_0[q(A_0)^2B_0^*u]\\\\}=4\\\\mathcal Ru. \\\\tag{9.5}\\n\\\\]\\nThese derivatives exist because the action flow is strongly continuous, all scalar derivatives of \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) are bounded, and the relevant operator actions are bounded.  Alternatively, (9.1)--(9.4) follow directly by dividing the integral equations by the indicated powers of \\\\(t\\\\) and using dominated convergence.\\n\\nThe two hidden velocity energies therefore satisfy\\n\\\\[\\nV_A(t)^2=16dt^2+o(t^2),qquad\\nV_S(t)^2=16\\\\|\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\|_2^2t^2+o(t^2),          \\\\tag{9.6}\\n\\\\]\\nand the displacements satisfy\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E|A(t)-A_0|^2=4dt^4+o(t^4),qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E|S(t)-Z|^2=4\\\\|\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\|_2^2t^4+o(t^4). \\\\tag{9.7}\\n\\\\]\\nBoth leading coefficients are strictly positive by (8.9)--(8.11).  Hence both hidden layers have finite positive integrated velocity and nonzero displacement on every sufficiently short nonzero interval.\\n\\nLet\\n\\\\[\\nk_3=\\\\mathbb E(\\\\arctan Z)^2>0.                       \\\\tag{9.8}\\n\\\\]\\nEquations (9.1)--(9.4) yield\\n\\\\[\\nK^A(t)=4dt^2+o(t^2),qquad\\nK^B(t)=4m\\\\nu t^2+o(t^2),                            \\\\tag{9.9}\\n\\\\]\\nand, since\\n\\\\(G(t)=G_0+2t^2q(Z)\\\\mathcal Ru+o_{L^2}(t^2)\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nK^C(t)=k_3+4(m\\\\nu+d)t^2+o(t^2).                    \\\\tag{9.10}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed \\\\(\\\\langle G_0,q(Z)\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\rangle=\\\\langle u,\\\\mathcal Ru\\\\rangle=m\\\\nu+d\\\\).  Therefore\\n\\\\[\\nK(t)=k_3+8(m\\\\nu+d)t^2+o(t^2),                      \\\\tag{9.11}\\n\\\\]\\nso every block has a finite positive time integral and the total kernel is nonconstant.\\n\\nAlso\\n\\\\[\\nf(t)=2k_3t+o(t),qquad\\nL(t)=1-4k_3t+o(t),                                  \\\\tag{9.12}\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(f(0)=0\\\\ne1\\\\) and \\\\(L(T)<L(0)\\\\) for all sufficiently small \\\\(T>0\\\\).\\n\\nIt remains only to verify hidden variance and surviving nonlinearity.  At zero the laws are \\\\(N(0,1)\\\\) and \\\\(N(0,m)\\\\).  Their variances are positive and, by (6.3), continuous in time; hence they stay positive on a short interval.  For a law \\\\(\\\\mu\\\\) with positive variance, the squared distance of \\\\(\\\\arctan z\\\\) from affine functions is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal N(\\\\mu)=\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}_\\\\mu(\\\\arctan Z)\\n-\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{Cov}_\\\\mu(Z,\\\\arctan Z)^2}\\n       {\\\\operatorname{Var}_\\\\mu(Z)}.                \\\\tag{9.13}\\n\\\\]\\nIt is strictly positive for either initial Gaussian: equality would make \\\\(\\\\arctan z\\\\) affine on a full-support measure and, by continuity, on \\\\(\\\\mathbb R\\\\).  The moments in (9.13) are continuous under \\\\(W_2\\\\); for the only unbounded mixed moment, an optimal coupling gives\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\mathbb E[X\\\\arctan X-Y\\\\arctan Y]|\\n\\\\le a_0\\\\|X-Y\\\\|_2+\\\\|Y\\\\|_2\\\\|X-Y\\\\|_2.              \\\\tag{9.14}\\n\\\\]\\nThus both nonlinear residuals remain positive and have positive time integrals after reducing \\\\(T_*\\\\) if necessary.\\n\\nAll assertions in the theorem have now been proved. \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n\\n## Audit note on external dependencies\\n\\nThe only research-level theorem used is the fixed finite, parameterless\\nreused-Gaussian Master Theorem (3.1).  Its hypotheses are met only by the\\ndeterministic finite program (5.3); empirical feedback, the width-dependent\\nsmall readout initialization, continuous time, and exact GD are handled\\nseparately in Sections 2 and 4--7.  No growing-program,\\nconditional-expectation, mean-convergence, DMFT, or operator-norm theorem\\nis assumed.\\n\\nThe audited primary source is Greg Yang, *Tensor Programs III: Neural\\nMatrix Laws*, arXiv:2009.10685v3 (8 May 2021),\\n<https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685v3>.  The source check covered Setup 2.2,\\nBox 1 and Remarks 2.11--2.12, Theorem 2.10, and the complete\\nMoments/CoreSet proof in Appendix L: simultaneous conditioning on every\\nearlier \\\\(BY\\\\) and \\\\(B^\\\\top V\\\\) constraint, rank and zero stability, the\\nzero-innovation and positive-innovation branches, and the projected\\nGaussian strong law.  Those arguments establish exactly (3.1), including\\nsingular initial Gaussian covariance and every reused-transpose response.\\nThey give no estimate uniform in program length, and none is used here.\\n\\nThe audit found and repaired six literal slips inside that proof rather\\nthan silently inheriting them.  In the transpose-adjunction Case 2\\n(source file proofs.tex, lines 1578--1617), the conditioning vector must be\\n\\\\[\\nU=(\\\\widehat Z^{u^1},\\\\ldots,\\\\widehat Z^{u^k}),\\n\\\\]\\nnot \\\\((Z^{u^1},\\\\ldots,Z^{u^k})\\\\); only the former is the Gaussian vector\\nwhose covariance is the displayed matrix \\\\(C\\\\), and conditioning on it\\ngives the asserted independence.  After Case 1 is substituted, the next\\ncolumn must be \\\\((Z^{v^1},\\\\ldots,Z^{v^k})\\\\), not its hatted version.  With\\nthose two replacements, ordinary possibly-singular Gaussian regression\\n\\\\(b^\\\\top C^+U\\\\) proves the printed adjunction identity.\\n\\nIn the high-moment collision count (source lines 1044--1125), the\\none-collision contribution is\\n\\\\[\\nn^{-(p-1)/4}n^{2p-1}B''_{2p}\\n=n^{(7p-3)/4}B''_{2p},\\n\\\\]\\nnot \\\\(n^{7(p-1)/4}B''_{2p}\\\\).  After the surrounding \\\\(n^{-2p}\\\\)\\nnormalization it is \\\\(n^{-(p+3)/4}B''_{2p}\\\\), which is summable for the\\nproof's choice \\\\(p\\\\ge6\\\\); the Borel--Cantelli conclusion is unchanged.\\nFinally, the Gaussian-conditioning variance near source line 3019 is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(\\\\widehat Z^g\\\\mid\\\\widehat Z)\\n=\\\\sigma_A^2\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\mathbb E(Z^h)^2\\n -\\\\mathring\\\\gamma^\\\\top\\\\mathring\\\\Upsilon^+\\n  \\\\mathring\\\\gamma\\\\right)=\\\\mathring\\\\sigma^2,\\n\\\\]\\nnot the dimensionally inconsistent printed\\n\\\\(\\\\mathbb E(Z^g)^2-\\\\cdots=\\\\mathring\\\\sigma\\\\).\\nThese are local symbol/count repairs; the corrected quantities are exactly\\nthose defined earlier in the source, and every subsequent bound uses the\\ncorrect squared variance.  The complete induction was reread with these\\nrepairs in place.\\n\\nThree earlier/later displays require the following additional corrections.\\nIn the simultaneous Gaussian-conditioning lemma (source lines 243--278),\\nthe conditional mean is\\n\\\\[\\nE=ZQ^+ +P^{+\\\\top}X^\\\\top-P^{+\\\\top}P^\\\\top ZQ^+;     \\\\tag{A.1}\\n\\\\]\\nthe source's \\\\(Y\\\\) is undefined and must be \\\\(Z\\\\), and the Lagrange\\nmultiplier calculation requires \\\\(Q^+\\\\), not \\\\(Q^\\\\top\\\\).  Direct\\nmultiplication, using the compatibility \\\\(X^\\\\top Q=P^\\\\top Z\\\\), verifies\\n\\\\(EQ=Z\\\\) and \\\\(P^\\\\top E=X^\\\\top\\\\); the displayed Lagrange stationarity\\nthen proves that (A.1) is the minimum-Frobenius-norm solution.  The\\northogonal Gaussian remainder in the lemma is therefore unchanged.\\n\\nIn the projected-variance rewrite (source lines 1866--1869), with\\n\\\\(\\\\gamma=Y^\\\\top h/n\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\Upsilon=Y^\\\\top Y/n\\\\), the exact identity is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sigma^2=\\\\sigma_A^2\\\\left(\\\\frac{h^\\\\top h}{n}\\n                         -\\\\gamma^\\\\top\\\\Upsilon^+\\\\gamma\\\\right), \\\\tag{A.2}\\n\\\\]\\nbecause \\\\(Y^\\\\top h=n\\\\gamma\\\\) and\\n\\\\((Y^\\\\top Y)^+=n^{-1}\\\\Upsilon^+\\\\).  This is precisely the expression whose\\nlimit the next source line states.  Finally, at source lines 3663--3668,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_\\\\alpha\\n\\\\max(\\\\sigma^2d_\\\\alpha,\\\\mathring\\\\sigma^2)^p\\n\\\\le\\\\max(\\\\sigma^2,\\\\mathring\\\\sigma^2)^p,             \\\\tag{A.3}\\n\\\\]\\nnot the same maximum to the power \\\\(p/2\\\\).  Since\\n\\\\(\\\\sigma\\\\to\\\\mathring\\\\sigma\\\\), (A.3) has the uniform boundedness required\\nthere.  None of (A.1)--(A.3) alters a definition or a subsequent estimate;\\nthey repair the literal algebra used to reach the intended quantities.\\n\\nTwo printed auxiliary claims were deliberately excluded after source-level\\ninspection: Appendix A.1's arbitrary varying-conditioning almost-sure\\nclaim is stronger than dominated convergence warrants, and the printed\\nAppendix A.2 mean-convergence proof omits a uniform-integrability step.\\nThe Gaussian operator-norm citation there is also not used; (2.13) is the\\ncomplete net proof required here.  Thus no conclusion of the external\\npaper beyond the verified assertion (3.1) enters this proof.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b319d63e887d2b78cfcb8fd350c9b\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-1c203cfb-ce19-4534-8aeb-fb12911eacda\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '361,720p' /tmp/L2_FULL_AUDITED_PROOF.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\bar H_{n,k}&=h(\\\\bar X_{n,k}),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar S_{n,k}&=B_{0,n}\\\\bar H_{n,k}\\n-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r\\\\bar D_{n,r}\\n                    \\\\langle h_r,h_k\\\\rangle_R,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar G_{n,k}&=\\\\arctan\\\\bar S_{n,k},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\bar D_{n,k}=\\\\bar C_{n,k}q(\\\\bar S_{n,k}),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar P_{n,k}&=B_{0,n}^\\\\top\\\\bar D_{n,k}\\n-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r\\\\bar H_{n,r}\\n                    \\\\langle d_r,d_k\\\\rangle_L,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar X_{n,k+1}&=\\\\bar X_{n,k}-2\\\\delta e_k\\\\bar P_{n,k},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar C_{n,k+1}&=\\\\bar C_{n,k}-2\\\\delta e_k\\\\bar G_{n,k}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}                                      \\\\tag{5.3}\\n\\\\]\\nAll coefficients in (5.3) are deterministic and independent of \\\\(n\\\\), and\\nthere are only finitely many \\\\(B_{0,n},B_{0,n}^\\\\top\\\\) calls.  It is therefore\\none fixed parameterless program: the language permits arbitrary fixed real\\ncoordinate maps, so the possibly irrational numbers \\\\(e_r\\\\) and the\\ncanonical Grams cause no parameter line or empirical feedback.  Induction\\non \\\\(k\\\\), using the canonical\\nconstruction of Section 4 and (5.2), identifies its symbolic limiting\\nvariables with the canonical Euler nodes.  Theorem (3.1) then gives, almost\\nsurely, every finite joint law and empirical moment needed below.\\n\\nDefine the finite oracle rank memory\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bar B_{n,k}=B_{0,n}-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r\\n                   \\\\bar D_{n,r}\\\\otimes_n\\\\bar H_{n,r}. \\\\tag{5.4}\\n\\\\]\\nThe program nodes in (5.3) are not asserted to be exact actions of (5.4).\\nTheir two exact residuals are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\bar B_{n,k}\\\\bar H_{n,k}-\\\\bar S_{n,k}\\n&=-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r\\\\bar D_{n,r}\\n \\\\bigl(\\\\langle\\\\bar H_{n,r},\\\\bar H_{n,k}\\\\rangle_n\\n       -\\\\langle h_r,h_k\\\\rangle_R\\\\bigr),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bar B_{n,k}^*\\\\bar D_{n,k}-\\\\bar P_{n,k}\\n&=-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_r\\\\bar H_{n,r}\\n \\\\bigl(\\\\langle\\\\bar D_{n,r},\\\\bar D_{n,k}\\\\rangle_n\\n       -\\\\langle d_r,d_k\\\\rangle_L\\\\bigr).\\n\\\\end{aligned}                                      \\\\tag{5.5}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is the point at which empirical Gram errors enter; they are not\\nsilently replaced by expectations.\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\mathscr M_N\\\\) be the finite list consisting of the output moment,\\nall \\\\(H\\\\)- and \\\\(D\\\\)-Grams in (5.5), and the squared norms of every oracle\\nvector in (5.3).  Set\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\zeta_n=\\\\max_{M\\\\in\\\\mathscr M_N}|M_n-M|.            \\\\tag{5.6}\\n\\\\]\\nTheorem (3.1) gives \\\\(\\\\zeta_n\\\\to0\\\\) almost surely.  In particular all\\noracle vector norms are bounded on that event for large \\\\(n\\\\), and (5.5)\\nis bounded by \\\\(C_{N,\\\\delta}\\\\zeta_n\\\\) in normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\).\\n\\n### 5.2  Coupling to the actual scalar-feedback Euler scheme\\n\\nUse the same \\\\(A_{n,0},B_{0,n}\\\\) for the actual and oracle schemes.  Let\\n\\\\(B_{n,k}\\\\) be the actual learned matrix, and define\\n\\\\[\\nE_{n,k}=\\\\|X_{n,k}-\\\\bar X_{n,k}\\\\|_n+\\n        \\\\|C_{n,k}-\\\\bar C_{n,k}\\\\|_n+\\n        \\\\|B_{n,k}-\\\\bar B_{n,k}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}.        \\\\tag{5.7}\\n\\\\]\\nOn the common bounded event, (2.5)--(2.9) and the residuals (5.5) give,\\nnode by node,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n&\\\\|H_{n,k}-\\\\bar H_{n,k}\\\\|_n+\\n \\\\|S_{n,k}-\\\\bar S_{n,k}\\\\|_n+\\n \\\\|G_{n,k}-\\\\bar G_{n,k}\\\\|_n\\\\\\\\\\n&\\\\quad+\\\\|D_{n,k}-\\\\bar D_{n,k}\\\\|_n+\\n \\\\|P_{n,k}-\\\\bar P_{n,k}\\\\|_n+|e_{n,k}-e_k|\\n \\\\le C_{N,\\\\delta}(E_{n,k}+\\\\zeta_n).               \\\\tag{5.8}\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\nFor clarity, the scalar step in (5.8) is\\n\\\\[\\n|e_{n,k}-e_k|\\\\le\\n|\\\\langle C_{n,k},G_{n,k}\\\\rangle_n-\\n  \\\\langle\\\\bar C_{n,k},\\\\bar G_{n,k}\\\\rangle_n|\\n+|\\\\langle\\\\bar C_{n,k},\\\\bar G_{n,k}\\\\rangle_n-\\n  \\\\langle c_k,g_k\\\\rangle_L|,\\n\\\\]\\nand the second term is one of (5.6).  The first is bounded by the preceding\\nvector differences.  Subtracting the three Euler updates and using\\n\\\\(\\\\|u\\\\otimes v-u'\\\\otimes v'\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\le\\n\\\\|u-u'\\\\|_2\\\\|v\\\\|_2+\\\\|u'\\\\|_2\\\\|v-v'\\\\|_2\\\\) yields\\n\\\\[\\nE_{n,k+1}\\\\le(1+C_{N,\\\\delta}\\\\delta)E_{n,k}\\n             +C_{N,\\\\delta}\\\\delta\\\\zeta_n.          \\\\tag{5.9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(E_{n,0}=\\\\|C_{n,0}\\\\|_n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\).  Since \\\\(N\\\\) is\\nfixed, discrete Gronwall proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{k\\\\le N}E_{n,k}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.                       \\\\tag{5.10}\\n\\\\]\\nThis proves fixed-mesh identification of the actual Euler network.  It\\ndoes not assert, or require, a tensor theorem with \\\\(N=N_n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\).\\n\\n### 5.3  Removing the mesh\\n\\nThe estimates of Section 2 hold verbatim on the canonical spaces.  Thus\\ncanonical Euler and the unique solution of (4.4) satisfy\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\\\{\\\\|x^\\\\delta-X\\\\|_2+\\n\\\\|b^\\\\delta-B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}+\\\\|c^\\\\delta-C\\\\|_2\\\\}\\n\\\\le C_T\\\\delta.                                     \\\\tag{5.11}\\n\\\\]\\nThe finite-width Euler estimate is (2.14).  We now define the topology and\\nprove the assertion for arbitrary nested operator probes.\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\mathscr L_R,\\\\mathscr L_L\\\\) be the countable current-rooted typed\\nlanguage with roots \\\\(x,c\\\\), rational linear combinations, current\\n\\\\({\\\\bf b},{\\\\bf b}^*\\\\) calls, the exact globally Lipschitz or bounded maps\\n\\\\(J,h,\\\\arctan,q\\\\), the controlled map \\\\((c,s)\\\\mapsto cq(s)\\\\), every\\nrational bounded-Lipschitz cylinder map, and\\n\\\\[\\n(a,p)\\\\mapsto q(a)^r\\\\rho_M(p),\\\\qquad r=1,2,\\\\quad M\\\\in\\\\mathbb N. \\\\tag{5.12}\\n\\\\]\\nIt is closed after every operation.  An \\\\(R\\\\)-**record** is a finite list\\nof same-side probe tuples from this language together with their\\ninner-products.  Its distance is the sum of the \\\\(W_2\\\\) distances of the\\ntuple laws and the absolute differences of the listed inner-products.\\nChoose an enumeration \\\\((R_j)\\\\) and define\\n\\\\[\\nd_{\\\\rm act}(\\\\Theta,\\\\widetilde\\\\Theta)\\n=\\\\sum_{j\\\\ge1}2^{-j}\\\\{1\\\\wedge d_{R_j}(\\\\Theta,\\\\widetilde\\\\Theta)\\\\}. \\\\tag{5.13}\\n\\\\]\\nWe work in the category carrying the common operator and \\\\(C\\\\)-supremum\\nbounds proved in Sections 2 and 4; finite records alone would not encode\\nsuch a bound.\\n\\nHere is the required structural estimate.  If two states live on the same\\nfinite normalized spaces, or on the same canonical spaces, and\\n\\\\[\\nd_0=\\\\|x-\\\\widetilde x\\\\|_2+\\n    \\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}+\\\\|c-\\\\widetilde c\\\\|_2,\\n\\\\]\\nthen for every fixed probe \\\\(v\\\\) in (5.12)\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|v(\\\\Theta)-v(\\\\widetilde\\\\Theta)\\\\|_2\\\\le C_vd_0.     \\\\tag{5.14}\\n\\\\]\\nThis follows by induction on the syntax tree.  The coordinate-map steps\\nuse their Lipschitz bounds; \\\\(cq(s)\\\\) uses the common \\\\(C\\\\)-supremum bound;\\nand the operator step is exactly\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|bv-\\\\widetilde b\\\\,\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|v\\\\|_2+\\n   \\\\|\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2, \\\\tag{5.15}\\n\\\\]\\nwith the identical adjoint estimate.  Rational combinations and\\ninner-products follow from the triangle and Cauchy--Schwarz inequalities.\\nThis proves (5.14) for arbitrarily nested \\\\(b,b^*\\\\) calls, and coupling\\ncoordinates gives the corresponding \\\\(d_R\\\\) estimate.\\n\\nAt a fixed mesh, a finite oracle record is again one fixed tensor program.\\nTo see this without hiding empirical feedback, recursively expand every\\nnew \\\\(b_kv\\\\) call as\\n\\\\[\\nB_0v-2\\\\delta\\\\sum_{r<k}e_rd_r\\\\langle h_r,v\\\\rangle_R, \\\\tag{5.16}\\n\\\\]\\nand every adjoint call analogously, using the deterministic canonical\\ninner-product.  Only finitely many calls occur.  Let\\n\\\\(\\\\zeta_{n,R}\\\\) be the maximum absolute empirical-minus-canonical\\ndifference of the finite list of Grams introduced by this recursion,\\ntogether with (5.6).  Theorem (3.1) gives\\n\\\\(\\\\zeta_{n,R}\\\\to0\\\\) almost surely.  Induction on probe depth, using the\\nexact finite-rank action and (5.15), gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{v\\\\in R}\\\\|v(\\\\bar\\\\Theta_{n,k})-\\\\bar v_{n,k}\\\\|_n\\n\\\\le C_{R,N,\\\\delta}\\\\zeta_{n,R},                    \\\\tag{5.17}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\bar v_{n,k}\\\\) is the deterministic-coefficient tensor-program\\nprobe from (5.16).  Equations (5.7)--(5.10) and (5.14) then identify the\\nactual Euler record with the canonical Euler record.\\n\\nFinally compare finite-width flow to its Euler scheme using (2.14) and\\n(5.14), take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\) using (5.17), and compare\\ncanonical Euler to canonical flow using (5.11) and (5.14).  Uniform time\\nmoduli replace the finite grid by all of \\\\([0,T]\\\\).  Hence, for every fixed\\nrecord and every \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_R(\\\\theta_n(t),\\\\Theta(t))\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.       \\\\tag{5.18}\\n\\\\]\\nFor the metric (5.13), first choose a finite prefix whose remaining weight\\nis below \\\\(\\\\varepsilon/2\\\\), and apply a union bound to that prefix.  This\\nproves uniform compact-time convergence in \\\\(d_{\\\\rm act}\\\\).\\nThe state carries the common operator bound furnished by (2.13) and the\\nfinite rank integral in (4.4); mere finite-record convergence without this\\nbound would not suffice.  Equations (4.4), (4.8), and (5.18) prove the\\nclaimed well-posed, autonomous, restartable law-level limit.\\n\\n## 6. Path laws, kernels, and velocities\\n\\nThe raw preactivations are \\\\(A=J(X)\\\\) and \\\\(S=Bh(X)\\\\).  From (1.3), (1.6), and the bounds of Section 2,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\\\bigl(\\\\|\\\\dot A(t)\\\\|_n+\\n\\\\|\\\\dot S(t)\\\\|_n\\\\bigr)<\\\\infty                       \\\\tag{6.1}\\n\\\\]\\non the high-probability initialization event.  For either \\\\(Z=A\\\\) or \\\\(Z=S\\\\), and an interval \\\\(I\\\\) of length \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\sup_{s,t\\\\in I}|Z_i(t)-Z_i(s)|^2\\n\\\\le\\\\delta\\\\int_I\\\\|\\\\dot Z(r)\\\\|_n^2\\\\,dr.            \\\\tag{6.2}\\n\\\\]\\nHence each empirical path law is within \\\\(C_T\\\\sqrt\\\\delta\\\\) in\\n\\\\(W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\) of its grid projection.  At a fixed grid, (5.18)\\nreduces the assertion to a fixed Euler program; (3.1) then gives weak\\nconvergence and convergence of the squared grid maximum, since that maximum\\nis a polynomially bounded test of finitely many program vectors.  First let\\n\\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then let the grid mesh tend to zero in (6.2).  This proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{A_i(\\\\cdot)}\\\\to\\\\mu_A,qquad\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_j\\\\delta_{S_j(\\\\cdot)}\\\\to\\\\mu_S\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in }W_2(C([0,T]))                       \\\\tag{6.3}\\n\\\\]\\nin probability, as well as uniform integrability of the squared path suprema.\\n\\nOne further argument is necessary for the gated backpropagated fields; they are not naively Lipschitz as maps \\\\(L^2\\\\times L^2\\\\to L^2\\\\).  Put again \\\\(P=B^*D\\\\).  Equations (2.5)--(2.9) show that\\n\\\\[\\n(X,B,C)\\\\longmapsto(A,S,D,P)                         \\\\tag{6.4}\\n\\\\]\\nis Lipschitz from the bounded regularized state into normalized \\\\(L^2\\\\).  Also\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot P=\\\\dot B^*D+B^*\\\\dot D,qquad\\n\\\\dot D=\\\\dot Cq(S)+Cq'(S)\\\\dot S,                    \\\\tag{6.5}\\n\\\\]\\nand (1.6) implies a uniform \\\\(L^2\\\\) bound on the right sides.  Consequently the joint empirical laws of \\\\((A,P)\\\\) converge uniformly in time in \\\\(W_2(\\\\mathbb R^2)\\\\), and the resulting family is compact in that topology.\\n\\nWe use the following elementary lemma.\\n\\n**Dominated pushforward lemma.**  If \\\\(\\\\mu_r\\\\to\\\\mu\\\\) in \\\\(W_2(\\\\mathbb R^2)\\\\), with coordinates \\\\((a,p)\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\Psi\\\\) is continuous with \\\\(|\\\\Psi(a,p)|\\\\le |p|\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\Psi_\\\\#\\\\mu_r\\\\to\\\\Psi_\\\\#\\\\mu\\\\) in \\\\(W_2(\\\\mathbb R)\\\\).  The assertion is uniform over a compact \\\\(W_2\\\\)-family.\\n\\n**Proof.**  \\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence is weak convergence plus uniform integrability of \\\\(a^2+p^2\\\\).  On \\\\(|p|\\\\le M\\\\), truncate further to a compact set and approximate \\\\(\\\\Psi\\\\) by bounded continuous functions.  Outside it,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int|\\\\Psi|^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\mu_r\\n\\\\le\\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\mu_r,              \\\\tag{6.6}\\n\\\\]\\nwhich tends to zero uniformly in \\\\(r\\\\) as \\\\(M\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\).  This proves weak convergence and convergence of second moments of the pushforwards, hence \\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence.  Compactness gives the uniform form. \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n\\nApply the lemma to\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Psi_1(a,p)=q(a)p,qquad \\\\Psi_2(a,p)=q(a)^2p.       \\\\tag{6.7}\\n\\\\]\\nBoth are bounded in magnitude by \\\\(|p|\\\\).  The required uniform truncation is as follows.\\nLet\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mu_{n,t}=\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{(A_{n,i}(t),P_{n,i}(t))},\\n\\\\qquad \\\\mu_t=\\\\mathcal L(A(t),P(t)).                 \\\\tag{6.8}\\n\\\\]\\nThe fixed-record convergence (5.18), the Lipschitz map (6.4), and the time\\nmodulus from (6.5) imply\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}W_2(\\\\mu_{n,t},\\\\mu_t)\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.                       \\\\tag{6.9}\\n\\\\]\\nThe image \\\\(\\\\{\\\\mu_t:0\\\\le t\\\\le T\\\\}\\\\) is compact in \\\\(W_2\\\\).  For any\\n\\\\(\\\\gamma,\\\\mu\\\\in\\\\mathcal P_2(\\\\mathbb R^2)\\\\), an optimal coupling of their\\nsecond coordinates gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\gamma\\n\\\\le4W_2(\\\\gamma,\\\\mu)^2\\n +2\\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M/2}\\\\,d\\\\mu.              \\\\tag{6.10}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed, if \\\\(|p_\\\\mu|\\\\le M/2<|p_\\\\gamma|\\\\), then\\n\\\\(|p_\\\\gamma|\\\\le2|p_\\\\gamma-p_\\\\mu|\\\\); otherwise use\\n\\\\(|p_\\\\gamma|^2\\\\le2|p_\\\\gamma-p_\\\\mu|^2+2|p_\\\\mu|^2\\\\).\\nA finite \\\\(W_2\\\\)-cover of the compact limiting family, followed by (6.10),\\ngives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lim_{M\\\\to\\\\infty}\\\\limsup_{n\\\\to\\\\infty}\\n\\\\mathbb P\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\n \\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\mu_{n,t}>\\\\varepsilon\\\\right)=0. \\\\tag{6.11}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\rho_M(p)=(-M)\\\\vee(p\\\\wedge M)\\\\) and\\n\\\\[\\nQ=q(A)^2P,\\\\qquad Q_M=q(A)^2\\\\rho_M(P),              \\\\tag{6.12}\\n\\\\]\\nwith identical finite-width notation.  Then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|Q-Q_M\\\\|_2^2\\\\le\\\\int p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>M}\\\\,d\\\\mu_t, \\\\tag{6.13}\\n\\\\]\\nand the same empirical inequality holds.  At fixed \\\\(M\\\\), the map in\\n(6.12) is bounded and globally Lipschitz, so \\\\(Q_M,BQ_M\\\\), and all their\\njoint Grams are fixed action probes and converge uniformly by (5.18).\\nPut\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_n=\\\\|H_n\\\\|_n^2,\\\\quad\\nY_n=\\\\alpha_nD_n+B_nQ_n,\\\\quad\\nY_{n,M}=\\\\alpha_nD_n+B_nQ_{n,M},                   \\\\tag{6.14}\\n\\\\]\\nand define \\\\(Y,Y_M\\\\) canonically.  Uniform operator bounds give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|Y_n-Y_{n,M}\\\\|_n\\\\le M_B\\\\|Q_n-Q_{n,M}\\\\|_n,\\n\\\\quad\\n\\\\|Y-Y_M\\\\|_2\\\\le M_B\\\\|Q-Q_M\\\\|_2.                 \\\\tag{6.15}\\n\\\\]\\nUsing\\n\\\\(|\\\\|u\\\\|^2-\\\\|v\\\\|^2|\\\\le(\\\\|u\\\\|+\\\\|v\\\\|)\\\\|u-v\\\\|\\\\), first take\\n\\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(M\\\\), and only then \\\\(M\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\).  Equations\\n(6.11)--(6.15) prove\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}|\\\\|Y_n(t)\\\\|_n^2-\\\\|Y(t)\\\\|_2^2|\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.      \\\\tag{6.16}\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(\\\\dot S_n=-2e_nY_n\\\\), (6.16) gives uniform convergence of its\\nsquared velocity.  The same argument applied to \\\\(q(A)P\\\\), whose magnitude\\nis at most \\\\(|P|\\\\), handles \\\\(\\\\dot A\\\\).  Hence\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\dot Z_n(t)\\\\|_n^2dt\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\int_0^T\\\\|\\\\dot Z(t)\\\\|_2^2dt,\\n\\\\qquad Z=A,S.                                     \\\\tag{6.17}\\n\\\\]\\nThe blocks \\\\(K^B,K^C\\\\) use bounded activations and \\\\(D\\\\), while \\\\(K^A\\\\)\\nuses the same dominated truncation.  Consequently\\n\\\\[\\nK_n^r\\\\to K^r\\\\quad(r=A,B,C),\\\\qquad K_n\\\\to K         \\\\tag{6.18}\\n\\\\]\\nuniformly on \\\\([0,T]\\\\), and \\\\(f_n,L_n\\\\) converge uniformly as well.\\n\\n## 7. Exact GD versus the finite-width flow\\n\\nIt remains to replace the auxiliary Euler schemes by the exact GD specified in the theorem.  Let\\n\\\\[\\nR=-2eB^*D.                                          \\\\tag{7.1}\\n\\\\]\\nOne raw GD step is \\\\(A^+=A+\\\\eta q(A)R\\\\); the \\\\(B,C\\\\) updates are exactly the Euler updates of (2.2).  Since \\\\(F\\\\) is cubic,\\n\\\\[\\nF(A+\\\\eta q(A)R)=F(A)+\\\\eta R\\n +\\\\eta^2Aq(A)^2R^2+\\\\frac{\\\\eta^3}{3}q(A)^3R^3       \\\\tag{7.2}\\n\\\\]\\ncoordinatewise, with no remainder.  Now\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_a|a|q(a)^2<\\\\infty,qquad\\n\\\\|R\\\\|_n\\\\le C_T,qquad\\n\\\\|R\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\sqrt n\\\\|R\\\\|_n.                  \\\\tag{7.3}\\n\\\\]\\nTherefore the one-step defect relative to Euler in the \\\\(X\\\\)-coordinate is at most\\n\\\\[\\nC_T(\\\\eta^2\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta^3n).                         \\\\tag{7.4}\\n\\\\]\\nThe ordinary ODE local defect is \\\\(O_T(\\\\eta^2)\\\\).  Applying the dimension-free stability estimate (2.10) step by step gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t),\\\\theta_n(t))\\n\\\\le C_T\\\\bigl(\\\\eta+\\\\eta\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta^2n\\\\bigr).       \\\\tag{7.5}\\n\\\\]\\nThe proof is first stopped on the enlarged bounded set from Section 2; the right side tends to zero, so the first-exit event is impossible for large \\\\(n\\\\).  With \\\\(\\\\eta=\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\), (7.5) tends to zero.\\n\\nWe linearly interpolate the parameter iterates and recompute all network\\nnodes from those interpolated parameters.  Let \\\\(V_{A,k},V_{B,k}\\\\) be the\\nconstant parameter slopes on step \\\\(k\\\\).  Then\\n\\\\[\\nV_{A,k}=-2e_kq(A_k)P_k,\\\\qquad\\nV_{B,k}=-2e_kD_k\\\\otimes_nH_k,                     \\\\tag{7.6}\\n\\\\]\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b31a1fd7487d2a71cd17307a7ddf5\",\"summary\":[\"**Developing regularized convergence proof**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b31ade28487d28cbda28158b86c29\",\"summary\":[\"**Developing regularized convergence proof**\",\"**Developing Gaussian lemma proof**\",\"**Proving perturbed Gaussian lemma**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9b31bab0e887d2a78cc61539ef1a6d\",\"text\":\"The attached proof has a useful structure: expand the trained matrix, construct the population action, identify its transpose action, then control the time discretization. For the two-layer proof, two details need explicit treatment: the oracle’s population coefficients differ from empirical coefficients, and \\\\(x^{(1)}=F(z^{(1)})\\\\) transforms continuous flow exactly but introduces a defect for discrete GD.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-584f00ef-5b6f-4611-a702-89a0d9c7714e\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'ls -ld /tmp/l2_restatement_review'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"failed\",\"exitCode\":2,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"ls: cannot access '/tmp/l2_restatement_review': No such file or directory\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-6462ce54-093d-49b6-9313-6818152476b1\",\"query\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10685\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10685\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b31c0cc8887d286e1a01cfee7e2b4\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying the gradient metric**\",\"**Computing the metric differential**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b31cddf9487d2bfb40b1e244634fb\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying the gradient metric**\",\"**Computing the metric differential**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b31d9401087d2a5d3478f858af99e\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying the gradient metric**\",\"**Computing the metric differential**\",\"**Defining the countable library**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b31e69df887d29c618772350acc2c\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying the gradient metric**\",\"**Computing the metric differential**\",\"**Defining the countable library**\",\"**Establishing perturbed Gram bounds**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-963dea3c-469d-494d-a4ea-f73dc7fae6f4\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'mktemp -d /tmp/l2-proof-review-XXXXXX'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"/tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-a4519d9c-2658-4640-97da-77d6c6412b27\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Two hidden layers: a complete proof of the joint width and learning-rate limit\\n\\nWe consider one input, equal to (1), and target (1). At width (n), the first preactivation (z^{(1)}=W^{(1)}) and output weight (W^{(3)}) are vectors in (mathbb R^n), and (W^{(2)}) is an (n)-by-(n) matrix. Throughout, (W^{(3)}) means the rescaled output weight. Put\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z^{(1)}),\\\\quad z^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_n=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad r_n=f_n-1,\\\\qquad L_n=r_n^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll initial entries are independent, with\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,i}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad W_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nDefine the derivatives without the loss residual by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_n/\\\\partial z^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\). The exact GD being analyzed is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{k+1}^{(1)}&=z_k^{(1)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}\\\\delta_k^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}}n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}(h_k^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}h_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nWe use the clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), interpolate these three parameters linearly, and recompute the other network quantities from them. The proof applies whenever \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\), in particular to \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\).\\n\\nWe will construct one deterministic population evolution, prove that every fixed finite collection of its forward and backward measurements is the limit of the corresponding finite-network measurements, and prove uniform convergence of predictions, losses, and all three kernel blocks on every finite time interval. We also prove convergence of the two hidden trajectory laws and their integrated squared speeds. At small positive times both hidden layers move, the activation remains non-affine on their distributions, and the kernel changes.\\n\\nCapital letters \\\\(Z^{(\\\\ell)},H^{(\\\\ell)},X^{(1)}\\\\) denote individual population coordinates. We keep \\\\(W^{(2)},W^{(3)},\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\) for their population counterparts, explicitly identifying when we have moved to population quantities. An expectation always concerns coordinates belonging to the same layer. For a scalar population variable \\\\(V\\\\), write \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\); finite vectors always use \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\), with the width normalization visible.\\n\\nFirst consider the continuous finite-width flow associated with (1). Set\\n\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+s^3/3,\\\\qquad x^{(1)}=F(z^{(1)}).\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(F'(s)\\\\phi'(s)=1\\\\), this flow becomes\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot x^{(1)}&=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-\\\\frac{2r_n}{n}\\\\delta^{(2)}(h^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2r_nh^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nThe change of coordinates is exact for the flow. Euler with proof mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) means replacing each derivative in (2) by its forward difference. In particular,\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}^{(1)}=x_k^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is Euler for the transformed flow; transforming the GD iterates in (1) produces an additional defect, estimated below.\\n\\nIterating the Euler matrix update gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently, for any appropriate vectors \\\\(v,u\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}v=W_0^{(2)}v-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top v}{n},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top u=(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}h_s^{(1)}\\n\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_s^{(2)})^\\\\top u}{n}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus training adds a finite sum of rank-one matrices. At fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\), only finitely many initial-matrix calls and scalar contractions need to be understood.\\n\\nThere is a logical detail here: the empirical coefficients in (5)–(6) are random. We first analyze an auxiliary finite system, the oracle, using deterministic population residuals and contractions in their place. Those numbers will be constructed below from the initial population action; they are not assumptions about the empirical network. We then prove that its empirical contractions approach the supplied numbers, and compare it with the empirical-feedback Euler system.\\n\\nThe matrix calculation needed for this construction is elementary Gaussian conditioning. For example, the first forward call is\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditional on \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), its coordinates are independent centered Gaussians of variance \\\\(\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\). Hence the initial population coordinates satisfy\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad H_0^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_0^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]).\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\nIn the oracle, \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\) and \\\\(r_0=-1\\\\). Its first update leaves \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) unchanged and gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(3)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)})\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_0^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nThe next transpose call depends on the matrix already used in the forward pass. Define, for nonzero \\\\(b\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nP_{b^\\\\perp}=I-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditioning each Gaussian row on its observed dot product with \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), and then summing the rows with coefficients \\\\(\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)}\\\\), gives\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d=\\n\\\\frac{(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}h_0^{(1)}\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nHere equality is conditional in distribution, and \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the preceding history. The first term supplies precisely the overlap required by\\n\\\\[\\n(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)};\\n\\\\]\\nthe second is perpendicular to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\).\\n\\nAfter the first layer moves, the next initial-matrix call is \\\\(W_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\). Condition on both previous observations. Multiplying the resulting conditional matrix by \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}z_0^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{[P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}]^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2^2}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}h_2^{(1)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nThe first response retains the old forward answer. The second supplies the remaining overlap required by the old transpose answer. The last term disturbs neither observation. Formula (5), with population coefficients for the oracle, then adds the learned rank-one correction to obtain its actual preactivation node.\\n\\nWe now prove the finite Gaussian fact needed to repeat this argument, including the case of dependent query directions. The proof below requires only empirical convergence against continuous measurements with at most quadratic growth. It does not claim convergence against arbitrary discontinuous functions.\\n\\nConsider a fixed finite calculation starting from iid coordinate tuples with finite second moments, independent of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\). Its instructions are fixed globally Lipschitz coordinate functions, linear combinations with deterministic coefficients, and calls to \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) or its transpose. Root tuples may include both \\\\(z_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(F(z_0^{(1)})\\\\). Then all same-layer joint empirical laws have deterministic limits, including their second moments.\\n\\nFor precision, the distance between two laws used here is the smallest root-mean-square distance between random vectors having those laws, usually denoted \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\). For empirical laws, matching coordinates gives the bound\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{v_i},\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{\\\\widetilde v_i}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\le\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\|v_i-\\\\widetilde v_i\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\nConvergence in this distance means weak convergence together with convergence of second moments. Thus it includes all dot products of a fixed joint tuple.\\n\\nFirst, the Gaussian matrix has a uniform operator bound with exponentially high probability. A \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net of the Euclidean unit sphere has at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) elements. Using two such nets,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le 2\\\\,9^{2n}\\\\exp(-nM^2/8).\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed the operator norm is at most twice the largest bilinear form on these nets, and each bilinear form is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). Choose a fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\).\\n\\nTo avoid inverting almost-dependent query directions, temporarily add \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) to the input of each matrix call, where each \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\) is a new independent standard Gaussian vector. Fix \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\). This is only a device for the proof; we will remove it.\\n\\nSuppose previous calls have revealed\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}V=Y,\\\\qquad (W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R.\\n\\\\]\\nThe columns of \\\\(V\\\\) are old first-layer input vectors, the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) are old second-layer input vectors, and \\\\(Y,R\\\\) contain the respective answers. Their column counts are fixed. All coordinates derived from these answers give no further information once the answers and roots are fixed. Gaussian conditioning therefore leaves\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}\\\\overset d=\\nY(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\n+U(U^\\\\top U)^{-1}R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}\\n+P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}P_{V^\\\\perp}.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(P_{V^\\\\perp}=I-V(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\\\), and similarly for \\\\(U\\\\); terms for an empty column list are zero. The fresh matrix has independent \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\) entries. The first two terms obey both recorded equations; the remaining Gaussian matrix lies in exactly the directions that leave those equations unchanged. Orthogonal components of a centered isotropic Gaussian are independent, which proves (12), including for adaptive queries: each input is chosen from the earlier answers before its new answer is observed.\\n\\nFor a new right-side input \\\\(v\\\\), multiplication gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}v\\\\overset d=Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma,\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_n=(V^\\\\top V/n)^{-1}(V^\\\\top v/n),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\beta_n=(U^\\\\top U/n)^{-1}(R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}v/n).\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose case exchanges the two layer populations.\\n\\nInductively, all the normalized products in these expressions converge. The perturbation guarantees invertibility of their limiting input Gram matrices: when a new column \\\\(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) is added to any old input list, conditional Gaussian second-moment calculations give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi)\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}a\\\\|_2^2}{n}+\\\\varepsilon^2+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe cross term has conditional variance at most \\\\(4\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\|a\\\\|_2^2/n^2\\\\), and only a fixed number of Gaussian directions are removed. Its limiting squared distance is at least \\\\(\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\). Successive Schur complements therefore stay positive; all the finite Gram inverses in (13) converge.\\n\\nConsequently the response coefficients and Gaussian variance converge. The projection removes finitely many directions, and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{U}\\\\gamma\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\,\\\\middle|\\\\,\\\\text{history}\\\\right]\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{rank}(U)}n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\nAfter removing this negligible projection and replacing coefficients by their limits, the new coordinates are an old coordinate function plus independent scalar Gaussians. For a bounded Lipschitz measurement, conditional empirical variance is at most a constant divided by \\\\(n\\\\). Its conditional mean is the old empirical average of the measurement averaged over one scalar Gaussian, which converges by the previous induction step. The conditional variance of the new squared norm is also \\\\(O((1+\\\\|Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n\\\\|_2^2/n)/n)\\\\). Thus second moments converge as well. This proves the enlarged joint \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence. Lipschitz coordinate operations preserve it, closing the induction at fixed \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\).\\n\\nFinally couple the perturbed and unperturbed calculations using the same roots and matrix. On the event in (11) and the event that the finitely many \\\\(\\\\|\\\\xi\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\) are bounded, induction through the finite instructions gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{\\\\text{constructed }v}\\\\frac{\\\\|v^{\\\\varepsilon}-v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n\\\\le C\\\\varepsilon,\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(C\\\\) depends on the fixed calculation but not on \\\\(n\\\\) or \\\\(0<\\\\varepsilon\\\\le1\\\\). Each coordinate map multiplies a difference by its fixed Lipschitz constant, and each matrix call by at most \\\\(M\\\\), plus the new perturbation. Both events have probability tending to one. By (10), the deterministic limiting laws for \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\) form a Cauchy family in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\); its limit is also the limit of the unperturbed empirical laws, first taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\to0\\\\). This proves the finite Gaussian fact without assuming stability of a singular unperturbed Gram matrix.\\n\\nNow we turn these compatible finite calculations into one population matrix action. Take a countable collection of initial calculations, closed under rational linear combinations, initial-matrix calls in both directions, the Lipschitz maps used above, and a countable family of bounded Lipschitz functions of finite coordinate lists that is dense among continuous functions on compact sets. Include constants, clipping at every integer level, and products with a clipped factor. Finite joint limiting laws are consistent because every union of finitely many calculations is another such calculation. They therefore define two countable random coordinate collections, one per layer. Let their probability spaces contain exactly the information generated by these coordinates.\\n\\nIdentify two first-layer probes whenever\\n\\\\[\\nv\\\\sim\\\\widetilde v\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability},\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\nand do the same separately on the second layer. The limiting mean-square distance is \\\\(\\\\mathbb E[(V-\\\\widetilde V)^2]\\\\), so these classes are precisely the corresponding population random variables, identified when equal almost surely. Define\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}[v]=[W_0^{(2)}v].\\n\\\\]\\nThe definition is independent of representatives because (11) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|W_0^{(2)}v-W_0^{(2)}\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\le M^2\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\]\\non an event of probability tending to one. Linearity passes from the finite matrix, as does\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2].\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\nTaking all mean-square limits extends this action uniquely to every square-integrable variable on the first-layer probability space. The density needed here follows from the included bounded coordinate functions: finite-coordinate measurable functions approximate any variable on the generated space, and bounded continuous functions approximate them in mean square. The transpose action extends the same way in the reverse direction. Passing the exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{[(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u]^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\nto population expectations shows that this reverse action is precisely the adjoint:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,W_0^{(2)}V]=\\\\mathbb E[((W_0^{(2)})^*U)V].\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\nArbitrary fixed real coefficients are obtained by rational approximation; the same stability estimates identify any finite computation using them with this population construction.\\n\\nOn these fixed spaces the population state is \\\\((X^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\\\). Its derived variables and dynamics are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{gathered}\\nZ^{(1)}=F^{-1}(X^{(1)}),\\\\quad H^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H^{(1)},\\\\quad H^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(2)}),\\\\\\\\\\nf=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H^{(2)}],\\\\quad r=f-1,\\\\quad\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)}),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot X^{(1)}=-2r(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}=-2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}=-2rH^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{gathered} \\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\nThe rank-one action means\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)})V\\n=\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H^{(1)}V].\\n\\\\]\\nInitially \\\\(X_0^{(1)}=F(Z_0^{(1)})\\\\), the matrix action is (18), and \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\). Only three evolving objects are used; the population coordinate spaces themselves are infinite dimensional.\\n\\nWe next prove that (20) has a unique solution at every finite time and that Euler approximates it uniformly. The same estimates will apply at finite width. Measure two population states by\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each variable's \\\\(L^2\\\\) norm by its Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\); call this distance \\\\(d_n\\\\). The operator norm is the largest amplification of root-mean-square size.\\n\\nBoth \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\circ F^{-1}\\\\) are 1-Lipschitz. The functions \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\) are bounded and Lipschitz. If both operator norms are at most \\\\(M\\\\) and both output weights are pointwise bounded by \\\\(B\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le (\\\\pi/2)\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+M\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+B\\\\operatorname{Lip}(\\\\phi')\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}-(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+M\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\nThe output difference obeys Cauchy–Schwarz with \\\\(|H^{(2)}|\\\\le\\\\pi/2\\\\), and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|a\\\\otimes b-\\\\widetilde a\\\\otimes\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n\\\\le\\\\|a-\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nHence the vector field is Lipschitz in (21) on these bounded sets, with a constant independent of width.\\n\\nExistence can be proved directly by iteration of the integral equations. On a sufficiently short time interval, the map \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\mapsto\\\\Theta(0)+\\\\int_0^t\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))\\\\,ds\\\\) preserves a closed set of continuous paths with the indicated bounds and contracts the supremum of (21). This set is complete: mean-square limits preserve a common pointwise bound on \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\). Taking the time interval shorter than the reciprocal of the Lipschitz constant proves existence and uniqueness.\\n\\nTo continue the solution, differentiation of the output, using (19), gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f=-2rK,\\\\quad \\\\dot L=-4r^2K,\\\\qquad\\nK=K^{(1)}+K^{(2)}+K^{(3)},\\n\\\\tag{23}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nK^{(1)}&=\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[(\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)})^2\\\\right],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(2)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\delta^{(2)})^2],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(3)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(2)})^2].\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\nThese formulas also follow by differentiating along square-integrable parameter directions: the gradients of (L) in (Z^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)}) are (2r\\\\delta^{(1)},2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},2rH^{(2)}). For matrix perturbations use the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product, under which the displayed rank-one action is the gradient. The update (W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}) lies in that space because it is the integral of rank-one actions of bounded Hilbert–Schmidt norm. Thus (20), transformed back to (Z^{(1)}), is gradient flow for this loss, with the same scaling as (1).\\n\\nThe chain rules used here hold along these mean-square differentiable paths: bounded scalar derivatives give convergence of difference quotients by domination, first for bounded directions and then by mean-square approximation. We are not asserting that every coordinatewise nonlinear map is differentiable in operator norm on all of (L^2).\\n\\nSince \\\\(K\\\\ge0\\\\), \\\\(|r(t)|\\\\le|r(0)|\\\\). Therefore, on \\\\([0,T]\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\\\pi|r(0)|T,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\le\\\\pi|r(0)|\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|\\\\dot X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\le2|r(0)|\\\\|W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{25}\\n\\\\]\\nThese bounds prevent finite-time blow-up. At finite width, (11), the initial law of large numbers for (F(z_0^{(1)})), and (\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\to0) in probability put the same bounds on an event of probability tending to one.\\n\\nLet the vector-field bound and Lipschitz constant on a slightly larger bounded set be \\\\(M_T,L_T\\\\). One exact flow step of length \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) differs from its Euler step by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\\\int_t^{t+\\\\Delta}[\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))-\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(t))],ds\\\\right\\\\|\\n\\\\le \\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\Delta^2.\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently the acc\",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":35186}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b32bdeb4c87d2a3af0acb11c7bfad\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9b32c1a1dc87d280600dbeb8025909\",\"text\":\"The draft now includes a direct proof of the Gaussian step. It handles nearly dependent query directions by adding a small Gaussian perturbation, proving convergence while the Gram matrices are invertible, then removing the perturbation using the matrix norm bound. I’m having the reviewers check that argument alongside the oracle comparison and the GD-to-flow limit.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_F1BQMpBitkruklNJV7rotzit\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e3e-5cc9-7593-a15c-70531ca3453d\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r1a\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_vU7GJaNnmlxj1Gc4Ba7pSwrZ\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e3e-7338-7463-9de3-80c2c2096986\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r1b\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_LMrLQNxOpPNrOtYu5OH1b8ff\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e3e-8d48-7801-b1be-ebac2f140a50\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r1c\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b32d7c65887d2900ed275b46d5ccd\",\"summary\":[\"**Proving epsilon removal**\",\"**Checking isometry law**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-e9c22026-3097-4cfa-acb0-077075e0a181\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,95p' /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Two hidden layers: a complete proof of the joint width and learning-rate limit\\n\\nWe consider one input, equal to (1), and target (1). At width (n), the first preactivation (z^{(1)}=W^{(1)}) and output weight (W^{(3)}) are vectors in (mathbb R^n), and (W^{(2)}) is an (n)-by-(n) matrix. Throughout, (W^{(3)}) means the rescaled output weight. Put\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z^{(1)}),\\\\quad z^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_n=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad r_n=f_n-1,\\\\qquad L_n=r_n^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll initial entries are independent, with\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,i}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad W_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nDefine the derivatives without the loss residual by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_n/\\\\partial z^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\). The exact GD being analyzed is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{k+1}^{(1)}&=z_k^{(1)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}\\\\delta_k^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}}n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}(h_k^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}h_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nWe use the clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), interpolate these three parameters linearly, and recompute the other network quantities from them. The proof applies whenever \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\), in particular to \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\).\\n\\nWe will construct one deterministic population evolution, prove that every fixed finite collection of its forward and backward measurements is the limit of the corresponding finite-network measurements, and prove uniform convergence of predictions, losses, and all three kernel blocks on every finite time interval. We also prove convergence of the two hidden trajectory laws and their integrated squared speeds. At small positive times both hidden layers move, the activation remains non-affine on their distributions, and the kernel changes.\\n\\nCapital letters \\\\(Z^{(\\\\ell)},H^{(\\\\ell)},X^{(1)}\\\\) denote individual population coordinates. We keep \\\\(W^{(2)},W^{(3)},\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\) for their population counterparts, explicitly identifying when we have moved to population quantities. An expectation always concerns coordinates belonging to the same layer. For a scalar population variable \\\\(V\\\\), write \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\); finite vectors always use \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\), with the width normalization visible.\\n\\nFirst consider the continuous finite-width flow associated with (1). Set\\n\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+s^3/3,\\\\qquad x^{(1)}=F(z^{(1)}).\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(F'(s)\\\\phi'(s)=1\\\\), this flow becomes\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot x^{(1)}&=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-\\\\frac{2r_n}{n}\\\\delta^{(2)}(h^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2r_nh^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nThe change of coordinates is exact for the flow. Euler with proof mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) means replacing each derivative in (2) by its forward difference. In particular,\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}^{(1)}=x_k^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is Euler for the transformed flow; transforming the GD iterates in (1) produces an additional defect, estimated below.\\n\\nIterating the Euler matrix update gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently, for any appropriate vectors \\\\(v,u\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}v=W_0^{(2)}v-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top v}{n},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top u=(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}h_s^{(1)}\\n\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_s^{(2)})^\\\\top u}{n}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus training adds a finite sum of rank-one matrices. At fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\), only finitely many initial-matrix calls and scalar contractions need to be understood.\\n\\nThere is a logical detail here: the empirical coefficients in (5)–(6) are random. We first analyze an auxiliary finite system, the oracle, using deterministic population residuals and contractions in their place. Those numbers will be constructed below from the initial population action; they are not assumptions about the empirical network. We then prove that its empirical contractions approach the supplied numbers, and compare it with the empirical-feedback Euler system.\\n\\nThe matrix calculation needed for this construction is elementary Gaussian conditioning. For example, the first forward call is\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditional on \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), its coordinates are independent centered Gaussians of variance \\\\(\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\). Hence the initial population coordinates satisfy\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad H_0^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_0^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]).\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\nIn the oracle, \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\) and \\\\(r_0=-1\\\\). Its first update leaves \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) unchanged and gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(3)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)})\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_0^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nThe next transpose call depends on the matrix already used in the forward pass. Define, for nonzero \\\\(b\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nP_{b^\\\\perp}=I-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\]\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-e54f3b36-cad5-4827-81f2-13193b541e4d\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'wc -w -l /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"  522  4122 /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof.md\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b32e90c3c87d2a2556fe7f0405627\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-bd1dfa1a-4029-4547-a905-5427ecaef210\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n '\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\x0c|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\x08|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\x0b|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\x0d|mathbb|imes|ilde|rac' /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"3:We consider one input, equal to (1), and target (1). At width (n), the first preactivation (z^{(1)}=W^{(1)}) and output weight (W^{(3)}) are vectors in (mathbb R^n), and (W^{(2)}) is an (n)-by-(n) matrix. Throughout, (W^{(3)}) means the rescaled output weight. Put\\n10:f_n=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad r_n=f_n-1,\\\\qquad L_n=r_n^2.\\n27:W_{k+1}^{(2)}&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}}n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}(h_k^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\n33:We will construct one deterministic population evolution, prove that every fixed finite collection of its forward and backward measurements is the limit of the corresponding finite-network measurements, and prove uniform convergence of predictions, losses, and all three kernel blocks on every finite time interval. We also prove convergence of the two hidden trajectory laws and their integrated squared speeds. At small positive times both hidden layers move, the activation remains non-affine on their distributions, and the kernel changes.\\n35:Capital letters \\\\(Z^{(\\\\ell)},H^{(\\\\ell)},X^{(1)}\\\\) denote individual population coordinates. We keep \\\\(W^{(2)},W^{(3)},\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\) for their population counterparts, explicitly identifying when we have moved to population quantities. An expectation always concerns coordinates belonging to the same layer. For a scalar population variable \\\\(V\\\\), write \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\); finite vectors always use \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\), with the width normalization visible.\\n45:\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-\\\\frac{2r_n}{n}\\\\delta^{(2)}(h^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\n58:W_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n65:\\\\frac{(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top v}{n},\\n70:\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_s^{(2)})^\\\\top u}{n}.\\n73:Thus training adds a finite sum of rank-one matrices. At fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\), only finitely many initial-matrix calls and scalar contractions need to be understood.\\n75:There is a logical detail here: the empirical coefficients in (5)–(6) are random. We first analyze an auxiliary finite system, the oracle, using deterministic population residuals and contractions in their place. Those numbers will be constructed below from the initial population action; they are not assumptions about the empirical network. We then prove that its empirical contractions approach the supplied numbers, and compare it with the empirical-feedback Euler system.\\n84:Z_0^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]).\\n87:In the oracle, \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\) and \\\\(r_0=-1\\\\). Its first update leaves \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) unchanged and gives\\n94:P_{b^\\\\perp}=I-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n100:\\\\frac{(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}h_0^{(1)}\\n101:+\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(1)}.\\n115:\\\\frac{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}z_0^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n116:&+\\\\frac{[P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}]^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2^2}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n117:&+\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}h_2^{(1)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n121:The first response retains the old forward answer. The second supplies the remaining overlap required by the old transpose answer. The last term disturbs neither observation. Formula (5), with population coefficients for the oracle, then adds the learned rank-one correction to obtain its actual preactivation node.\\n129:\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{v_i},\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{\\\\widetilde v_i}\\\\right)^2\\n130:\\\\le\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\|v_i-\\\\widetilde v_i\\\\|_2^2.\\n137:\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n154:+P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}P_{V^\\\\perp}.\\n162:\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma,\\n174:\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi)\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n175:=\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}a\\\\|_2^2}{n}+\\\\varepsilon^2+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1).\\n182:\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{U}\\\\gamma\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\,\\\\middle|\\\\,\\\\text{history}\\\\right]\\n183:=\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{rank}(U)}n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n190:\\\\max_{\\\\text{constructed }v}\\\\frac{\\\\|v^{\\\\varepsilon}-v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n200:v\\\\sim\\\\widetilde v\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\\n201:\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n205:and do the same separately on the second layer. The limiting mean-square distance is \\\\(\\\\mathbb E[(V-\\\\widetilde V)^2]\\\\), so these classes are precisely the corresponding population random variables, identified when equal almost surely. Define\\n211:\\\\frac{\\\\|W_0^{(2)}v-W_0^{(2)}\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n212:\\\\le M^2\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n216:\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2].\\n221:\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n222:=\\\\frac{[(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u]^\\\\top v}{n}\\n226:\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,W_0^{(2)}V]=\\\\mathbb E[((W_0^{(2)})^*U)V].\\n236:f=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H^{(2)}],\\\\quad r=f-1,\\\\quad\\n239:\\\\dot W^{(2)}=-2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},\\\\qquad\\n245:(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)})V\\n246:=\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H^{(1)}V].\\n252:d=\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n253:+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n254:+\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n262:\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n263:&\\\\le (\\\\pi/2)\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n264:+M\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n265:\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n266:&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n267:+B\\\\operatorname{Lip}(\\\\phi')\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n268:\\\\|(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}-(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n269:&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n270:+M\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n275:\\\\|a\\\\otimes b-\\\\widetilde a\\\\otimes\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n276:\\\\le\\\\|a-\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b\\\\|_{L^2}\\n277:+\\\\|\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n281:Existence can be proved directly by iteration of the integral equations. On a sufficiently short time interval, the map \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\mapsto\\\\Theta(0)+\\\\int_0^t\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))\\\\,ds\\\\) preserves a closed set of continuous paths with the indicated bounds and contracts the supremum of (21). This set is complete: mean-square limits preserve a common pointwise bound on \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\). Taking the time interval shorter than the reciprocal of the Lipschitz constant proves existence and uniqueness.\\n292:K^{(1)}&=\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[(\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)})^2\\\\right],\\\\\\\\\\n293:K^{(2)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\delta^{(2)})^2],\\\\\\\\\\n294:K^{(3)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(2)})^2].\\n297:These formulas also follow by differentiating along square-integrable parameter directions: the gradients of (L) in (Z^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)}) are (2r\\\\delta^{(1)},2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},2rH^{(2)}). For matrix perturbations use the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product, under which the displayed rank-one action is the gradient. The update (W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}) lies in that space because it is the integral of rank-one actions of bounded Hilbert–Schmidt norm. Thus (20), transformed back to (Z^{(1)}), is gradient flow for this loss, with the same scaling as (1).\\n315:\\\\le \\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\Delta^2.\\n328:Up to the stop, (22) and (26) make \\\\(|r_s|\\\\le|r(0)|+C_T\\\\Delta\\\\); choose the supremum margin larger than \\\\(\\\\pi T(|r(0)|+1)\\\\). For small \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) no first exit is possible. Thus (26), also between grid times, holds for both population and finite-width Euler.\\n330:We can now define and compare the oracle precisely. Attach the word “oracle” as a subscript to its finite arrays; it denotes the auxiliary system, not another normalization. For fixed population Euler mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), all the numbers\\n332:r_k=\\\\mathbb E[W_k^{(3)}H_k^{(2)}]-1,\\\\quad\\n333:\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}],\\\\quad\\n334:\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}]\\n337:are deterministic. Start the oracle from the same (z_0^{(1)},W_0^{(2)}) as the finite network and from (W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},0}^{(3)}=0). In order at each step set\\n339:h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}=\\\\phi(F^{-1}(x_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)})),\\n342:z_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}\\n343:-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_s\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}],\\n347:h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n348:\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}=W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}),\\n352:x_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k+1}^{(1)}=x_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_k\\n353:\\\\bigg((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\n354:-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_sh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(1)}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}]\\\\bigg),\\\\\\\\\\n355:W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k+1}^{(3)}=W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(3)}-2\\\\Delta r_kh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}.\\n358:This is a fixed finite Gaussian calculation. Its output weights obey the deterministic coordinate bound (\\\\pi\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}|r_s|). Hence the product defining (\\\\delta^{(2)}) can be replaced, without changing any oracle value, by a globally Lipschitz map that clips its first argument above this bound. All other maps meet the finite Gaussian lemma. Its limiting variables are exactly the population Euler variables: this follows instruction by instruction from (5)–(6) at population level and the already constructed action (18)–(19).\\n362:W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\\\sum_{s<k}\\n363:r_s\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(2)}(h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n365:Its actual matrix action is not exactly the oracle preactivation (28). Their difference is\\n368:W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}-z_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\n369:=-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_s\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(2)}\\n370:\\\\left(\\\\frac{(h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(1)})^\\\\top h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}}n-\\n371:\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}]\\\\right).\\n374:The analogous backward discrepancy is the same sum with (h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(1)}) multiplying the empirical-minus-population (\\\\delta^{(2)}) contraction. These are exact identities.\\n376:Let (\\\\zeta_n) be the largest absolute error among the finitely many contractions in (30), their backward versions, the output averages, and the squared normalized norms of the oracle vectors. The finite Gaussian result proves (\\\\zeta_n\\\\to0) in probability. In particular the oracle vector norms remain bounded. Put\\n378:e_{n,k}=\\\\frac{\\\\|x_k^{(1)}-x_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n379:+\\\\|W_k^{(2)}-W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n380:+\\\\frac{\\\\|W_k^{(3)}-W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(3)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}.\\n382:Equations (22) and (30) bound every forward, backward, and residual difference by (C_{T,\\\\Delta}(e_{n,k}+\\\\zeta_n)). Subtracting the updates gives\\n387:Initially (e_{n,0}=\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})). Since (N=\\\\lceil T/\\\\Delta\\\\rceil) is fixed, summing this recurrence proves (\\\\max_{k\\\\le N}e_{n,k}\\\\to0). We have therefore proved the actual finite Euler width limit without feeding random scalar coefficients into the Gaussian lemma.\\n389:The same argument controls any fixed finite set of extra forward and backward measurements: expand each learned-matrix use by (5) or (6), use the population contraction to construct its oracle version, and add its empirical contraction errors to (\\\\zeta_n). There are only finitely many such errors. For continuity of these measurements, allow arbitrary finite compositions of bounded Lipschitz coordinate maps, (F^{-1}), linear combinations, and the current (W^{(2)},(W^{(2)})^*). Products use a clipped bounded factor. Induction on these operations, using (22), gives an error at most (C_{\\\\mathrm{measurement}}d_n) for two states on the same finite space, and (C_{\\\\mathrm{measurement}}d) on the population spaces.\\n393:C_T\\\\Delta+o_{\\\\mathbb P,n\\\\to\\\\infty;\\\\,\\\\Delta}(1)+C_T\\\\Delta.\\n396:For any requested accuracy, choose a small fixed (\\\\Delta), then take (n\\\\to\\\\infty). Uniform time continuity of each fixed measurement, supplied by (22) and the bounded derivatives, gives uniformity on ([0,T]). The summable weights extend this to the action-law distance. This statement also holds for any finitely many time points jointly, by appending all those measurements to the same finite oracle calculation before taking the limit.\\n398:The population evolution is autonomous on the state just constructed. To see this also at the level of its measured law, start the collection of measurements at a current state instead of at initialization, and take all their mean-square limits on each layer. These spaces are preserved by the current action and adjoint and by the coordinate operations. If two current measured laws agree, sending each measurement to its counterpart preserves every squared norm and inner product. It extends to a surjective linear isometry between the two generated spaces, commutes with the coordinate functions, intertwines the current action and adjoint, and sends (\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)}) to its counterpart. The integral iteration proving existence stays in these spaces. Uniqueness therefore identifies the future laws of the two states. Restarting at time (s) and evolving for time (t) gives the same law as evolving for (s+t); no extra historical information is required.\\n405:+\\\\frac{\\\\eta_n^3}{3}\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})^3\\\\odot b^3.\\n418:with probability tending to one. Stop first on a larger bounded set, as before. The output supremum is controlled separately by its sum of bounded activation updates and the residual bound transferred from the flow. This closes the stop. At intermediate times (33) with (0\\\\le\\\\eta\\\\le\\\\eta_n) controls the transformed coordinate of the linearly interpolated raw parameter, so (34) holds for the specified interpolation.\\n425:\\\\mathbb P\\\\left(\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i p_i(t)^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p_i(t)|>A}>\\\\varepsilon\\\\right)=0.\\n430:\\\\mathbb E[p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>A}]\\n431:\\\\le4\\\\mathbb E[(p-P)^2]+2\\\\mathbb E[P^2\\\\mathbf1_{|P|>A/2}].\\n440:\\\\dot Z^{(2)}=-2r\\\\left\\\\{\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n446:For GD, the first-layer interpolated slope is its gradient at the left endpoint. The derivative of (z^{(2)}=W^{(2)}\\\\phi(z^{(1)})) uses those constant parameter slopes and the current interpolated activation. To compare it with (36) at the endpoint, on (|p_i|\\\\le A) the change in (phi'(z_i^{(1)})) times its first-layer slope is bounded by (C_T\\\\eta_nA^2); on the complement both terms are bounded by (C_T|p_i|). The other product-rule errors are (O(\\\\eta_n)) in root-mean-square norm by bounded matrix slopes. Equation (35), then (n\\\\to\\\\infty) at fixed (A), then (A\\\\to\\\\infty), proves\\n448:\\\\int_0^T\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\partial_tz_n^{(\\\\ell),\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t)\\\\|_2^2}{n},dt\\n449:\\\\longrightarrow\\\\int_0^T\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\dot Z^{(\\\\ell)}(t))^2],dt,\\n460:After coordinate averaging, (37) bounds the empirical distance to this finite-grid representation by (O_{\\\\mathbb P}(\\\\sqrt a)). The population bound is identical; mean-square continuous velocities admit absolutely continuous sample paths by integrating a jointly measurable version and applying Fubini. At fixed partition, joint grid-law convergence follows from (32) and (34), including second moments. Polygonal interpolation is a continuous linear map of the finite grid vector. Let (n\\\\to\\\\infty) at fixed partition, then (a\\\\to0) in (38). This proves, separately for the two layers,\\n462:\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{z_{n,i}^{(\\\\ell),\\\\mathrm{GD}}(\\\\cdot)}\\n470:m=\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]>0,\\\\quad\\n471:U=H_0^{(2)}\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(2)}),\\\\quad \\\\nu=\\\\mathbb E[U^2]>0,\\\\quad\\n476:P_0=\\\\frac{\\\\mathbb E[Z_0^{(2)}U]}mH_0^{(1)}+\\\\sqrt\\\\nu\\\\,\\\\Gamma^{(1)},\\n481:d=\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(1)})P_0)^2]>0,\\\\qquad\\n486:\\\\mathbb E[US_0]=m\\\\nu+d>0,\\n497:W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}&=2t^2U\\\\otimes H_0^{(1)}+o_{\\\\rm op}(t^2),\\\\\\\\\\n503:Let (k_3=\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(2)})^2]>0). Equations (24), (41), and (42) yield\\n512:The two squared displacements have leading terms (4dt^4) and (4\\\\mathbb E[S_0^2]t^4); their squared speeds have leading terms (16dt^2) and (16\\\\mathbb E[S_0^2]t^2). Thus both hidden layers move and every kernel block has a positive integral on sufficiently short nonzero intervals. Also\\n519:\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{Cov}(Z^{(\\\\ell)},\\\\phi(Z^{(\\\\ell)}))^2}{\\\\operatorname{Var}(Z^{(\\\\ell)})}.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-0687a964-4d03-49bd-aa10-6a27d79d09a9\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '255,330p' /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each variable's \\\\(L^2\\\\) norm by its Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\); call this distance \\\\(d_n\\\\). The operator norm is the largest amplification of root-mean-square size.\\n\\nBoth \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\circ F^{-1}\\\\) are 1-Lipschitz. The functions \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\) are bounded and Lipschitz. If both operator norms are at most \\\\(M\\\\) and both output weights are pointwise bounded by \\\\(B\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le (\\\\pi/2)\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+M\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+B\\\\operatorname{Lip}(\\\\phi')\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}-(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+M\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\nThe output difference obeys Cauchy–Schwarz with \\\\(|H^{(2)}|\\\\le\\\\pi/2\\\\), and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|a\\\\otimes b-\\\\widetilde a\\\\otimes\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n\\\\le\\\\|a-\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nHence the vector field is Lipschitz in (21) on these bounded sets, with a constant independent of width.\\n\\nExistence can be proved directly by iteration of the integral equations. On a sufficiently short time interval, the map \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\mapsto\\\\Theta(0)+\\\\int_0^t\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))\\\\,ds\\\\) preserves a closed set of continuous paths with the indicated bounds and contracts the supremum of (21). This set is complete: mean-square limits preserve a common pointwise bound on \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\). Taking the time interval shorter than the reciprocal of the Lipschitz constant proves existence and uniqueness.\\n\\nTo continue the solution, differentiation of the output, using (19), gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f=-2rK,\\\\quad \\\\dot L=-4r^2K,\\\\qquad\\nK=K^{(1)}+K^{(2)}+K^{(3)},\\n\\\\tag{23}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nK^{(1)}&=\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[(\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)})^2\\\\right],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(2)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\delta^{(2)})^2],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(3)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(2)})^2].\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\nThese formulas also follow by differentiating along square-integrable parameter directions: the gradients of (L) in (Z^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)}) are (2r\\\\delta^{(1)},2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},2rH^{(2)}). For matrix perturbations use the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product, under which the displayed rank-one action is the gradient. The update (W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}) lies in that space because it is the integral of rank-one actions of bounded Hilbert–Schmidt norm. Thus (20), transformed back to (Z^{(1)}), is gradient flow for this loss, with the same scaling as (1).\\n\\nThe chain rules used here hold along these mean-square differentiable paths: bounded scalar derivatives give convergence of difference quotients by domination, first for bounded directions and then by mean-square approximation. We are not asserting that every coordinatewise nonlinear map is differentiable in operator norm on all of (L^2).\\n\\nSince \\\\(K\\\\ge0\\\\), \\\\(|r(t)|\\\\le|r(0)|\\\\). Therefore, on \\\\([0,T]\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\\\pi|r(0)|T,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\le\\\\pi|r(0)|\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|\\\\dot X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\le2|r(0)|\\\\|W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{25}\\n\\\\]\\nThese bounds prevent finite-time blow-up. At finite width, (11), the initial law of large numbers for (F(z_0^{(1)})), and (\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\to0) in probability put the same bounds on an event of probability tending to one.\\n\\nLet the vector-field bound and Lipschitz constant on a slightly larger bounded set be \\\\(M_T,L_T\\\\). One exact flow step of length \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) differs from its Euler step by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\\\int_t^{t+\\\\Delta}[\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))-\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(t))],ds\\\\right\\\\|\\n\\\\le \\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\Delta^2.\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently the accumulated error satisfies\\n\\\\[\\ne_{k+1}\\\\le(1+L_T\\\\Delta)e_k+C_T\\\\Delta^2,\\n\\\\qquad \\\\max_{k\\\\Delta\\\\le T}e_k\\\\le C_T'\\\\Delta.\\n\\\\tag{26}\\n\\\\]\\nTo justify the bounded set for Euler, stop at a first exit. The bound proves that the (X^{(1)}) and operator components remain inside their margins. Although (21) does not control the output supremum, its update gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W_k^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\n\\\\pi\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}|r_s|.\\n\\\\]\\nUp to the stop, (22) and (26) make \\\\(|r_s|\\\\le|r(0)|+C_T\\\\Delta\\\\); choose the supremum margin larger than \\\\(\\\\pi T(|r(0)|+1)\\\\). For small \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) no first exit is possible. Thus (26), also between grid times, holds for both population and finite-width Euler.\\n\\nWe can now define and compare the oracle precisely. Attach the word “oracle” as a subscript to its finite arrays; it denotes the auxiliary system, not another normalization. For fixed population Euler mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), all the numbers\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b32f1170c87d289b737aeb684ebd9\",\"summary\":[\"**Fixing proof formatting**\",\"**Waiting for review feedback**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b32fd8d4887d29091b96f15a4600a\",\"summary\":[\"**Fixing proof formatting**\",\"**Waiting for review feedback**\",\"**Repairing the Lipschitz library**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b330c526487d2b5cb3c11cde3b257\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking Gaussian norm derivation**\",\"**Adding input-extension proof**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b331942bc87d288c945c898e5c701\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking Gaussian norm derivation**\",\"**Adding input-extension proof**\",\"**Adding matrix norm definition**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b332912cc87d289d9c9b4b58dc56e\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking the proof draft**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3333a02887d288cb2fbf0525af85\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking the proof draft**\",\"**Preparing revised proof draft**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b333e989487d284948aeae5dc62db\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking the proof draft**\",\"**Preparing revised proof draft**\",\"**Revising the proof notation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3343ac6087d2b37760eb4748b5b9\",\"text\":\"The first reviews have checked the conditioning formulas and the perturbation argument without finding a gap so far. They have identified an overbroad phrase: clipping one factor does not make an arbitrary product Lipschitz. I’m restricting the statement to the bounded products the network uses and retaining the tail argument for unbounded backward fields.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-46aee120-3920-4102-928d-5b499d6e7cdd\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# Two hidden layers: a complete proof of the joint width and learning-rate limit\\n\\nWe consider one input, equal to (1), and target (1). At width (n), the first preactivation (z^{(1)}=W^{(1)}) and output weight (W^{(3)}) are vectors in (mathbb R^n), and (W^{(2)}) is an (n)-by-(n) matrix. Throughout, (W^{(3)}) means the rescaled output weight. Put\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z^{(1)}),\\\\quad z^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_n=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad r_n=f_n-1,\\\\qquad L_n=r_n^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll initial entries are independent, with\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,i}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad W_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nDefine the derivatives without the loss residual by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_n/\\\\partial z^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\). The exact GD being analyzed is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{k+1}^{(1)}&=z_k^{(1)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}\\\\delta_k^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}}n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}(h_k^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}h_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nWe use the clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), interpolate these three parameters linearly, and recompute the other network quantities from them. The proof applies whenever \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\), in particular to \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\).\\n\\nWe will construct one deterministic population evolution, prove that every fixed finite collection of its forward and backward measurements is the limit of the corresponding finite-network measurements, and prove uniform convergence of predictions, losses, and all three kernel blocks on every finite time interval. We also prove convergence of the two hidden trajectory laws and their integrated squared speeds. At small positive times both hidden layers move, the activation remains non-affine on their distributions, and the kernel changes.\\n\\nCapital letters \\\\(Z^{(\\\\ell)},H^{(\\\\ell)},X^{(1)}\\\\) denote individual population coordinates. We keep \\\\(W^{(2)},W^{(3)},\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\) for their population counterparts, explicitly identifying when we have moved to population quantities. An expectation always concerns coordinates belonging to the same layer. For a scalar population variable \\\\(V\\\\), write \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\); finite vectors always use \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\), with the width normalization visible.\\n\\nFirst consider the continuous finite-width flow associated with (1). Set\\n\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+s^3/3,\\\\qquad x^{(1)}=F(z^{(1)}).\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(F'(s)\\\\phi'(s)=1\\\\), this flow becomes\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot x^{(1)}&=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-\\\\frac{2r_n}{n}\\\\delta^{(2)}(h^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2r_nh^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nThe change of coordinates is exact for the flow. Euler with proof mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) means replacing each derivative in (2) by its forward difference. In particular,\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}^{(1)}=x_k^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is Euler for the transformed flow; transforming the GD iterates in (1) produces an additional defect, estimated below.\\n\\nIterating the Euler matrix update gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently, for any appropriate vectors \\\\(v,u\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}v=W_0^{(2)}v-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top v}{n},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top u=(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}h_s^{(1)}\\n\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_s^{(2)})^\\\\top u}{n}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus training adds a finite sum of rank-one matrices. At fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\), only finitely many initial-matrix calls and scalar contractions need to be understood.\\n\\nThere is a logical detail here: the empirical coefficients in (5)–(6) are random. We first analyze an auxiliary finite system, the oracle, using deterministic population residuals and contractions in their place. Those numbers will be constructed below from the initial population action; they are not assumptions about the empirical network. We then prove that its empirical contractions approach the supplied numbers, and compare it with the empirical-feedback Euler system.\\n\\nThe matrix calculation needed for this construction is elementary Gaussian conditioning. For example, the first forward call is\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditional on \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), its coordinates are independent centered Gaussians of variance \\\\(\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\). Hence the initial population coordinates satisfy\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad H_0^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_0^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]).\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\nIn the oracle, \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\) and \\\\(r_0=-1\\\\). Its first update leaves \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) unchanged and gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(3)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)})\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_0^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nThe next transpose call depends on the matrix already used in the forward pass. Define, for nonzero \\\\(b\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nP_{b^\\\\perp}=I-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditioning each Gaussian row on its observed dot product with \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), and then summing the rows with coefficients \\\\(\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)}\\\\), gives\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d=\\n\\\\frac{(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}h_0^{(1)}\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nHere equality is conditional in distribution, and \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the preceding history. The first term supplies precisely the overlap required by\\n\\\\[\\n(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)};\\n\\\\]\\nthe second is perpendicular to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\).\\n\\nAfter the first layer moves, the next initial-matrix call is \\\\(W_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\). Condition on both previous observations. Multiplying the resulting conditional matrix by \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}z_0^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{[P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}]^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2^2}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}h_2^{(1)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nThe first response retains the old forward answer. The second supplies the remaining overlap required by the old transpose answer. The last term disturbs neither observation. Formula (5), with population coefficients for the oracle, then adds the learned rank-one correction to obtain its actual preactivation node.\\n\\nWe now prove the finite Gaussian fact needed to repeat this argument, including the case of dependent query directions. The proof below requires only empirical convergence against continuous measurements with at most quadratic growth. It does not claim convergence against arbitrary discontinuous functions.\\n\\nConsider a fixed finite calculation starting from iid coordinate tuples with finite second moments, independent of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\). Its instructions are fixed globally Lipschitz coordinate functions, linear combinations with deterministic coefficients, and calls to \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) or its transpose. Root tuples may include both \\\\(z_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(F(z_0^{(1)})\\\\). Then all same-layer joint empirical laws have deterministic limits, including their second moments.\\n\\nFor precision, the distance between two laws used here is the smallest root-mean-square distance between random vectors having those laws, usually denoted \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\). For empirical laws, matching coordinates gives the bound\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{v_i},\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{\\\\widetilde v_i}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\le\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\|v_i-\\\\widetilde v_i\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\nConvergence in this distance means weak convergence together with convergence of second moments. Thus it includes all dot products of a fixed joint tuple.\\n\\nFirst, the Gaussian matrix has a uniform operator bound with exponentially high probability. A \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net of the Euclidean unit sphere has at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) elements. Using two such nets,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le 2\\\\,9^{2n}\\\\exp(-nM^2/8).\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed the operator norm is at most twice the largest bilinear form on these nets, and each bilinear form is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). Choose a fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\).\\n\\nTo avoid inverting almost-dependent query directions, temporarily add \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) to the input of each matrix call, where each \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\) is a new independent standard Gaussian vector. Fix \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\). This is only a device for the proof; we will remove it.\\n\\nSuppose previous calls have revealed\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}V=Y,\\\\qquad (W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R.\\n\\\\]\\nThe columns of \\\\(V\\\\) are old first-layer input vectors, the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) are old second-layer input vectors, and \\\\(Y,R\\\\) contain the respective answers. Their column counts are fixed. All coordinates derived from these answers give no further information once the answers and roots are fixed. Gaussian conditioning therefore leaves\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}\\\\overset d=\\nY(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\n+U(U^\\\\top U)^{-1}R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}\\n+P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}P_{V^\\\\perp}.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(P_{V^\\\\perp}=I-V(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\\\), and similarly for \\\\(U\\\\); terms for an empty column list are zero. The fresh matrix has independent \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\) entries. The first two terms obey both recorded equations; the remaining Gaussian matrix lies in exactly the directions that leave those equations unchanged. Orthogonal components of a centered isotropic Gaussian are independent, which proves (12), including for adaptive queries: each input is chosen from the earlier answers before its new answer is observed.\\n\\nFor a new right-side input \\\\(v\\\\), multiplication gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}v\\\\overset d=Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma,\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_n=(V^\\\\top V/n)^{-1}(V^\\\\top v/n),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\beta_n=(U^\\\\top U/n)^{-1}(R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}v/n).\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose case exchanges the two layer populations.\\n\\nInductively, all the normalized products in these expressions converge. The perturbation guarantees invertibility of their limiting input Gram matrices: when a new column \\\\(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) is added to any old input list, conditional Gaussian second-moment calculations give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi)\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}a\\\\|_2^2}{n}+\\\\varepsilon^2+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe cross term has conditional variance at most \\\\(4\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\|a\\\\|_2^2/n^2\\\\), and only a fixed number of Gaussian directions are removed. Its limiting squared distance is at least \\\\(\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\). Successive Schur complements therefore stay positive; all the finite Gram inverses in (13) converge.\\n\\nConsequently the response coefficients and Gaussian variance converge. The projection removes finitely many directions, and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{U}\\\\gamma\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\,\\\\middle|\\\\,\\\\text{history}\\\\right]\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{rank}(U)}n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\nAfter removing this negligible projection and replacing coefficients by their limits, the new coordinates are an old coordinate function plus independent scalar Gaussians. For a bounded Lipschitz measurement, conditional empirical variance is at most a constant divided by \\\\(n\\\\). Its conditional mean is the old empirical average of the measurement averaged over one scalar Gaussian, which converges by the previous induction step. The conditional variance of the new squared norm is also \\\\(O((1+\\\\|Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n\\\\|_2^2/n)/n)\\\\). Thus second moments converge as well. This proves the enlarged joint \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence. Lipschitz coordinate operations preserve it, closing the induction at fixed \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\).\\n\\nFinally couple the perturbed and unperturbed calculations using the same roots and matrix. On the event in (11) and the event that the finitely many \\\\(\\\\|\\\\xi\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\) are bounded, induction through the finite instructions gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{\\\\text{constructed }v}\\\\frac{\\\\|v^{\\\\varepsilon}-v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n\\\\le C\\\\varepsilon,\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(C\\\\) depends on the fixed calculation but not on \\\\(n\\\\) or \\\\(0<\\\\varepsilon\\\\le1\\\\). Each coordinate map multiplies a difference by its fixed Lipschitz constant, and each matrix call by at most \\\\(M\\\\), plus the new perturbation. Both events have probability tending to one. By (10), the deterministic limiting laws for \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\) form a Cauchy family in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\); its limit is also the limit of the unperturbed empirical laws, first taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\to0\\\\). This proves the finite Gaussian fact without assuming stability of a singular unperturbed Gram matrix.\\n\\nNow we turn these compatible finite calculations into one population matrix action. Take a countable collection of initial calculations, closed under rational linear combinations, initial-matrix calls in both directions, the Lipschitz maps used above, and a countable family of bounded Lipschitz functions of finite coordinate lists that is dense among continuous functions on compact sets. Include constants, clipping at every integer level, and products with a clipped factor. Finite joint limiting laws are consistent because every union of finitely many calculations is another such calculation. They therefore define two countable random coordinate collections, one per layer. Let their probability spaces contain exactly the information generated by these coordinates.\\n\\nIdentify two first-layer probes whenever\\n\\\\[\\nv\\\\sim\\\\widetilde v\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability},\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\nand do the same separately on the second layer. The limiting mean-square distance is \\\\(\\\\mathbb E[(V-\\\\widetilde V)^2]\\\\), so these classes are precisely the corresponding population random variables, identified when equal almost surely. Define\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}[v]=[W_0^{(2)}v].\\n\\\\]\\nThe definition is independent of representatives because (11) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|W_0^{(2)}v-W_0^{(2)}\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\le M^2\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\]\\non an event of probability tending to one. Linearity passes from the finite matrix, as does\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2].\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\nTaking all mean-square limits extends this action uniquely to every square-integrable variable on the first-layer probability space. The density needed here follows from the included bounded coordinate functions: finite-coordinate measurable functions approximate any variable on the generated space, and bounded continuous functions approximate them in mean square. The transpose action extends the same way in the reverse direction. Passing the exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{[(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u]^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\nto population expectations shows that this reverse action is precisely the adjoint:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,W_0^{(2)}V]=\\\\mathbb E[((W_0^{(2)})^*U)V].\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\nArbitrary fixed real coefficients are obtained by rational approximation; the same stability estimates identify any finite computation using them with this population construction.\\n\\nOn these fixed spaces the population state is \\\\((X^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\\\). Its derived variables and dynamics are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{gathered}\\nZ^{(1)}=F^{-1}(X^{(1)}),\\\\quad H^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H^{(1)},\\\\quad H^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(2)}),\\\\\\\\\\nf=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H^{(2)}],\\\\quad r=f-1,\\\\quad\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)}),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot X^{(1)}=-2r(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}=-2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}=-2rH^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{gathered} \\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\nThe rank-one action means\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)})V\\n=\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H^{(1)}V].\\n\\\\]\\nInitially \\\\(X_0^{(1)}=F(Z_0^{(1)})\\\\), the matrix action is (18), and \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\). Only three evolving objects are used; the population coordinate spaces themselves are infinite dimensional.\\n\\nWe next prove that (20) has a unique solution at every finite time and that Euler approximates it uniformly. The same estimates will apply at finite width. Measure two population states by\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each variable's \\\\(L^2\\\\) norm by its Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\); call this distance \\\\(d_n\\\\). The operator norm is the largest amplification of root-mean-square size.\\n\\nBoth \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\circ F^{-1}\\\\) are 1-Lipschitz. The functions \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\) are bounded and Lipschitz. If both operator norms are at most \\\\(M\\\\) and both output weights are pointwise bounded by \\\\(B\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le (\\\\pi/2)\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+M\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+B\\\\operatorname{Lip}(\\\\phi')\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}-(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+M\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\nThe output difference obeys Cauchy–Schwarz with \\\\(|H^{(2)}|\\\\le\\\\pi/2\\\\), and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|a\\\\otimes b-\\\\widetilde a\\\\otimes\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n\\\\le\\\\|a-\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nHence the vector field is Lipschitz in (21) on these bounded sets, with a constant independent of width.\\n\\nExistence can be proved directly by iteration of the integral equations. On a sufficiently short time interval, the map \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\mapsto\\\\Theta(0)+\\\\int_0^t\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))\\\\,ds\\\\) preserves a closed set of continuous paths with the indicated bounds and contracts the supremum of (21). This set is complete: mean-square limits preserve a common pointwise bound on \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\). Taking the time interval shorter than the reciprocal of the Lipschitz constant proves existence and uniqueness.\\n\\nTo continue the solution, differentiation of the output, using (19), gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f=-2rK,\\\\quad \\\\dot L=-4r^2K,\\\\qquad\\nK=K^{(1)}+K^{(2)}+K^{(3)},\\n\\\\tag{23}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nK^{(1)}&=\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[(\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)})^2\\\\right],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(2)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\delta^{(2)})^2],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(3)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(2)})^2].\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\nThese formulas also follow by differentiating along square-integrable parameter directions: the gradients of (L) in (Z^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)}) are (2r\\\\delta^{(1)},2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},2rH^{(2)}). For matrix perturbations use the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product, under which the displayed rank-one action is the gradient. The update (W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}) lies in that space because it is the integral of rank-one actions of bounded Hilbert–Schmidt norm. Thus (20), transformed back to (Z^{(1)}), is gradient flow for this loss, with the same scaling as (1).\\n\\nThe chain rules used here hold along these mean-square differentiable paths: bounded scalar derivatives give convergence of difference quotients by domination, first for bounded directions and then by mean-square approximation. We are not asserting that every coordinatewise nonlinear map is differentiable in operator norm on all of (L^2).\\n\\nSince \\\\(K\\\\ge0\\\\), \\\\(|r(t)|\\\\le|r(0)|\\\\). Therefore, on \\\\([0,T]\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\\\pi|r(0)|T,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\le\\\\pi|r(0)|\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|\\\\dot X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\le2|r(0)|\\\\|W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{25}\\n\\\\]\\nThese bounds prevent finite-time blow-up. At finite width, (11), the initial law of large numbers for (F(z_0^{(1)})), and (\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\to0) in probability put the same bounds on an event of probability tending to one.\\n\\nLet the vector-field bound and Lipschitz constant on a slightly larger bounded set be \\\\(M_T,L_T\\\\). One exact flow step of length \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) differs from its Euler step by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\\\int_t^{t+\\\\Delta}[\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))-\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(t))],ds\\\\right\\\\|\\n\\\\le \\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\Delta^2.\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently the acc\",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":35186}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3348f15487d2889ad6e5a8231f98\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"collabAgentToolCall\",\"id\":\"call_khD8MPC0kzZnsblcJjAQZwz5\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"senderThreadId\":\"01a04927-31c8-79d1-bd23-8196b13a1d9b\",\"receiverThreadIds\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"model\":null,\"reasoningEffort\":null},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b33558b9087d285561221ccfe69f9\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing reproducibility audit logs**\",\"**Checking finite-Gaussian lemma**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b336600a087d2bdddd894668276f0\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing r2 replacements**\",\"**Clarifying metric notation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a06e3e-5d06-7113-88be-f7c5f8c96d86\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e3e-5cc9-7593-a15c-70531ca3453d\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r1a\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a06e3e-8d88-7023-bef7-40322225d74c\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e3e-8d48-7801-b1be-ebac2f140a50\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r1c\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3370204087d2bdec4888842f3fa8\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing r2 replacements**\",\"**Clarifying metric notation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a06e3e-7378-7d02-90f3-a8b2d0ac017c\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e3e-7338-7463-9de3-80c2c2096986\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r1b\"},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-518839f9-25f3-4fed-97c3-9b7e65ebc18e\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof-r2.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Two hidden layers: a complete proof of the joint width and learning-rate limit\\n\\nWe consider one input, equal to \\\\(1\\\\), and target \\\\(1\\\\). At width \\\\(n\\\\), the first preactivation \\\\(z^{(1)}=W^{(1)}\\\\) and output weight \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) are vectors in \\\\(\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\). Throughout, \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) means the rescaled output weight. Put\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z^{(1)}),\\\\quad z^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_n=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad r_n=f_n-1,\\\\qquad L_n=r_n^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll initial entries are independent, with\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,i}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad W_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nDefine the derivatives without the loss residual by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_n/\\\\partial z^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\). The exact GD being analyzed is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{k+1}^{(1)}&=z_k^{(1)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}\\\\delta_k^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}}n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}(h_k^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}h_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nWe use the clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), interpolate these three parameters linearly, and recompute the other network quantities from them. The proof applies whenever \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\), in particular to \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\).\\n\\nWe will construct one deterministic population evolution, prove that every fixed finite collection of its forward and backward measurements is the limit of the corresponding finite-network measurements, and prove uniform convergence of predictions, losses, and all three kernel blocks on every finite time interval. We also prove convergence of the two hidden trajectory laws and their integrated squared speeds. At small positive times both hidden layers move, the activation remains non-affine on their distributions, and the kernel changes.\\n\\nCapital letters \\\\(Z^{(\\\\ell)},H^{(\\\\ell)},X^{(1)}\\\\) denote individual population coordinates. We keep \\\\(W^{(2)},W^{(3)},\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\) for their population counterparts, explicitly identifying when we have moved to population quantities. An expectation always concerns coordinates belonging to the same layer. For a scalar population variable \\\\(V\\\\), write \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\); finite vectors always use \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\), with the width normalization visible.\\n\\nFirst consider the continuous finite-width flow associated with (1). Set\\n\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+s^3/3,\\\\qquad x^{(1)}=F(z^{(1)}).\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(F'(s)\\\\phi'(s)=1\\\\), this flow becomes\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot x^{(1)}&=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-\\\\frac{2r_n}{n}\\\\delta^{(2)}(h^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2r_nh^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nThe change of coordinates is exact for the flow. Euler with proof mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) means replacing each derivative in (2) by its forward difference. In particular,\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}^{(1)}=x_k^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is Euler for the transformed flow; transforming the GD iterates in (1) produces an additional defect, estimated below.\\n\\nIterating the Euler matrix update gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently, for any appropriate vectors \\\\(v,u\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}v=W_0^{(2)}v-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top v}{n},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top u=(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}h_s^{(1)}\\n\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_s^{(2)})^\\\\top u}{n}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus training adds a finite sum of rank-one matrices. At fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\), only finitely many initial-matrix calls and scalar contractions need to be understood.\\n\\nThere is a logical detail here: the empirical coefficients in (5)–(6) are random. We first analyze an auxiliary finite system, the oracle, using deterministic population residuals and contractions in their place. Those numbers will be constructed below from the initial population action; they are not assumptions about the empirical network. We then prove that its empirical contractions approach the supplied numbers, and compare it with the empirical-feedback Euler system.\\n\\nThe matrix calculation needed for this construction is elementary Gaussian conditioning. For example, the first forward call is\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditional on \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), its coordinates are independent centered Gaussians of variance \\\\(\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\). Hence the initial population coordinates satisfy\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad H_0^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_0^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]).\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\nIn the oracle, \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\) and \\\\(r_0=-1\\\\). Until the oracle comparison below, the vectors in these first-call examples are oracle vectors. Its first update leaves \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) unchanged and gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(3)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)})\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_0^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nThe next transpose call depends on the matrix already used in the forward pass. Define, for nonzero \\\\(b\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nP_{b^\\\\perp}=I-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditioning each Gaussian row on its observed dot product with \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), and then summing the rows with coefficients \\\\(\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)}\\\\), gives\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d=\\n\\\\frac{(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}h_0^{(1)}\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nHere equality is conditional in distribution, and \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the preceding history. The first term supplies precisely the overlap required by\\n\\\\[\\n(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)};\\n\\\\]\\nthe second is perpendicular to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\).\\n\\nThe next update is\\n\\\\[\\nx_2^{(1)}=x_0^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_1(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\nh_2^{(1)}=\\\\phi(F^{-1}(x_2^{(1)})),\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(r_1=2\\\\Delta\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi(Z_0^{(2)})^2]-1\\\\).\\nThus the next initial-matrix call is \\\\(W_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\). Condition on both previous observations. Multiplying the resulting conditional matrix by \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}z_0^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{[P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}]^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2^2}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}h_2^{(1)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the previous history. The denominators in (8)–(9) are positive almost surely for \\\\(\\\\Delta>0\\\\), because the initial Gaussians are nondegenerate and \\\\(\\\\phi'(s)>0\\\\). The first response retains the old forward answer. The second supplies the remaining overlap required by the old transpose answer. The last term disturbs neither observation. Formula (5), with population coefficients for the oracle, then adds the learned rank-one correction to obtain its actual preactivation node.\\n\\nWe now prove the finite Gaussian fact needed to repeat this argument, including the case of dependent query directions. The proof below requires only empirical convergence against continuous measurements with at most quadratic growth. It does not claim convergence against arbitrary discontinuous functions.\\n\\nConsider a fixed finite calculation starting from iid coordinate tuples with finite second moments, independent of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\). Its instructions are fixed globally Lipschitz coordinate functions, linear combinations with deterministic coefficients, and calls to \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) or its transpose. Root tuples may include both \\\\(z_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(F(z_0^{(1)})\\\\). Then all same-layer joint empirical laws have deterministic limits, including their second moments.\\n\\nFor precision, the distance between two laws used here is the smallest root-mean-square distance between random vectors having those laws, usually denoted \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\). For empirical laws, matching coordinates gives the bound\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{v_i},\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{\\\\widetilde v_i}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\le\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\|v_i-\\\\widetilde v_i\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\nConvergence in this distance means weak convergence together with convergence of second moments. Thus it includes all dot products of a fixed joint tuple.\\n\\nFirst, the Gaussian matrix has a uniform operator bound with exponentially high probability. A \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net of the Euclidean unit sphere has at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) elements. Using two such nets,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le 2\\\\,9^{2n}\\\\exp(-nM^2/8).\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed the operator norm is at most twice the largest bilinear form on these nets, and each bilinear form is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). Choose a fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\).\\n\\nTo avoid inverting almost-dependent query directions, temporarily add \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) to the input of each matrix call, where each \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\) is a new independent standard Gaussian vector. Fix \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\). This is only a device for the proof; we will remove it.\\n\\nSuppose previous calls have revealed\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}V=Y,\\\\qquad (W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R.\\n\\\\]\\nThe columns of \\\\(V\\\\) are old first-layer input vectors, the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) are old second-layer input vectors, and \\\\(Y,R\\\\) contain the respective answers. Their column counts are fixed. All coordinates derived from these answers give no further information once the answers and roots are fixed. Gaussian conditioning therefore leaves\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}\\\\overset d=\\nY(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\n+U(U^\\\\top U)^{-1}R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}\\n+P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}P_{V^\\\\perp}.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(P_{V^\\\\perp}=I-V(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\\\), and similarly for \\\\(U\\\\); terms for an empty column list are zero. The fresh matrix has independent \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\) entries. The first two terms obey both recorded equations; the remaining Gaussian matrix lies in exactly the directions that leave those equations unchanged. Orthogonal components of a centered isotropic Gaussian are independent, which proves (12), including for adaptive queries: each input is chosen from the earlier answers before its new answer is observed.\\n\\nFor a new right-side input \\\\(v\\\\), multiplication gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}v\\\\overset d=Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma,\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_n=(V^\\\\top V/n)^{-1}(V^\\\\top v/n),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\beta_n=(U^\\\\top U/n)^{-1}(R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}v/n).\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose case exchanges the two layer populations.\\n\\nThe induction statement is joint \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence of every finite tuple of previously constructed vectors on each layer. Equivalently, the average of any continuous measurement \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\) with\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\psi(s)|\\\\le C(1+\\\\|s\\\\|_2^2)\\n\\\\]\\nconverges to its population expectation. The choices \\\\(\\\\psi(s)=s^2\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\psi(s,t)=st\\\\) give the required squared norms and contractions. Revealing each fresh input noise first extends the old tuple by independent Gaussian coordinates; conditional averaging proves this extension exactly as for the fresh output noise below. Thus all the normalized products in (13) converge. The perturbation guarantees invertibility of their limiting input Gram matrices: when a new column \\\\(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) is added to any old input list, conditional Gaussian second-moment calculations give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi)\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}a\\\\|_2^2}{n}+\\\\varepsilon^2+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe cross term has conditional variance at most \\\\(4\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\|a\\\\|_2^2/n^2\\\\), and only a fixed number of Gaussian directions are removed. Its limiting squared distance is at least \\\\(\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\). Successive Schur complements therefore stay positive; all the finite Gram inverses in (13) converge.\\n\\nConsequently the response coefficients and Gaussian variance converge. Write \\\\(P_U=I-P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\) for projection onto the column span of \\\\(U\\\\). This projection removes finitely many directions, and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{U}\\\\gamma\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\,\\\\middle|\\\\,\\\\text{history}\\\\right]\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{rank}(U)}n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\nAfter removing this negligible projection and replacing coefficients by their limits, the new coordinates are an old coordinate function plus independent scalar Gaussians. For a bounded Lipschitz measurement, conditional empirical variance is at most a constant divided by \\\\(n\\\\). Its conditional mean is the old empirical average of the measurement averaged over one scalar Gaussian, which converges by the previous induction step. The conditional variance of the new squared norm is also \\\\(O((1+\\\\|Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n\\\\|_2^2/n)/n)\\\\). Thus second moments converge as well. This proves the enlarged joint \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence. Lipschitz coordinate operations preserve it, closing the induction at fixed \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\).\\n\\nFinally couple the perturbed and unperturbed calculations using the same roots and matrix. On the event in (11) and the event that the finitely many \\\\(\\\\|\\\\xi\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\) are bounded, induction through the finite instructions gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{\\\\text{constructed }v}\\\\frac{\\\\|v^{\\\\varepsilon}-v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n\\\\le C\\\\varepsilon,\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(C\\\\) depends on the fixed calculation but not on \\\\(n\\\\) or \\\\(0<\\\\varepsilon\\\\le1\\\\). Each coordinate map multiplies a difference by its fixed Lipschitz constant, and each matrix call by at most \\\\(M\\\\), plus the new perturbation. Both events have probability tending to one. By (10), the deterministic limiting laws for \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\) form a Cauchy family in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\); its limit is also the limit of the unperturbed empirical laws, first taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\to0\\\\). This proves the finite Gaussian fact without assuming stability of a singular unperturbed Gram matrix.\\n\\nNow we turn these compatible finite calculations into one population matrix action. Take a countable collection of initial calculations, closed under rational linear combinations, initial-matrix calls in both directions, the Lipschitz maps used above, and a countable family of bounded Lipschitz functions of finite coordinate lists that is dense among continuous functions on compact sets. Include constants, clipping at every integer level, and products of bounded globally Lipschitz factors. In every such product, clip each factor that is not already bounded; all coordinate instructions in this collection must be globally Lipschitz. Finite joint limiting laws are consistent because every union of finitely many calculations is another such calculation. They therefore define two countable random coordinate collections, one per layer. Let their probability spaces contain exactly the information generated by these coordinates.\\n\\nIdentify two first-layer probes whenever\\n\\\\[\\nv\\\\sim\\\\widetilde v\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability},\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\nand do the same separately on the second layer. The limiting mean-square distance is \\\\(\\\\mathbb E[(V-\\\\widetilde V)^2]\\\\), so these classes are precisely the corresponding population random variables, identified when equal almost surely. Define\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}[v]=[W_0^{(2)}v].\\n\\\\]\\nThe definition is independent of representatives because (11) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|W_0^{(2)}v-W_0^{(2)}\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\le M^2\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\]\\non an event of probability tending to one. Linearity passes from the finite matrix, as does\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2].\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\nTaking all mean-square limits extends this action uniquely to every square-integrable variable on the first-layer probability space. The density needed here follows from the included bounded coordinate functions: finite-coordinate measurable functions approximate any variable on the generated space, and bounded continuous functions approximate them in mean square. The transpose action extends the same way in the reverse direction. Passing the exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{[(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u]^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\nto population expectations shows that this reverse action is precisely the adjoint:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,W_0^{(2)}V]=\\\\mathbb E[((W_0^{(2)})^*U)V].\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\nArbitrary fixed real coefficients are obtained by rational approximation; the same stability estimates identify any finite computation using them with this population construction.\\n\\nOn these fixed spaces the population state is \\\\((X^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\\\). Its derived variables and dynamics are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{gathered}\\nZ^{(1)}=F^{-1}(X^{(1)}),\\\\quad H^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H^{(1)},\\\\quad H^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(2)}),\\\\\\\\\\nf=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H^{(2)}],\\\\quad r=f-1,\\\\quad\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)}),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot X^{(1)}=-2r(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}=-2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}=-2rH^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{gathered} \\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\nThe rank-one action means\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)})V\\n=\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H^{(1)}V].\\n\\\\]\\nInitially \\\\(X_0^{(1)}=F(Z_0^{(1)})\\\\), the matrix action is (18), and \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\). Only three evolving objects are used; the population coordinate spaces themselves are infinite dimensional.\\n\\nWe next prove that (20) has a unique solution at every finite time and that Euler approximates it uniformly. The same estimates will apply at finite width. Measure two population states by\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each variable's \\\\(L^2\\\\) norm by its Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\); call this distance \\\\(d_n\\\\). The operator norm is the largest amplification of root-mean-square size.\\n\\nBoth \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\circ F^{-1}\\\\) are 1-Lipschitz. The functions \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\) are bounded and Lipschitz. If both operator norms are at most \\\\(M\\\\) and both output weights are pointwise bounded by \\\\(B\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le (\\\\pi/2)\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+M\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+B\\\\operatorname{Lip}(\\\\phi')\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}-(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+M\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\nThe output difference obeys Cauchy–Schwarz with \\\\(|H^{(2)}|\\\\le\\\\pi/2\\\\), and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|a\\\\otimes b-\\\\widetilde a\\\\otimes\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n\\\\le\\\\|a-\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nHence the vector field is Lipschitz in (21) on these bounded sets, with a constant independent of width.\\n\\nExistence can be proved directly by iteration of the integral equations. On a sufficiently short time interval, the map \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\mapsto\\\\Theta(0)+\\\\int_0^t\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))\\\\,ds\\\\) preserves a closed set of continuous paths with the indicated bounds and contracts the supremum of (21). This set is complete: mean-square limits preserve a common pointwise bound on \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\). Taking the time interval shorter than the reciprocal of the Lipschitz constant proves existence and uniqueness.\\n\\nTo continue the solution, differentiation of the output, using (19), gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f=-2rK,\\\\quad \\\\dot L=-4r^2K,\\\\qquad\\nK=K^{(1)}+K^{(2)}+K^{(3)},\\n\\\\tag{23}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nK^{(1)}&=\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[(\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)})^2\\\\right],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(2)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\delta^{(2)})^2],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(3)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(2)})^2].\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\nThese formulas also follow by differentiating along square-integrable parameter directions: the gradients of \\\\(L\\\\) in \\\\(Z^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)}\\\\) are \\\\(2r\\\\delta^{(1)},2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},2rH^{(2)}\\\\). For matrix perturbations the relevant inner product is defined first on rank-one changes by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle a\\\\otimes b,c\\\\otimes d\\\\rangle\\n=\\\\mathbb E[ac]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[bd],\\n\\\\]\\nand extended by linearity and completion. This is the population version o\",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":37248}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b339b1f9487d28d52f677b3995243\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_USq2ee6WRCQcnDy57DTpRxs4\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e41-9f99-7b10-a2d0-df59d85da605\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r2a\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_OV6Gp33BaN5BMCPJZPoesYpp\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e41-b752-74a0-afbe-28e0dc6040e0\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r2b\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_4l0CvXsQj29bkjupbZI0MxoS\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e41-ccbd-7590-8b52-a7a0e0132499\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r2c\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b33ad0dac87d2b9eace0107e6c393\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-bb37eb41-4059-40fa-98ed-c08825df1d6e\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '400,555p' /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof-r2.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"+\\\\frac{\\\\|W_k^{(3)}-W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(3)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}.\\n\\\\]\\nEquations (22) and (30) bound every forward, backward, and residual difference by \\\\(C_{T,\\\\Delta}(e_{n,k}+\\\\zeta_n)\\\\). Subtracting the updates gives\\n\\\\[\\ne_{n,k+1}\\\\le(1+C_{T,\\\\Delta}\\\\Delta)e_{n,k}+C_{T,\\\\Delta}\\\\Delta\\\\zeta_n.\\n\\\\tag{31}\\n\\\\]\\nInitially \\\\(e_{n,0}=\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\). Since \\\\(N=\\\\lceil T/\\\\Delta\\\\rceil\\\\) is fixed, summing this recurrence proves \\\\(\\\\max_{k\\\\le N}e_{n,k}\\\\to0\\\\). We have therefore proved the actual finite Euler width limit without feeding random scalar coefficients into the Gaussian lemma.\\n\\nThe same argument controls any fixed finite set of extra forward and backward measurements: expand each learned-matrix use by (5) or (6), use the population contraction to construct its oracle version, and add its empirical contraction errors to \\\\(\\\\zeta_n\\\\). There are only finitely many such errors. For continuity of these measurements, allow arbitrary finite compositions of bounded Lipschitz coordinate maps, \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\), linear combinations, and the current \\\\(W^{(2)},(W^{(2)})^*\\\\). All coordinate instructions must be globally Lipschitz. In a product, both varying factors must be bounded, either intrinsically or by clipping. Products containing the unbounded backward field are added only later through the explicit tail argument. Induction on these operations, using (22), gives an error at most \\\\(C_{\\\\mathrm{measurement}}d_n\\\\) for two states on the same finite space, and \\\\(C_{\\\\mathrm{measurement}}d\\\\) on the population spaces.\\n\\nChoose a countable dense collection of those measurements. For each finite list of same-layer measurements, compare its joint empirical law in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\), including dot products. The action-law distance is the sum of these bounded distances with weights \\\\(2^{-j}\\\\), over a fixed enumeration of finite lists. By (26) and (31), its finite-flow versus population-flow error, for any fixed list, has the form\\n\\\\[\\nC_T\\\\Delta+o_{\\\\mathbb P,n\\\\to\\\\infty;\\\\,\\\\Delta}(1)+C_T\\\\Delta.\\n\\\\tag{32}\\n\\\\]\\nFor any requested accuracy, choose a small fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), then take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). Uniform time continuity of each fixed measurement, supplied by (22) and the bounded derivatives, gives uniformity on \\\\([0,T]\\\\). The summable weights extend this to the action-law distance. This statement also holds for any finitely many time points jointly, by appending all those measurements to the same finite oracle calculation before taking the limit.\\n\\nThe population evolution is autonomous on the state just constructed. To see this also at the level of its measured law, start the collection of measurements at a current state instead of at initialization, and take all their mean-square limits on each layer. These spaces are preserved by the current action and adjoint and by the coordinate operations. If two current measured laws agree, sending each measurement to its counterpart preserves every squared norm and inner product. It extends to a surjective linear isometry between the two generated spaces, commutes with the coordinate functions, intertwines the current action and adjoint, and sends \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)}\\\\) to its counterpart. The integral iteration proving existence stays in these spaces. Uniqueness therefore identifies the future laws of the two states. Restarting at time \\\\(s\\\\) and evolving for time \\\\(t\\\\) gives the same law as evolving for (s+t); no extra historical information is required.\\n\\nIt remains to pass from flow to the exact GD in (1), and to justify the unbounded observables. For a single GD step let \\\\(b=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\), the transformed first-layer velocity. The letter (b) is used only in the following cubic calculation. Coordinatewise,\\n\\\\[\\nF(z^{(1)}+\\\\eta_n\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\odot b)\\n=F(z^{(1)})+\\\\eta_nb\\n+\\\\eta_n^2z^{(1)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})^2\\\\odot b^2\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\eta_n^3}{3}\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})^3\\\\odot b^3.\\n\\\\tag{33}\\n\\\\]\\nOn the bounded state set, \\\\(\\\\|b\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\le C_T\\\\), hence \\\\(\\\\|b\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le C_T\\\\sqrt n\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\sup_s|s|\\\\phi'(s)^2<\\\\infty\\\\), the extra terms in root-mean-square norm are at most\\n\\\\[\\nC_T(\\\\eta_n^2\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta_n^3n).\\n\\\\]\\nThe other two parameter updates already are Euler updates. Adding the \\\\(O(\\\\eta_n^2)\\\\) local flow error and summing the recurrence as in (26) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}d_n(\\\\Theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t),\\\\Theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{flow}}(t))\\n\\\\le C_T(\\\\eta_n+\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta_n^2n)\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\tag{34}\\n\\\\]\\nwith probability tending to one. Stop first on a larger bounded set, as before. The output supremum is controlled separately by its sum of bounded activation updates and the residual bound transferred from the flow. This closes the stop. At intermediate times (33) with \\\\(0\\\\le\\\\eta\\\\le\\\\eta_n\\\\) controls the transformed coordinate of the linearly interpolated raw parameter, so (34) holds for the specified interpolation.\\n\\nHere is the required control for quantities containing a possibly large backward coordinate. For this paragraph only, write \\\\(p=(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) at finite width and \\\\(P=(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) in the population. This abbreviation names the same repeatedly used backward measurement, not a new network state. Equations (22), (32), and (34) show uniformly in time that the empirical joint law of \\\\((z^{(1)},p)\\\\) converges in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) to that of \\\\((Z^{(1)},P)\\\\). The latter laws form a compact \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) family because these population variables are continuous in mean square.\\n\\nConsequently the squared tails vanish uniformly:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lim_{A\\\\to\\\\infty}\\\\limsup_{n\\\\to\\\\infty}\\n\\\\mathbb P\\\\left(\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i p_i(t)^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p_i(t)|>A}>\\\\varepsilon\\\\right)=0.\\n\\\\tag{35}\\n\\\\]\\nFor completeness, if (p,P) are coupled, splitting according to \\\\(|P|\\\\le A/2\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>A}]\\n\\\\le4\\\\mathbb E[(p-P)^2]+2\\\\mathbb E[P^2\\\\mathbf1_{|P|>A/2}].\\n\\\\]\\nCompactness gives uniformly small population tails by a finite cover; the uniform \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence then proves (35).\\n\\nClip (p) to ([-A,A]). For fixed \\\\(A\\\\), both \\\\(\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\operatorname{clip}_A(p)\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})^2\\\\operatorname{clip}_A(p)\\\\) are Lipschitz measurements. Their squared errors from the unclipped fields are bounded by the tail in (35), since \\\\(|\\\\phi'|\\\\le1\\\\). Applying \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) to their difference multiplies its root-mean-square size by at most its operator bound. Thus, first taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(A\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), proves uniform convergence of the three kernel blocks in (24), with finite expectations replaced by normalized coordinate sums. It also proves convergence of the squared speeds computed from\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot Z^{(1)}=-2r\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot Z^{(2)}=-2r\\\\left\\\\{\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n+W^{(2)}[\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})^2(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}]\\\\right\\\\}.\\n\\\\tag{36}\\n\\\\]\\nThe prediction and loss converge uniformly as well, directly from bounded activations and the output-weight bound.\\n\\nFor GD, the first-layer interpolated slope is its gradient at the left endpoint. The derivative of \\\\(z^{(2)}=W^{(2)}\\\\phi(z^{(1)})\\\\) uses those constant parameter slopes and the current interpolated activation. To compare it with (36) at the endpoint, on \\\\(|p_i|\\\\le A\\\\) the change in \\\\(\\\\phi'(z_i^{(1)})\\\\) times its first-layer slope is bounded by \\\\(C_T\\\\eta_nA^2\\\\); on the complement both terms are bounded by (C_T|p_i|). The other product-rule errors are \\\\(O(\\\\eta_n)\\\\) in root-mean-square norm by bounded matrix slopes. Equation (35), then \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(A\\\\), then \\\\(A\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), proves\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int_0^T\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\partial_tz_n^{(\\\\ell),\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t)\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\,dt\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\int_0^T\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\dot Z^{(\\\\ell)}(t))^2]\\\\,dt,\\n\\\\qquad \\\\ell=1,2.\\n\\\\tag{37}\\n\\\\]\\nThese statements are in probability. No fourth-moment assumption on the evolving backward field is needed.\\n\\nFor the trajectory-law claim, use \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) on continuous scalar paths with the supremum distance. If \\\\(I_\\\\pi z\\\\) is polygonal interpolation on a partition of mesh at most (a), absolute continuity and Cauchy–Schwarz give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|z-I_\\\\pi z\\\\|_\\\\infty^2\\\\le4a\\\\int_0^T|\\\\dot z(t)|^2\\\\,dt.\\n\\\\tag{38}\\n\\\\]\\nAfter coordinate averaging, (37) bounds the empirical distance to this finite-grid representation by \\\\(O_{\\\\mathbb P}(\\\\sqrt a)\\\\). The population bound is identical; mean-square continuous velocities admit absolutely continuous sample paths by integrating a jointly measurable version and applying Fubini. At fixed partition, joint grid-law convergence follows from (32) and (34), including second moments. Polygonal interpolation is a continuous linear map of the finite grid vector. Let \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed partition, then \\\\(a\\\\to0\\\\) in (38). This proves, separately for the two layers,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{z_{n,i}^{(\\\\ell),\\\\mathrm{GD}}(\\\\cdot)}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\operatorname{Law}(Z^{(\\\\ell)}(\\\\cdot))\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in }\\\\mathcal W_2(C([0,T])).\\n\\\\tag{39}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFinally we verify the claimed feature learning. Only for this calculation define the following fixed initial population quantities, each of which recurs:\\n\\\\[\\nm=\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]>0,\\\\quad\\nU=H_0^{(2)}\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(2)}),\\\\quad \\\\nu=\\\\mathbb E[U^2]>0,\\\\quad\\nP_0=(W_0^{(2)})^*U.\\n\\\\]\\nThe row-conditioning calculation (8), without its \\\\(2\\\\Delta\\\\) factor, gives\\n\\\\[\\nP_0=\\\\frac{\\\\mathbb E[Z_0^{(2)}U]}mH_0^{(1)}+\\\\sqrt\\\\nu\\\\,\\\\Gamma^{(1)},\\n\\\\tag{40}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\Gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1)\\\\) is independent of the first-layer root. Put\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(1)})P_0)^2]>0,\\\\qquad\\nS_0=mU+W_0^{(2)}[\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(1)})^2P_0].\\n\\\\]\\nThe positivity of (d) follows from the positive independent Gaussian variance in (40) and (phi'>0). By adjunction,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[US_0]=m\\\\nu+d>0,\\n\\\\tag{41}\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(S_0\\\\ne0\\\\) in mean square.\\n\\nInitially (r=-1) and \\\\(W^{(3)}=0\\\\). Dividing the integral equations (20) by successive powers of \\\\(t\\\\), using bounded scalar derivatives and mean-square continuity, gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW^{(3)}(t)&=2tH_0^{(2)}+O_{L^2}(t^2),&\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}(t)&=2tU+O_{L^2}(t^2),\\\\\\\\\\nZ^{(1)}(t)-Z_0^{(1)}&=2t^2\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(1)})P_0+o_{L^2}(t^2),\\\\\\\\\\nW^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}&=2t^2U\\\\otimes H_0^{(1)}+o_{\\\\rm op}(t^2),\\\\\\\\\\nZ^{(2)}(t)-Z_0^{(2)}&=2t^2S_0+o_{L^2}(t^2).\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{42}\\n\\\\]\\nTo check the orders directly, (25) first gives \\\\(W^{(3)}=O(t)\\\\), then \\\\(delta^{(2)}=O(t)\\\\), and hence \\\\(X^{(1)}-X_0^{(1)},W^{(2)}-W_0^{(2)},Z^{(2)}-Z_0^{(2)}=O(t^2)\\\\). Substitution into the output-weight and derivative equations gives the first line. Substitution once more gives the remaining lines. For example \\\\(dot X^{(1)}(t)/t\\\\to4P_0\\\\), and applying the bounded derivative of \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\) gives the first preactivation expansion.\\n\\nLet \\\\(k_3=\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(2)})^2]>0\\\\). Equations (24), (41), and (42) yield\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}(t)=4dt^2+o(t^2),\\\\quad K^{(2)}(t)=4m\\\\nu t^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(3)}(t)=k_3+4(m\\\\nu+d)t^2+o(t^2),\\\\quad\\nK(t)=k_3+8(m\\\\nu+d)t^2+o(t^2).\\n\\\\tag{43}\\n\\\\]\\nThe two squared displacements have leading terms \\\\(4dt^4\\\\) and \\\\(4\\\\mathbb E[S_0^2]t^4\\\\); their squared speeds have leading terms \\\\(16dt^2\\\\) and \\\\(16\\\\mathbb E[S_0^2]t^2\\\\). Thus both hidden layers move and every kernel block has a positive integral on sufficiently short nonzero intervals. Also\\n\\\\[\\nf(t)=2k_3t+o(t),\\\\qquad L(t)=1-4k_3t+o(t).\\n\\\\]\\nBoth initial preactivation laws in (7) are nondegenerate Gaussians. Their variances remain positive by mean-square continuity. For either layer the error of the best affine fit to the activation is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(\\\\phi(Z^{(\\\\ell)}))-\\n\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{Cov}(Z^{(\\\\ell)},\\\\phi(Z^{(\\\\ell)}))^2}{\\\\operatorname{Var}(Z^{(\\\\ell)})}.\\n\\\\tag{44}\\n\\\\]\\nIt is initially positive: equality would force (arctan) to agree with an affine function on a full-support Gaussian law and hence everywhere by continuity. The moments in (44) are continuous in mean square because (phi) is bounded and Lipschitz. This error therefore remains positive for both layers on a common interval ([0,T_*]). Equations (34), (37), (39), and (43) establish all the asserted convergence and feature-learning claims.\\n\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-ba430358-4220-4c76-aaea-f8372f6360bd\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n ',dt|,ds|mathbb|\\\\\\\\(phi|\\\\\\\\(L\\\\\\\\)|\\\\\\\\(n\\\\\\\\)' /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof-r2.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"3:We consider one input, equal to \\\\(1\\\\), and target \\\\(1\\\\). At width \\\\(n\\\\), the first preactivation \\\\(z^{(1)}=W^{(1)}\\\\) and output weight \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) are vectors in \\\\(\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\). Throughout, \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) means the rescaled output weight. Put\\n35:Capital letters \\\\(Z^{(\\\\ell)},H^{(\\\\ell)},X^{(1)}\\\\) denote individual population coordinates. We keep \\\\(W^{(2)},W^{(3)},\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\) for their population counterparts, explicitly identifying when we have moved to population quantities. An expectation always concerns coordinates belonging to the same layer. For a scalar population variable \\\\(V\\\\), write \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\); finite vectors always use \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\), with the width normalization visible.\\n84:Z_0^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]).\\n116:where \\\\(r_1=2\\\\Delta\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi(Z_0^{(2)})^2]-1\\\\).\\n143:\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n185:=\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}a\\\\|_2^2}{n}+\\\\varepsilon^2+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1).\\n192:\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{U}\\\\gamma\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\,\\\\middle|\\\\,\\\\text{history}\\\\right]\\n215:and do the same separately on the second layer. The limiting mean-square distance is \\\\(\\\\mathbb E[(V-\\\\widetilde V)^2]\\\\), so these classes are precisely the corresponding population random variables, identified when equal almost surely. Define\\n226:\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2].\\n236:\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,W_0^{(2)}V]=\\\\mathbb E[((W_0^{(2)})^*U)V].\\n246:f=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H^{(2)}],\\\\quad r=f-1,\\\\quad\\n256:=\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H^{(1)}V].\\n291:Existence can be proved directly by iteration of the integral equations. On a sufficiently short time interval, the map \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\mapsto\\\\Theta(0)+\\\\int_0^t\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))\\\\,ds\\\\) preserves a closed set of continuous paths with the indicated bounds and contracts the supremum of (21). This set is complete: mean-square limits preserve a common pointwise bound on \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\). Taking the time interval shorter than the reciprocal of the Lipschitz constant proves existence and uniqueness.\\n302:K^{(1)}&=\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[(\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)})^2\\\\right],\\\\\\\\\\n303:K^{(2)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\delta^{(2)})^2],\\\\\\\\\\n304:K^{(3)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(2)})^2].\\n310:=\\\\mathbb E[ac]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[bd],\\n315:=\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\,A H^{(1)}],\\n334:\\\\left\\\\|\\\\int_t^{t+\\\\Delta}[\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))-\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(t))]\\\\,ds\\\\right\\\\|\\n352:r_k=\\\\mathbb E[W_k^{(3)}H_k^{(2)}]-1,\\\\quad\\n353:\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}],\\\\quad\\n354:\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}]\\n363:-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_s\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}],\\n374:-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_sh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(1)}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}]\\\\bigg),\\\\\\\\\\n391:\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}]\\\\right).\\n407:Initially \\\\(e_{n,0}=\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n=O_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1})\\\\). Since \\\\(N=\\\\lceil T/\\\\Delta\\\\rceil\\\\) is fixed, summing this recurrence proves \\\\(\\\\max_{k\\\\le N}e_{n,k}\\\\to0\\\\). We have therefore proved the actual finite Euler width limit without feeding random scalar coefficients into the Gaussian lemma.\\n413:C_T\\\\Delta+o_{\\\\mathbb P,n\\\\to\\\\infty;\\\\,\\\\Delta}(1)+C_T\\\\Delta.\\n445:\\\\mathbb P\\\\left(\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i p_i(t)^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p_i(t)|>A}>\\\\varepsilon\\\\right)=0.\\n450:\\\\mathbb E[p^2\\\\mathbf1_{|p|>A}]\\n451:\\\\le4\\\\mathbb E[(p-P)^2]+2\\\\mathbb E[P^2\\\\mathbf1_{|P|>A/2}].\\n460:\\\\dot Z^{(2)}=-2r\\\\left\\\\{\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n468:\\\\int_0^T\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\partial_tz_n^{(\\\\ell),\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t)\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\,dt\\n469:\\\\longrightarrow\\\\int_0^T\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\dot Z^{(\\\\ell)}(t))^2]\\\\,dt,\\n477:\\\\|z-I_\\\\pi z\\\\|_\\\\infty^2\\\\le4a\\\\int_0^T|\\\\dot z(t)|^2\\\\,dt.\\n480:After coordinate averaging, (37) bounds the empirical distance to this finite-grid representation by \\\\(O_{\\\\mathbb P}(\\\\sqrt a)\\\\). The population bound is identical; mean-square continuous velocities admit absolutely continuous sample paths by integrating a jointly measurable version and applying Fubini. At fixed partition, joint grid-law convergence follows from (32) and (34), including second moments. Polygonal interpolation is a continuous linear map of the finite grid vector. Let \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed partition, then \\\\(a\\\\to0\\\\) in (38). This proves, separately for the two layers,\\n490:m=\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]>0,\\\\quad\\n491:U=H_0^{(2)}\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(2)}),\\\\quad \\\\nu=\\\\mathbb E[U^2]>0,\\\\quad\\n496:P_0=\\\\frac{\\\\mathbb E[Z_0^{(2)}U]}mH_0^{(1)}+\\\\sqrt\\\\nu\\\\,\\\\Gamma^{(1)},\\n501:d=\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(1)})P_0)^2]>0,\\\\qquad\\n504:The positivity of (d) follows from the positive independent Gaussian variance in (40) and (phi'>0). By adjunction,\\n506:\\\\mathbb E[US_0]=m\\\\nu+d>0,\\n523:Let \\\\(k_3=\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(2)})^2]>0\\\\). Equations (24), (41), and (42) yield\\n532:The two squared displacements have leading terms \\\\(4dt^4\\\\) and \\\\(4\\\\mathbb E[S_0^2]t^4\\\\); their squared speeds have leading terms \\\\(16dt^2\\\\) and \\\\(16\\\\mathbb E[S_0^2]t^2\\\\). Thus both hidden layers move and every kernel block has a positive integral on sufficiently short nonzero intervals. Also\\n542:It is initially positive: equality would force (arctan) to agree with an affine function on a full-support Gaussian law and hence everywhere by continuity. The moments in (44) are continuous in mean square because (phi) is bounded and Lipschitz. This error therefore remains positive for both layers on a common interval ([0,T_*]). Equations (34), (37), (39), and (43) establish all the asserted convergence and feature-learning claims.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-2c3b0db4-1ae5-4ced-b3df-d04d4ed3cf2b\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '300,380p' /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof-r2.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nK^{(1)}&=\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[(\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)})^2\\\\right],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(2)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\delta^{(2)})^2],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(3)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(2)})^2].\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\nThese formulas also follow by differentiating along square-integrable parameter directions: the gradients of \\\\(L\\\\) in \\\\(Z^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)}\\\\) are \\\\(2r\\\\delta^{(1)},2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},2rH^{(2)}\\\\). For matrix perturbations the relevant inner product is defined first on rank-one changes by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle a\\\\otimes b,c\\\\otimes d\\\\rangle\\n=\\\\mathbb E[ac]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[bd],\\n\\\\]\\nand extended by linearity and completion. This is the population version of the ordinary sum-of-squared-entries geometry, called the Hilbert–Schmidt geometry. In particular,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle \\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},A\\\\rangle\\n=\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\,A H^{(1)}],\\n\\\\]\\nso the displayed rank-one action is the matrix gradient. Here \\\\(A\\\\) denotes a matrix-action perturbation. The update \\\\(W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}\\\\) lies in that space because it is the integral of rank-one actions of bounded Hilbert–Schmidt norm. Thus (20), transformed back to \\\\(Z^{(1)}\\\\), is gradient flow for this loss, with the same scaling as (1).\\n\\nThe chain rules used here hold along these mean-square differentiable paths: bounded scalar derivatives give convergence of difference quotients by domination, first for bounded directions and then by mean-square approximation. We are not asserting that every coordinatewise nonlinear map is differentiable in operator norm on all of \\\\(L^2\\\\).\\n\\nSince \\\\(K\\\\ge0\\\\), \\\\(|r(t)|\\\\le|r(0)|\\\\). Therefore, on \\\\([0,T]\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\\\pi|r(0)|T,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\le\\\\pi|r(0)|\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|\\\\dot X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\le2|r(0)|\\\\|W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{25}\\n\\\\]\\nThese bounds prevent finite-time blow-up. At finite width, (11), the initial law of large numbers for \\\\(F(z_0^{(1)})\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\to0\\\\) in probability put the same bounds on an event of probability tending to one.\\n\\nLet the vector-field bound and Lipschitz constant on a slightly larger bounded set be \\\\(M_T,L_T\\\\). One exact flow step of length \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) differs from its Euler step by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\\\int_t^{t+\\\\Delta}[\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))-\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(t))]\\\\,ds\\\\right\\\\|\\n\\\\le \\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\Delta^2.\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently the accumulated error satisfies\\n\\\\[\\ne_{k+1}\\\\le(1+L_T\\\\Delta)e_k+C_T\\\\Delta^2,\\n\\\\qquad \\\\max_{k\\\\Delta\\\\le T}e_k\\\\le C_T'\\\\Delta.\\n\\\\tag{26}\\n\\\\]\\nTo justify the bounded set for Euler, stop at a first exit. The bound proves that the \\\\(X^{(1)}\\\\) and operator components remain inside their margins. Although (21) does not control the output supremum, its update gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W_k^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\n\\\\pi\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}|r_s|.\\n\\\\]\\nUp to the stop, (22) and (26) make \\\\(|r_s|\\\\le|r(0)|+C_T\\\\Delta\\\\); choose the supremum margin larger than \\\\(\\\\pi T(|r(0)|+1)\\\\). For small \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) no first exit is possible. Thus (26), also between grid times, holds for both population and finite-width Euler.\\n\\nWe can now define and compare the oracle precisely. Attach the word “oracle” as a subscript to its finite arrays; it denotes the auxiliary system, not another normalization. For fixed population Euler mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), all the numbers\\n\\\\[\\nr_k=\\\\mathbb E[W_k^{(3)}H_k^{(2)}]-1,\\\\quad\\n\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}],\\\\quad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}]\\n\\\\tag{27}\\n\\\\]\\nare deterministic. Start the oracle from the same \\\\(z_0^{(1)},W_0^{(2)}\\\\) as the finite network and from \\\\(W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},0}^{(3)}=0\\\\). In order at each step set\\n\\\\[\\nh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}=\\\\phi(F^{-1}(x_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)})),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nz_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}\\n-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_s\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}],\\n\\\\tag{28}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}=W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nx_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k+1}^{(1)}=x_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_k\\n\\\\bigg((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\n-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_sh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(1)}\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}]\\\\bigg),\\\\\\\\\\nW_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k+1}^{(3)}=W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(3)}-2\\\\Delta r_kh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{29}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is a fixed finite Gaussian calculation. Its output weights obey the deterministic coordinate bound \\\\(\\\\pi\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}|r_s|\\\\). Hence the product defining \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) can be replaced, without changing any oracle value, by a globally Lipschitz map that clips its first argument above this bound. All other maps meet the finite Gaussian lemma. Its limiting variables are exactly the population Euler variables: this follows instruction by instruction from (5)–(6) at population level and the already constructed action (18)–(19).\\n\\nRecord its finite matrix memory as\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b33b74e2c87d2b60c9ff8f922ac0b\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying the δ¹ definition**\",\"**Adding the clipped metric**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b33c31bd087d285288ea30a32d8da\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying the δ¹ definition**\",\"**Adding the clipped metric**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b33d109ac87d2b2b4ada025cc2c76\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying the δ¹ definition**\",\"**Adding the clipped metric**\",\"**Refining theorem quantifiers**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-0e9c802b-b9a8-48a3-a411-dcdf35f24835\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n '(\\\"'^|[''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\])\\\\\\\\((s\\\\\\\\+t|b|p,P|p|\\\\\\\\[-A,A\\\\\\\\]|C_T\\\\\\\\|p_i\\\\\\\\||a|d|phi|phi.>0|r=-1|arctan|\\\\\\\\[0,T_\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\])\\\\\\\\)|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\((delta|dot)' /tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/proof-r2.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"418:The population evolution is autonomous on the state just constructed. To see this also at the level of its measured law, start the collection of measurements at a current state instead of at initialization, and take all their mean-square limits on each layer. These spaces are preserved by the current action and adjoint and by the coordinate operations. If two current measured laws agree, sending each measurement to its counterpart preserves every squared norm and inner product. It extends to a surjective linear isometry between the two generated spaces, commutes with the coordinate functions, intertwines the current action and adjoint, and sends \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)}\\\\) to its counterpart. The integral iteration proving existence stays in these spaces. Uniqueness therefore identifies the future laws of the two states. Restarting at time \\\\(s\\\\) and evolving for time \\\\(t\\\\) gives the same law as evolving for (s+t); no extra historical information is required.\\n420:It remains to pass from flow to the exact GD in (1), and to justify the unbounded observables. For a single GD step let \\\\(b=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\), the transformed first-layer velocity. The letter (b) is used only in the following cubic calculation. Coordinatewise,\\n448:For completeness, if (p,P) are coupled, splitting according to \\\\(|P|\\\\le A/2\\\\) gives\\n455:Clip (p) to ([-A,A]). For fixed \\\\(A\\\\), both \\\\(\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\operatorname{clip}_A(p)\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})^2\\\\operatorname{clip}_A(p)\\\\) are Lipschitz measurements. Their squared errors from the unclipped fields are bounded by the tail in (35), since \\\\(|\\\\phi'|\\\\le1\\\\). Applying \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) to their difference multiplies its root-mean-square size by at most its operator bound. Thus, first taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(A\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), proves uniform convergence of the three kernel blocks in (24), with finite expectations replaced by normalized coordinate sums. It also proves convergence of the squared speeds computed from\\n466:For GD, the first-layer interpolated slope is its gradient at the left endpoint. The derivative of \\\\(z^{(2)}=W^{(2)}\\\\phi(z^{(1)})\\\\) uses those constant parameter slopes and the current interpolated activation. To compare it with (36) at the endpoint, on \\\\(|p_i|\\\\le A\\\\) the change in \\\\(\\\\phi'(z_i^{(1)})\\\\) times its first-layer slope is bounded by \\\\(C_T\\\\eta_nA^2\\\\); on the complement both terms are bounded by (C_T|p_i|). The other product-rule errors are \\\\(O(\\\\eta_n)\\\\) in root-mean-square norm by bounded matrix slopes. Equation (35), then \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(A\\\\), then \\\\(A\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), proves\\n475:For the trajectory-law claim, use \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) on continuous scalar paths with the supremum distance. If \\\\(I_\\\\pi z\\\\) is polygonal interpolation on a partition of mesh at most (a), absolute continuity and Cauchy–Schwarz give\\n504:The positivity of (d) follows from the positive independent Gaussian variance in (40) and (phi'>0). By adjunction,\\n511:Initially (r=-1) and \\\\(W^{(3)}=0\\\\). Dividing the integral equations (20) by successive powers of \\\\(t\\\\), using bounded scalar derivatives and mean-square continuity, gives\\n521:To check the orders directly, (25) first gives \\\\(W^{(3)}=O(t)\\\\), then \\\\(delta^{(2)}=O(t)\\\\), and hence \\\\(X^{(1)}-X_0^{(1)},W^{(2)}-W_0^{(2)},Z^{(2)}-Z_0^{(2)}=O(t^2)\\\\). Substitution into the output-weight and derivative equations gives the first line. Substitution once more gives the remaining lines. For example \\\\(dot X^{(1)}(t)/t\\\\to4P_0\\\\), and applying the bounded derivative of \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\) gives the first preactivation expansion.\\n542:It is initially positive: equality would force (arctan) to agree with an affine function on a full-support Gaussian law and hence everywhere by continuity. The moments in (44) are continuous in mean square because (phi) is bounded and Lipschitz. This error therefore remains positive for both layers on a common interval ([0,T_*]). Equations (34), (37), (39), and (43) establish all the asserted convergence and feature-learning claims.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b33dc4d9887d29c06a19cc24c5704\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b33ea7a0887d2a9ec33d78fc5714d\",\"summary\":[\"**Polishing final formatting**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b33f7121087d295ccc1507a1077b5\",\"summary\":[\"**Polishing final formatting**\",\"**Preparing W2 metric clarification**\",\"**Clarifying countable construction**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3407cd6887d28d137534268b0fd7\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking the proof format**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3414853487d2ace2f34ed0ddad67\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3414c6d887d281c146bf3a7745c3\",\"text\":\"The second set of three reviewers is checking the revised proof independently. They have not identified another gap so far. I’ve also checked that it covers the full result: an autonomous population flow, convergence from exact GD, path laws and velocity energies, and the small-time feature-learning claims.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a06e41-9fde-7971-8a7b-04d1c9d05246\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e41-9f99-7b10-a2d0-df59d85da605\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r2a\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b341ffa6487d2a0521775c873206f\",\"summary\":[\"**Refining isometry definitions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a06e41-b795-7042-9a28-e19a0760294e\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e41-b752-74a0-afbe-28e0dc6040e0\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r2b\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b342fa09087d287c8f0834101eef4\",\"summary\":[\"**Checking reviewer passes**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b343e7eb087d2a4e41e8bc94b304e\",\"summary\":[\"**Structuring the full proof**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3449858087d295231ca9008b4447\",\"summary\":[\"**Structuring the full proof**\",\"**Refining theorem notation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3454318087d28a67e06aaedc03a4\",\"summary\":[\"**Structuring the full proof**\",\"**Refining theorem notation**\",\"**Replacing empirical-law notation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a06e41-ccfb-7c81-a0a8-bd361139a47c\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a06e41-ccbd-7590-8b52-a7a0e0132499\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/blind_l2_r2c\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b345e966c87d2b808f96f9419fbf6\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9b346902c487d280c287cb185c87d9\",\"text\":\"All three reviewers in the second blind round have returned PASS, with no remaining mathematical gap identified. The proof below includes the corrected product restriction, the direct Gaussian argument, and the full passage from exact GD to the autonomous population flow.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-82b8a029-033d-4e0d-87c8-af9cc82618db\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/REVIEW.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Independent blind review record\\n\\nRequested review: complete adversarial mathematical audit; reviewers received only the candidate proof path and the review assignment. All six reviewers were freshly spawned with no conversation history. Each was instructed not to read sources, other drafts, or other reviews, and not to delegate.\\n\\n## Round 1 — proof.md\\n\\n- blind_l2_r1a: FAIL. The phrase “products with a clipped factor” allows multiplication of a bounded factor by an unbounded factor and does not justify a global Lipschitz estimate. No other material gap identified.\\n- blind_l2_r1b: FAIL. Same issue, with a finite-state counterexample showing a vanishing state distance but a nonvanishing measurement difference. No other material gap identified.\\n- blind_l2_r1c: FAIL. Same issue, with an explicit backward-field counterexample. No additional gap found in tails, speeds, paths, or small-time claims.\\n\\nRevision: restrict every coordinate operation in the Gaussian lemma and measurement class to globally Lipschitz maps; both varying factors in a product must be bounded intrinsically or by clipping. The particular products involving an unbounded backward field are passed separately using the existing uniform square-tail estimate. The revision also spells out the next forward-call input, the empirical induction statement, and the gradient geometry.\\n\\n## Round 2 — proof-r2.md\\n\\n- blind_l2_r2a: PASS. Reviewed the full candidate; specifically confirmed adaptive conditioning, perturbation and its removal, population operator and adjoint construction, the convergence chain, and small-time coefficients.\\n- blind_l2_r2b: PASS. Reviewed the full candidate; specifically confirmed wellposedness, gradient scaling, oracle residuals, feedback stability, Euler/GD bootstrap, law-level autonomy, tails and activity.\\n- blind_l2_r2c: PASS. Reviewed the full candidate; specifically confirmed the measurement topology, uniform second-moment tails, GD squared speeds, trajectory-law convergence and feature-learning expansions.\\n\\nTWO_HIDDEN_LAYER_PROOF.md is the reviewed second-round proof with inline mathematical delimiters and missing LaTeX escapes corrected. No mathematical argument was changed after the second round. Reviewer passes report that no material gap was identified; they are not a formal machine verification.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"path\":\"/tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/TWO_HIDDEN_LAYER_PROOF.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Two hidden layers: a complete proof of the joint width and learning-rate limit\\n\\nWe consider one input, equal to \\\\(1\\\\), and target \\\\(1\\\\). At width \\\\(n\\\\), the first preactivation \\\\(z^{(1)}=W^{(1)}\\\\) and output weight \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) are vectors in \\\\(\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\), and \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n}\\\\). Throughout, \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) means the rescaled output weight. Put\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z^{(1)}),\\\\quad z^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h^{(1)},\\\\quad\\nh^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad \\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nf_n=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h^{(2)}}n,\\\\qquad r_n=f_n-1,\\\\qquad L_n=r_n^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll initial entries are independent, with\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,i}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad W_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\\\quad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\nDefine the derivatives without the loss residual by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta^{(1)}=\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\odot(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_n/\\\\partial z^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\). The exact GD being analyzed is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{k+1}^{(1)}&=z_k^{(1)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}\\\\delta_k^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}&=W_k^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}}n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}(h_k^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}&=W_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}h_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nWe use the clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), interpolate these three parameters linearly, and recompute the other network quantities from them. The proof applies whenever \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\to0\\\\), in particular to \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\).\\n\\nWe will construct one deterministic population evolution, prove that every fixed finite collection of its forward and backward measurements is the limit of the corresponding finite-network measurements, and prove uniform convergence of predictions, losses, and all three kernel blocks on every finite time interval. We also prove convergence of the two hidden trajectory laws and their integrated squared speeds. At small positive times both hidden layers move, the activation remains non-affine on their distributions, and the kernel changes.\\n\\nCapital letters \\\\(Z^{(\\\\ell)},H^{(\\\\ell)},X^{(1)}\\\\) denote individual population coordinates. We keep \\\\(W^{(2)},W^{(3)},\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\) for their population counterparts, explicitly identifying when we have moved to population quantities. An expectation always concerns coordinates belonging to the same layer. For a scalar population variable \\\\(V\\\\), write \\\\(\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=(\\\\mathbb E V^2)^{1/2}\\\\); finite vectors always use \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\), with the width normalization visible.\\n\\nFirst consider the continuous finite-width flow associated with (1). Set\\n\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+s^3/3,\\\\qquad x^{(1)}=F(z^{(1)}).\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(F'(s)\\\\phi'(s)=1\\\\), this flow becomes\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot x^{(1)}&=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}&=-\\\\frac{2r_n}{n}\\\\delta^{(2)}(h^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}&=-2r_nh^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nThe change of coordinates is exact for the flow. Euler with proof mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) means replacing each derivative in (2) by its forward difference. In particular,\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}^{(1)}=x_k^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\nThis is Euler for the transformed flow; transforming the GD iterates in (1) produces an additional defect, estimated below.\\n\\nIterating the Euler matrix update gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}-\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently, for any appropriate vectors \\\\(v,u\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}v=W_0^{(2)}v-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top v}{n},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\n(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top u=(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}r_{n,s}h_s^{(1)}\\n\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_s^{(2)})^\\\\top u}{n}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\nThus training adds a finite sum of rank-one matrices. At fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\), only finitely many initial-matrix calls and scalar contractions need to be understood.\\n\\nThere is a logical detail here: the empirical coefficients in (5)–(6) are random. We first analyze an auxiliary finite system, the oracle, using deterministic population residuals and contractions in their place. Those numbers will be constructed below from the initial population action; they are not assumptions about the empirical network. We then prove that its empirical contractions approach the supplied numbers, and compare it with the empirical-feedback Euler system.\\n\\nThe matrix calculation needed for this construction is elementary Gaussian conditioning. For example, the first forward call is\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditional on \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), its coordinates are independent centered Gaussians of variance \\\\(\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\). Hence the initial population coordinates satisfy\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\\\quad H_0^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_0^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]).\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\nIn the oracle, \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\) and \\\\(r_0=-1\\\\). Until the oracle comparison below, the vectors in these first-call examples are oracle vectors. Its first update leaves \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) unchanged and gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(3)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)}),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)})\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_0^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\nThe next transpose call depends on the matrix already used in the forward pass. Define, for nonzero \\\\(b\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nP_{b^\\\\perp}=I-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\]\\nConditioning each Gaussian row on its observed dot product with \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), and then summing the rows with coefficients \\\\(\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)}\\\\), gives\\n\\\\[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d=\\n\\\\frac{(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}h_0^{(1)}\\n+\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(1)}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\nHere equality is conditional in distribution, and \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the preceding history. The first term supplies precisely the overlap required by\\n\\\\[\\n(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)};\\n\\\\]\\nthe second is perpendicular to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\).\\n\\nThe next update is\\n\\\\[\\nx_2^{(1)}=x_0^{(1)}-2\\\\Delta r_1(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\nh_2^{(1)}=\\\\phi(F^{-1}(x_2^{(1)})),\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(r_1=2\\\\Delta\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi(Z_0^{(2)})^2]-1\\\\).\\nThus the next initial-matrix call is \\\\(W_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\). Condition on both previous observations. Multiplying the resulting conditional matrix by \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}z_0^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{[P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}]^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2^2}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}h_2^{(1)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the previous history. The denominators in (8)–(9) are positive almost surely for \\\\(\\\\Delta>0\\\\), because the initial Gaussians are nondegenerate and \\\\(\\\\phi'(s)>0\\\\). The first response retains the old forward answer. The second supplies the remaining overlap required by the old transpose answer. The last term disturbs neither observation. Formula (5), with population coefficients for the oracle, then adds the learned rank-one correction to obtain its actual preactivation node.\\n\\nWe now prove the finite Gaussian fact needed to repeat this argument, including the case of dependent query directions. The proof below requires only empirical convergence against continuous measurements with at most quadratic growth. It does not claim convergence against arbitrary discontinuous functions.\\n\\nConsider a fixed finite calculation starting from iid coordinate tuples with finite second moments, independent of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\). Its instructions are fixed globally Lipschitz coordinate functions, linear combinations with deterministic coefficients, and calls to \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) or its transpose. Root tuples may include both \\\\(z_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(F(z_0^{(1)})\\\\). Then all same-layer joint empirical laws have deterministic limits, including their second moments.\\n\\nFor precision, the distance between two laws used here is the smallest root-mean-square distance between random vectors having those laws, usually denoted \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\). For empirical laws, matching coordinates gives the bound\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{v_i},\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{\\\\widetilde v_i}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\le\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\|v_i-\\\\widetilde v_i\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\nConvergence in this distance means weak convergence together with convergence of second moments. Thus it includes all dot products of a fixed joint tuple.\\n\\nFirst, the Gaussian matrix has a uniform operator bound with exponentially high probability. A \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net of the Euclidean unit sphere has at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) elements. Using two such nets,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>M)\\n\\\\le 2\\\\,9^{2n}\\\\exp(-nM^2/8).\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed the operator norm is at most twice the largest bilinear form on these nets, and each bilinear form is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). Choose a fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\).\\n\\nTo avoid inverting almost-dependent query directions, temporarily add \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) to the input of each matrix call, where each \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\) is a new independent standard Gaussian vector. Fix \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\). This is only a device for the proof; we will remove it.\\n\\nSuppose previous calls have revealed\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}V=Y,\\\\qquad (W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R.\\n\\\\]\\nThe columns of \\\\(V\\\\) are old first-layer input vectors, the columns of \\\\(U\\\\) are old second-layer input vectors, and \\\\(Y,R\\\\) contain the respective answers. Their column counts are fixed. All coordinates derived from these answers give no further information once the answers and roots are fixed. Gaussian conditioning therefore leaves\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}\\\\overset d=\\nY(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\n+U(U^\\\\top U)^{-1}R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}\\n+P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}P_{V^\\\\perp}.\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\nHere \\\\(P_{V^\\\\perp}=I-V(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\\\), and similarly for \\\\(U\\\\); terms for an empty column list are zero. The fresh matrix has independent \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\) entries. The first two terms obey both recorded equations; the remaining Gaussian matrix lies in exactly the directions that leave those equations unchanged. Orthogonal components of a centered isotropic Gaussian are independent, which proves (12), including for adaptive queries: each input is chosen from the earlier answers before its new answer is observed.\\n\\nFor a new right-side input \\\\(v\\\\), multiplication gives\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}v\\\\overset d=Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma,\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_n=(V^\\\\top V/n)^{-1}(V^\\\\top v/n),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\beta_n=(U^\\\\top U/n)^{-1}(R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}v/n).\\n\\\\]\\nThe transpose case exchanges the two layer populations.\\n\\nThe induction statement is joint \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence of every finite tuple of previously constructed vectors on each layer. Equivalently, the average of any continuous measurement \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\) with\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\psi(s)|\\\\le C(1+\\\\|s\\\\|_2^2)\\n\\\\]\\nconverges to its population expectation. The choices \\\\(\\\\psi(s)=s^2\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\psi(s,t)=st\\\\) give the required squared norms and contractions. Revealing each fresh input noise first extends the old tuple by independent Gaussian coordinates; conditional averaging proves this extension exactly as for the fresh output noise below. Thus all the normalized products in (13) converge. The perturbation guarantees invertibility of their limiting input Gram matrices: when a new column \\\\(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) is added to any old input list, conditional Gaussian second-moment calculations give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi)\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}a\\\\|_2^2}{n}+\\\\varepsilon^2+o_{\\\\mathbb P}(1).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\nThe cross term has conditional variance at most \\\\(4\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\|a\\\\|_2^2/n^2\\\\), and only a fixed number of Gaussian directions are removed. Its limiting squared distance is at least \\\\(\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\). Successive Schur complements therefore stay positive; all the finite Gram inverses in (13) converge.\\n\\nConsequently the response coefficients and Gaussian variance converge. Write \\\\(P_U=I-P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\) for projection onto the column span of \\\\(U\\\\). This projection removes finitely many directions, and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{U}\\\\gamma\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\,\\\\middle|\\\\,\\\\text{history}\\\\right]\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{rank}(U)}n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\nAfter removing this negligible projection and replacing coefficients by their limits, the new coordinates are an old coordinate function plus independent scalar Gaussians. For a bounded Lipschitz measurement, conditional empirical variance is at most a constant divided by \\\\(n\\\\). Its conditional mean is the old empirical average of the measurement averaged over one scalar Gaussian, which converges by the previous induction step. The conditional variance of the new squared norm is also \\\\(O((1+\\\\|Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n\\\\|_2^2/n)/n)\\\\). Thus second moments converge as well. This proves the enlarged joint \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence. Lipschitz coordinate operations preserve it, closing the induction at fixed \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\).\\n\\nFinally couple the perturbed and unperturbed calculations using the same roots and matrix. On the event in (11) and the event that the finitely many \\\\(\\\\|\\\\xi\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\) are bounded, induction through the finite instructions gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{\\\\text{constructed }v}\\\\frac{\\\\|v^{\\\\varepsilon}-v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n\\\\le C\\\\varepsilon,\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(C\\\\) depends on the fixed calculation but not on \\\\(n\\\\) or \\\\(0<\\\\varepsilon\\\\le1\\\\). Each coordinate map multiplies a difference by its fixed Lipschitz constant, and each matrix call by at most \\\\(M\\\\), plus the new perturbation. Both events have probability tending to one. By (10), the deterministic limiting laws for \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\) form a Cauchy family in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\); its limit is also the limit of the unperturbed empirical laws, first taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\to0\\\\). This proves the finite Gaussian fact without assuming stability of a singular unperturbed Gram matrix.\\n\\nNow we turn these compatible finite calculations into one population matrix action. Take a countable collection of initial calculations, closed under rational linear combinations, initial-matrix calls in both directions, the Lipschitz maps used above, and a countable family of bounded Lipschitz functions of finite coordinate lists that is dense among continuous functions on compact sets. Include constants, clipping at every integer level, and products of bounded globally Lipschitz factors. In every such product, clip each factor that is not already bounded; all coordinate instructions in this collection must be globally Lipschitz. Finite joint limiting laws are consistent because every union of finitely many calculations is another such calculation. They therefore define two countable random coordinate collections, one per layer. Let their probability spaces contain exactly the information generated by these coordinates.\\n\\nIdentify two first-layer probes whenever\\n\\\\[\\nv\\\\sim\\\\widetilde v\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability},\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\nand do the same separately on the second layer. The limiting mean-square distance is \\\\(\\\\mathbb E[(V-\\\\widetilde V)^2]\\\\), so these classes are precisely the corresponding population random variables, identified when equal almost surely. Define\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}[v]=[W_0^{(2)}v].\\n\\\\]\\nThe definition is independent of representatives because (11) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|W_0^{(2)}v-W_0^{(2)}\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\le M^2\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\]\\non an event of probability tending to one. Linearity passes from the finite matrix, as does\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\\\le M^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2].\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\nTaking all mean-square limits extends this action uniquely to every square-integrable variable on the first-layer probability space. The density needed here follows from the included bounded coordinate functions: finite-coordinate measurable functions approximate any variable on the generated space, and bounded continuous functions approximate them in mean square. The transpose action extends the same way in the reverse direction. Passing the exact finite identity\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\\\frac{[(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u]^\\\\top v}{n}\\n\\\\]\\nto population expectations shows that this reverse action is precisely the adjoint:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,W_0^{(2)}V]=\\\\mathbb E[((W_0^{(2)})^*U)V].\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\nArbitrary fixed real coefficients are obtained by rational approximation; the same stability estimates identify any finite computation using them with this population construction.\\n\\nOn these fixed spaces the population state is \\\\((X^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)})\\\\). Its derived variables and dynamics are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{gathered}\\nZ^{(1)}=F^{-1}(X^{(1)}),\\\\quad H^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(1)}),\\\\quad\\nZ^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H^{(1)},\\\\quad H^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(2)}),\\\\\\\\\\nf=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H^{(2)}],\\\\quad r=f-1,\\\\quad\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)}),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot X^{(1)}=-2r(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}=-2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}=-2rH^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{gathered} \\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\nThe rank-one action means\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)})V\\n=\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H^{(1)}V].\\n\\\\]\\nInitially \\\\(X_0^{(1)}=F(Z_0^{(1)})\\\\), the matrix action is (18), and \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\). Only three evolving objects are used; the population coordinate spaces themselves are infinite dimensional.\\n\\nWe next prove that (20) has a unique solution at every finite time and that Euler approximates it uniformly. The same estimates will apply at finite width. Measure two population states by\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\nAt finite width replace each variable's \\\\(L^2\\\\) norm by its Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\); call this distance \\\\(d_n\\\\). The operator norm is the largest amplification of root-mean-square size.\\n\\nBoth \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\circ F^{-1}\\\\) are 1-Lipschitz. The functions \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\) are bounded and Lipschitz. If both operator norms are at most \\\\(M\\\\) and both output weights are pointwise bounded by \\\\(B\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le (\\\\pi/2)\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n+M\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+B\\\\operatorname{Lip}(\\\\phi')\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\|(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}-(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n&\\\\le\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+M\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\nThe output difference obeys Cauchy–Schwarz with \\\\(|H^{(2)}|\\\\le\\\\pi/2\\\\), and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|a\\\\otimes b-\\\\widetilde a\\\\otimes\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n\\\\le\\\\|a-\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\\\|\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nHence the vector field is Lipschitz in (21) on these bounded sets, with a constant independent of width.\\n\\nExistence can be proved directly by iteration of the integral equations. On a sufficiently short time interval, the map \\\\(\\\\Theta\\\\mapsto\\\\Theta(0)+\\\\int_0^t\\\\dot\\\\Theta(\\\\Theta(s))\\\\,ds\\\\) preserves a closed set of continuous paths with the indicated bounds and contracts the supremum of (21). This set is complete: mean-square limits preserve a common pointwise bound on \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\). Taking the time interval shorter than the reciprocal of the Lipschitz constant proves existence and uniqueness.\\n\\nTo continue the solution, differentiation of the output, using (19), gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f=-2rK,\\\\quad \\\\dot L=-4r^2K,\\\\qquad\\nK=K^{(1)}+K^{(2)}+K^{(3)},\\n\\\\tag{23}\\n\\\\]\\nwhere\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nK^{(1)}&=\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[(\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)})^2\\\\right],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(2)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\delta^{(2)})^2],\\\\\\\\\\nK^{(3)}&=\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(2)})^2].\\n\\\\end{aligned} \\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\nThese formulas also follow by differentiating along square-integrable parameter directions: the gradients of \\\\(L\\\\) in \\\\(Z^{(1)},W^{(2)},W^{(3)}\\\\) are \\\\(2r\\\\delta^{(1)},2r\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},2rH^{(2)}\\\\). For matrix perturbations the relevant inner product is defined first on rank-one changes by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle a\\\\otimes b,c\\\\otimes d\\\\rangle\\n=\\\\mathbb E[ac]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[bd],\\n\\\\]\\nand extended by linearity and completion. This is the population version o\",\"truncated\":true,\"originalChars\":37279}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b3476fe3c87d2940ebaedce8d7e86\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9b34821f2087d2a469847ed49bf75c\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9b34858e5c87d2b3bbaa1eb26748c9\",\"text\":\"I completed two blind review rounds, with three fresh reviewers in each. The final three reported no remaining mathematical gaps. The [complete proof file](/tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/TWO_HIDDEN_LAYER_PROOF.md) and [review record](/tmp/l2-proof-review-wBzgFS/REVIEW.md) are saved.\\n\\nWe work at width \\\\(n\\\\), keeping the width dependence implicit in the network arrays. Every normalization by width is written explicitly.\\n\\nThere is one input, equal to \\\\(1\\\\), and target \\\\(1\\\\). The parameters are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz^{(1)}=W^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n,\\n\\\\qquad\\nW^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThroughout, \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) means the rescaled output weight. The network is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh^{(1)}=\\\\phi(z^{(1)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\nz^{(2)}=W^{(2)}h^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh^{(2)}=\\\\phi(z^{(2)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_n=\\\\frac{(W^{(3)})^\\\\top h^{(2)}}n,\\n\\\\qquad\\nr_n=f_n-1,\\n\\\\qquad\\nL_n=r_n^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll initial entries are independent, with\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,i}^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\\\sim N(0,1/n^2).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDefine the backpropagation derivatives without the loss residual:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n=\\nW^{(3)}\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\n\\\\odot\\n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus \\\\(\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}=n\\\\,\\\\partial f_n/\\\\partial z^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\). The exact GD updates are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n&=\\nz_k^{(1)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}\\\\delta_k^{(1)},\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n&=\\nW_k^{(2)}\\n-\\\\frac{2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}}n\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}(h_k^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n&=\\nW_k^{(3)}-2\\\\eta_n r_{n,k}h_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUse the clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), interpolate these three parameters linearly, and recompute the remaining quantities from them. Assume the positive learning rates satisfy\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIn particular, this includes the original choice \\\\(\\\\eta_n=n^{-2}\\\\).\\n\\nWe will construct an autonomous population gradient flow and prove convergence to it on every fixed interval \\\\([0,T]\\\\). The convergence will retain both directions of the learned matrix action, the hidden trajectory laws, predictions, losses, kernel blocks, and integrated squared hidden velocities. We will also verify that both hidden layers move and that the limiting kernel changes.\\n\\nCapital letters \\\\(Z^{(\\\\ell)},H^{(\\\\ell)},X^{(1)}\\\\) denote typical population coordinates. We retain \\\\(W^{(2)},W^{(3)},\\\\delta^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\) for their population counterparts, identifying explicitly when we pass to the population system. For a scalar population variable \\\\(V\\\\), write\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|V\\\\|_{L^2}=\\\\sqrt{\\\\mathbb E[V^2]}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is its root-mean-square size. The corresponding finite-vector size is always written as \\\\(\\\\|v\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\).\\n\\nFirst consider the finite-width continuous flow associated with (1). Define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+\\\\frac{s^3}{3},\\n\\\\qquad\\nx^{(1)}=F(z^{(1)}).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince \\\\(F'(s)\\\\phi'(s)=1\\\\), the flow becomes\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot x^{(1)}\\n&=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)},\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}\\n&=-\\\\frac{2r_n}{n}\\\\delta^{(2)}(h^{(1)})^\\\\top,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}\\n&=-2r_nh^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis coordinate change is exact in continuous time. Euler with an auxiliary mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) replaces each derivative in (2) by its forward difference. In particular,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n=\\nx_k^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTransforming the actual GD iterates produces an additional error, which we estimate later.\\n\\nIterating the Euler matrix update gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n-\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k}\\nr_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore every forward action can be written as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}v\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}v\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\nr_{n,s}\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{(h_s^{(1)})^\\\\top v}{n},\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand every backward action as\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(W_k^{(2)})^\\\\top u\\n=\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\nr_{n,s}h_s^{(1)}\\n\\\\frac{(\\\\delta_s^{(2)})^\\\\top u}{n}.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTraining therefore adds a finite sum of rank-one matrices. For fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\), there are only finitely many calls to the initial Gaussian matrix and finitely many scalar contractions.\\n\\nThe coefficients in (5)–(6) are random empirical quantities. We first analyze an auxiliary finite system—the oracle—that uses deterministic population residuals and contractions instead. Those numbers will be constructed from the population action below. We then prove that the oracle’s empirical quantities approach the supplied numbers and compare it with the actual Euler system.\\n\\nThe first matrix call is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditional on \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), its coordinates are independent centered Gaussians with variance \\\\(\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2/n\\\\). Thus\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1),\\n\\\\qquad\\nH_0^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z_0^{(1)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\sim\\nN\\\\!\\\\left(0,\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe finite vectors in the following first-call examples are oracle vectors. The oracle starts with \\\\(W_0^{(3)}=0\\\\), so \\\\(r_0=-1\\\\). Its first update leaves the hidden parameters unchanged and gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(3)}=2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\phi(z_0^{(2)})\\n\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_0^{(2)}).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe next transpose call uses the same matrix that produced \\\\(z_0^{(2)}\\\\). For a nonzero vector \\\\(b\\\\), define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_{b^\\\\perp}\\n=\\nI-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nCondition each Gaussian row of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) on its observed dot product with \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), then sum the rows with coefficients \\\\(\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)}\\\\). This gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}}\\n{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}\\nh_0^{(1)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(1)},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere the equality is conditional in distribution and \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the preceding history.\\n\\nThe first term supplies exactly the overlap required by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=\\n(z_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe new Gaussian part is perpendicular to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), so it cannot supply that overlap.\\n\\nThe next first-layer update is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_2^{(1)}\\n=\\nx_0^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_1(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_2^{(1)}=\\\\phi(F^{-1}(x_2^{(1)})),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nr_1\\n=\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi(Z_0^{(2)})^2]-1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nNow condition on both the previous forward and transpose observations. The next initial-matrix call satisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\n\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}}\\n{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\n[P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}]^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}\\n}{\\n\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2^2\\n}\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\|P_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}h_2^{(1)}\\\\|_2\\n}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere \\\\(\\\\gamma^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\\\) is independent of the previous history. The denominators are positive almost surely for \\\\(\\\\Delta>0\\\\).\\n\\nThe first response retains the old forward answer. The second supplies the remaining overlap required by the old transpose answer. The Gaussian term disturbs neither observation. Formula (5), with the oracle’s population coefficients, then adds the learned rank-one correction to obtain its preactivation node.\\n\\nWe now prove that this calculation can be repeated through any fixed finite sequence of the operations needed here.\\n\\nConsider a finite calculation whose initial coordinate tuples are iid with finite second moments and independent of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\). Its instructions are globally Lipschitz coordinate functions, linear combinations with fixed deterministic coefficients, and calls to \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) or its transpose. The initial tuples may include both \\\\(z_0^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(F(z_0^{(1)})\\\\), since the latter has a finite second moment.\\n\\nThe claim is that every same-layer joint empirical law has a deterministic limit, including its second moments.\\n\\nWe use the distance \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\): the smallest root-mean-square distance between random vectors with two specified laws. Matching corresponding coordinates gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\!\\\\left(\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{v_i},\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\delta_{\\\\widetilde v_i}\\n\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\\\|v_i-\\\\widetilde v_i\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere \\\\(\\\\delta_a\\\\) denotes a unit probability mass at \\\\(a\\\\). Convergence in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) means weak convergence together with convergence of second moments. It therefore includes convergence of all dot products within a fixed joint tuple.\\n\\nFirst, the initial matrix has a uniform operator bound. A \\\\(1/4\\\\)-net of the Euclidean unit sphere contains at most \\\\(9^n\\\\) points. Using two such nets and the Gaussian tail of each bilinear form gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\|W_0^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}>M\\\\right)\\n\\\\le\\n2\\\\,9^{2n}e^{-nM^2/8}.\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIndeed, the operator norm is at most twice the largest bilinear form on the two nets, and each such form is \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\). Choose a fixed sufficiently large \\\\(M\\\\).\\n\\nThe remaining difficulty is that previous query directions may become linearly dependent. To handle this without assuming stable inverses of singular Gram matrices, temporarily add \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) to the input of each matrix call, where each \\\\(\\\\xi\\\\) is a fresh independent standard Gaussian vector. Fix \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\); we will remove it after proving convergence.\\n\\nSuppose previous calls have revealed\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}V=Y,\\n\\\\qquad\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top U=R.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe columns of \\\\(V\\\\) are previous first-layer input vectors, and those of \\\\(U\\\\) are previous second-layer input vectors. The columns of \\\\(Y,R\\\\) are their answers. Their numbers of columns are fixed.\\n\\nConditioning on the roots and previous answers leaves\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d={}&\\nY(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\nU(U^\\\\top U)^{-1}R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\nP_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}P_{V^\\\\perp},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_{V^\\\\perp}=I-V(V^\\\\top V)^{-1}V^\\\\top,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand similarly for \\\\(U\\\\). Terms associated with empty column lists are zero.\\n\\nThe first two terms satisfy both recorded equations. The last term lies precisely in the matrix directions that preserve both equations. Orthogonal components of an isotropic Gaussian are independent, which proves the formula. It also applies to adaptive queries: each new input is determined before its answer is observed, using previous answers and independent roots.\\n\\nFor the next first-layer input \\\\(v\\\\), multiplication gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}v\\n\\\\overset d=\\nY\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n\\n+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\gamma,\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_n\\n=\\n(V^\\\\top V/n)^{-1}(V^\\\\top v/n),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\beta_n\\n=\\n(U^\\\\top U/n)^{-1}\\n(R^\\\\top P_{V^\\\\perp}v/n).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe transpose case exchanges the two layers.\\n\\nThe induction statement is joint \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence of every previously constructed tuple on each layer. Equivalently, its empirical averages converge for every continuous measurement \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\) satisfying\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\psi(s)|\\\\le C(1+\\\\|s\\\\|_2^2).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe choices \\\\(\\\\psi(s)=s^2\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\psi(s,t)=st\\\\) give the squared norms and contractions in (13). Revealing a new independent input noise also preserves this statement by conditional Gaussian averaging.\\n\\nThe perturbation ensures that the limiting input Gram matrices are invertible. When a new column \\\\(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi\\\\) is added to a previous input list,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}(a+\\\\varepsilon\\\\xi)\\\\|_2^2\\n}{n}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_{V^\\\\perp}a\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n+\\n\\\\varepsilon^2\\n+\\no_{\\\\mathbb P}(1).\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe cross term has conditional variance at most\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{4\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\|a\\\\|_2^2}{n^2},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the projection removes only a fixed number of Gaussian directions. Thus the new column has limiting squared distance at least \\\\(\\\\varepsilon^2\\\\) from the old span. Each enlarged limiting Gram matrix is consequently positive definite, and its inverse converges.\\n\\nTherefore the response coefficients and Gaussian variance in (13) converge. Also, writing \\\\(P_U=I-P_{U^\\\\perp}\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|P_U\\\\gamma\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\,\\\\middle|\\\\,\\n\\\\text{history}\\n\\\\right]\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\operatorname{rank}(U)}n\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWe may therefore remove the projection at a vanishing mean-square cost. Replacing the convergent coefficients by their limits also has vanishing mean-square cost.\\n\\nWhat remains is an old coordinate function plus independent scalar Gaussians. For a bounded Lipschitz measurement, its empirical average has conditional variance bounded by a constant divided by \\\\(n\\\\). Its conditional expectation is an empirical average of a function of the old coordinates, obtained by averaging over one scalar Gaussian; the previous induction hypothesis controls it.\\n\\nThe new squared norm also concentrates: its conditional variance is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nO\\\\!\\\\left(\\n\\\\frac{1+\\\\|Y\\\\alpha_n+U\\\\beta_n\\\\|_2^2/n}{n}\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus both the joint law and its second moments converge. Globally Lipschitz coordinate operations preserve this convergence, closing the induction for fixed \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\).\\n\\nFinally, couple the perturbed and unperturbed calculations using the same roots and matrix. On the event in (11), and the event that the finitely many \\\\(\\\\|\\\\xi\\\\|_2/\\\\sqrt n\\\\) are bounded, induction through the instructions gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{\\\\text{constructed }v}\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|v^\\\\varepsilon-v\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n\\\\le C\\\\varepsilon.\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEach coordinate map multiplies an error by its fixed Lipschitz constant; each matrix call multiplies it by at most \\\\(M\\\\), plus the newly injected error. The constant depends on the fixed calculation, but not on \\\\(n\\\\) or \\\\(0<\\\\varepsilon\\\\le1\\\\).\\n\\nBoth events have probability tending to one. By (10), the deterministic limiting laws for positive \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\) form a Cauchy family in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\). Its limit is also the limit of the unperturbed empirical laws: first take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\to0\\\\).\\n\\nThis proves the finite Gaussian statement, including dependent unperturbed query directions. No central limit theorem is needed.\\n\\nWe next turn these finite calculations into one population matrix action.\\n\\nTake a countable collection of calculations closed under rational linear combinations, initial-matrix calls in both directions, the Lipschitz maps just used, and a countable family of bounded Lipschitz functions of finite coordinate lists that is dense among continuous functions on compact sets. Include constants and clipping at every integer level.\\n\\nEvery product included in this calculation class must have both varying factors bounded—either intrinsically or by clipping—so that the complete coordinate function is globally Lipschitz. Products involving an unbounded backward field will be handled separately by truncation.\\n\\nThe finite joint limiting laws are consistent because any finite union of calculations is another finite calculation. They therefore define two countable random coordinate collections, one per layer. Take their probability spaces to contain precisely the information generated by those coordinates.\\n\\nIdentify two first-layer probes when\\n\\n\\\\[\\nv\\\\sim\\\\widetilde v\\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability},\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand do the same separately on the second layer. The limiting squared distance is \\\\(\\\\mathbb E[(V-\\\\widetilde V)^2]\\\\), so these classes correspond to population random variables identified when equal almost surely.\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}[v]:=[W_0^{(2)}v].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is independent of the representative because, on the event in (11),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\|W_0^{(2)}v-W_0^{(2)}\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2\\n}{n}\\n\\\\le\\nM^2\\\\frac{\\\\|v-\\\\widetilde v\\\\|_2^2}{n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLinearity passes directly from the finite matrix, as does the bound\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[(W_0^{(2)}V)^2]\\n\\\\le\\nM^2\\\\mathbb E[V^2].\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTaking mean-square limits extends the action uniquely to all square-integrable variables on the generated first-layer probability space. The included bounded coordinate functions give the required density: finite-coordinate functions approximate variables on the generated space, and bounded continuous functions approximate those in mean square.\\n\\nConstruct the reverse action from \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\) in the same way. At finite width,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{u^\\\\top W_0^{(2)}v}{n}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{[(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top u]^\\\\top v}{n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nPassing to the limit proves\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[U\\\\,W_0^{(2)}V]\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[((W_0^{(2)})^*U)V].\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus the reverse action is the adjoint of the same population operator. Arbitrary fixed real coefficients are obtained by rational approximation, using the preceding stability bounds.\\n\\nOn these fixed spaces, the population state consists of\\n\\n\\\\[\\nX^{(1)},\\\\qquad W^{(2)},\\\\qquad W^{(3)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIts derived quantities and dynamics are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{gathered}\\nZ^{(1)}=F^{-1}(X^{(1)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\nH^{(1)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(1)}),\\n\\\\\\\\\\nZ^{(2)}=W^{(2)}H^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nH^{(2)}=\\\\phi(Z^{(2)}),\\n\\\\\\\\\\nf=\\\\mathbb E[W^{(3)}H^{(2)}],\\n\\\\qquad\\nr=f-1,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}=W^{(3)}\\\\phi'(Z^{(2)}),\\n\\\\\\\\[2mm]\\n\\\\dot X^{(1)}\\n=\\n-2r(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)},\\n\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(2)}\\n=\\n-2r\\\\,\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},\\n\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot W^{(3)}\\n=\\n-2rH^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{gathered}\\n\\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe rank-one action means\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)})V\\n=\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\mathbb E[H^{(1)}V].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nInitially,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nX_0^{(1)}=F(Z_0^{(1)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW^{(2)}(0)=W_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW^{(3)}(0)=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThere are three evolving objects, but the spaces of population coordinates are infinite dimensional.\\n\\nWe now prove that this evolution exists uniquely for all finite times and that Euler approximates it uniformly. The same estimates will work at finite width.\\n\\nMeasure the distance between two population states by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nd={}&\\n\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n+\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAt finite width replace each variable’s \\\\(L^2\\\\) norm by its Euclidean norm divided by \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\); call the resulting distance \\\\(d_n\\\\).\\n\\nBoth \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\circ F^{-1}\\\\) are 1-Lipschitz. The functions \\\\(\\\\phi,\\\\phi'\\\\) are bounded and Lipschitz. If both operator norms are at most \\\\(M\\\\), and both output weights are pointwise bounded by \\\\(B\\\\), then\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le{}&\\n\\\\frac\\\\pi2\\n\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\nM\\\\|X^{(1)}-\\\\widetilde X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le{}&\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\nB\\\\operatorname{Lip}(\\\\phi')\\n\\\\|Z^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde Z^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n&\\n\\\\|(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n-(\\\\widetilde W^{(2)})^*\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&\\\\le\\n\\\\|W^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\nM\\\\|\\\\delta^{(2)}-\\\\widetilde\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nCauchy–Schwarz gives the corresponding output estimate. For the matrix update,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\|a\\\\otimes b-\\\\widetilde a\\\\otimes\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\le{}&\\n\\\\|a-\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\|\\\\widetilde a\\\\|_{L^2}\\\\|b-\\\\widetilde b\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTogether these make the vector field Lipschitz in (21) on the indicated bounded sets, with constants independent of width.\\n\\nFor local existence, replace a candidate path by the initial state plus the integral of the right-hand side of (20). On a sufficiently short interval this map preserves a closed set of continuous paths with the stated bounds and contracts the supremum of (21). The set is complete: mean-square limits preserve a common pointwise bound on \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\). Iteration therefore converges to a unique solution.\\n\\nTo continue the solution, differentiate the output using (19):\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f=-2rK,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot L=-4r^2K,\\n\\\\qquad\\nK=K^{(1)}+K^{(2)}+K^{(3)},\\n\\\\tag{23}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\n\\\\bigl(\\n\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigr)^2\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\,\\n\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\delta^{(2)})^2],\\n\\\\qquad\\nK^{(3)}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(2)})^2].\\n\\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese equations also verify the gradient-flow interpretation. In the untransformed population coordinates, the loss gradients are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n2r\\\\delta^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\n2r\\\\,\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\n2rH^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor matrix changes, the population counterpart of the ordinary sum-of-squared-entries geometry is defined on rank-one changes by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle a\\\\otimes b,c\\\\otimes d\\\\rangle\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[ac]\\\\,\\\\mathbb E[bd],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthen extended by linearity and completion. In particular, for a matrix-action perturbation \\\\(A\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},A\\\\rangle\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\,AH^{(1)}].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis verifies the matrix gradient. The change \\\\(W^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}\\\\) belongs to this completed space because it is the integral of rank-one changes of bounded norm.\\n\\nThe chain rules here hold along the constructed mean-square differentiable paths. Bounded scalar derivatives justify difference quotients first for bounded directions, then for general square-integrable directions by approximation.\\n\\nSince \\\\(K\\\\ge0\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|r(t)|\\\\le |r(0)|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently, on \\\\([0,T]\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}(0)\\\\|_\\\\infty+\\\\pi|r(0)|T,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\pi|r(0)|\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot X^{(1)}\\\\|_{L^2}\\n\\\\le\\n2|r(0)|\\\\|W^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\|W^{(3)}\\\\|_{L^2}.\\n\\\\tag{25}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese bounds prevent finite-time blow-up. At finite width, the same bounds hold on an event of probability tending to one, using (11), the initial law of large numbers for \\\\(F(z_0^{(1)})\\\\), and\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet \\\\(M_T\\\\) bound the vector field and let \\\\(L_T\\\\) be its Lipschitz constant on a slightly larger bounded set. The local Euler error satisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n&\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\n\\\\int_t^{t+\\\\Delta}\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\dot\\\\Theta(s)-\\\\dot\\\\Theta(t)\\\\bigr)\\\\,ds\\n\\\\right\\\\|\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&\\\\le\\n\\\\int_t^{t+\\\\Delta}L_TM_T(s-t)\\\\,ds\\n=\\n\\\\frac12L_TM_T\\\\Delta^2.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus the accumulated error obeys\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne_{k+1}\\n\\\\le\\n(1+L_T\\\\Delta)e_k+C_T\\\\Delta^2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand summing the geometric series gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{k\\\\Delta\\\\le T}e_k\\\\le C_T'\\\\Delta.\\n\\\\tag{26}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTo justify the bounded set for Euler, stop at its first exit. The estimate keeps \\\\(X^{(1)}\\\\) and \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) within their margins. The output supremum requires a separate observation:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|W_k^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_\\\\infty\\n+\\n\\\\pi\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}|r_s|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBefore the stop, (22) and (26) give\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|r_s|\\\\le |r(0)|+C_T\\\\Delta.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nChoose the output supremum margin larger than \\\\(\\\\pi T(|r(0)|+1)\\\\). For small \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), no first exit can occur. This proves (26), including between grid times, both for finite-width Euler and population Euler.\\n\\nWe can now define the oracle precisely. Add the subscript “oracle” to distinguish its finite arrays; this changes no normalization.\\n\\nFor fixed population Euler mesh \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), the numbers\\n\\n\\\\[\\nr_k=\\\\mathbb E[W_k^{(3)}H_k^{(2)}]-1,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}]\\n\\\\tag{27}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nare deterministic. Start the oracle from the same \\\\(z_0^{(1)},W_0^{(2)}\\\\) as the finite network and from \\\\(W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},0}^{(3)}=0\\\\). At each step set\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi(F^{-1}(x_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)})),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nz_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\n={}&\\nW_0^{(2)}h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&-\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\nr_s\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(2)}\\n\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}],\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{28}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi(z_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(3)}\\n\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand then\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nx_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k+1}^{(1)}\\n={}&x_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&-\\n2\\\\Delta r_k\\n\\\\Bigg[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&\\\\hspace{17mm}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\nr_sh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(1)}\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}]\\n\\\\Bigg],\\n\\\\\\\\\\nW_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k+1}^{(3)}\\n={}&\\nW_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(3)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_kh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{29}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is a fixed finite Gaussian calculation. Its output coordinates are bounded by the deterministic number\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\pi\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}|r_s|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe product defining \\\\(\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\\\) can therefore be replaced, without changing any value, by a globally Lipschitz map that clips its first argument above this bound. All remaining coordinate operations meet the finite Gaussian statement.\\n\\nIts limiting variables are exactly the population Euler variables: apply the definition of the population action instruction by instruction, together with the population versions of (5)–(6).\\n\\nRecord the oracle’s finite matrix memory as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n-\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{s<k}\\nr_s\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(2)}\\n(h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(1)})^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIts actual action differs from the oracle preactivation by the exact identity\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n&\\nW_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\nh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}\\n-\\nz_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&=\\n-2\\\\Delta\\\\sum_{s<k}\\nr_s\\\\delta_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(2)}\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\frac{\\n(h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(1)})^\\\\top\\nh_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n-\\n\\\\mathbb E[H_s^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}]\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{30}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe backward discrepancy has the same form, with \\\\(h_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},s}^{(1)}\\\\) multiplying the empirical-minus-population contraction of the two \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) vectors.\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\zeta_n\\\\) be the largest absolute error among these finitely many contractions, the output averages, and the squared normalized norms of the oracle vectors. The finite Gaussian result gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\zeta_n\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in probability}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIn particular, the oracle vector norms remain bounded.\\n\\nCompare the actual Euler state and the oracle memory through\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\ne_{n,k}\\n={}&\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\|x_k^{(1)}-x_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(1)}\\\\|_2\\n}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\|W_k^{(2)}-W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(2)}\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n+\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\|W_k^{(3)}-W_{\\\\mathrm{oracle},k}^{(3)}\\\\|_2\\n}{\\\\sqrt n}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEquations (22) and (30) bound the forward, backward, and residual differences by\\n\\n\\\\[\\nC_{T,\\\\Delta}(e_{n,k}+\\\\zeta_n).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSubtracting the updates gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne_{n,k+1}\\n\\\\le\\n(1+C_{T,\\\\Delta}\\\\Delta)e_{n,k}\\n+\\nC_{T,\\\\Delta}\\\\Delta\\\\zeta_n.\\n\\\\tag{31}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nInitially,\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne_{n,0}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|W_0^{(3)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\n=\\nO_{\\\\mathbb P}(n^{-1}).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince \\\\(N=\\\\lceil T/\\\\Delta\\\\rceil\\\\) is fixed, summing the recurrence proves\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{k\\\\le N}e_{n,k}\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis establishes the actual finite Euler width limit.\\n\\nThe argument also controls any fixed finite collection of additional forward and backward measurements. Expand each learned-matrix call by (5) or (6), construct its oracle version with the population contraction, and include the new empirical contraction errors in \\\\(\\\\zeta_n\\\\). There are finitely many.\\n\\nFor the continuity estimates, permit finite compositions of globally Lipschitz coordinate maps, \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\), linear combinations, and the current matrix and transpose actions. Both varying factors in a product must be bounded, intrinsically or by clipping. Induction through these operations, using (22), bounds each measurement difference by a constant times \\\\(d_n\\\\), or times \\\\(d\\\\) on the population spaces.\\n\\nChoose a countable collection of such measurements containing the coordinate functions used in the population construction. For each finite same-layer list, compare its joint laws in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\). Define the action-law distance by truncating each such distance at \\\\(1\\\\) and summing with weights \\\\(2^{-j}\\\\).\\n\\nFor any fixed list, the finite-flow versus population-flow comparison is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\underbrace{C_T\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{finite flow versus Euler}}\\n+\\n\\\\underbrace{o_{\\\\mathbb P,n\\\\to\\\\infty;\\\\,\\\\Delta}(1)}\\n_{\\\\text{finite Euler versus population Euler}}\\n+\\n\\\\underbrace{C_T\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{population Euler versus flow}}.\\n\\\\tag{32}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe constants here may depend on the fixed measurement list.\\n\\nChoose \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) small, then take \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). Uniform time continuity of each fixed measurement, supplied by the state bounds and (22), makes the convergence uniform on \\\\([0,T]\\\\). The summable weights extend it to the action-law distance.\\n\\nThe same reasoning applies jointly at finitely many different times: append all those measurements to one finite oracle calculation before taking the width limit.\\n\\nThe limiting evolution is also autonomous at the level of this measured law. Start the measurement collection at a current state and take all its mean-square limits on each layer. These spaces are preserved by the current action, its adjoint, and the coordinate operations.\\n\\nIf two current measured laws agree, sending each measurement to its counterpart preserves all squared norms and inner products. This extends to an invertible linear map preserving distances between the two generated spaces. It commutes with coordinate functions, intertwines the current matrix and adjoint, and sends each rank-one update to its counterpart.\\n\\nThe integral iteration proving existence stays within these generated spaces. Uniqueness therefore identifies the future laws of the two states. Restarting at time \\\\(s\\\\) and evolving for time \\\\(t\\\\) gives the same law as evolving for \\\\(s+t\\\\).\\n\\nWe now return to exact GD.\\n\\nFor this cubic calculation only, let\\n\\n\\\\[\\nb=-2r_n(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nbe the transformed first-layer velocity. A GD step updates \\\\(z^{(1)}\\\\) by \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\odot b\\\\). Since \\\\(F\\\\) is cubic,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n&\\nF\\\\!\\\\left(\\nz^{(1)}+\\\\eta_n\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\odot b\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&=\\nF(z^{(1)})+\\\\eta_nb\\n+\\n\\\\eta_n^2z^{(1)}\\n\\\\odot\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})^2\\\\odot b^2\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&\\\\quad+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\eta_n^3}{3}\\n\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})^3\\\\odot b^3.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{33}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOn the bounded state set,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|b\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\\\le C_T,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|b\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\le C_T\\\\sqrt n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAlso \\\\(\\\\sup_s|s|\\\\phi'(s)^2<\\\\infty\\\\). Consequently, the extra terms in (33) have root-mean-square size at most\\n\\n\\\\[\\nC_T(\\\\eta_n^2\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta_n^3n).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe other two updates are already Euler updates. Add the \\\\(O(\\\\eta_n^2)\\\\) local flow error and sum the recurrence:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\nd_n\\\\!\\\\left(\\n\\\\Theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t),\\n\\\\Theta_n^{\\\\mathrm{flow}}(t)\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\le\\nC_T\\\\left(\\n\\\\eta_n+\\\\eta_n\\\\sqrt n+\\\\eta_n^2n\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{34}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAs in the Euler proof, first stop on a larger bounded set. The output supremum is controlled separately by its sum of bounded activation updates, using the residual estimate transferred from the flow. This closes the stop.\\n\\nAt intermediate times, apply (33) with a partial step between \\\\(0\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\). Thus (34) holds for the specified interpolation of the original parameters.\\n\\nIt remains to justify quantities containing an unbounded backward coordinate. To keep the following tail calculation readable, temporarily write\\n\\n\\\\[\\np=(W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nP=(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nfor the finite and population backward fields.\\n\\nEquations (22), (32), and (34) show uniformly in time that the empirical joint law of \\\\((z^{(1)},p)\\\\) converges in \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) to the law of \\\\((Z^{(1)},P)\\\\). The population variables are continuous in mean square, so their laws on \\\\([0,T]\\\\) form a compact \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) family.\\n\\nTheir squared tails therefore vanish uniformly:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lim_{A\\\\to\\\\infty}\\\\limsup_{n\\\\to\\\\infty}\\n\\\\mathbb P\\\\!\\\\left(\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\np_i(t)^2\\\\mathbf1_{\\\\{|p_i(t)|>A\\\\}}\\n>\\n\\\\varepsilon\\n\\\\right)\\n=0.\\n\\\\tag{35}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere is the explicit estimate. For any coupling of \\\\(p,P\\\\), splitting according to \\\\(|P|\\\\le A/2\\\\) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[p^2\\\\mathbf1_{\\\\{|p|>A\\\\}}]\\n\\\\le\\n4\\\\mathbb E[(p-P)^2]\\n+\\n2\\\\mathbb E[P^2\\\\mathbf1_{\\\\{|P|>A/2\\\\}}].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nA finite cover of the compact population family makes the population tails uniformly small. Uniform \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) convergence then proves (35).\\n\\nClip \\\\(p\\\\) to \\\\([-A,A]\\\\). For fixed \\\\(A\\\\), both\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})\\\\operatorname{clip}_A(p),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\phi'(z^{(1)})^2\\\\operatorname{clip}_A(p)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nare globally Lipschitz measurements. Their squared errors from the unclipped fields are bounded by the tail in (35), since \\\\(|\\\\phi'|\\\\le1\\\\).\\n\\nApplying \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) multiplies the root-mean-square error by at most its operator bound. Thus, first taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(A\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), proves uniform convergence of all three kernel blocks in (24). The finite blocks are obtained by replacing each expectation with the corresponding normalized coordinate sum.\\n\\nThe same argument controls the squared velocities given by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot Z^{(1)}\\n=\\n-2r\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot Z^{(2)}\\n=\\n-2r\\\\Bigl\\\\{\\n&\\n\\\\mathbb E[(H^{(1)})^2]\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\nW^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigl[\\n\\\\phi'(Z^{(1)})^2\\n(W^{(2)})^*\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigr]\\n\\\\Bigr\\\\}.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{36}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nPredictions and losses converge uniformly directly from the bounded activations and output-weight bound.\\n\\nFor GD, the interpolated first-layer slope is its update velocity at the left endpoint. Differentiating\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz^{(2)}(t)=W^{(2)}(t)\\\\phi(z^{(1)}(t))\\n\\\\]\\n\\nuses the constant parameter slopes and the current interpolated activation.\\n\\nOn coordinates where \\\\(|p_i|\\\\le A\\\\), the change in \\\\(\\\\phi'(z_i^{(1)})\\\\) multiplied by the first-layer slope is bounded by \\\\(C_T\\\\eta_nA^2\\\\). On the complement, both terms are bounded by \\\\(C_T|p_i|\\\\). The other product-rule errors are \\\\(O(\\\\eta_n)\\\\) in root-mean-square norm, using the bounded matrix slopes.\\n\\nEquation (35), followed by \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) at fixed \\\\(A\\\\), then \\\\(A\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), therefore gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\int_0^T\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\|\\\\partial_tz_n^{(\\\\ell),\\\\mathrm{GD}}(t)\\\\|_2^2\\n}{n}\\\\,dt\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\int_0^T\\n\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\dot Z^{(\\\\ell)}(t))^2]\\\\,dt,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\ell=1,2.\\n\\\\tag{37}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll these convergence statements are in probability.\\n\\nFor the trajectory laws, use \\\\(\\\\mathcal W_2\\\\) with the supremum distance between continuous scalar paths. Let \\\\(I_\\\\pi z\\\\) be polygonal interpolation on a time partition of mesh at most \\\\(a\\\\). Absolute continuity and Cauchy–Schwarz give\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|z-I_\\\\pi z\\\\|_\\\\infty^2\\n\\\\le\\n4a\\\\int_0^T|\\\\dot z(t)|^2\\\\,dt.\\n\\\\tag{38}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAfter coordinate averaging, (37) bounds the empirical distance to this finite-grid representation by \\\\(O_{\\\\mathbb P}(\\\\sqrt a)\\\\). The population bound is identical. The population paths have absolutely continuous versions: integrate the mean-square continuous velocities and apply Fubini.\\n\\nAt a fixed partition, joint grid-law convergence, including second moments, follows from (32) and (34). Polygonal interpolation is a continuous linear function of the finite grid vector. Taking \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), then \\\\(a\\\\to0\\\\) in (38), proves separately for both layers that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\n\\\\delta_{z_{n,i}^{(\\\\ell),\\\\mathrm{GD}}(\\\\cdot)}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\operatorname{Law}(Z^{(\\\\ell)}(\\\\cdot))\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in }\\\\mathcal W_2(C([0,T])).\\n\\\\tag{39}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFinally, we verify that the limit exhibits feature learning. For this initial-time calculation, define the following fixed population quantities, each of which will recur:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nm=\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(1)})^2]>0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU=H_0^{(2)}\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(2)}),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\nu=\\\\mathbb E[U^2]>0,\\n\\\\qquad\\nP_0=(W_0^{(2)})^*U.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe row-conditioning calculation (8), without its \\\\(2\\\\Delta\\\\) factor, gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_0\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathbb E[Z_0^{(2)}U]}{m}H_0^{(1)}\\n+\\n\\\\sqrt\\\\nu\\\\,\\\\Gamma^{(1)},\\n\\\\tag{40}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\Gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1)\\\\) is independent of the first-layer root.\\n\\nDefine the initial motion coefficient and second-layer response\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\mathbb E[(\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(1)})P_0)^2]>0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS_0\\n=\\nmU\\n+\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigl[\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(1)})^2P_0\\\\bigr].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe coefficient \\\\(d\\\\) is positive because (40) has positive independent Gaussian variance and \\\\(\\\\phi'>0\\\\). Adjunction gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E[US_0]=m\\\\nu+d>0,\\n\\\\tag{41}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso \\\\(S_0\\\\) is nonzero in mean square.\\n\\nInitially \\\\(r=-1\\\\) and \\\\(W^{(3)}=0\\\\). Dividing the integral equations by successive powers of \\\\(t\\\\) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW^{(3)}(t)\\n=\\n2tH_0^{(2)}+O_{L^2}(t^2),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}(t)\\n=\\n2tU+O_{L^2}(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ^{(1)}(t)-Z_0^{(1)}\\n=\\n2t^2\\\\phi'(Z_0^{(1)})P_0\\n+\\no_{L^2}(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW^{(2)}(t)-W_0^{(2)}\\n=\\n2t^2U\\\\otimes H_0^{(1)}\\n+\\no_{\\\\mathrm{op}}(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ^{(2)}(t)-Z_0^{(2)}\\n=\\n2t^2S_0+o_{L^2}(t^2).\\n\\\\tag{42}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTo check these orders, (25) first gives \\\\(W^{(3)}=O(t)\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}=O(t)\\\\). Hence the changes in \\\\(X^{(1)},W^{(2)},Z^{(2)}\\\\) are \\\\(O(t^2)\\\\). Substitution into the output-weight equation and the definition of \\\\(\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\) gives the first line. Substitution once more gives the remaining lines. For instance,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\dot X^{(1)}(t)}t\\\\longrightarrow4P_0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the bounded derivative of \\\\(F^{-1}\\\\) gives the first preactivation expansion.\\n\\nPut \\\\(k_3=\\\\mathbb E[(H_0^{(2)})^2]>0\\\\). Equations (24), (41), and (42) yield\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(1)}(t)=4dt^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\qquad\\nK^{(2)}(t)=4m\\\\nu t^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK^{(3)}(t)\\n=\\nk_3+4(m\\\\nu+d)t^2+o(t^2),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK(t)\\n=\\nk_3+8(m\\\\nu+d)t^2+o(t^2).\\n\\\\tag{43}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe squared hidden displacements have leading terms\\n\\n\\\\[\\n4dt^4,\\n\\\\qquad\\n4\\\\mathbb E[S_0^2]t^4,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand their squared speeds have leading terms\\n\\n\\\\[\\n16dt^2,\\n\\\\qquad\\n16\\\\mathbb E[S_0^2]t^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBoth hidden layers therefore move, and every kernel block has a positive integral on sufficiently short nonzero intervals. Also,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf(t)=2k_3t+o(t),\\n\\\\qquad\\nL(t)=1-4k_3t+o(t).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBoth initial preactivation laws in (7) are nondegenerate Gaussians. Their variances remain positive by mean-square continuity.\\n\\nFor either layer, the error of the best affine fit to the activation is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(\\\\phi(Z^{(\\\\ell)}))\\n-\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\operatorname{Cov}(Z^{(\\\\ell)},\\\\phi(Z^{(\\\\ell)}))^2\\n}{\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(Z^{(\\\\ell)})\\n}.\\n\\\\tag{44}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt is initially positive: equality would force \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) to agree with an affine function on a Gaussian law with full support, and therefore everywhere by continuity. The moments in (44) are continuous under mean-square convergence because \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) is bounded and Lipschitz. This error remains positive for both layers on a common interval \\\\([0,T_*]\\\\).\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a05c40-9d17-79e0-b3ff-b011a2fc20ce\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1788254133,\"completedAt\":1788254759,\"durationMs\":625702,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a05c40-9d6d-7880-9375-8c865deaa16b\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"\\n# Files pasted by the user:\\n\\n## \\\"Yes. We should define \\\\\\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\\\\\) from the beginning to mean the rescaled outpu…\\\": /home/codex-b/.codex/attachments/52fe5b35-423f-4720-80a0-78c7ff153e30/pasted-text.txt\\n\\n## My request:\\nthe notes and explaination above is horrible to read in terms of symbols and notation and mathematical expressions, I've noted down what I desire to keep from here onwards, and I've attached the cleaned up notes for you to see what I find acceptable, please re-output the cleaned up version now, and read very very carefully my notes below so you execute them from here onwards\\n\\n\\\\# Notation and exposition specification\\n\\nUse these rules when rewriting or teaching the two-hidden-layer mean-field/gradient-flow proof.\\n\\n\\\\## Canonical network notation\\n\\nLet \\\\\\\\(k\\\\\\\\) denote time/Euler step, \\\\\\\\(\\\\ell\\\\\\\\) denote layer, and \\\\\\\\(i,j\\\\\\\\) denote neuron coordinates.\\n\\nUse\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nz\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{for preactivation},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}=\\\\phi\\\\\\\\!\\\\left(z\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\right)\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{for activation},\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nW\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{for the weights entering layer \\\\\\\\(\\\\ell\\\\\\\\)}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nAlways retain the layer superscript. Write\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nW\\\\_0^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\bigl(W\\\\_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top,\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nnever a bare \\\\\\\\(W\\\\\\\\).\\n\\nThe forward pass is\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nh\\\\_k^{(1)}=\\\\phi\\\\\\\\!\\\\left(z\\\\_k^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\qquad\\nz\\\\_k^{(2)}=W\\\\_k^{(2)}h\\\\_k^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh\\\\_k^{(2)}=\\\\phi\\\\\\\\!\\\\left(z\\\\_k^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nWrite \\\\\\\\(\\\\phi'(z)\\\\\\\\) explicitly. Do not replace it with an unnecessary function such as \\\\\\\\(q(z)\\\\\\\\).\\n\\n\\\\## Output-weight normalization\\n\\nDefine \\\\\\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\\\\\) from the beginning to mean the rescaled output weight used throughout the proof. Never introduce a raw output weight or barred notation.\\n\\nUse\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nf\\\\_{n,k}\\n\\\\=\\n\\\\frac1n\\n\\\\bigl(W\\\\_k^{(3)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h\\\\_k^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nr\\\\_{n,k}=f\\\\_{n,k}-1,\\n\\\\qquad\\nL\\\\_{n,k}=r\\\\_{n,k}^2.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nThe finite initialization is\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nW\\\\_{0,j}^{(3)}\\n\\\\sim N\\\\left(0,\\\\frac1{n^2}\\\\right),\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nwhile the population oracle and limiting system use\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nW\\\\_0^{(3)}=0.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nThis same \\\\\\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\\\\\) must be used consistently in the finite network, Euler scheme, oracle, empirical limits, and autonomous population flow.\\n\\n\\\\## Backpropagation notation\\n\\nUse the canonical preactivation derivative\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\delta\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\n:=\\nn\\\\frac{\\\\partial f\\\\_{n,k}}\\n&#x20;       {\\\\partial z\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nThus\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\delta\\\\_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\=\\nW\\\\_k^{(3)}\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\phi'\\\\\\\\!\\\\left(z\\\\_k^{(2)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\delta\\\\_k^{(1)}\\n\\\\=\\n\\\\phi'\\\\\\\\!\\\\left(z\\\\_k^{(1)}\\\\right)\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\bigl(W\\\\_k^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta\\\\_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nThe loss residual remains outside \\\\\\\\(\\\\delta\\\\\\\\):\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial L\\\\_{n,k}}\\n&#x20;    {\\\\partial z\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}}\\n\\\\=\\n\\\\frac{2r\\\\_{n,k}}{n}\\\\delta\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nWhen useful, express derivatives directly with partial notation:\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\delta\\\\_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\=\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\\\\\,\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial}{\\\\partial z\\\\_0^{(2)}}\\n\\\\frac12\\n\\\\left\\\\\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\\\\\!\\\\left(z\\\\_0^{(2)}\\\\right)\\\\right\\\\\\\\|\\\\_2^2.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nDo not introduce an auxiliary scalar name merely to differentiate it.\\n\\n\\\\## Avoid redundant aliases\\n\\nDo not rename existing network objects with generic symbols such as\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nv:=h\\\\_0^{(1)},\\\\qquad\\ny:=z\\\\_0^{(2)},\\\\qquad\\nu:=\\\\frac{\\\\delta\\\\_1^{(2)}}{2\\\\Delta},\\n\\\\qquad\\np:=\\\\bigl(W\\\\_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top u,\\n\\\\qquad\\na:=h\\\\_2^{(1)}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nWrite the canonical objects directly:\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nz\\\\_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\=\\nW\\\\_0^{(2)}h\\\\_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W\\\\_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta\\\\_1^{(2)},\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nW\\\\_0^{(2)}h\\\\_2^{(1)}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nIntroduce shorthand only when it recurs enough to provide genuine compression and has a clear mathematical meaning.\\n\\n\\\\## Ordinary linear-algebra notation\\n\\nDo not introduce special normalized notation such as\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\langle b,c\\\\rangle\\\\_n\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad\\n\\\\\\\\|b\\\\\\\\|\\\\_n.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nWrite the normalization explicitly:\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{b^\\\\top c}{n},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac{\\\\\\\\|b\\\\\\\\|\\\\_2^2}{n}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nUse \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\|\\\\cdot\\\\\\\\|\\\\_2\\\\\\\\) only for the ordinary Euclidean norm.\\n\\nOrthogonal projection may be used because it genuinely compresses the conditioning formulas, but define it:\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nP\\\\_{b^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\=\\nI-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nUse \\\\\\\\((W^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\\\\\) for the finite matrix transpose and \\\\\\\\((W^{(2)})^\\\\*\\\\\\\\) for the corresponding population adjoint action.\\n\\n\\\\## Exact finite dynamics\\n\\nWith\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+\\\\frac{s^3}{3},\\n\\\\qquad\\nx\\\\_k^{(1)}=F\\\\\\\\!\\\\left(z\\\\_k^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nwrite the Euler updates as\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nx\\\\_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n\\\\=\\nx\\\\_k^{(1)}\\n\\\\-\\n2\\\\Delta r\\\\_{n,k}\\n\\\\bigl(W\\\\_k^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta\\\\_k^{(2)},\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nW\\\\_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n\\\\=\\nW\\\\_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\-\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta r\\\\_{n,k}}{n}\\n\\\\delta\\\\_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigl(h\\\\_k^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top,\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nW\\\\_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n\\\\=\\nW\\\\_k^{(3)}\\n\\\\-\\n2\\\\Delta r\\\\_{n,k}h\\\\_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nEvery factor of \\\\\\\\(n\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(2\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(r\\\\_{n,k}\\\\\\\\) should remain visible.\\n\\n\\\\## Population notation\\n\\nUse lowercase arrays for finite-width vectors and capitalized versions for typical population coordinates:\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nz\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\n\\\\longleftrightarrow\\nZ\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\n\\\\longleftrightarrow\\nH\\\\_k^{(\\\\ell)}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nThe population output is\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nf\\\\_k\\n\\\\=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nW\\\\_k^{(3)}H\\\\_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nThe limiting state uses the same symbols\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\nX^{(1)},\\\\qquad W^{(2)},\\\\qquad W^{(3)}\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nas the finite theory, with \\\\\\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\\\\\) now an action between the two population coordinate spaces.\\n\\n\\\\## Exposition order\\n\\nMake every explanation self-contained. Introduce, in order:\\n\\n1\\\\. the network objects and their dimensions;\\n2\\\\. the forward pass, output, residual, and loss;\\n3\\\\. initialization and scaling;\\n4\\\\. backpropagation derivatives;\\n5\\\\. exact Euler updates;\\n6\\\\. the oracle and what it is supplied;\\n7\\\\. the first forward call;\\n8\\\\. the first reused transpose call;\\n9\\\\. the next reused forward call;\\n10\\\\. only then the abstract induction hypothesis.\\n\\nBuild the abstract argument from concrete network operations rather than beginning with a generic tensor-program theorem.\\n\\n\\\\## Meaning before abstraction\\n\\nExplain the reused-matrix mechanism concretely as\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\text{new matrix result}\\n\\\\=\\n\\\\text{response forced by all previous matrix uses}\\n\\\\+\\n\\\\text{Gaussian randomness in unexplored directions}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nExplain why each response term is forced using exact identities such as\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(h\\\\_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\bigl(W\\\\_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta\\\\_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\=\\n\\\\bigl(z\\\\_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta\\\\_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nOnly after concrete examples introduce the general empirical statement\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum\\\\_i\\n\\\\psi(\\\\text{previous coordinates})\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\\\\\,\\n\\\\psi(\\\\text{population coordinates}).\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nExplain \\\\\\\\(\\\\psi\\\\\\\\) as an arbitrary measurement function and give tangible choices:\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\psi(s)=s^2\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nfor moments and\\n\\n\\\\\\\\[\\n\\\\psi(s,t)=st\\n\\\\\\\\]\\n\\nfor empirical contractions.\\n\\n\\\\## Conceptual distinctions to preserve\\n\\nDo not describe the finite coordinates as iid after matrix reuse. Say that their joint empirical averages converge to the population expectations.\\n\\nKeep first-layer and second-layer neuron populations separate. The indices \\\\\\\\(i\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(j\\\\\\\\) do not create a natural coordinatewise pairing across layers.\\n\\nDistinguish:\\n\\n\\\\- deterministic population residuals and contractions supplied to the oracle;\\n\\\\- empirical convergence proved for the oracle vectors;\\n\\\\- the later stability argument transferring the result to the actual empirical-feedback system.\\n\\nDo not call the argument a CLT. It proves unscaled empirical errors vanish, not their \\\\\\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\\\\\)-scaled fluctuations.\\n\\nDo not impose unnecessary sub-Gaussian assumptions. State the actual requirement: sufficiently high polynomial moments and stable Gram-matrix ranks.\\n\\n\\\\## Writing style\\n\\nUse a cohesive narrative with few headings. Avoid fragmenting one argument into many apparently unrelated sections.\\n\\nUse plain mathematical language and explain unfamiliar concepts through elementary linear algebra and probability.\\n\\nDerive central equations rather than paraphrasing them. State what each term does and why it is present.\\n\\nAvoid unnecessary jargon, undefined abstractions, redundant notation, and phrases such as “clearly” that conceal an argument.\\n\\nDo not use boxed equations. Use display equations only when they materially improve readability.\\n\\nKeep the explanation mathematically deep but compact: concrete first, abstract second, and no extra symbols unless they genuinely reduce the reader’s work.\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9697b7e1d487d2a937dd9384fb5570\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing to read and re-output cleaned notes**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9697be3b0087d2b4895c4d1e6b98bb\",\"text\":\"I’m using the technical-math teaching skill again because this is a source-faithful notation rewrite. I’ll treat your specification as binding, read the cleaned notes in full, and re-output that version without reintroducing aliases, normalized inner-product notation, barred output weights, or bare \\\\(W\\\\).\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-8bd8562d-4f54-47dd-90b5-80d4c6162d6e\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' /etc/codex/skills/teach-technical-math/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: teach-technical-math\\ndescription: Teach the mathematical content of technical documents—papers, reports, notes, appendices, and linked revisions—through rigorous, self-contained, one-concept-at-a-time lessons. Use when the user supplies or references one or more sources and wants to understand their definitions, equations, derivations, proofs, models, limiting constructions, evidence, assumptions, contradictions across versions, or open gaps over multiple turns; also use when they say “next block” or ask to rebuild a confusing part of such a lesson. Reconcile source versions before teaching, preserve stable typed notation, derive rather than paraphrase, and distinguish exact results from formal, heuristic, empirical, conjectural, and open claims.\\n---\\n\\n# Teach Mathematics from Technical Documents\\n\\nAct as a mathematical and theoretical teacher, not a summarizer. Teach a motivated non-specialist at genuine mathematical depth. Expose the simple structure beneath advanced formalism without suppressing difficult steps.\\n\\n## Prepare from the sources\\n\\nRead every source the user identifies in full mathematical detail before teaching source-dependent claims. If a source is inaccessible, incomplete, or missing cited material, state the limitation and do not imply that it was read.\\n\\nBuild an internal source ledger:\\n\\n- record each source's identity, date, and status, such as preliminary, revised, audited, or final;\\n- map notation and assumption changes across versions;\\n- identify the precise objects, problem, model, regime, observables, main claims, constructions, arguments, evidence, and unresolved gaps;\\n- separate compatible claims from incompatible ones;\\n- determine which claims survived later corrections or audits;\\n- retain theorem, equation, section, and page locations when available.\\n\\nDo not dump this ledger as an opening summary. Use it to teach the requested block faithfully. Attribute source claims and distinguish them from your own interpretation.\\n\\nMaintain an internal lesson ledger across turns: stable notation, completed blocks, the last point the user clearly understood, unresolved confusion, and the next logical block. Minimally recall any earlier definition needed in the current response.\\n\\n## Control the pace\\n\\nTeach exactly one natural conceptual block per response. If the user specifies a starting point, begin there. Otherwise begin with the document's objects, assumptions, and concrete setup.\\n\\nUse this domain-neutral progression as a guide:\\n\\n1. objects, assumptions, and setup;\\n2. exact concrete or finite formulation;\\n3. meaning and type of the central variables or maps;\\n4. derivation of the main relations;\\n5. proof or mechanism architecture;\\n6. obstruction, non-closure, or source of difficulty;\\n7. proposed abstraction, approximation, or limiting construction;\\n8. assumptions connecting the construction to the original problem;\\n9. theoretical and empirical evidence;\\n10. remaining gaps and falsifying cases.\\n\\nAdjust the sequence to the sources. Do not jump ahead merely because later material is related. When the user says “next block,” continue with only the next logically necessary idea. When the user is confused, stop advancing and rebuild from the last understood point.\\n\\nWrite each ordinary lesson turn as a compact, seamless blackboard explanation: a few short paragraphs, only the equations needed now, few or no bullets, and normally no headings. Do not begin or end with a broad recap. Stop naturally after the current block and wait for the user before moving deeper.\\n\\n## Construct each block\\n\\nUse this internal order without displaying it as a template:\\n\\n1. **Minimal recall:** restate only the definitions needed now.\\n2. **New object:** define one new object and give its mathematical type.\\n3. **Derivation:** obtain its relation from the preceding setup.\\n4. **Meaning:** explain the role of every term or condition.\\n5. **Analogue or example:** use one structurally faithful simple case when it genuinely helps, then return to the exact mathematics.\\n6. **Role:** connect the object to the document's argument.\\n7. **Status:** state what is established and what remains unproved.\\n\\nMake the block self-contained for its present conceptual burden, not for the entire project.\\n\\n## Enforce notation and type discipline\\n\\nUse a small, stable notation. Prefer the source's symbols unless they collide or obscure meaning. If renaming is necessary, state the mapping explicitly.\\n\\nDefine every symbol before using it. For each object, state as relevant:\\n\\n- its domain, codomain, or ambient space;\\n- whether it is a scalar, vector, matrix, function, operator, random variable, measure, index, or coordinate;\\n- whether it is fixed, sampled, or evolving;\\n- what each index means;\\n- its dependence on other variables or parameters.\\n\\nDistinguish carefully between:\\n\\n- one function of an entire vector and a vector of coordinatewise functions;\\n- an index and a coordinate;\\n- a fixed label or parameter and an evolving state;\\n- a representative element and a probability law over elements;\\n- a mathematical state and a numerical representation of that state.\\n\\nWhen writing an integral, identify the integration variable, its space, the measure, and what the integral averages or accumulates. When a distribution is specified only at initialization, do not imply that an evolved quantity retains that distribution merely because it remains a function of the initial label.\\n\\nDo not introduce shorthand unless the explicit expression is genuinely cumbersome and recurs often. Never reuse one symbol for two roles.\\n\\n## Derive rather than paraphrase\\n\\nFor every central equation or implication:\\n\\n1. state the definition or prior relation from which it follows;\\n2. show the relevant algebraic, differential, variational, probabilistic, logical, or limiting step;\\n3. identify the origin and action of every term;\\n4. explain the closest elementary analogue when useful;\\n5. state why the relation matters to the main result.\\n\\nDo not make the user reverse-engineer a displayed formula. Avoid phrases such as “clearly,” “standard,” or “it follows” when they conceal a nontrivial step.\\n\\nWhen invoking an external theorem, state the exact form needed, list its hypotheses, verify them in the present setting, and explain the resulting conclusion. Check a primary or otherwise authoritative source before relying on a recent, specialized, or uncertain result.\\n\\nWhen teaching a long proof, devote one block to its architecture before teaching the difficult steps. Explain how the claims, cases, or lemmas fit together without pretending that the architecture is itself the proof.\\n\\nUse analogies only when they preserve the relevant mathematical structure. State where the analogy stops and return immediately to the exact object.\\n\\n## Relate levels of description\\n\\nBegin with the document's concrete objects and exact relations. Introduce an abstract, aggregate, continuum, asymptotic, or numerical description only after showing how it arises from the concrete one.\\n\\nWhenever the source changes levels, identify:\\n\\n- the map from the original objects to the new description;\\n- what information is retained and discarded;\\n- which step is an exact identity;\\n- which step uses a theorem and its assumptions;\\n- which step is formal, heuristic, empirical, or conjectural.\\n\\nIf a proposed state or summary evolves autonomously, distinguish internal closure from fidelity to the original system. An autonomous reduced description may still omit information that changes the original system's future.\\n\\nIf the source uses truncation or approximation, distinguish pointwise or static approximation from the stronger control required by an evolving or iterative system. Examine separately:\\n\\n- error that leaves the retained representation;\\n- feedback from omitted components;\\n- amplification or accumulation of error;\\n- the time, parameter, or regime over which stability is claimed.\\n\\nWhen the source calls a construction “finite,” state precisely whether this means finitely many coordinates, finitely many fields, a finite-dimensional state, a finite cutoff, or merely a finite numerical discretization.\\n\\n## Label epistemic status\\n\\nClassify each important source-dependent claim using the most accurate category:\\n\\n- exact identity;\\n- proved theorem under stated assumptions;\\n- rigorous result for a restricted or finite setting;\\n- formal argument;\\n- heuristic mechanism;\\n- empirical or numerical evidence;\\n- conjectural identification;\\n- open implication;\\n- counterexample or negative result.\\n\\nDo not silently promote a claim. Express each important assumption as a missing implication:\\n\\n1. what is assumed;\\n2. what conclusion it would justify;\\n3. what connecting step is not established;\\n4. what observation or counterexample would refute it.\\n\\nInternal consistency of a proposed theory does not prove that it represents the target object.\\n\\n## Evaluate evidence and alternatives\\n\\nFor every numerical comparison, identify:\\n\\n- the systems or quantities compared;\\n- what the reference actually represents;\\n- the metric or norm;\\n- the scale of the predicted effect and the discrepancy;\\n- discretization, optimization, rounding, and sampling uncertainty as applicable;\\n- the alternative explanation ruled out;\\n- what the experiment leaves unresolved.\\n\\nDo not infer convergence or a scaling law from too few resolutions. Treat numerical thresholds as decision conventions unless the source proves a mathematical boundary.\\n\\nWhen the user requests a hostile or devil's-advocate analysis, include only alternatives that engage the declared target and make a distinct mathematical or quantitative prediction. Do not infer global behavior from a local or instantaneous property without proving the connecting step. Do not use possible error cancellation as a complete explanation unless it predicts the observed pattern.\\n\\n## Repair confusion completely\\n\\nWhen the user identifies an undefined symbol, ambiguity, missing derivation, or conceptual gap:\\n\\n- acknowledge the failure directly;\\n- do not defend the prior wording;\\n- do not append a one-sentence patch;\\n- rewrite the entire relevant block self-containedly;\\n- preserve compactness and stable notation.\\n\\nTreat confusion as evidence that the explanation needs rebuilding, not that the mathematics is inherently inaccessible.\\n\\nBefore sending each block, verify silently that it is faithful to the sources, introduces only one conceptual burden, defines every needed symbol, derives rather than paraphrases, states the status of important claims, stays in the user's language, avoids unnecessary notation and formula dumps, and is compact enough to absorb in one sitting.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-614775e8-7263-4d68-ade5-264f66610f9e\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,320p' /home/codex-b/.codex/attachments/52fe5b35-423f-4720-80a0-78c7ff153e30/pasted-text.txt\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"Yes. We should define \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) from the beginning to mean the rescaled output weight used by the proof. There is then no raw output-weight symbol and no bar notation anywhere.\\n\\nThe factor \\\\(1/n\\\\) in the output and the initialization and update of \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) are adjusted accordingly.\\n\\n## Complete finite-width setup\\n\\nFor width \\\\(n\\\\), the evolving parameters are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_k^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n,\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_k^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_k^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere \\\\(W_k^{(3)}\\\\) always denotes the rescaled output weight. It is the only output-weight variable used anywhere below.\\n\\nThe forward pass is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_k^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_k^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(2)}h_k^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_k^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(2)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,k}\\n=\\n\\\\frac1n\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(3)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s\\n\\\\]\\n\\nacts coordinatewise.\\n\\nThe target is \\\\(1\\\\), so\\n\\n\\\\[\\nr_{n,k}=f_{n,k}-1,\\n\\\\qquad\\nL_{n,k}=r_{n,k}^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe initialization is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,i}^{(1)}\\n\\\\overset{\\\\mathrm{iid}}{\\\\sim}N(0,1),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset{\\\\mathrm{iid}}{\\\\sim}\\nN\\\\left(0,\\\\frac1n\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\n\\\\overset{\\\\mathrm{iid}}{\\\\sim}\\nN\\\\left(0,\\\\frac1{n^2}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith all initial quantities independent.\\n\\nThus \\\\(W_0^{(3)}\\\\) is already written in its rescaled coordinates. Its coordinates vanish as \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), so the population oracle sets\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(3)}=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe difference between the small random finite initialization and zero is handled later by the stability comparison.\\n\\nDefine the residual-free backpropagation derivatives by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\n:=\\nn\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_{n,k}}\\n        {\\\\partial z_k^{(\\\\ell)}}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor the second hidden layer,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBackpropagating through \\\\(W_k^{(2)}\\\\) and then through the first activation gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_k^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(1)}\\\\right)\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe corresponding loss derivatives are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial L_{n,k}}\\n     {\\\\partial z_k^{(\\\\ell)}}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{2r_{n,k}}{n}\\\\delta_k^{(\\\\ell)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor \\\\(\\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s\\\\), define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+\\\\frac{s^3}{3},\\n\\\\qquad\\nx_k^{(1)}\\n=\\nF\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(1)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF'(s)=1+s^2=\\\\frac1{\\\\phi'(s)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe first-layer activation derivative cancels in the evolution of \\\\(x_k^{(1)}\\\\).\\n\\nWith Euler step \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), the exact induced updates in the rescaled coordinates are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n=\\nx_k^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(2)}\\n-\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}}{n}\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigl(h_k^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}h_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe last equation is already the update for the rescaled output weights \\\\(W_k^{(3)}\\\\). No conversion to or from another output-weight variable is needed.\\n\\nThe population oracle is analyzed for fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\). For a fixed time horizon \\\\(T\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nN=\\\\left\\\\lceil\\\\frac T\\\\Delta\\\\right\\\\rceil\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis fixed while \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). Therefore only finitely many forward and transpose calls must be controlled.\\n\\n## The first forward call\\n\\nAt initialization,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_0^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditional on \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,j}^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\sum_{i=1}^n\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}h_{0,i}^{(1)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis Gaussian with variance\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}\\n\\\\left(\\nz_{0,j}^{(2)}\\n\\\\mid h_0^{(1)}\\n\\\\right)\\n=\\n\\\\frac1n\\n\\\\sum_{i=1}^n\\n\\\\left(h_{0,i}^{(1)}\\\\right)^2\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}{n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDifferent second-layer coordinates \\\\(j\\\\) use different rows of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\), so they are conditionally independent.\\n\\nThe initial empirical law gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(H_0^{(1)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore the coordinates of \\\\(z_0^{(2)}\\\\) behave empirically like\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\sim\\nN\\\\left(\\n0,\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(H_0^{(1)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe second-layer activation is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_0^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is a coordinatewise function and introduces no new matrix randomness.\\n\\nIn the oracle,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(3)}=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_0=0,\\n\\\\qquad\\nr_0=-1,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_0^{(2)}=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first update gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_1^{(1)}=x_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(3)}\\n=\\n2\\\\Delta h_0^{(2)}\\n=\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt follows that\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_1^{(1)}=h_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nz_1^{(2)}=z_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_1^{(2)}=h_0^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe next second-layer backpropagation derivative is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n&=\\nW_1^{(3)}\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(z_1^{(2)}\\\\right)\\\\\\\\\\n&=\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9697c3d71087d2b4d04c8e5bbe1566\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-03f50f1f-ff82-4d07-90fc-cd009d0c425a\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '321,760p' /home/codex-b/.codex/attachments/52fe5b35-423f-4720-80a0-78c7ff153e30/pasted-text.txt\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"2\\\\Delta\\\\,\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(2)}\\\\right)\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEquivalently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\,\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial}{\\\\partial z_0^{(2)}}\\n\\\\frac12\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(2)}\\\\right)\\n\\\\right\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe population residual used by the oracle at this step is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nr_1\\n=\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\,\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(Z_0^{(2)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n-1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n## The first transpose call\\n\\nThe first nontrivial transpose operation is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is not multiplication by an independent Gaussian matrix because\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\ndepends on\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor any nonzero vector \\\\(b\\\\), define the ordinary orthogonal projection\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_{b^\\\\perp}\\n=\\nI-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor row \\\\(j\\\\) of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\), the previous forward call supplies the constraint\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,j:}^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_{0,j}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditioning the Gaussian row on this equation gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left(W_{0,j:}^{(2)}\\\\right)^\\\\top\\n\\\\overset d=\\n\\\\frac{\\nz_{0,j}^{(2)}\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}\\nh_0^{(1)}\\n+\\n\\\\widetilde W_{0,j:}^{(2)\\\\top},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere, conditionally,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\widetilde W_{0,j:}^{(2)\\\\top}\\n\\\\sim\\nN\\\\left(\\n0,\\n\\\\frac1nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nMultiplying row \\\\(j\\\\) by \\\\(\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)}\\\\) and summing gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(z_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}\\nh_0^{(1)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\gamma^{(1)},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis independent of the previously constructed vectors.\\n\\nThe first term is the response forced by the old forward call:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(z_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}\\nh_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe second term is the genuinely new Gaussian part:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\gamma^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe response is necessary because of the exact transpose identity\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\bigl(z_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe new Gaussian term is perpendicular to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), so it contributes zero to the left-hand side. The response term supplies the required overlap exactly.\\n\\nThe induction already knows\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(H_0^{(1)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(z_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore the corresponding population adjoint action is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^*\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n={}&\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]\\n}{\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(H_0^{(1)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n}\\nH_0^{(1)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\sqrt{\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n}\\n\\\\,\\\\Gamma^{(1)},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis independent of the previous first-layer population coordinates.\\n\\nThe finite coordinates of\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nare not claimed to be iid. The conclusion is that their joint empirical averages with all previous first-layer coordinates approach the corresponding population expectations.\\n\\n## The next forward call\\n\\nThe first-layer transformed coordinate now moves according to\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_2^{(1)}\\n=\\nx_1^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_1\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_2^{(1)}\\n=\\nF^{-1}\\\\!\\\\left(x_2^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_2^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_2^{(1)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEach coordinate of \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) is a deterministic scalar function of previously controlled first-layer coordinates. Therefore its joint empirical behavior follows from the existing induction hypothesis.\\n\\nThe next difficult operation is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt is not an independent Gaussian multiplication because \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) depends on\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAt this point the matrix history contains both\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditioning \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) simultaneously on both observations gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\left[\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]^\\\\top\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}\\\\) is a fresh independent Gaussian matrix with entries \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\).\\n\\nThe first term retains\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_0^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe second retains\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe final term is invisible to both previously inspected matrix directions.\\n\\nMultiplying by \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\n\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\left[\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]^\\\\top\\nh_2^{(1)}\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\nh_2^{(1)}\\n\\\\right\\\\|_2\\n}{\\n\\\\sqrt n\\n}\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\gamma^{(2)},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\gamma^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis fresh and independent of the previous history.\\n\\nThe first response term comes from the component of \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) parallel to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\). Since\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthat component must be mapped to the corresponding multiple of \\\\(z_0^{(2)}\\\\).\\n\\nThe second response term is forced by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\left[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]^\\\\top\\nh_2^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first response term supplies part of the required overlap. The second response term supplies the remainder.\\n\\nThe fresh Gaussian term is perpendicular to \\\\(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\), so it does not disturb this exact constraint.\\n\\nEvery coefficient in this formula is an empirical contraction of previously controlled vectors. For example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nH_0^{(1)}H_2^{(1)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9697c6d42c87d2b70b58c1c3aa0214\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-835cadcf-f044-43c5-ae36-ba4aa1d22090\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '761,1200p' /home/codex-b/.codex/attachments/52fe5b35-423f-4720-80a0-78c7ff153e30/pasted-text.txt\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore all response coefficients and Gaussian variances converge to their population values.\\n\\n## The exact induction hypothesis\\n\\nThe first-layer collection now contains quantities such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nx_2^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_2^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor every fixed polynomially bounded function \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\), the induction asserts\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_{i=1}^n\\n\\\\psi\\\\Bigl(\\n&z_{0,i}^{(1)},\\nh_{0,i}^{(1)},\\n\\\\left[\\n(W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]_i,\\nx_{2,i}^{(1)},\\nh_{2,i}^{(1)},\\n\\\\ldots\\n\\\\Bigr)\\n\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\;\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\,\\\\psi\\\\Bigl(\\n&Z_0^{(1)},\\nH_0^{(1)},\\n(W_0^{(2)})^*\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\nX_2^{(1)},\\nH_2^{(1)},\\n\\\\ldots\\n\\\\Bigr).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe second-layer collection contains quantities such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_1^{(3)},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIts empirical statement is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_{j=1}^n\\n\\\\psi\\\\Bigl(\\n&z_{0,j}^{(2)},\\nh_{0,j}^{(2)},\\nW_{1,j}^{(3)},\\n\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)},\\n\\\\left[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\n\\\\right]_j,\\n\\\\ldots\\n\\\\Bigr)\\n\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\;\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\,\\\\psi\\\\Bigl(\\n&Z_0^{(2)},\\nH_0^{(2)},\\nW_1^{(3)},\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\nW_0^{(2)}H_2^{(1)},\\n\\\\ldots\\n\\\\Bigr).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first-layer index \\\\(i\\\\) and second-layer index \\\\(j\\\\) label different neuron populations. There is no artificial coordinatewise pairing between them.\\n\\nPolynomially bounded means that, for some fixed constants \\\\(C\\\\) and \\\\(q\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\psi(s)|\\n\\\\le\\nC\\\\left(1+\\\\|s\\\\|_2^q\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTaking\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\psi(s)=s^2\\n\\\\]\\n\\ngives empirical second-moment convergence.\\n\\nTaking\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\psi(s,t)=st\\n\\\\]\\n\\ngives contractions such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_r^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nH_r^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(3)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nW_k^{(3)}H_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe last convergence is exactly the convergence of the oracle prediction.\\n\\nHigher powers provide the moment control needed to prevent a small number of unusually large coordinates from dominating these averages.\\n\\nThe induction also maintains stable linear relations among the vectors previously supplied to \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) and \\\\((W_0^{(2)})^\\\\top\\\\).\\n\\nThis is necessary because simultaneous Gaussian conditioning produces finite Gram matrices with entries such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_r^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_s^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\n}{n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIf a limiting Gram matrix is singular, entrywise convergence alone does not guarantee convergence of its inverse or pseudoinverse. The proof must distinguish a genuinely new direction from a direction that is already represented by previous ones.\\n\\nFor example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_1^{(1)}=h_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_1^{(1)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nexactly. This repeated source direction creates no fresh Gaussian component.\\n\\nMore generally, the induction proves:\\n\\n- a genuinely new source direction has a positive limiting distance from the old source span and therefore a positive Gaussian innovation variance;\\n- a direction with zero limiting distance is eventually an exact combination of the retained core directions;\\n- the relevant Gram ranks and pseudoinverses therefore stabilize.\\n\\nThus the induction maintains both joint empirical-moment convergence and stable linear relations.\\n\\nCoordinatewise operations are easy to add. For example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_kh_k^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(2)}\\\\right)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nare coordinatewise functions of already controlled second-layer vectors, with deterministic oracle coefficient \\\\(r_k\\\\). They introduce no new matrix randomness.\\n\\nThe difficult operation is a new call to\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditioning on every previous forward and transpose call gives a finite response made from old network vectors plus Gaussian randomness projected away from all previously inspected directions.\\n\\nTo prove the enlarged empirical-average property, the new error is divided into:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{fresh Gaussian empirical average}\\n-\\n\\\\text{its conditional expectation},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{conditional expectation using finite-width contractions}\\n-\\n\\\\text{the same expression using population contractions},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{empirical average of the resulting function of old coordinates}\\n-\\n\\\\text{its population expectation}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first difference vanishes by a sufficiently high-moment law for the fresh Gaussian vector.\\n\\nThe projected Gaussian coordinates are dependent, but only finitely many directions are removed. For example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\n\\\\left(\\nI-P_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\right)\\\\gamma^{(2)}\\n\\\\right\\\\|_2^2\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus the projection is negligible at the level of empirical averages while remaining essential for preserving the exact previous matrix constraints.\\n\\nThe second difference vanishes because every finite-width contraction, response coefficient, and Gaussian variance converges.\\n\\nThe third difference vanishes by the previous induction hypothesis because it involves only previously controlled coordinates.\\n\\nNo central limit theorem is used. The proof establishes that the unscaled empirical errors tend to zero; it does not identify their \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\)-scaled fluctuations.\\n\\nWhat is needed is:\\n\\n- convergence of all required polynomial moments;\\n- sufficiently high moments for the conditional Gaussian sampling argument;\\n- stable ranks when a limiting Gram matrix is singular.\\n\\nIt is not necessary for every constructed vector to remain sub-Gaussian.\\n\\n## The oracle and the trained matrix\\n\\nIterating the second-layer matrix update gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n-\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nr_r\\\\,\\n\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigl(h_r^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}h_k^{(1)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}h_k^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nr_r\\\\,\\n\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_r^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(1)}\\n}{n},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nr_r\\\\,\\nh_r^{(1)}\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n}{n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe rescaled output weights satisfy\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(3)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nr_rh_r^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIn the oracle,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(3)}=0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n=\\n-2\\\\Delta\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nr_rh_r^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe population oracle supplies the deterministic values\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nH_r^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nr_k\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nW_k^{(3)}H_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]-1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe finite oracle uses those deterministic population values in its instructions. Its only matrix-random operations are finitely many calls to\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{and}\\\\quad\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe reused-Gaussian induction proves that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_r^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n-\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nH_r^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\longrightarrow0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n-\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\longrightarrow0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(3)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n-\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nW_k^{(3)}H_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9697c9c32087d2abedbe1f81d3fea1\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-67598072-c085-4392-82cc-5b9a603b0991\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1201,1600p' /home/codex-b/.codex/attachments/52fe5b35-423f-4720-80a0-78c7ff153e30/pasted-text.txt\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"\\\\]\\n\\nThe actual finite Euler network uses its own empirical residuals and contractions. It is compared with the oracle afterward by a deterministic stability estimate.\\n\\nThus the proof separates the reused-Gaussian empirical-law argument from the stability argument for empirical feedback.\\n\\n## The population evolution in the same notation\\n\\nThe same rescaled \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) is used in the limiting autonomous system. There is no change of output-weight variables when passing to the population limit.\\n\\nThe population state consists of\\n\\n\\\\[\\nX^{(1)}(t),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW^{(2)}(t),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW^{(3)}(t).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe derived population quantities are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ^{(1)}\\n=\\nF^{-1}\\\\!\\\\left(X^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nH^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(Z^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ^{(2)}\\n=\\nW^{(2)}H^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nH^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(Z^{(2)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nW^{(3)}H^{(2)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nr=f-1,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n=\\nW^{(3)}\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(Z^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) is the limiting action from first-layer population coordinates to second-layer population coordinates, and \\\\((W^{(2)})^*\\\\) is its adjoint action in the reverse direction.\\n\\nThe autonomous population gradient flow is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{dX^{(1)}}{dt}\\n=\\n-2r\\\\,\\n\\\\bigl(W^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^*\\n\\\\delta^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{dW^{(2)}}{dt}\\n=\\n-2r\\\\,\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{dW^{(3)}}{dt}\\n=\\n-2rH^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe rank-one operator is defined explicitly by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)}\\n\\\\right)q\\n=\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n\\\\,\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nH^{(1)}q\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe initial population state is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ^{(1)}(0)\\\\sim N(0,1),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW^{(2)}(0)=W_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW^{(3)}(0)=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) means the rescaled output weight at every level:\\n\\n- in the finite network;\\n- in the Euler scheme;\\n- in the oracle;\\n- in the empirical convergence statements;\\n- and in the limiting autonomous population flow.\\n\\nNo raw output-weight variable and no barred notation are needed.\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9697cdcc6087d2b5da59ef05be2c94\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying mathematical formulas in cleaned notes**\",\"**Confirming notation consistency in cleaned notes**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9697db535c87d2afb1d38cdc6ae23f\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying mathematical formulas in cleaned notes**\",\"**Confirming notation consistency in cleaned notes**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-7b787b37-51d7-41b9-acee-3f6f378985c1\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'wc -l -w -c /home/codex-b/.codex/attachments/52fe5b35-423f-4720-80a0-78c7ff153e30/pasted-text.txt'\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\" 1328  2403 20386 /home/codex-b/.codex/attachments/52fe5b35-423f-4720-80a0-78c7ff153e30/pasted-text.txt\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9697e33ee087d2b28b70432fdc1594\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_D6M9FTKCiR1JA61l3YtXs3Rc\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a05c41-5c5c-7042-af89-0c951deca811\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/notation_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9697e8525087d2a91768708702db79\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying source scaling calculations**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9697f81c0887d2b373b17f9373e114\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying source scaling calculations**\",\"**Analyzing Euler update transformations and population formulas**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9697ff12a887d2b458e973b1af09b2\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying source scaling calculations**\",\"**Analyzing Euler update transformations and population formulas**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"collabAgentToolCall\",\"id\":\"call_6smRloCNd7p1jmqRm96ibOYF\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"senderThreadId\":\"01a04927-31c8-79d1-bd23-8196b13a1d9b\",\"receiverThreadIds\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"model\":null,\"reasoningEffort\":null},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a96987cb71887d299185ece43109b78\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"collabAgentToolCall\",\"id\":\"call_fboGvN7X41cjN5bEiMCx1rcC\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"senderThreadId\":\"01a04927-31c8-79d1-bd23-8196b13a1d9b\",\"receiverThreadIds\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"model\":null,\"reasoningEffort\":null},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9698f79e8c87d2b6c9fb6b33caf4b6\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9698f83f5087d28db73c39afdaee21\",\"text\":\"I’ve read the entire cleaned version and checked the normalization through the first forward, transpose, and reused-forward calculations. I’m preserving the cleaned document’s notation exactly; the output below will be the rewritten notes themselves, without commentary layered into them.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9698fa373087d29f72b3af107cbd0f\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9698ff074487d28763bb43ddbfe7c3\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"collabAgentToolCall\",\"id\":\"call_BsZ7K6ZnLVzAazlQZ8p5dKSP\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"senderThreadId\":\"01a04927-31c8-79d1-bd23-8196b13a1d9b\",\"receiverThreadIds\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"model\":null,\"reasoningEffort\":null},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a96996f708c87d29ec1a636c243b10e\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing exact cleaned notes re-output**\",\"**Clarifying notation distinctions in oracle section**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a96997cc56487d28ed032dfb0793183\",\"summary\":[\"**Preparing exact cleaned notes re-output**\",\"**Clarifying notation distinctions in oracle section**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"collabAgentToolCall\",\"id\":\"call_H5n80vLhHlwpK1Cj4ldL7klj\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"senderThreadId\":\"01a04927-31c8-79d1-bd23-8196b13a1d9b\",\"receiverThreadIds\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"model\":null,\"reasoningEffort\":null},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a9699c6a51c87d2b47691bb0c13b57b\",\"summary\":[\"**Re-outputting exact cleaned notes**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a05c41-5c84-72c1-bde0-27743ae7597c\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a05c41-5c5c-7042-af89-0c951deca811\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/notation_audit\"},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a9699cdc91487d29397f9b372eccfcd\",\"text\":\"Yes. We should define \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) from the beginning to mean the rescaled output weight used by the proof. There is then no raw output-weight symbol and no bar notation anywhere.\\n\\nThe factor \\\\(1/n\\\\) in the output and the initialization and update of \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) are adjusted accordingly.\\n\\n## Complete finite-width setup\\n\\nFor width \\\\(n\\\\), the evolving parameters are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_k^{(1)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n,\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_k^{(2)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^{n\\\\times n},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_k^{(3)}\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere \\\\(W_k^{(3)}\\\\) always denotes the rescaled output weight. It is the only output-weight variable used anywhere below.\\n\\nThe forward pass is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_k^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_k^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(2)}h_k^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_k^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(2)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,k}\\n=\\n\\\\frac1n\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(3)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s\\n\\\\]\\n\\nacts coordinatewise.\\n\\nThe target is \\\\(1\\\\), so\\n\\n\\\\[\\nr_{n,k}=f_{n,k}-1,\\n\\\\qquad\\nL_{n,k}=r_{n,k}^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe initialization is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,i}^{(1)}\\n\\\\overset{\\\\mathrm{iid}}{\\\\sim}N(0,1),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset{\\\\mathrm{iid}}{\\\\sim}\\nN\\\\left(0,\\\\frac1n\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,j}^{(3)}\\n\\\\overset{\\\\mathrm{iid}}{\\\\sim}\\nN\\\\left(0,\\\\frac1{n^2}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith all initial quantities independent.\\n\\nThus \\\\(W_0^{(3)}\\\\) is already written in its rescaled coordinates. Its coordinates vanish as \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\), so the population oracle sets\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(3)}=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe difference between the small random finite initialization and zero is handled later by the stability comparison.\\n\\nDefine the residual-free backpropagation derivatives by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\n:=\\nn\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_{n,k}}\\n        {\\\\partial z_k^{(\\\\ell)}}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor the second hidden layer,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBackpropagating through \\\\(W_k^{(2)}\\\\) and then through the first activation gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_k^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(1)}\\\\right)\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe corresponding loss derivatives are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial L_{n,k}}\\n     {\\\\partial z_k^{(\\\\ell)}}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{2r_{n,k}}{n}\\\\delta_k^{(\\\\ell)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor \\\\(\\\\phi(s)=\\\\arctan s\\\\), define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF(s)=s+\\\\frac{s^3}{3},\\n\\\\qquad\\nx_k^{(1)}\\n=\\nF\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(1)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF'(s)=1+s^2=\\\\frac1{\\\\phi'(s)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe first-layer activation derivative cancels in the evolution of \\\\(x_k^{(1)}\\\\).\\n\\nWith Euler step \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\), the exact induced updates in the rescaled coordinates are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_{k+1}^{(1)}\\n=\\nx_k^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(2)}\\n-\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}}{n}\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigl(h_k^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_{n,k}h_k^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe last equation is already the update for the rescaled output weights \\\\(W_k^{(3)}\\\\). No conversion to or from another output-weight variable is needed.\\n\\nThe population oracle is analyzed for fixed \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\). For a fixed time horizon \\\\(T\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nN=\\\\left\\\\lceil\\\\frac T\\\\Delta\\\\right\\\\rceil\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis fixed while \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\). Therefore only finitely many forward and transpose calls must be controlled.\\n\\n## The first forward call\\n\\nAt initialization,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_0^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditional on \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_{0,j}^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\sum_{i=1}^n\\nW_{0,ji}^{(2)}h_{0,i}^{(1)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis Gaussian with variance\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}\\n\\\\left(\\nz_{0,j}^{(2)}\\n\\\\mid h_0^{(1)}\\n\\\\right)\\n=\\n\\\\frac1n\\n\\\\sum_{i=1}^n\\n\\\\left(h_{0,i}^{(1)}\\\\right)^2\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}{n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDifferent second-layer coordinates \\\\(j\\\\) use different rows of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\), so they are conditionally independent.\\n\\nThe initial empirical law gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|h_0^{(1)}\\\\|_2^2}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(H_0^{(1)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore the coordinates of \\\\(z_0^{(2)}\\\\) behave empirically like\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\sim\\nN\\\\left(\\n0,\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(H_0^{(1)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe second-layer activation is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_0^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is a coordinatewise function and introduces no new matrix randomness.\\n\\nIn the oracle,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(3)}=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_0=0,\\n\\\\qquad\\nr_0=-1,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_0^{(2)}=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first update gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_1^{(1)}=x_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(2)}=W_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_1^{(3)}\\n=\\n2\\\\Delta h_0^{(2)}\\n=\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt follows that\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_1^{(1)}=h_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nz_1^{(2)}=z_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_1^{(2)}=h_0^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe next second-layer backpropagation derivative is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n&=\\nW_1^{(3)}\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(z_1^{(2)}\\\\right)\\\\\\\\\\n&=\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\,\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(2)}\\\\right)\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEquivalently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\,\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial}{\\\\partial z_0^{(2)}}\\n\\\\frac12\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_0^{(2)}\\\\right)\\n\\\\right\\\\|_2^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe population residual used by the oracle at this step is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nr_1\\n=\\n2\\\\Delta\\\\,\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(Z_0^{(2)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n-1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n## The first transpose call\\n\\nThe first nontrivial transpose operation is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is not multiplication by an independent Gaussian matrix because\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\ndepends on\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor any nonzero vector \\\\(b\\\\), define the ordinary orthogonal projection\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_{b^\\\\perp}\\n=\\nI-\\\\frac{bb^\\\\top}{b^\\\\top b}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor row \\\\(j\\\\) of \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\), the previous forward call supplies the constraint\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{0,j:}^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_{0,j}^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditioning the Gaussian row on this equation gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left(W_{0,j:}^{(2)}\\\\right)^\\\\top\\n\\\\overset d=\\n\\\\frac{\\nz_{0,j}^{(2)}\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}\\nh_0^{(1)}\\n+\\n\\\\widetilde W_{0,j:}^{(2)\\\\top},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere, conditionally,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\widetilde W_{0,j:}^{(2)\\\\top}\\n\\\\sim\\nN\\\\left(\\n0,\\n\\\\frac1nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nMultiplying row \\\\(j\\\\) by \\\\(\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)}\\\\) and summing gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(z_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}\\nh_0^{(1)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\gamma^{(1)},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis independent of the previously constructed vectors.\\n\\nThe first term is the response forced by the old forward call:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(z_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}\\nh_0^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe second term is the genuinely new Gaussian part:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\|\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\|_2}{\\\\sqrt n}\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\gamma^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe response is necessary because of the exact transpose identity\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\bigl(z_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe new Gaussian term is perpendicular to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\), so it contributes zero to the left-hand side. The response term supplies the required overlap exactly.\\n\\nThe induction already knows\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(H_0^{(1)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(z_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore the corresponding population adjoint action is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^*\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n={}&\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nZ_0^{(2)}\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]\\n}{\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(H_0^{(1)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n}\\nH_0^{(1)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\sqrt{\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right]\\n}\\n\\\\,\\\\Gamma^{(1)},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Gamma^{(1)}\\\\sim N(0,1)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis independent of the previous first-layer population coordinates.\\n\\nThe finite coordinates of\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nare not claimed to be iid. The conclusion is that their joint empirical averages with all previous first-layer coordinates approach the corresponding population expectations.\\n\\n## The next forward call\\n\\nThe first-layer transformed coordinate now moves according to\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_2^{(1)}\\n=\\nx_1^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_1\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_2^{(1)}\\n=\\nF^{-1}\\\\!\\\\left(x_2^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_2^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_2^{(1)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEach coordinate of \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) is a deterministic scalar function of previously controlled first-layer coordinates. Therefore its joint empirical behavior follows from the existing induction hypothesis.\\n\\nThe next difficult operation is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt is not an independent Gaussian multiplication because \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) depends on\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAt this point the matrix history contains both\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditioning \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) simultaneously on both observations gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\left[\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]^\\\\top\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\widetilde W_0^{(2)}\\\\) is a fresh independent Gaussian matrix with entries \\\\(N(0,1/n)\\\\).\\n\\nThe first term retains\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_0^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe second retains\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe final term is invisible to both previously inspected matrix directions.\\n\\nMultiplying by \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\n\\\\overset d={}&\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_0^{(1)}\\n}\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\left[\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]^\\\\top\\nh_2^{(1)}\\n}{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\nP_{(h_0^{(1)})^\\\\perp}\\nh_2^{(1)}\\n\\\\right\\\\|_2\\n}{\\n\\\\sqrt n\\n}\\nP_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\gamma^{(2)},\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\gamma^{(2)}\\\\sim N(0,I_n)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis fresh and independent of the previous history.\\n\\nThe first response term comes from the component of \\\\(h_2^{(1)}\\\\) parallel to \\\\(h_0^{(1)}\\\\). Since\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthat component must be mapped to the corresponding multiple of \\\\(z_0^{(2)}\\\\).\\n\\nThe second response term is forced by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\left[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]^\\\\top\\nh_2^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first response term supplies part of the required overlap. The second response term supplies the remainder.\\n\\nThe fresh Gaussian term is perpendicular to \\\\(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\), so it does not disturb this exact constraint.\\n\\nEvery coefficient in this formula is an empirical contraction of previously controlled vectors. For example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_0^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_2^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nH_0^{(1)}H_2^{(1)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\left(\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\\\right)^2\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore all response coefficients and Gaussian variances converge to their population values.\\n\\n## The exact induction hypothesis\\n\\nThe first-layer collection now contains quantities such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nx_2^{(1)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_2^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor every fixed polynomially bounded function \\\\(\\\\psi\\\\), the induction asserts\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_{i=1}^n\\n\\\\psi\\\\Bigl(\\n&z_{0,i}^{(1)},\\nh_{0,i}^{(1)},\\n\\\\left[\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\delta_1^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]_i,\\nx_{2,i}^{(1)},\\nh_{2,i}^{(1)},\\n\\\\ldots\\n\\\\Bigr)\\n\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\;\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\,\\\\psi\\\\Bigl(\\n&Z_0^{(1)},\\nH_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^*\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\nX_2^{(1)},\\nH_2^{(1)},\\n\\\\ldots\\n\\\\Bigr).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe second-layer collection contains quantities such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\nz_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_1^{(3)},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\n\\\\qquad\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIts empirical statement is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_{j=1}^n\\n\\\\psi\\\\Bigl(\\n&z_{0,j}^{(2)},\\nh_{0,j}^{(2)},\\nW_{1,j}^{(3)},\\n\\\\delta_{1,j}^{(2)},\\n\\\\left[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_2^{(1)}\\n\\\\right]_j,\\n\\\\ldots\\n\\\\Bigr)\\n\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\\\;\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\,\\\\psi\\\\Bigl(\\n&Z_0^{(2)},\\nH_0^{(2)},\\nW_1^{(3)},\\n\\\\delta_1^{(2)},\\nW_0^{(2)}H_2^{(1)},\\n\\\\ldots\\n\\\\Bigr).\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first-layer index \\\\(i\\\\) and second-layer index \\\\(j\\\\) label different neuron populations. There is no artificial coordinatewise pairing between them.\\n\\nPolynomially bounded means that, for some fixed constants \\\\(C\\\\) and \\\\(q\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\psi(s)|\\n\\\\le\\nC\\\\left(1+\\\\|s\\\\|_2^q\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTaking\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\psi(s)=s^2\\n\\\\]\\n\\ngives empirical second-moment convergence.\\n\\nTaking\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\psi(s,t)=st\\n\\\\]\\n\\ngives contractions such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_r^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nH_r^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(3)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nW_k^{(3)}H_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe last convergence is exactly the convergence of the oracle prediction.\\n\\nHigher powers provide the moment control needed to prevent a small number of unusually large coordinates from dominating these averages.\\n\\nThe induction also maintains stable linear relations among the vectors previously supplied to \\\\(W_0^{(2)}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\\\).\\n\\nThis is necessary because simultaneous Gaussian conditioning produces finite Gram matrices with entries such as\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_r^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_s^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_s^{(2)}\\n}{n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIf a limiting Gram matrix is singular, entrywise convergence alone does not guarantee convergence of its inverse or pseudoinverse. The proof must distinguish a genuinely new direction from a direction that is already represented by previous ones.\\n\\nFor example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_1^{(1)}=h_0^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}h_1^{(1)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}h_0^{(1)}\\n=\\nz_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nexactly. This repeated source direction creates no fresh Gaussian component.\\n\\nMore generally, the induction proves:\\n\\n- a genuinely new source direction has a positive limiting distance from the old source span and therefore a positive Gaussian innovation variance;\\n- a direction with zero limiting distance is eventually an exact combination of the retained core directions;\\n- the relevant Gram ranks and pseudoinverses therefore stabilize.\\n\\nThus the induction maintains both joint empirical-moment convergence and stable linear relations.\\n\\nCoordinatewise operations are easy to add. For example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nh_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{k+1}^{(3)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta r_kh_k^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n\\\\odot\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(z_k^{(2)}\\\\right)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nare coordinatewise functions of already controlled second-layer vectors, with deterministic oracle coefficient \\\\(r_k\\\\). They introduce no new matrix randomness.\\n\\nThe difficult operation is a new call to\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConditioning on every previous forward and transpose call gives a finite response made from old network vectors plus Gaussian randomness projected away from all previously inspected directions.\\n\\nTo prove the enlarged empirical-average property, the new error is divided into:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{fresh Gaussian empirical average}\\n-\\n\\\\text{its conditional expectation},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{conditional expectation using finite-width contractions}\\n-\\n\\\\text{the same expression using population contractions},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{empirical average of the resulting function of old coordinates}\\n-\\n\\\\text{its population expectation}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first difference vanishes by a sufficiently high-moment law for the fresh Gaussian vector.\\n\\nThe projected Gaussian coordinates are dependent, but only finitely many directions are removed. For example,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\n\\\\left(\\nI-P_{(\\\\delta_1^{(2)})^\\\\perp}\\n\\\\right)\\\\gamma^{(2)}\\n\\\\right\\\\|_2^2\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus the projection is negligible at the level of empirical averages while remaining essential for preserving the exact previous matrix constraints.\\n\\nThe second difference vanishes because every finite-width contraction, response coefficient, and Gaussian variance converges.\\n\\nThe third difference vanishes by the previous induction hypothesis because it involves only previously controlled coordinates.\\n\\nNo central limit theorem is used. The proof establishes that the unscaled empirical errors tend to zero; it does not identify their \\\\(\\\\sqrt n\\\\)-scaled fluctuations.\\n\\nWhat is needed is:\\n\\n- convergence of all required polynomial moments;\\n- sufficiently high moments for the conditional Gaussian sampling argument;\\n- stable ranks when a limiting Gram matrix is singular.\\n\\nIt is not necessary for every constructed vector to remain sub-Gaussian.\\n\\n## The oracle and the trained matrix\\n\\nIterating the second-layer matrix update gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n-\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\Delta}{n}\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nr_r\\\\,\\n\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\n\\\\bigl(h_r^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(2)}h_k^{(1)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(2)}h_k^{(1)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nr_r\\\\,\\n\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_r^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(1)}\\n}{n},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nr_r\\\\,\\nh_r^{(1)}\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n}{n}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe rescaled output weights satisfy\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n=\\nW_0^{(3)}\\n-\\n2\\\\Delta\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nr_rh_r^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIn the oracle,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(3)}=0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_k^{(3)}\\n=\\n-2\\\\Delta\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\nr_rh_r^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe population oracle supplies the deterministic values\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nH_r^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nr_k\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nW_k^{(3)}H_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]-1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe finite oracle uses those deterministic population values in its instructions. Its only matrix-random operations are finitely many calls to\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_0^{(2)}\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{and}\\\\quad\\n\\\\bigl(W_0^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe reused-Gaussian induction proves that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(h_r^{(1)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(1)}\\n}{n}\\n-\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nH_r^{(1)}H_k^{(1)}\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\longrightarrow0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top\\n\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n-\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\n\\\\delta_r^{(2)}\\\\delta_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\longrightarrow0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\n\\\\bigl(W_k^{(3)}\\\\bigr)^\\\\top h_k^{(2)}\\n}{n}\\n-\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nW_k^{(3)}H_k^{(2)}\\n\\\\right]\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe actual finite Euler network uses its own empirical residuals and contractions. It is compared with the oracle afterward by a deterministic stability estimate.\\n\\nThus the proof separates the reused-Gaussian empirical-law argument from the stability argument for empirical feedback.\\n\\n## The population evolution in the same notation\\n\\nThe same rescaled \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) is used in the limiting autonomous system. There is no change of output-weight variables when passing to the population limit.\\n\\nThe population state consists of\\n\\n\\\\[\\nX^{(1)}(t),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW^{(2)}(t),\\n\\\\qquad\\nW^{(3)}(t).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe derived population quantities are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ^{(1)}\\n=\\nF^{-1}\\\\!\\\\left(X^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nH^{(1)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(Z^{(1)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ^{(2)}\\n=\\nW^{(2)}H^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nH^{(2)}\\n=\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(Z^{(2)}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nW^{(3)}H^{(2)}\\n\\\\right],\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nr=f-1,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n=\\nW^{(3)}\\n\\\\phi'\\\\!\\\\left(Z^{(2)}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere \\\\(W^{(2)}\\\\) is the limiting action from first-layer population coordinates to second-layer population coordinates, and \\\\(\\\\bigl(W^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^*\\\\) is its adjoint action in the reverse direction.\\n\\nThe autonomous population gradient flow is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{dX^{(1)}}{dt}\\n=\\n-2r\\\\,\\n\\\\bigl(W^{(2)}\\\\bigr)^*\\n\\\\delta^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{dW^{(2)}}{dt}\\n=\\n-2r\\\\,\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{dW^{(3)}}{dt}\\n=\\n-2rH^{(2)}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe rank-one operator is defined explicitly by\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}\\\\otimes H^{(1)}\\n\\\\right)a\\n=\\n\\\\delta^{(2)}\\n\\\\,\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\left[\\nH^{(1)}a\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe initial population state is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ^{(1)}(0)\\\\sim N(0,1),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW^{(2)}(0)=W_0^{(2)},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW^{(3)}(0)=0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus \\\\(W^{(3)}\\\\) means the rescaled output weight at every level:\\n\\n- in the finite network;\\n- in the Euler scheme;\\n- in the oracle;\\n- in the empirical convergence statements;\\n- and in the limiting autonomous population flow.\\n\\nNo raw output-weight variable and no barred notation are needed.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a05c13-0304-7151-8d22-f31a3da77650\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1788251144,\"completedAt\":1788251855,\"durationMs\":710499,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_08e791b925547dcf016a968c33501487d2ba9281b7d76cbb70\",\"text\":\"The delicate point is the first matrix call whose input already depends on earlier calls. I’m checking the primary reused-Gaussian proof at that exact conditioning step so the explanation distinguishes what follows from elementary linear algebra from what needs the strong empirical-law argument.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-8f97e83f-28ad-4235-81fc-87b57836fe92\",\"query\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685v3\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.10685v3\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a968c39237087d28b31cec7fddc392c\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-f3b84573-9bb8-4aec-babf-5419c49839e1\",\"query\":\"site:arxiv.org/abs/2009.10685 Tensor Programs III Neural Matrix Laws v3\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"search\",\"query\":\"site:arxiv.org/abs/2009.10685 Tensor Programs III Neural Matrix Laws v3\",\"queries\":null}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_08e791b925547dcf016a968c41893c87d280d82b20e128894c\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-8b7940b9-2b5d-41b3-8688-7bbd0bb55934\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg --files /tmp /home/amir/Codes/PDE | rg '2009\\\\\\\\.10685|Tensor.?Programs|proofs\\\\\\\\.tex|Neural.Matrix' | head -100\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"rg: /tmp/pymp-9llwo46d: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/torchelastic_7mb2qntr: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/pymp-d5qy2izj: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/tmpmn1bcb4d: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/tmp_6gc3cvy: Permission denied (os error 13)\\nrg: /tmp/torchelastic_4mkba